    skos:prefLabel "Enablers of and barriers to successful implementation of each of the 14" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.12> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Actor typologies are those discussed in Chapter 4, section 4.3.4. Influencer actors are people and institutions that influence decision-making processes related to the nexus and therefore have an impact on those who implement decisions; this includes those that can act as lobbyists or as watchdogs to foster accountability through advocacy. Affected actors are people, including communities and marginalized groups, who are directly involved in (and dependent on) the implementation of nexus-related decisions and who have their own stakes and interests. Key players are people and institutions that can both influence and become affected by decisions. Actors not involved neither exercise influence nor are they affected (see Chapter 4, section 4.2.6)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actor involvement in the 14 biodiversity response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.13> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The plot shows multiple actors (left) related to each biodiversity response option (right) according to the description of more than 2000 examples of implementations of response options examined. Boxes and line width are proportional to the number of examples in the assessed literature where the actors are involved in a given type of response option." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Involvement of different categories of actors in the planning and implementation of biodiversity response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.14> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Implementation, lead or target role of actors of different categories as described in the more that 2000 examples of implementation of response options for biodiversity examined." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of different actors and institutions implementing biodiversity response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.15> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The networks show the interaction among multiple actors in six examples of implementations of different response options examined. Two implementations of area-based conservation: (A) locally managed marine areas in Kenya and (B) Kristianstads Vattenrike Biosphere Reserve, Sweden.Three implementations of integrated landscape-seascape approaches: (C) Yasuni Biosphere Reserve, Ecuador, (D) Scolel Té, México and (E) Xinshe socioecological production landscape and seascape approach to well-being, the Satoyama Initiative, Taiwan, Province of China. One implementation of urban nature-based solutions: (F) Singapore Green Plan 2030. Node colours represent different categories of actors." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Examples of collaboration networks among actors in the planning and implementation of biodiversity response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.16> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The number of nexus elements per response option that contain a negative score for any of their scores for the impacts on nexus elements represented in Figure 5.1.2 (left); and the number of elements with a median positive impact score (right). The size of the end points indicates the number of sources supporting a response option." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.16" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Benefits and trade-offs of identified response options with respect to nexus elements." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.17> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Participation increases from informing, the lowest level of participation, to supporting community interests. Points are coloured based on whether the response option being scored benefits more than three nexus elements (median positive score) with no negative scores at all or incurs possible trade-offs. A response option was assumed to incur a trade-off if any of the indicator scores for impacts on each nexus element were negative (see Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.17" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Social effectiveness, economic effectiveness and distributional equity scores for different participation levels." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.18> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "(A) Centroids of countries with the number of sources originating from the country. (B) Direct drivers targeted by response options per region (Africa: AFR; Americas: AME; Asia and the Pacific: ASP; Europe and Central Asia: ECA). Drivers: habitat loss and degradation (HAB); exploitation of wild populations (EXP); climate change (CLM); pollution of soil, water and air (POLL); species introductions (SPE); other (OTH); and no info (MSG). (C) Indirect drivers targeted by response options per region: economic (ECO); demographic (DEM); governance (GOV); values (VAL); technological (TEC); no info (MSG)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.18" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Geographical spread of literature sources supporting biodiversity response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.1.19> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.1.19" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Biodiversity response options supporting achievement of global goals." .

