<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.12> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Governance approaches for climate action involve actors, institutions and policy instruments. Actors include a diverse group, from governments to grassroots communities, each with varying degrees of power. Policy mechanisms encompass laws, financial incentives and culturally based approaches to enable climate action, such as facilitating the uptake of response options for systems transitions. Power distribution among actors is visually represented through colour gradation, with darker shades indicating more influence. These elements create a complex but integral framework for effective climate governance." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Governance approaches for climate action." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.13> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Actor typologies are those discussed in Chapter 4, section 4.3.4. Influencer actors are people and institutions that influence decision-making processes related to the nexus and therefore have an impact on those who implement decisions; this includes those that can act as lobbyists or as watchdogs to foster accountability through advocacy. Affected actors are people, including communities and marginalized groups, who are directly involved in (and dependent on) the implementation of nexus-related decisions and who have their own stakes and interests. Key players are people and institutions that can both influence and become affected by decisions. Actors not involved neither exercise influence nor are they affected (see Chapter 4, section 4.2.6)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actor involvement in the 15 climate change response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.14> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "See Chapter 4, section 4.2.4 and Table 4.4 for descriptions of the policy typologies. Note: Climate action-related policies and procedures contributing to the nexus are provided in Table 5.5.15 and therefore are not included in this figure." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Classification of each of the 15 climate change response options as an action or management (direct implementation on the ground), a policy instrument that guides action and/or an enabler that facilitates or improves an action, management or policy." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.15> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "See Chapter 4, section 4.2.5 and Table 4.6 for descriptions of the enablers and barriers." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enablers of and barriers to successful implementation of each of the" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.16> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.16" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate change response options supporting achievement of global goals." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The figure visualizes the interplay between the diverse actors involved in climate governance, who require orchestration to ensure coordination, equity of opportunity, implementation gaps and associated values for all stakeholders. The “top-down” and “bottom-up” refer to the authority levels of actors in achieving outcomes. Top-down involves central decision-making, often through command and control, while bottom-up entails actions from groups intended to implement policies (Kuyper et al., 2018). *Note: The level of action is not prescribed, as some actors operate at multiple levels and arenas. Figure based on Morrison et. al (2019) and Petzold et. al (2023)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actors, action and challenges in climate change mitigation and adaptation." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.3> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Impacts of climate change response options on nexus elements." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.4> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate change response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, international frameworks and transformative change potential." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.5> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Ranges shown temporal and spatial scales and feasibility indicated by Technology Readiness Level." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the climate response" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.6> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Data for anthropogenic emissions by sectors and regions are from (Hoesly et al., 2018) and the main impact pathways are adapted from (Naik et al., 2021). Short-lived climate pollutants affect the climate system by inducing changes in solar radiation, surface albedo and clouds or precipitation, resulting in either cooling or warming effects. Climate change can in turn induce changes in emissions of short-lived climate pollutants and atmospheric chemistry and composition (climate feedbacks). Short-lived climate pollutants also affect human health, biodiversity, water and food systems through a variety of mechanisms. Interventions aiming to reduce emissions of short-lived climate pollutants are thus linked to multiple implications for the nexus elements and are usually associated with abatements in emissions of CO2 (as they are typically co-emitted from combustion processes). SOx = sulphates; NOx = nitrogen oxides; BC = black carbon; OC = organic carbon; CO – carbon monoxide; VOC = volatile organic carbon; NH3 = ammonium; PM = particulate matter; O3 = ozone precursors." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sources, processes and effects across the nexus elements of mitigation of emissions of short-lived climate pollutants." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.7> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Andhra Pradesh Community Managed Natural Farming initiative in" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.8> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Gross Domestic Product (GDP) data from the Brazilian Statistics Institute (IBGE, 2023); deforestation data from the Brazilian National Institute for Space Research (INPE, 2023). Data refers to the Brazilian states within the Legal Amazon." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon over time." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.5.9> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Mercury river contamination and deforestation deplete fish and game populations, bringing about food insecurity as traditional sources decline." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.5.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Deforestation caused by illegal mining in the Yanomami Indigenous Peoples’ territory." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The 71 response options assessed in Chapter 5 are displayed in wedges representing 10 broad categories of actions (labelled on the outer edge of the circle). Unique alphanumeric codes for each response option indicate its nexus element (B for biodiversity, W for water, F for food, H for health and C for climate change) and its corresponding number in the relevant subchapter of Chapter 5. These codes are used to identify response options throughout the report. Several response options correspond to more than one category but, for clarity, are displayed only in their primary category. The “Others” category includes four important response options that do not correspond easily to any of the nine main categories. Response options with an asterisk (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) were each assessed in two different subchapters and both alphanumeric codes are therefore shown." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Many response options already exist that a range of actors can implement." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Points indicate the location of each case study itself, but case studies are examples of response options with broader applicability. Each text box lists the name of the case study, the location for implementation and the subchapter, including the corresponding response option alphanumeric code and, in parentheses, the subchapter section number." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Locations of case studies representing the 24 focal response options presented throughout Chapter 5." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.3> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For each of the response options assessed in Chapter 5, circles indicate the estimated impacts on each element of the nexus. Larger circles indicate stronger impacts on that element, and several large circles in a row indicate more widespread impacts across elements of the nexus. Most impacts are positive (blue), but a few response options have negative impacts (red) on some nexus elements. Response options are organized into the same categories used in Figure 5.6.1. Unique alphanumeric codes for each response option indicate its nexus element (B for biodiversity, W for water, F for food, H for health and C for climate change) and its corresponding number in the relevant subchapter of Chapter 5. These codes are used to identify response options throughout the report. Impact scores are based on a thorough review of existing evidence, synthesized and averaged across several component criteria on a scale of -3 to +3 for each nexus element. Response options for which evidence for component indicators was inconclusive (IC) or non-existent (NE) are labelled as such, and those for which evidence was deemed inconclusive for some criteria and non-existent for others are labelled as IC/NE. Response options with an asterisk (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) were each assessed in two different subchapters, and both alphanumeric codes are therefore shown. For these response options, circles represent an average score from the two subchapters in which they were assessed." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Response options have substantial but widely varying impacts on the five nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.4> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Each vertical line represents a shared response option, with a general label. Dots represent the subchapters that identified and scored a given response option. Specific response options that comprise each shared response option are listed in Table 5.6.2. For example, Restoration – coastal and marine systems is a response option chosen and scored by the biodiversity (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.6, response option B06), health (Chapter 5.4, section 5.4.3.8, response option H08) and climate change (Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.13, response option C13) subchapters. Numbers and bar lengths to the right indicate the number of shared response options in each subchapter. Those numbers can exceed the number of dots for that element because in several cases multiple specific response options contribute to a shared response option." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Response options shared by two or more elements of the nexus." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.5> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "From left to right: single response options implemented alone have certain impacts on one or more nexus elements; bundling several response options and implementing them together can result in greater total impacts on more nexus elements, because some response options enable or synergize with others; similarly, by sequencing response options, those implemented first can establish enabling conditions for others implemented later, increasing their impacts compared to individual implementation." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Response options can have amplified effects when implemented together, either in simultaneous bundles or in strategic sequence." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.6> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For seven types of enablers/barriers, dots indicate the number of response options for which successful implementation is enabled (enabler) or hindered (barrier) or for which the role of the enabler/barrier is unclear or where the role cannot be determined. A given enabler/barrier can be an enabler for some response options, a barrier for others and both an enabler and barrier for the same response option. This figure presents pooled information across all elements; for analogous figures for each nexus element, see Chapter 5.6, online Supplementary material 5.6.1, Figure SM.5.6.1. See Chapter 4, Table 4.6 for descriptions of the enablers and barriers." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enablers and barriers affecting implementation of the response options assessed in Chapter 5." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.7> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This figure presents six main criteria relevant to key attributes, out of the 10 total criteria used by subchapters to assess response options. Each main criterion has two to three indicators (columns). As in the original subchapter tables from which this table is assembled, blue shades indicate positive impacts and red shades indicate negative impacts. Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey indicates a score of 0. White indicates either inconclusive evidence (INC) or no evidence (NE). Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. Note the United Nations Food Systems Pathways were only assessed by the subchapter focused on the nexus element of food (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key attributes of focal response options assessed in Chapter 5." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.8> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Panels depict nexus impact score versus (A) distributional equity, (B) technical feasibility and (C) gross economic costs. In each panel, points represent each of the response options assessed in this chapter (Table 5.6.1). Colours denote the nexus element of origin. All scores are based on assessed criteria in subchapters for each nexus element (Chapter 5.0.3, Table 5.0.1). Criteria scored as minimum/maximum ranges due to uncertainty are represented here as the average of the minimum and maximum scores. Labelled points indicate response options noted in the text as examples." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Relationships between nexus-wide impacts and three intrinsic attributes of response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure5.6.9> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options provide broad and varying support to goals and targets of global policy frameworks. (A) Number of response options supporting achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals. Stacked bars correspond to each Goal, with each section of a given bar indicating the number of response options from each nexus element. (B) Number of response options supporting achievement of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, using the same format as panel A. (C) Number of goals or targets under the three global policy frameworks supported by each category of response options. For each of the 10 categories of response options indicated in Figure 5.6.1, bars represent the degree of support for each policy framework. Bar length represents the percentage of all goals or targets supported by response options in that category, while numbers indicate the number of goals or targets supported. For example, the category “Conserve ecosystems” supports 11 (out of 17) of the Sustainable Development Goals, 15 (out of 23) of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, and both the long-term goal for mitigation and the long-term goal for adaptation of the Paris Agreement (response option support for the third long term goal, which addresses climate finance, was not assessed in the subchapters of Chapter 5)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure5.6.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Linkages between response options and global policy frameworks." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chapter structure." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.10> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Business-as-usual financial structures and harmful, inequitable and fragmented actions entrench unsustainable economic and societal systems (left panel; see sections 6.1 and 6.2). Transformative finance and societal transformation drive system transitions within industrial, energy, urban and infrastructure and land, water and ecosystems (right panel). Such interconnected system transitions support sustainable outcomes including strengthened biodiversity, water and food security, health and well-being and reduced climate risk (Chapter 5)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Transformative finance and societal transformation enable interconnected system transitions and help build resilient societies." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.11> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The left panel exemplifies (from the bottom to the top) people-nature separation and values underpinning siloed and inequitable governance approaches. Such governance interacts with drivers of change and fragmented, unsustainable actions to produce negative trends, adverse outcomes and increasing risks across the nexus elements that together constrain future response options. The right panel exemplifies (from the bottom to the top) people-nature connected and related values and world views that underpin nexus governance and equitable response options. Such transformative values, governance and response options can advance nexus interlinkages and outcomes and enable human well-being and planetary health." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Transformative, interconnected and just response options are urgently needed." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "(A) Estimated financial flows to nature/biodiversity by the OECD and the Paulson Institute (Deutz et al., 2020; OECD, 2020a, 2021), and towards NbS estimated by UNEP (UNEP, 2023e; UNEP et al., 2021; UNEP & ELD, 2022) are disaggregated by public, private or mixed (both public and private financing) sources, with error bars plotted wherever they were provided in the original reports, years relate to the year of publication (note, while UNEP 2023 shows error bars in their report they do not provide the underlying data). (B) Average estimated financial flows to nature/biodiversity/NbS categorized by levels of uncertainty reported by these data sources. As shown, these sources rarely provide any statement regarding the certainty of these data, and only the UNEP’s State of Finance for Nature in 2022 provide upper and lower estimates for their financial flows. (C) Average estimated financial flows to nature/biodiversity/NbS categorized by various instruments which are used to channel these flows, with ODA indicating “Official Development Assistance”. The OECD 2020 estimates shown here are higher than the headline figures for the source report – $78 billion to $91 billion per year (OECD, 2020a), as they include approximately $43 billion from impact investment which is estimated in the report, but not integrated into the headline results given under certainty on their scale and impact; they are included here to try to give a more holistic view. In this regard, how different sources have chosen to classify specific flows / instruments highly influences how much total financial flows are estimated and reported (Box 6.4)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Estimated financial flows to nature varying across selected sources." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.3> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The most up-to-date (2022) estimation of financial flows directed towards nature-based solutions (NbS) compiled by (UNEP, 2023e) are used. The total volume of flows represented is $200 billion, the majority of which ($165 billion) comes from public sources. Estimates include both mainstream biodiversity/conservation finance, as well as – in seeking to assess NbS finance – capturing some finance flows which build on nexus relationships, e.g., biodiversity and water, or biodiversity and agriculture. Connections of financial flows to nexus elements are based on the documentation provided by the report; for instance, public funds that direct domestic budgets towards “sustainable agriculture, forestry and fisheries” were classified as “food”, “biodiversity” and “water”. As this figure reflects UNEP’s accounting of flows towards NbS, it does not reflect either (i) financial flows that have a nature-negative impact; nor (ii) financial flows directed towards particular nexus elements (e.g., climate change, health) that were not considered to also have benefits for nature and therefore not identified as NbS. Note for instance that financial flows to climate change more broadly are estimated to be over $1 trillion annually (Buchner et al., 2019), and thus much larger than represented here. An interactive version of this figure is available. The methodology to create the figure is described in the associated data management report." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Current sources of financial flows to nature-based solutions and their likely connection to nexus elements." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.4> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Financial support amounts are in billions USD. Public and private sectors contribute to nature-positive activities via different instruments. Public financial support is primarily dispersed via domestic budgets for biodiversity conservation, agriculture, forestry and fisheries, water, as well as pollution, wastewater management and other environmental protection and environmental policy measures. Official development assistance accounts for a small proportion of public financial support ($2.2 billion). Financial support from the private sector is dispersed via instruments such as investments in sustainable supply chains, biodiversity offsets, impact investments (financial investments that seek measurable environmental improvements alongside financial returns), other voluntary credit markets and philanthropy (UNEP, 2023e)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Annual public and private financial support for nature-based solutions." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.5> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Amounts are in billions per year. Left side (red): direct negative impacts. Right side (blue): finance for nature-based solutions (NbS) as described in Figure 6.3 and Figure 6.4. Estimates are based on the latest available data (UNEP, 2023a), which estimates sector-specific private financing for the first time. This data is supplemented with estimates of the global value of environmental crimes shown in the bottom right (red): illegal wildlife trade, illegal mining and extraction and illegal forestry and fishing (OECD, 2021). Sizes of the boxes indicate the average of estimates between upper and lower ranges where provided." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Annual public and private financial flows with direct negative impacts on nature compared to finance for nature-based solutions." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.6> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Under a business-as-usual political economy, there are multiple extractive flows of resources from societies and ecosystems concentrating wealth in particular areas and among some groups and leading to ecological damage (often expressed as externalities) and social injustices. Only a small portion of finance flows support people and nature due to a combination of factors including inadequate environmental and development finance; predominance of nature-negative investments; and inequitable relations and siloed forms of governance." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Finance and resource flows under a business-as-usual political economy generate inequitable and unsustainable pressures across the nexus elements." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.7> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The perspectives (corners of the triangle) are represented through three gradients that reflect key transformative change debates: (1) land sparing versus land sharing, (2) half Earth versus whole Earth and (3) green growth versus post-growth economic development (Durán et al., 2023; IPBES, 2022b)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The three value perspectives of nature in the Nature Futures Framework." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.8> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Financial and economic arenas of engagement encompass decisions taking place through interactions between diverse actors at different scales. In business-as-usual arenas of engagement (left), interactions and decisions are characterized by domination by some actors, knowledge systems and values as well as weak collaboration, leading to fragmented and unsustainable actions. The quality of interactions that enable transformative financial decisions are exemplified in the right panel. These actions are embedded in nature-society connectedness and bounded by holistic values, ethics and world views. Decisions made through these interactions can cumulatively lead to finance and political economy outcomes that transform development to advance more just and sustainable futures." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Decision-making interactions between diverse actors in economic and financial arenas can lead to transformative finance response options." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure6.9> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Economic and financial arenas support response options (illustrated on the left-hand side, see section 6.2) that can drive transformative, just and interconnected action within the political, community, sociocultural, ecological and knowledge-technology arenas of engagement." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure6.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Transformative decision-making in economic and financial arenas of engagement can transform practice and interactions in other arenas, together enabling societal transformation to enhance nexus outcomes." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure7.1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Addressing nexus challenges will require a number of interlinked, flexible steps to ensure that the right interactions are being addressed across the nexus elements, the right actors are involved in the process and the right decisions are being negotiated that are consistent with values and visions of stakeholders. Actors working together across each of the suggested eight steps can move towards context-relevant and appropriate outcomes, including just and sustainable futures, particularly if enacted through negotiated and collaborative problem-solving in an iterative manner. Clusters of steps cover exploration of the contexts of nexus problems, coordination and strategic action to address problems using a nexus approach, and implementation and scaling of solutions, with decision-support tools available along the way. The clusters of steps also highlight the importance of iterative monitoring and evaluation. Each step along the road map is important but not necessarily sequential, as demonstrated by the black arrows showing steps that move decision-making processes forward and the grey dashed arrows showing where steps may need to be revisited or implemented in a different order, thus entailing experimentation. Some steps need to be taken multiple times in an iterative manner (e.g., monitoring of change to re-negotiate and update planning and implementation over time as needed, with moves back to step 1 and ongoing social learning and adaptation). Green dotted lines indicate course correction, which can be supported by consideration of specific safeguards (see 7.3.7) to ensure recalibration back to sustainable pathways." ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Schematic of different steps of the road map for nexus action." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure7.2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    dcterms:identifier "Figure7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Models and tools for characterizing nexus interactions can range from qualitative to quantitative and from limited to more intensive participation." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Detailed definitions of the five nexus elements are given in Chapter 1, Box 1.1. Each nexus element is represented by a unique colour throughout the figures in this assessment: biodiversity is represented by light green; water is represented by teal; food is represented by orange; health is represented by dark red; and climate change is represented by light purple." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key concepts and definitions used in the nexus assessment." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.A1> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Confidence increases towards the top-right corner, as suggested by the increasing strength of shading. Source: IPBES (2016). Further details of the approach are documented in the IPBES Guide on the Production of Assessments." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.A1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The four-box model for the qualitative communication of confidence." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.A2> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Illustration of how the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change intersect with the broad categories from the IPBES conceptual framework of “nature”, “nature’s contributions to people”, “good quality of life”, “direct drivers”, “anthropogenic assets” and “institutions, governance and other indirect drivers” (boxes). Examples, where relevant, are added in each box in the order of the nexus elements: biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change {1.2.3}." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.A2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus elements and interactions in the IPBES conceptual framework." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.A3> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For each nexus element (biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change), the figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaption of the Paris Agreement. The figure includes all response options assessed in Chapter 5; the response options independently assessed by two subchapters (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) are presented separately in this figure for each of the subchapters that assessed these options. Response options are grouped by nexus element and listed in numerical order by response option alphanumeric code (B for biodiversity, W for water, F for food, H for health and C for climate change). Filled cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.A3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Response options supporting achievement of global goals." .

