    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.4.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Crossing all five nexus elements are the series of Global Environment Outlook (GEO) reports by the UN Environment Programme, which serve as comprehensive and authoritative assessments of the state of the global environment. The most recently published, the sixth Global Environment Outlook (GEO-6) (UNEP, 2019), used the Drivers-Pressures-State-Impact-Response approach to assess the global situation of the state of the environment, its relevant challenges, policies and ways forward. The elements of the nexus were represented in GEO-6 from the Healthy Planet, Healthy People perspective with an emphasis on the socio-economic dimensions affecting the state of the global environment (terrestrial and marine biodiversity, freshwater, land and soil). Climate change was listed among the five main drivers affecting the health of the planet, with food (coupled with energy and waste) as the main socio-economic systems with far-reaching environmental effects. The upcoming GEO-7 report will expand on GEO-6 by evaluating the condition and trajectories of the global environment, their repercussions on human welfare, and the attainment of the SDGs for various stakeholders. The report aims to provide insights into the potential environmental and socio-economic consequences of transformative changes in the interdependent systems of food, energy and pollution. The GEO reports also consider the interconnections between different nexus elements and their impact on ecosystems and human well-being." ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.4.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cross-cutting reports" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7.4.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VHXRK8T8> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Many of the scenarios reported here were based on the SSP/RCP scenario framing that emerged from the climate change assessment community. The SSPs/RCPs have been used extensively to inform climate policy, notably through IPCC assessments and Special Reports. While attempts have been made to adapt the SSPs/RCPs for application in biodiversity assessments, the fundamental climate-focus of this scenario framing limits its applicability in biodiversity decision-making. Consequently, more nature-focused scenario framings have emerged such as the NFF (Box 3.2). In contrast to the exploratory SSPs, the NFF takes a normative approach based on alternative nature value perspectives. This is an attempt to make the approach more solutions-orientated with a focus on nature-oriented futures that can inform decision-making. How this might happen in practice is likely to emerge through time, as this relatively recent approach is used more extensively. Developments to advance the use of the NFF in decision-making could include the creation of NFF narratives based on interpretation of indirect drivers for the alternative value perspectives, and quantification of the direct drivers and biodiversity consequences using models (see section 3.7.4.3).

Other approaches to scenario development are more explicit about policy targets, including, for example, protected area targets of 30 per cent or 50 per cent (e.g. Henry et al., 2022). Such approaches are highly policy relevant and are also able to demonstrate the trade-offs and co-benefits of achieving policy targets. A commonly applied and useful scenario framing is a BAU scenario reflecting the continuation of current trends. BAU scenarios are useful to decision-making by demonstrating the consequences of inaction since these scenarios inevitably continue the trend of declines in biodiversity.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scenario framing" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.6.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XC4AC5PM> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework presents valuable opportunities to use decision-support tools as a resource to integrate nexus thinking as implementation is being operationalized. On a fundamental level, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework Target 21 is explicitly around data and knowledge, aiming to make the best available science accessible to decision makers as well as the public. The tool family on assembling data and knowledge can directly address this need.

Broader opportunities for using decision-support tools lie with supporting governments and other interested parties to align strategies across the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets as well as with other multilateral environmental agreements. For example, many of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets have direct linkages to the SDGs (Obura, 2023). Many of the tools in this review can strengthen capacities to achieve these aligned targets by helping to: (1) understand ways for reducing threats to biodiversity; (2) meet people’s needs through sustainable use and benefit-sharing; and (3) assist with implementation and mainstreaming.

Tools to help understand ways to reduce threats to biodiversity fall predominantly in the category focused on addressing response options; namely, the assembling data and knowledge, assessment and evaluation, and selection and design of policy instruments tool families of this review. Biodiversity was well-represented across these tool families, which comprise the overwhelming majority of the tools in this review (Table 4.13). However, it is important to note that these tools are among the least holistic or transformative, but they often require a technical champion to implement (Table 4.13).

Tools that help meet people’s needs through sustainable use and benefit-sharing have high dimensions of inclusivity. The social learning, innovation and adaptive governance tool family along with the selection and design of policy instruments and implementation, outreach and enforcement families are among the tools with the highest dimensions of inclusivity (Table 4.13). These tools can help address gender issues, including ILK, and can be participatory.

Tools that help with implementation and mainstreaming are those with the highest transformative potential (Table 4.13). For example, tools that address context and tools that address scaling generally have more transformative potential than those that address response options. Interestingly, though these tools have massive potential for affecting positive change, the diversity of stakeholders involved is quite limited and IPLC are rarely key actors. Therefore, equity and empowerment may not be well addressed by these tools.

Ultimately, these tools can showcase examples of how to support nexus governance. There is substantial and intentional overlap between the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework goals and targets and those in other multilateral environmental agreements. By design, countries can use similar metrics, indicators or approaches to make progress towards achieving goals and targets across multiple agreements as well, and these tools can help ensure that limited resources are well spent.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Integration of decision support tools with global policy frameworks" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.0.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8GSB7D32> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.0> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """This chapter focuses on response options available to actors within each of the five elements represented in the nexus – biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. In this assessment, a response option is defined as actions or policies available to a range of actors that address specific challenges or opportunities in the governance and management of one or more elements of the nexus, with the goal of achieving positive and equitable outcomes across multiple nexus elements. Response options can include voluntary or mandatory policies, strategies, measures and interventions intended to change nexus elements, interlinkages between them and direct or indirect drivers shaping them (see Chapter 1, section 1.3 and Chapter 4). Effective response options account for a diversity of world views, behaviours, norms and values. Response options include actions (e.g., shifts to sustainable healthy diets), policies (e.g., repurposing public spending) and enablers (e.g., ensuring participation of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) in resource decisions).

Response options represent opportunities to address the issues reported earlier in this assessment (Figure 5.0.1). Chapters 1 to 3 lay out the conceptual basis for the nexus and assess past trends, current status and future trends in nexus interactions. Chapter 4 provides an overview of nexus governance, drawing on theoretical frameworks and tools to evaluate a broad range of policy and sociopolitical options that address the nexus challenges introduced in Chapter 1 and can accelerate a transition to sustainable futures. These previous chapters provide the conceptual foundations for the specific response options identified and assessed in this chapter.

This chapter builds upon Chapter 4 by focusing on the nexus from the perspective of actors within each of its component elements (i.e., biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change) through five subchapters that identify and assess the evidence for a range of response options. These response options are assessed and compared using a set of common criteria (Table 5.0.1), including their impacts on the other elements of the nexus. Critical actors, governance issues and equity dimensions are identified, and examples are provided of existing efforts to put the response options into action.

This chapter is a resource for actors, particularly decision-makers and other stakeholders, within and across each of the five nexus elements. It addresses two of the policy-relevant questions of the overall assessment introduced in Chapter 1 (section 1.1.3), which are decomposed here into five parts:

What response options are available to actors within each nexus element? For example, sustainable healthy diets, forest-based climate solutions and water-sensitive urban infrastructure are all response options originating within one or more nexus elements with co-benefits for other elements.

How do response options differ in the number and strength of their impacts on the nexus elements? For example, offshore wind power and reforestation can both reduce net greenhouse gas emissions (and thereby mitigate climate change). However, while offshore wind power is known to impact marine species (biodiversity) and fisheries (food), forest restoration is likely to impact threatened species (biodiversity), water quality and quantity (water), the availability of arable land (food) and vectors of infectious disease (health).

Which response options are complementary and synergistic with others, and which will involve trade-offs, in achieving pathways towards sustainable futures? For example, grassland restoration for biodiversity conservation may involve synergies with climate change by storing carbon and may be suitable for extensive meat production, but also risks trade-offs with food by removing land from crop production.

What attributes or enabling conditions are associated with response options that have nexus-wide benefits and potential for transformative change? For example, decisions that ensure equity among stakeholders may be more likely to lead to nexus-wide benefits and transformative change by ensuring buy-in and broad support.

How do response options support existing global policy frameworks and their targets and goals to achieve sustainable futures? The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement aim to accelerate and coordinate international efforts to conserve biodiversity, address climate change and chart a sustainable future. Response options that align with these frameworks can reinforce one or more global agreements already in place.

Assessing the evidence supporting the answers to these questions is key to designing holistic policies and actions for managing socioecological systems and safeguarding nature and its contributions to people (Díaz et al., 2015). While this assessment focuses on five specific nexus elements, the findings are applicable to consideration of other sectors and trends, such as transportation or urbanization, providing a general model for addressing nexus challenges.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.0.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of this chapter" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The evidence presented in this chapter underlines that water is fundamental for life, interacting with nature and society in diverse ways, and being shaped by various discourses, conceptual framings and entry points for dealing with water processes and challenges. Water, within a nexus framing, acts as a connector to all nexus elements and with water-related challenges never just being about water. Many decisions made outside of the water sector have impacts on water yet are too rarely considered, resulting in unintended consequences for other nexus elements, as well as for water (Figure 5.2.3).

This subchapter reviewed the evidence for 15 (clusters of) response options originating from the nexus element of water but that were linked to other nexus elements. The water-oriented response options in general have synergies with many of the other nexus elements (Figure 5.2.3). This is particularly evident with biodiversity, and less so with food, health and climate change. Of the 15 response options, most had some impact on at least one aspect of each of the other nexus elements. Three response options (W05, Sustainable inland fisheries (section 5.2.3.5); W09, Groundwater governance (section 5.2.3.9); W12, Reducing pollution (section 5.2.3.12) have positive effects across all nexus elements. Most (9 of 15) response options had moderate to high positive impact on the change in state and function of biodiversity, suggesting actors from these elements could have synergistic positive effects on (at least) water and biodiversity. However, water response option effects on food were mixed, with four response options (W03, Dam operation (section 5.2.3.3); W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (section 5.2.3.4); W10, Finance for water infrastructure (section 5.2.3.10); W11, Manage alien species (section 5.2.3.11)) having negative effects on some aspects of food, especially on changes in the environmental performance of food production. However, opportunities exist that show co-benefits between water, biodiversity and food by increasing the efficient use of water in agriculture (section 5.2.3.4, W04), improving groundwater governance (section 5.2.3.9, W09) and reducing pollution (section 5.2.3.12, W12). Dam operation (section 5.2.3.3, W03), one of the most established response options, has large potential to provide both water for agriculture and energy security but this often comes at the expense of biodiversity and the carbon accumulation potential of freshwater habitats, and can increase the risk of vector borne diseases (5.2.3.3). They also have negative impacts on climate change adaptation potential through release of greenhouse gas emissions or through environmental flows that may contradict flows needed for hydropower. Thus, the review highlights that several response options such as dam operations and financial and economic instruments, while well studied, can fail to deliver across the nexus.

A nexus framing encourages water governance to move from a sectoral perspective limited in geographies such as watersheds and river basins to that of “problemsheds” that focus on interdependencies, trade-offs and synergies among nexus elements and promote interaction among traditional sectoral governance structures. Moving beyond unilateral water governance can be operationalized by nexus governance that stresses action that is informed, integrative (across sectors and scales) and inclusive of multiple stakeholders. Actors in the water element have a diversity of response options that can be implemented to benefit other nexus elements, particularly biodiversity and health, and to better manage trade-offs with other elements. The water-related response options can also be combined in bundles or sequences to address these trade-offs or enhance synergies. This is particularly important as the 15 response options were in themselves bundles of over 100 specific response options (see section 5.2.3). This provides decision-makers the flexibility to implement response options based on evaluation of their synergies and trade-offs. For example, implementing the response options on W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (section 5.2.3.2), W03, Dam operation (section 5.2.3.3), W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (section 5.2.3.4) and W09, Groundwater governance (section 5.2.3.9) may collectively be used to reduce water stress. By promoting conjunctive use of surface and groundwater including water storage and regulation by natural ecosystems as well as built infrastructure, integrating environmental flows in dam operations and efficient use of fertilizers may alleviate the trade-offs between water security, food production and biodiversity conservation.

Implementation of the 15 response options is influenced by a range of enablers and barriers. Cross-sectoral coordination among actors from the different nexus elements is a common enabler across the response options and includes collaboration across scales and jurisdictions. Another common enabler is the need for the co-production of knowledge on interactions between water and the other nexus elements, to enable collaboration amongst actors based on managing for synergies and trade-offs. Policy effectiveness and institutional capacities were underpinning enablers across all response options, suggesting that response options are feasible if the right policies and capacities are in place. Fewer strong barriers than enablers were identified, with the most common barrier being material endowments. However, many of the response options have not yet been widely implemented, suggesting that these few barriers may be stronger than the many enablers combined. Furthermore, not all of the response options are equitable when considering distributional equity (Figure 5.2.4). This creates inequalities regarding who benefits from these response options, which can be a barrier to action. This highlights the importance of attention to power structures, politics and relationships, recognizing diverse world views and knowledge systems, and placing specific emphasis on integrating IPLC knowledge, beliefs and practices to provide an enabling environment for implementing nexus governance.

The response options have social and political support (Figure 5.2.4) and all but two have high technical feasibility (Figure 5.2.5). However, despite being technologically ready, some response options, such as dam operation (W03) had some of the largest negative impacts on other nexus elements, and response options related to integrated water infrastructure (W02) and reducing pollution (W12) are associated with high economic costs (Figure 5.2.4). This may also be interlinked with the generally negative scoring for the economic cost of response options. Several response options do have high economic costs for implementation yet given that there are positive economic benefits provided by most response options, even if modest, this might suggest that benefit-cost analyses might need updating.

Water-oriented response options are generally well-aligned with political priorities and international frameworks (Figure 5.2.4). All water response options contribute to at least one SDG, with five options addressing at least five SDGs (W02, Integrated water infrastructure (section 5.2.3.2); W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (section 5.2.3.4), W08, Transboundary water cooperation (section 5.2.3.8); W12, Manage wastewater (section 5.2.3.12); W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (section 5.2.3.13)) (Figure 5.2.9). Clean water and sanitation (SDG6) is supported by all 15 response options. Only three SDGs (SDG8, Decent work and economic growth, SDG10 Reduced inequalities and SDG17, Partnerships for the goals) are not supported by any of the response options. Every response option also supported the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, with W08 Transboundary water cooperation (section 5.2.3.8) addressing 10 targets followed by W15, Community water management (section 5.2.3.15) addressing 9 targets. Three response options (W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (section 5.2.3.2); W08, Transboundary water cooperation (section 5.2.3.8); W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (section 5.2.3.13)) addressed at least five SDGs and five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, suggesting these options provide substantial benefits across multiple global frameworks.

The assessment presented in this section underlines the value of approaching contemporary water challenges from a nexus framing, and upscaling investments in implementation of response options with benefits across nexus elements. Implementation of these response options, however, requires not just technical fixes but a deeper intervention at the water governance level, enabling a range of water sector actors to engage across policy domains, governance levels and public, private and civic spheres. Advancing the application of response options also needs concurrent investments in ensuring these responses reflect diverse world views and cosmovisions, co-production of knowledge on interactions between water and the other nexus elements, and development of data and indicators on the nexus interactions of water spanning across space and time scales.

Notes
8. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The global food system faces many challenges associated with feeding a growing population, while addressing the environmental impacts of many conventional agricultural systems and tackling over-consumption, food loss and waste. The response options assessed demonstrate clear pathways through these challenges. The individual response options offer many nexus benefits, however, it is through synergistic interactions between response options that transformative change will occur (Box 5.3.16). Indeed, it is through the collective analysis of the response options against nexus elements that synergies and inconsistencies becomes apparent.

The 16 response options described in section 5.3.3 can be classified into five action categories: (i) actions that conserve natural ecosystems; (ii) actions that restore degraded lands and soils; (iii) actions that regenerate ecosystem functions in food production spaces, notably croplands, rangelands and aquatic systems; (iv) actions that reduce the impacts of food production and consumption on biodiversity, climate, water and health; and (v) actions that involve fundamental transformations in how food systems operate (Table 5.3.3). As noted in Box 5.3.16, food system transformation will require appropriate enabling environments and adapting response options to specific contexts. This will entail the development and alignment of context-specific, inclusive and innovative practices, policies, institutions and markets. Supportive policies that are coherent and aligned across nexus elements are particularly important but absent in many cases (Figures 5.3.17 and 5.3.19).

Technologies and innovations are particularly important for production practices, including greater investment and innovations in practices that are compatible with both sustainability and health objectives; examples comprise no-till machinery, polyculture seeders and harvesters, machinery adapted to fruit, nut and vegetable cultivation, including adapted to small scale producers. Investments in breeding for environmental performance (e.g., belowground carbon capture) are also important, notably for currently under-consumed crops such as fruits, nuts, and vegetables.

Most response options do not operate in isolation but rather there can be strong interdependencies between response options which are important to ensuring success (Figure 5.3.21). Bundling and aligning multiple response options can provide synergistic benefits and avoid trade-offs but will be strongly influenced by the nature and strength of interactions and interdependencies among response options. These in turn will be extremely context dependent. For example, halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (section 5.3.3.1) has a strong dependence on restoring soil health (section 5.3.3.2), sustainable intensification (section 5.3.3.3) and ecological intensification (sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6). Without these response options operating, it becomes difficult if not impossible to halt conversion. Halting conversion, reducing pollution, dietary shifts and urban food systems were the response options which had the greatest dependencies on other actions. In contrast, reforming public funding enabled the greatest number of response options followed by gender-inclusive approaches and reducing pollution.

These strong interdependencies between response options are important to ensuring success. Such bundling and aligning of multiple response options can provide synergistic benefits and avoid trade-offs. For example, the substantial environmental impacts of agricultural expansion into natural habitats can be prevented by halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (section 5.3.3.1). However, halting conversion is only possible – or serious impacts on local food security can only be avoided – through the combined impacts of other response options that restore soils (section 5.3.3.2), increase the efficiency and quantity of food production (sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and reduce its negative externalities (sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9), as well as demand-facing response options such as reducing food loss and waste (section 5.3.3.10) and encouraging dietary shifts to sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11). Combining response options in this fashion will, in turn, only be possible using response options that enable the food system to be sustainably reorientated and reformed to benefit the nexus (sections 5.3.3.12, 5.3.3.13, 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.15 and 5.3.3.16).

Of the individual response options, consumption-based approaches, including a focus on sustainable healthy diets and reducing food loss and waste, have potential to guide interventions with nexus-wide benefits – notably on climate, biodiversity and health. They can also bring important economic benefits. For these response options to gain traction, however, it is important to consider interactions and interdependencies influencing how, where and why they might face opposition, and how to overcome or circumvent such barriers. The special interests for whom any interruption to the status quo represents a threat need to be recognized and addressed.

While the response options provide clear pathways forward, there is no one-size-fits-all approach for sustainable food systems (Figure 5.3.21). For production-facing options (sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6), global lands and waters cannot be treated homogenously. Some will need to be appropriated for food production, but others must be set-aside and used to safeguard other nexus elements. The variation in global lands and waters in their biodiversity and carbon stocks, their current and potential food production, and their vulnerability to different anthropogenic pressures means that not all response options will be appropriate in every location, and each will have to be designed for, or adapted to, local conditions. However, context-dependent evidence on achieving response options is often missing: for example, field-based trials rarely provide data across multiple nexus elements; global models struggle to integrate the diversity of plausible practices and fine resolution data; and farmers frequently adopt multiple practices to varying degrees based on local knowledge and capabilities that confound reductionist scientific methods.

It is not possible to separate this biophysical variation from the human: the social, economic and cultural variability that will determine which approaches are appropriate or possible in different circumstances. Changing any system – let alone one as complex and interlinked as the global food system – risks worsening or entrenching inequities, even while attempting to reduce them. This is particularly true for the food system because of its fundamental importance to all human life. Almost everyone is directly affected by the global food system and the teleconnections of global trade can result in well-meaning interventions having disastrous unintended consequences for people’s health and livelihoods, or the global environment.

While there is significant risk to food security, health and resilience to environmental change with one-size-fits-all approaches applied at scale, the sheer number of practices, interventions and proposals from multiple actors in the food system community is hugely encouraging. Many are local adaptations of global solutions driven by mixes of individual motivation and enabling environments. This diversity of practices, capabilities and innovations should be celebrated and encouraged and is perhaps our greatest asset in facing the challenges of the global food system.

Aligning individual interests and capabilities with the goals of international frameworks (Figure 5.3.22), combined with specific actions and the equitable distributions of costs, risks and benefits, will be essential for achieving meaningful change. Food system response options are particularly important enablers of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework Goal A targets, notably the response options halting conversion and loss of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (section 5.3.3.1) and reducing different forms of pollution (sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9). They are also a key means of achieving Goal B targets, notably sustainable production (target 10) and nature’s contributions to people (target 11) as food systems are fundamentally dependent on nature’s contributions to people. Reorienting food to sustainable healthy diets is a necessary precondition for achieving SDG2 and SDG3, which in turn are enablers of achieving SDG13, SDG14 and SDG15 as well as the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for mitigation and adaptation. Recognizing and leveraging the significant synergy between sustainable healthy diets and global goals has transformative potential.

Notes
6. See the Chapter 5 data management report describing the response option scoring criteria and process at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Changing course towards a sustainable future for people and nature will require truly transformative changes in economic, social and governance systems. The response options assessed in each subchapter can contribute to this fundamental course correction. Each option was assessed on three dimensions of transformative potential: the degree to which the response option can generate new practices and innovations; the degree to which a response option can shift values and views; and the degree to which it can change structures and institutions (Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1). These indicators were derived from the definition of transformative change in the IPBES Thematic Assessment of the Underlying Causes of Biodiversity Loss, Determinants of Transformative Change and Options for Achieving the 2050 Vision for Biodiversity (Transformative Change Assessment).

Several response options aim primarily to shift practices. Examples include response options that shift food production practices such as B03/C11, Agroecology (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.11); F04-F06, ecological intensification of croplands, rangelands and aquatic foods (Chapter 5.3, sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and C03, Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.3). Response options that radically change conservation practices such as B08, Rewilding (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.8) or dietary-related response options such as F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.15) and H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Chapter 5.4, section 5.4.3.6). In addition, C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.7) approaches also harness innovations for changes in practices around life cycles of production and use.

Other response options aim to change views and values, often regarding nature. These include sociolegal approaches such as B10, Rights-based approaches to conservation (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.10) and W07, Rights of nature (Chapter 52, section 5.2.3.7). Options that call attention to gender such as W14, Addressing gendered burdens of water collection (Chapter 5.2, section 5.2.3.14) and F14, Foster gender transformative approaches (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.14). Options that recognize Indigenous Peoples as key drivers of transformative such as B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.9); F15, Indigenous food systems (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.15) and H02, Intercultural health services (Chapter 5.4, section 5.4.3.2) as well as options influencing attitudes and behaviours through B14, Reconnecting people with nature (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.14). Response options that shift practices can also shift views and values or be dependent on views and values changing first. For example, B08, Rewilding (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.8) and dietary-related response options represent major shifts in practices that evidence suggests often first require shifting values and views regarding nature and health.

Fewer response options provide opportunities to shape changes in structures and institutions. These include political changes to empower marginalized groups, such as IPLC in B10, Rights-based approaches (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.10); F16, Access to natural resources and land (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.16); B12, Land and sea planning (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.12) and W15, Community water management (Chapter 5.2, section 5.2.3.15) that allow local groups to engage more in decision-making. Response options that link institutions across scales can also contribute to transformative change; e.g., B11, Multilateral environmental agreements (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.11); W08, Transboundary water cooperation (Chapter 5.2, section 5.2.3.8) and C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.7). Finally, changing economic and financial incentives through options such as B13, Natural capital accounting (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.13) and F13, Reform public spending (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.13), can have large transformative potential.

While many response options on their own are likely to lead to incremental changes, bundling response options can increase the likelihood of transformative change. Table 5.6.3 describes several case studies in which response options were bundled or sequenced, leading to synergies and magnified impacts. Bundling of response options to achieve more transformative change will require co-implementation of social, political and technical options to create the desired changes in values, the enabling environment, and the institutions and technologies for supporting equitable and just change.

Because response options tend to integrate across multiple nexus elements, they may lead to more systemic changes which are necessary for transforming economic, social and governance systems. Looking across the assessed response options and case studies, it is evident that those with low transformative potential also tend to have low nexus-wide benefits. However, response options with high transformative potential include those with a wide range of positive nexus impact scores and from all nexus elements.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Contributions to transformative change" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.4.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch7> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Many capacity gaps exist for implementing decision-making in more inclusive, integrative and harmonized ways aligned with the governance of nexus interactions in ways that address nexus challenges (see Chapter 4, sections 4.2 and 4.6; section 7.3.7). However, a number of platforms have been developed that seek to highlight opportunities to bridge capacity gaps and create global partnerships to facilitate implementation. For example, mechanisms aligned with the CBD include the BioBridge Initiative (https://www.cbd.int/biobridge/platform); Clearing House Mechanisms to support parties to improve on capacity gaps by sharing capacity-building resources (https://chm.cbd.int/); High Ambition initiatives, such as the NBSAP Accelerator Partnership (https://nbsapaccelerator.org/) and the High Ambition Coalition for Nature and People in support of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (https://www.hacfornatureandpeople.org/). These initiatives support the creation of global networks to strengthen capacities, improve on knowledge gaps and facilitate global implementation. While they are CBD-specific, CBD Decision 15/13 acknowledges the need for synergies and the sharing of these tools to achieve synergetic implementation. These CBD-specific tools can be used to achieve the aims of other biodiversity-related conventions, and set out steps to facilitate a nexus approach.

Capacity strengthening initiatives aligned with the UNFCCC include the Paris Committee on Capacity Building (PCCB), Nairobi Work Programme which is the UNFCCC knowledge-to-action hub for climate resilience and adaptation focusing on capacity strengthening initiatives through enhancing access to evidence and knowledge and Action for Climate Empowerment (ACE), which supports work under Article 6 of the Convention and Article 12 of the Paris Agreement with the aim to empower all members of society to engage in climate action, through the six ACE elements: climate change education and public awareness, training, public participation, public access to information, and international cooperation on these issues, especially in relation to changing behaviours in relation to sustainable consumption and production. In terms of addressing the impacts of climate change within the context of the nexus, the UN Alliance on Climate Change seeks to bring various UN organizations together to build green and climate-resilient societies.

Despite these expanding initiatives, this assessment identified several capacity gaps (Table 7.11) that if addressed can enhance the governance of nexus interactions and strengthen capacities of diverse actors to identify and implement policy and sociopolitical options, including response options, and the scaling of these options that show transformative potential.

Notes
2. Review of progress in the implementation of the Convention and the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011-2020 and the achievement of the Aichi Biodiversity Targets adopted by the Conference of the Parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity in decision 15/13.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capacity gaps" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch7> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """This concluding chapter has focused on the why, how and who of action to address nexus challenges that have been the focus of the entire assessment. Ensuring that future pathways and options for sustainability remain open by acting now across key governance actors in tackling multiple systemic, compounding and cascading crises can be enabled through the processes and steps outlined in the chapter. Selecting, negotiating and implementing response options, such as those identified in Chapters 4, 5 and 6, are key actions that can help actors achieve more synergistic, coordinated and enhanced outcomes. Yet as the entire assessment has noted, many interlinkages continue to be unaddressed by policies, actions and options, and a range of actors and institutions not represented in decision-making, due in part to the persistence of key nexus challenges around complexity, governance, values, scaling and finance as well as lack of capacities.

Addressing these problems head-on can lead to both important incremental steps forward as well as facilitating shifts towards transformative change, which will involve transforming the way new structures, practices and values are reflected in response options and governance that underpins decision-making. Such transformative change is both an appropriate response and a future target, given the large costs of inaction in not resolving existing crises, including very large amounts of monetary and non-monetary damages from current and projected climate change to nature and people and rising welfare impacts of disasters, biodiversity loss and ecosystem degradation. The costs of not acting are creating not only current problems and foreclosing future decisions, but also ultimately will require larger amounts of money to fix at a later time point if deferred.

The road map for nexus action has provided a use-inspired process and set of sets that can help actors from many different sectors, regions and backgrounds to approach nexus challenges in a consistent, iterative and inclusive manner, consistent with the components of nexus governance needs that the assessment (and particularly Chapter 4) have identified. Clusters of steps cover exploration of the contexts of nexus problems, coordination and strategic action to address problems using a nexus approach, and implementation and scaling of solutions, with decision-support tools available along the way. Other steps in the road map aim to shift power dynamics and vested interests blocking transitions and transformations to just and sustainable futures, including through expanding enablers for nexus approaches and response options.

Decisions about what response options to pursue and how to do so can be enabled by co-design with diverse actors and institutions through transparent and equitable processes and approaches, which can be enabled by structured decision-making through the road map, and which are likely to lead to more successful implementation. A road map process can also help facilitate acting with more attention and understanding of other sectors through collaborative and coordinated action across multiple sectors and actors. Building shared visions of alternative futures is a key goal of road map use, with many processes and models available to envision sustainable transition pathways that recognize complexity and uncertainty and help find futures through these.

Drawing attention to enabling conditions, such as institutional capacities, financial and economic enablers, changes in behaviours and values, technological readiness and material endowments can help ensure better response option choice and implementation. Many response options are highly cost-effective or provide livelihood and income opportunities, and others pay for themselves in cost savings and higher efficiencies. While no response option is likely to be a simple win-win, the road map steps can help with understanding of the limitations, trade-offs and potential inequities involved in any policy choice. Additionally, appropriate scaling of response options accelerates their adoption elsewhere. These processes can be helped by a focus on improved capacities for actors using the road map, including helping developed more transformative capacities of stewarding, unlocking, innovation and orchestrating.

The assessment has also revealed a number of knowledge gaps across key areas of nexus interlinkages; data availability and access; assessment methods, tools, scenarios and models; ILK/IPLC issues; nexus response options; nexus governance; and nexus financing. There are also capacity gaps related to enhanced learning and engagement; facilitating expertise and information; and the need for strengthened national and regional capabilities. Technology gaps (e.g., effectiveness of emerging technologies in delivering outcomes across the nexus) exist and need attention, particularly around creating enabling conditions and policies; the importance of identifying pre-emergent technologies; assessments of technology-equity links; and attention to the emerging digital technology challenges of complexity, consistency and need for open science approaches. However, on-going research is filling many of these gaps, and in many cases, better understanding of these gaps can be accomplished through active co-production of knowledge while simultaneously taking action (e.g., through iterative experimentation), providing further evidence for the reasons for acting now.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conclusion" .

