<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XKCV5UHZ> a ipbes:Reference ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.1.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.2.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.3.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.1.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4.1.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Liu, J., Hull, V., Godfray, H. C. J., Tilman, D., Gleick, P., Hoff, H., Pahl-Wostl, C., Xu, Z., Chung, M. G., Sun, J., & Li, S. (2018). Nexus approaches to global sustainable development. Nature Sustainability, 1(9), Article 9. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41893-018-0135-8" ;
    ipbes:hasDoi "10.1038/s41893-018-0135-8" ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.zotero.org/groups/4596166/items/XKCV5UHZ> .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.4.2.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/22DXJ2AH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4CWFH4WQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BBM7YLX9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BEPCNXJA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CAVGM4IP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IWRYNNG7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NAHXIP5D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W563S4UK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WUK5S5Z8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XNSUUJKH> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.4.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Numerous water assessments have focused on three nexus-related themes. First, there is a growing need to resolve conflicts over limited and changing freshwater resources for biodiversity and human and food systems (FAO, 2014, 2023; IPBES, 2019b; MacAlister et al., 2023; WHO, 2022). Second, there is a need to effectively govern and manage aquatic ecosystems and natural resources for both humans and nature along with their critical role in climate processes (IPCC, 2019b). Third, there is a need for the identification of overlooked resources, either because they are physically hidden (e.g., groundwater (United Nations, 2022) or undervalued in current global metrics (e.g., inland fisheries (Cooke et al., 2016)). A common message across these assessments is the call for nexus governance and nexus approaches that break down historic silos (United Nations, 2018b). The recent proposed Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform for Water Sustainability represents a step toward understanding the needs for the water sector, like the role IPBES and IPCC play for biodiversity and climate change, respectively." ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.4.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Water reports" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5ZXZKFEC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DTJRMGAI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R2IDS8S4> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Management for nature conservation through protected areas and other effective area-based conservation measures (OECMs) is used in scenarios to facilitate positive biodiversity outcomes by averting further declines and influencing other nexus elements. Nature conservation refers to a site-based legal, tenure or governance system with the primary goal of preserving biodiversity, its processes or NCP, and supporting the sustainable use of nature. The specific form of nature conservation to be established, by whom and when is dependent on local contexts and appropriateness of management (Arneth et al., 2023; Barnes et al., 2018; Visconti et al., 2019). Scenarios for nature conservation are usually constructed by assessing either the potential opportunities, synergies and trade-offs that might arise from implementing nature conservation measures in an area or by simulating nature conservation actions as land-or water-use practices that displace or affect other nexus elements. A total of 52 studies (22 terrestrial, 17 marine and 13 freshwater) were found to have investigated nature conservation scenarios." ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scenarios focused on nature conservation" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5.4.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3S9XDCME>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8FN88U35>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AGEA3IAB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HZ2WYFXA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LCXT7J5W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MQ25ZEWV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P6GWI5GJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RPPALVKJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RWFVBUE4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W563S4UK> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Current governance systems can not only take better account of the nexus, but can also be more transformative (Bulkeley et al., 2020). The growing body of transformative change literature, as well as the IPBES Global Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (Global Assessment) (IPBES, 2019), the IPBES Values Assessment (IPBES, 2022a) and IPBES Thematic Assessment of the Underlying Causes of Biodiversity Loss, Determinants of Transformative Change and Options for Achieving the 2050 Vision for Biodiversity (Transformative Change Assessment) suggest that for biodiversity governance to be transformative, it needs to address the underlying causes and indirect drivers of biodiversity loss (Pascual et al., 2022; Razzaque et al., 2019; Visseren-Hamakers et al., 2021; Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022). The IPBES Values Assessment (IPBES, 2022a) identifies that response options that support sustainability transitions are underpinned by the following features: (i) addressing the status quo by changing underlying direct and indirect drivers through social processes and innovations (see also Chapter 2); (ii) incorporating and acknowledging the importance of including diverse values; (iii) fostering institutional change by enhancing learning and experimentation; (iv) mobilizing and enhancing the capacities of different actors through transformative literacy; and (v) supporting integrative and adaptive governance (Kelemen et al., 2022; Pascual et al., 2022). Thus, facilitating transformative change and implementing response options across the nexus requires creating and supporting enabling contexts for mobilizing and translating knowledge, negotiating and acknowledging diverse values, and encouraging participation, deliberation and negotiation for the implementation of response options between and within different actor groups (Tengö et al., 2017).

There is a strong relationship between the proposed components of nexus governance and those that are associated with transformative governance, although they may have different justifications and applications. Recent scholarship has focused on attention to inclusive, transdisciplinary, adaptive, anticipatory and accountable components of transformative governance to address underlying causes and indirect drivers of biodiversity loss and climate change (Visseren-Hamakers et al., 2021; Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022). These mirror some of the foci of the nexus governance components and can be specifically related to transformative change and/or governance as summarized by Table 4.10. The IPBES Transformative Change Assessment identifies four principles as guidelines that govern behaviour, decision-making or actions for deliberate transformative change: equity and justice; pluralism and inclusion; respectful and reciprocal human-nature relationship; and adaptive learning and action. These principles/components need to be further embodied and promoted to deepen nexus governance or to make nexus governance transformative.

Box 4.15 offers an example of integrative, adaptive and experimental approaches to promote transformative nexus governance through implementing multi-stakeholder collaboration and integrated local policies including biodiversity friendly rice farming, and conservation of cultural landscape as well as revitalization of local communities in Sado Island, Japan.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Relationship of nexus governance to transformative governance" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2JMADRU4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4VF3XQCR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5D76P6SL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7H39ARIT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8SEASWB7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FQ9KW5TY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RRERTF58>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U2L8X3PC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VYU2KSEL> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Formal and informal environmental education is a preventive policy and management instrument which contributes to promoting behaviours and recognizing cosmovision and values related to water protection using a holistic approach, including spiritual and traditional approaches and customary governance systems (Sarkar, 2022). This can also include consideration of biodiversity. Water education, with a gender perspective and including traditional knowledge of IPLC in schools (e.g., using water use and footprint calculations) (Box 5.2.3), is a significant first step towards building water consciousness (Sultana, 2018) and effecting behaviour change (Afzal et al., 2022; Koop et al., 2019). In cases where this is not yet achieved, it is important to progress the generation of knowledge so that communities feel challenged and motivated to assume autonomy, responsibility and choice, which enables a transformation of water culture (Lara & Otano, 2017).

It is difficult to measure the impact of environmental water education in connection with the nexus elements, except for health (Chapter 5.4). Some studies revealed that higher water knowledge was associated with higher education levels, confirming its importance and allowing identification of potential subgroups that may benefit from targeted support of water management initiatives (Dean et al., 2016).

The recognition of the need for education programs is widespread in the different global policy frameworks, e.g., explicitly mentioned in Targets 16 and 21 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework), SDG6 (clean water and sanitation), SDG4 (ensure inclusive and equitable quality education and promote lifelong learning opportunities for all) and SDG5 (gender equality) to have a holistic education (COP15, n.d.; Rad et al., 2022). Such awareness-raising activities are important parts of environmental education, for example, to increase knowledge and values promoting the protection and conservation of the environment, e.g., by protecting water sources from contamination and more generally to inform and empower people to help protect water (Woldetsadik et al., 2018).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inclusive water education (W01)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7N3SBZLZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/K2DQ9NQT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KP9KJYT4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LV28RGSC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMJ22R7V> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The governance process involves state and non-state actors collaborating to establish and accomplish shared objectives. It also determines how collective interests are defined, negotiated, pursued and steered for the collective interests of the economy and society (Sørensen & Torfing, 2018). Therefore, governance is pivotal in facilitating climate action for both adaptation and mitigation efforts. The modes of governance comprise (i) actors and institutions who engage themselves in different arenas (political, economic, ecological, socio-cultural and technological) with asymmetric power relations, each contributing uniquely to climate narratives and actions; and (ii) policy instruments (policies and procedures) that operationalize and harmonize climate action across various levels and sectors (Schipper et al., 2022; Singh & Chudasama, 2021b; Sørensen, 2017; Treib et al., 2007) (Figure 5.5.12)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus governance to support climate change response options" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7N3SBZLZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BDY4UX6R>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DCPPL9R4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MRP3ERIQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMJ22R7V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q5ZDRBEB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/S5WDCXMY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VD85WMV5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZLVSFGMB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/NXS24-ch5-4933> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Equity and justice are foundational elements in the design of climate governance, influencing the effectiveness of climate change adaptation, mitigation and financial transfers from developed to developing nations (Schipper et al., 2022). Moreover, they facilitate a nexus approach that links climate action with the broader needs of biodiversity, water security and fair food systems (N. Dawson et al., 2018; Kroll et al., 2019). Furthermore, incorporating equity and justice into climate governance does not operate in isolation but synergizes with a comprehensive, nexus-based approach to environmental management (N. Dawson et al., 2018; Kroll et al., 2019).

At the grassroots level, dialogue is enriched by environmental justice perspectives and Indigenous wisdom. These voices insist on procedural justice, particularly in community-led adaptation efforts after climate-related disasters (Schlosberg & Collins, 2014). In the quest for transformative change, justice pushes for gender parity and social equality, in line with the SDGs. This broad view also questions the traditional focus on GDP growth, advocating for a balance considering environmental and social factors (J. Gupta et al., 2023; Schipper et al., 2022; Singh & Chudasama, 2021b). (J. Gupta et al., 2023; e.g. Rammelt et al., 2023; Rockström et al., 2023). Finally, tackling equity and justice is not just an ethical move but a catalyst for robust climate action and community resilience (IPCC, 2023). Where market mechanisms (e.g., carbon markets) run the risk of disproportionate impacts on some marginalized people (J. Gupta et al., 2023), ensuring fair access to distributional and procedural equity becomes particularly important.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Equity and justice" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> a ipbes:Illustration ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The 71 response options assessed in Chapter 5 are displayed in wedges representing 10 broad categories of actions (labelled on the outer edge of the circle). Unique alphanumeric codes for each response option indicate its nexus element (B for biodiversity, W for water, F for food, H for health and C for climate change) and its corresponding number in the relevant subchapter of Chapter 5. These codes are used to identify response options throughout the report. Several response options correspond to more than one category but, for clarity, are displayed only in their primary category. The “Others” category includes four important response options that do not correspond easily to any of the nine main categories. Response options with an asterisk (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) were each assessed in two different subchapters and both alphanumeric codes are therefore shown. For brief descriptions of the response options, see Appendix 4." ;
    dcterms:identifier "FigureSPM.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Many response options already exist that a range of actors can implement." .

