<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.13> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/496BB9XX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4CKXWRMP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4XPZN8KV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CXNHWAD4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HK5VL8HE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LPNB29LV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MM5XYNMB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NWUAUWNI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QKYCFFK3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QY72UTVC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R4MAKXCW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/T2XXZF9M> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """This response option focuses on applying principles of Water Sensitive Urban Design in the design of urban infrastructure to conserve water while simultaneously protecting the health and biodiversity of waterways and mitigating the risk of damage to life and property from flooding (Rashetnia et al., 2022). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems are applicable globally in all urban areas, being of greatest value in those that are prone to water-related risks (Ahammed, 2017; Radcliffe et al., 2017) (Box 5.2.6). They are implemented at a range of scales from the household to city levels (Figure 5.2.5). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can encapsulate a range of interventions from building infrastructure for the drainage and harvesting of stormwater to the installation of treatment facilities for the removal of water-borne pollutants (A. K. Sharma et al., 2016). They can also be implemented to improve urban landscapes by incorporating built reservoirs/wetlands into the planning process (de Graaf & van der Brugge, 2010; Rashetnia et al., 2022). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems that implement infrastructure for the collection and storage of stormwater improve the availability of water for human use (Fewkes, 2012; Rashetnia et al., 2022) (Figure 5.2.3). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can also enhance biodiversity and the ecological functioning of urban environments and natural lands (Findlay & Taylor, 2006; Ranta et al., 2021), and can make urban-adjacent environments more ecologically resilient (Ranta et al., 2021).

As one of the primary objectives of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems is to mitigate urban flooding risk, they can benefit the health and well-being of urban residents (Schubert et al., 2017). Improving the ecological health of waterways by reducing the occurrence of water-borne pollutants can also benefit the health of the population using the drainage catchment as a water source (Findlay & Taylor, 2006). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems implemented with landscape architects and city planners that prioritize urban water bodies and green spaces can contribute to mitigating urban heat island effects (Coutts et al., 2013; Rashetnia et al., 2022). Furthermore, Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can make cities more resilient to climate change-induced water scarcity (Radcliffe et al., 2017). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems provide substantial non-financial benefits and also have positive returns on investments (Iftekhar & Pannell, 2022; Whiteoak, 2019). Hence, local communities are in broad alignment with the objectives of Water Sensitive Urban Design practices in urban infrastructure (A. K. Sharma et al., 2016).

The key impediment to the uptake of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems is the lack of regulatory guidelines (due in part to their complexity) mandating their implementation within urban development projects (Rashetnia et al., 2022; A. K. Sharma et al., 2016). Suitable amendments to policy frameworks are necessary to ensure their widespread adoption, along with increased community awareness of the costs/benefits of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems (A. K. Sharma et al., 2016). The implementation of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems without the consultation of local residents, urban developers, civil engineers, landscape architects and ecologists will lead to increased costs and unrealized benefits (Rashetnia et al., 2022, 2022; A. K. Sharma et al., 2016). Lack of engagement of community stakeholders and the costs of capacity building and maintenance and upkeep of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can also present major challenges to the adoption of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems and the realization of their benefits (Rashetnia et al., 2022, 2022; A. K. Sharma et al., 2016). The adoption of Water Sensitive Urban Design practices in urban infrastructure projects contributes towards Targets 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 11, 12 and 14 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and SDGs 3, 6, 11, 13, and 15.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (W13)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.14> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6WAVL279>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9Q2JFCAW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FQ9KW5TY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GZLN7BQQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IKMJ9875>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IP8LYDBN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JW94JI3A>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KWYND8YI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SJ8LVUTU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TRGGDVMN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y426SYKH> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Women often experience increased exposure to impacts of environmental changes (Brown et al., 2023). For example, women can be more vulnerable to the impacts of climate change since they are generally responsible for ensuring the survival of families by providing water and food. Natural disasters such as floods can destroy water systems established specifically for communities that depend on untreated water and health resources, which leads to water pollution (Casas Varez, 2017)). Given that women use different ecosystem services than men, these differences are important to understand in order to implement more sustainable and equitable policies and interventions (Fortnam et al., 2019). Water collection is challenging due to distance, scarcity and lack of protection. Women face increased risks of disease due to direct contact with poor quality water and attacks during their journey to natural resource sources. For example, water tankers in western Rajasthan benefit upper-caste households, but poor and low-caste households remain dependent on drying water sources, particularly women from low-caste households who walk long distances to access water (Sarkar, 2022). These long distances result in detrimental effect on the health of women of all ages, where water-carrying methods impose physical loads with the potential to cause musculoskeletal disorders and related disability (Geere et al., 2010).

This response option focuses on technological tools, educational strategies and economic support that can reduce the burden of water collection on women and girls and, as a result, provide more time to study, work and/or for leisure (Kayser et al., 2019; Pouramin et al., 2020) (Box 5.2.7). It benefits water for humans and provides increased access to healthy foods, which improves human well-being (Figure 5.2.3). There is substantial evidence showing connections between water availability and accessibility and well-being including psychosocial health, physical health, personal beliefs, social relationships and human-environment interactions (Kangmennaang & Elliott, 2021). Moreover, diseases increase especially in women and girls due to water access inequalities (Packett et al., 2020) (Pouramin et al., 2020). Improvements in rural water supply allows women to transform the time saved from water collection into money (Crow et al., 2012; James et al., 2002). No evidence was found to show an effect of this response option on biodiversity (Figure 5.2.3).

Technologies for facilitating improved access to water for women and girls to alleviate time burdens and the detrimental impacts on their health and safety from carrying water can change rapidly and hence evidence on their feasibility is inconclusive. There are international instruments that regulate or recognize gender and water access rights, but some of these include the need for more infrastructure technology, such as connecting water to homes and solar-powered pumps to reduce labour for collection.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Address gendered burdens of water collection (W14)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3.10> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4MXS2BDG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6UUVVM5N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7ZAWKCB2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AZ6XWCJF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BPVILGI8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DVZSI26D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/I7MN3F67>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LEJ2EYQY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N8PEAZV4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NBFD4IIU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QMZ6EE5K>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/NXS24-ch5-3738> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Globally, about one third of all produced food for human consumption is lost and wasted across the entire supply chain annually (Ishangulyyev et al., 2019). Such loss and waste are 1.3 billion tons per annum, equivalent to $1 trillion, and enough to feed more than double the number of undernourished people. Similarly, 8-10 percent of total greenhouse gas emissions are associated with food loss and waste (IPCC, 2019).

Reducing food loss and waste (food loss and waste) can be applied from local to global levels in different production and post-harvest supply chains (Figure 5.3.5), with particularly strong impacts on climate change mitigation (global potential: 1.4-3.3 GtCO2e yr⁻¹) (FAO, 2019b; Hayashi et al., 2022) and adaptation (1.26 billion people) (FAO, 2022a) (Figure 5.3.4). Reducing food loss and waste can save up to 21 per cent of freshwater which could otherwise be used in growing food, or the equivalent of 21 m³of freshwater per capita per year (Marston et al., 2021).

Reducing food loss and waste has the potential to close the gap between food needed in 2050 and food available in 2010 by more than 20 per cent. It would avoid approximately 2.78 million km² of intact land being converted to agriculture (Kummu, 2012). Halving food loss and waste reduces the global need for cropland area by around 14 per cent (IPCC, 2019). Reducing food loss and waste by 75 per cent could yield a 24 per cent decline in environmental pressures. If diets are optimized to reduce their environmental footprints, the pressure on cropland could be reduced by 50 per cent (Benton et al., 2021). There is also a risk that food loss and waste would increase due to a shift to sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11) considering increased demand for more perishable foods (fruits and vegetables); the land savings offered by reduced food loss and waste require strong governance measures, and complementary measures to reduce expansion of agricultural lands (section 5.3.3.1). Food loss and waste and public health intersect primarily in the domains of food security, food safety and nutrition, though it has no impact on food access or the nutritional value of food (Neff et al., 2015). The use of cold chain and packaging technologies, which can help reduce food loss and waste, increase energy and materials use in the food system (IPCC, 2019).

There are no known social acceptability issues associated with reducing food loss and waste (Figure 5.3.4). Economic analyses propose that reducing 25 per cent of food loss and waste would yield a $455 billion economic benefit, with an investment cost of $30 billion per year to 2025, and creating $255 billion in business opportunities (FOLU, 2019). Reducing food loss and waste is strongly aligned with multiple international frameworks such as the SDGs (Champions, 2021), the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, Paris Agreement and United Nations Food Systems Pathways.

Approaches that have been adopted at different contexts and scales to reduce food loss and waste include developing technologies and infrastructures, changing behaviour, enhancing food management skills (e.g., sorting, processing, cooking), developing protocols and legislation, and redistributing food (Nicastro & Carillo, 2021). Despite adopting different approaches, the SDG12.3 is woefully off-target. In order to achieve this target by 2030, many governments and businesses need to aggressively pursue the target-measure-act approach to reduce food waste, set a reduction target aligned with SDG target 12.3, measure food loss and waste to identify hot spots and monitor progress, and act more boldly to reduce food loss and waste (Champions, 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reduce food loss and waste (F10)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3.8> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3NXQ4833>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7B3C79YX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ANAM7355>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DB4RZLDP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DFJTP53M>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/I2HDIFCY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R95JZWYC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U2MJAIFU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WNZRL57W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WYCCZXDI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XR8KW8PP> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Disaster risks from coastal hazards that threaten lives and livelihoods such as flooding, storms, tsunamis and sea level rise can be mitigated by nature-based solutions provided by mangroves. Mangrove conservation and restoration can be applied globally within the natural range of mangroves which are also key ecological niches at the interface between land and water in the tropics and subtropics (Figure 5.4.7) (see also Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.1, B01, Area-based conservation and section 5.1.3.6, B06, Restoration of coastal and marine systems; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.6, F06, Ecological intensification – aquatic foods; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.13, C13, Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration). The ongoing destruction of mangrove is a threat to human, animal and ecosystem health (see also Chapter 2, sections 2.3.1.1, 2.4.1 and 2.4.4).

Mangrove conservation and restoration has synergies across health and other elements of the nexus (Figure 5.4.5). Its health benefit is based on the combination of significant reduction of lives lost from extreme events (Das & Vincent, 2009) and the provision of medicinal plants and animals (UNEP, 2006), although associated with mosquitoes (Awuku-Sowah et al., 2022; Ewel et al., 1998). More than 1 billion people live within 50 km of mangrove forests potentially benefit from them (UNEP, 2006). This response option improves water quality by filtration and absorption of effluents from aquaculture operations (Sulochanan et al., 2022; UNEP, 2006) and land-based sources of pollution (Mohamed et al., 2009). Mangroves provide shrimp and fish for healthy and culturally appropriate food (Ewel et al., 1998) and are an important support for fisheries due to their function as fish nurseries (UNEP, 2006). Maintaining and restoring mangroves helps to promote biodiversity by increasing the area of mangroves in good condition and providing critical habitat for an estimated 341 threatened species of reptiles, amphibians, mammals, fishes and birds (Carugati et al., 2018; Maricé & Spalding, 2022).

Restoring and conserving mangroves aligns with multiple international frameworks and is explicitly included in and actioned through the national priorities of many countries (Figure 5.4.6). Recognition of broad health benefits of mangroves is uneven but disaster risk reduction, including for climate resilient health systems, is one driver of action (WHO, 2022b). In the absence of health coverage, mangroves are protected locally as an important source of traditional medicines (Awuku-Sowah et al., 2023) (see also section 5.4.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants). Summing up all measured goods and services, the total economic value of managing intact mangroves is substantially higher than conversion to aquaculture (UNEP, 2006). The response option is technically feasible (Figure 5.4.7), provides fair distribution of costs and benefits to communities involved, and allows for participation of local stakeholders in decision-making and implementation. It is equitable in that it can protect the local use of mangroves by local, vulnerable groups from competition with outsiders (Glaser & Da Silva Oliveira, 2004) (Figure 5.4.6). It also facilitates a shift in views and values of mangrove forest from short-term extraction to long-term stewardship and sustainable use.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mangrove conservation and restoration for health (H08)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3JR833HA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7M2UFHXP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7N3SBZLZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8A4ZZI28>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9LID65R5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BSLSTIKC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DCPPL9R4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GMHAQ4TA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JP5NH64G>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/M7FMSIUJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMJ22R7V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/NXS24-ch5-4933> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Key conditions for effective governance of climate action are (i) ethics, values and world views; (ii) equity and justice; and (iii) harnessing ILK; (Babiker et al., 2022). Engaging with ethics, values and world views becomes indispensable in addressing the multifaceted challenges of climate change – spanning adaptation, mitigation and financial flows from developed to developing nations. These elements are not merely abstract notions but serve as the operative machinery guiding interactions and decisions across a spectrum of stakeholders (Hayward, 2012; Rokeach, 1980; van Egmond & de Vries, 2011).

Ethics can be construed as normative frameworks that delineate what ought to be, offering lofty visions that transcend self-interest (Hayward, 2012). In domain of climate action, ethics elucidates broader societal imperatives such as intergenerational equity and the rights of non-human entities, laying the groundwork for concerted public support. Ethical considerations can shape each climate action’s policy formulations, legal frameworks and operational guidelines.

Values are cognitive structures that orientate human judgments and behaviours (Rokeach, 1980). These internal metrics influence both macro-level policy configurations and micro-level individual choices. For example, an emphasis on the value of environmental stewardship could galvanise public sentiment in favour of adaptive measures such as ecosystem-based solutions, thereby shaping the enabling conditions for effective policy implementation. Values can affect public opinion and stakeholder engagement, which can facilitate or constrain the implementation of climate action.

World views constitute integrative systems of meaning, interweaving numerous value orientations into a coherent narrative (De Witt et al., 2016; van Egmond & de Vries, 2011). In the climate discourse, world views arbitrate how complex phenomena are cognitively processed and translated into action. They infuse existing policy structures with a philosophical anchor, whether a techno-optimistic outlook favouring market-based solutions or a deep ecological perspective advocating a systemic transformation (De Witt et al., 2016; Lacroix & Gifford, 2018). World views can shape overarching strategies and development pathways, affecting how policies and actions relating to climate action are integrated into broader societal and environmental contexts.

These concepts are interrelated, each shaping and being shaped by the other, constituting a complex adaptive system. Their interconnected nature steers the mechanics of climate action, demarcating the conceptual space where adaptation, mitigation and financial strategies intermingle (Singh & Chudasama, 2021b). This adaptive system generates the enabling conditions required for climate actions that are technically effective and socially equitable (IPCC, 2023). Thus, it is crucial to understand the interacting dynamics of ethics, values and world views for nuanced and inclusive climate strategies. They form the intellectual scaffolding upon which political and institutional arrangements can construct climate policies that are both scientifically sound and socially just (Brink & Wamsler, 2019; De Witt et al., 2016; Sanganyado et al., 2018; Schipper et al., 2022). Table 5.5.16 provides an understanding of how these ideas contextualize climate action for the 15 selected response options.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ethics, values and world views" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2.2.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4GECS6S2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8X4BMQH3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JAR5EMWN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L7VGARIT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MCHDTDMB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMKP32ER>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SLTFEEK9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VYKUR55P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YLYK3LFL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YVCEWMG3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z5PKN7EU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZUPX4RCZ> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Available evidence shows that there is clear bias in the distribution of biodiversity finance, with much of it remaining within developed countries, particularly the United States of America, Canada, Europe and China. The picture is potentially even more imbalanced for private financial flows, as only 5.3 per cent of private finance went to least developed countries and other low-income countries in 2017-2018 (OECD, 2020a). Another recent assessment notes that impact investing (e.g., with positive returns on social and environmental aspects) shows the same distributional bias, as most is concentrated in the United States of America and Europe and has only recently reached developing countries (BIOFIN, 2022). These patterns are also evident in financing for ocean and coastal sectors, where funding from international financial institutions is still dominated by large capital projects for European renewable marine energy projects (UNEP-FI, 2021).

Funding shortfalls for lower income countries are particularly concerning given the concept of ecological debt, which notes that historic ecological damages related to the impacts of international trade have often fallen on lower income countries, where biodiversity is also the most rich (A. Chaudhary & Brooks, 2019; Goeminne & Paredis, 2010; Hickel, Dorninger, et al., 2022; Hickel, O’Neill, et al., 2022; R. Jones et al., 2020; Lenzen et al., 2012; UNRISD, 2023). Countering this historical financial accumulation by recalibrating the distribution of equitable financing through "telecoupled conservation", "connected conservation" and/or "collaborative conservation" has been suggested as a solution that would rebalance the relationship between countries with regard to ecological and financial debt (Carmenta et al., 2023).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The geographical distribution of spending on biodiversity" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-A2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-A3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2L6F9BZS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2U2RHGL8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5NK57VXD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6DMIZX9W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8L4RWIMA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HZ2WYFXA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RVAEIFR5> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Global environmental and sustainable development targets remain challenging to meet, given gaps in human and technological capacity, as well as existing financial realities and mechanisms (Clark et al., 2018). The empirical evidence base for the effectiveness of different financing approaches to sustainability is still lacking in many places (Miller, 2014; Sayer et al., 2017). Important concerns also remain regarding the broader social, cultural and political effects of mainstreaming the economic valuation of biodiversity, including questions of culturally variable forms of value pluralism and particularly those values held by IPLC, which may be in opposition to attaching monetary values to nature (IPBES, 2022a; Palola et al., 2022; Spash & Aslaksen, 2015). These and other barriers to improving the nexus funding gap are indicated below." ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The persistence of funding gaps: barriers to nexus financing" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3.4.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch7> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6PCH4VRR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6RBETH49>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8A8MSWEM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B89DURIV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FTS6SIWR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JQU9XDHQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L42DLE3Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LKAT7PYP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NTVFF8UV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SGH5KK7P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/T7YWJYC7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TVNHQS26>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VMV78M3I> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Different kinds of alternative futures and visions can help inform and interrogate transformation processes (Mangnus et al., 2021; Muiderman et al., 2020). These include efforts that consider what alternative futures tell us about current sociocultural, political and economic dynamics, efforts that imagine plausible futures and what challenges and opportunities these futures suggest and efforts that build shared visions of desirable futures to inspire action (Cork et al., 2023). Many organizations have worked to develop processes to vision sustainable transition pathways that engage with both complexity and uncertainty (Bill Sharpe et al., 2016). These processes, similar to structured expert elicitations, are iterative; they can, but do not have to, build consensus as they can also be used to explore opposing world views in alternative futures, helping to highlight where there are common goals and interpretations, and where there is less agreement (Hemming et al., 2018). This requires (i) envisioning alternative futures, which are plausible and logically consistent, and (ii) developing a shared understanding of the present (A. Wilkinson, 2009; Vervoort et al., 2015). Both these exercises are challenging, given that socioeconomic and biophysical systems are complex, dynamic and deeply intertwined with linkages across spatial and temporal scales. How the present and future are envisioned are intermediated by social processes and values, often highlighting the challenges and injustices that need to be addressed. Developing shared understanding of the present and views of plausible futures are therefore a critical precursor to the development of road maps of change, as they focus attention on addressing a particular set of problems.

One framework suggests that accelerating sustainable transformation requires efforts that establish permission to change (building trust, transforming mindsets, enabling social license); develop responsive enabling environments (policies and regulations, market incentives, stable finance); and focus on responsible transitions (considering power dynamics and undesirable consequences) (Herrero et al. (Herrero et al., 2020, 2021). As another example (Costanza, 2000) describes a shared vision of a sustainable society, encompassing diverse visions that share some ethics and values and providing an alternative to other basic less desirable visions of the future. A diversity of values and knowledges are required to build such a broad shared vision of sustainability because the process itself involves deliberation, embracing uncertainty and reconciling divergent interests in order to generate wisdom to act in just, inclusive, interconnected and transformative ways. Choices about the future are grounded in ethical judgments, hence going beyond an integration of diverse strands of technical knowledge to a thickening of knowledge (Fazey et al., 2020; Hulme, 2018).

Methodologically, these steps towards common visions of the future can be taken using a range of approaches such as participatory scenarios to develop logically consistent alternative futures (viability and desirability); backcasting (Robinson, 1990, 2003); and three horizon approaches (Bill Sharpe et al., 2016) that explicitly consider the actions that would be required to move from the present to a desired future (achievability and viability) (see also Box 7.1).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.3.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Visioning pathways: processes and approaches" .

