<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.1.2.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2X5R8JQB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ALUK2PPY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EHFVCENA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FPXPZ62U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ILG8J2FA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IVT3ZL58>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L8CUSEF3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NR3RWUDL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PHGI6KA4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R2KUUZ77>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UFJHMJS4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UN45CYCL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VLI388FL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VWVZ3EML> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.1.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """One of the biggest strengths and challenges of a nexus approach is its complexity. A nexus approach moves away from siloed ways of thinking, anchored in particular disciplines, sectors, knowledge systems and world views, towards a more holistic and pluralistic approach that embraces complexity and uncertainty, spans boundaries and considers the interdependencies, dynamics, fluxes and feedback loops between different nexus elements (e.g., biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change), in both positive and negative ways (Howarth & Monasterolo, 2016; O’Connor et al., 2021; Walker et al., 2003). For example, adopting policies and practices to enhance the positive interactions between food production and biodiversity can provide benefits for both people and nature (Sietz et al., 2022).

However, complexity among elements and their interrelations may also lead to a range of positive, neutral and negative outcomes for different actors and elements that are expected, unexpected, unbalanced, emergent and unintentional (Bennett et al., 2019; Blythe et al., 2018; Gill et al., 2023). Adopting a dynamic and adaptive approach to nexus thinking that embeds continuous evaluation, learning and the ability to course correct is vital for developing integrated solutions (Chambers et al., 2022; Fazey et al., 2018; Howarth & Monasterolo, 2016; Sellers et al., 2019). Such an approach must be able to respond as new knowledge on nexus elements, outcomes and dynamics becomes available as well as external influences and unforeseen events that can influence the nexus system in unexpected ways. One example for tackling these challenges is integrated landscape and seascape approaches, which are ‘wholescape’ approaches (Ross et al., 2022) for exploring human-environment interactions and their positive (synergies) and negative (trade-offs) outcomes (Nishi & Yamazaki, 2020). Such approaches embrace adaptive multi-actor co-management arrangements to dynamically explore synergistic solutions (Karimova & Lee, 2022; Ross et al., 2022) (see Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.9 for detailed examples).

Overcoming this nexus challenge by embracing complexity enables agility through change, while enhancing the potential for achieving synergistic outcomes across biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change together. Complexity is discussed in more detail across the assessment, but especially in Chapters 4 and 5.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.1.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus complexity challenges" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.6.2.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3466RECX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4JM6NZ85>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9U5BPEIW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F8Q9JWCS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KVZ8Q4J5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QDU45JCB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SRHMX3M5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SRN8GWCW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TAV9XN76>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UNCZWZJA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VHXRK8T8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VUSA23JN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W9B54N6N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YNNFZTQ6> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """All nexus elements are directly related to one or more SDGs as well as targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement. This indicates the increasing recognition of the significant complex relationships among, and cascading effects between, different nexus elements among decision-makers. While global policy frameworks are important instruments for international governance, they have also been criticized and previously set targets have repeatedly been missed (Mace et al., 2018). Since progress towards the SDGs and other policy frameworks is measured via a suite of different indicators, informative yet efficient summarization of progress remains a challenge, prone to simplified conclusions and usually assessed within national boundaries (Chapter 7, section 7.3.8). Considering various indirect drivers (e.g., global trade) regularly affect direct drivers (e.g., land-use change), such an assessment is neither meaningful nor fair as environmental costs of resource consumption are often externalized. Such externalization can be measured by means of environmental footprints (Hoekstra & Wiedmann, 2014; Vanham et al., 2019), including the ecological footprint, carbon footprint, water footprint and others. The ecological footprint, for example, indicates that human demand for resources and ecological services increased from 7.0 billion global hectares (gha) in 1961 to 20.6 billion gha in 2014, reaching a point where the planet’s bio-productive area is not sufficient to support the competing demands (D. Lin et al., 2018).

The SDGs recognize that international trade may act as an engine for inclusive economic growth and poverty reduction (Blicharska et al., 2019), but also that trade can have negative impacts on sustainable development. Yet the existing indicator framework to assess sustainability of consumption and production fails to consider this sufficiently because it lacks some indicators of interest across both the domestic and foreign footprint. For example, although the SDGs consider material footprint as indicators for SDG12 (responsible consumption and production) and SDG8 (decent work and economic growth), other well-established footprints, such as the carbon, ecological or water footprint are not included. In general, there is a bias in the SDG monitoring towards indicators that focus on efficiency gains rather than total resource consumption (Wackernagel et al., 2017). As per capita footprints across and within countries differ substantially, not including them puts countries or socio-economic groups with low overall consumption rates at a disadvantage. As an example, countries may decrease their domestic footprints by outsourcing carbon intensive industries. In the meantime, they can increase their consumption related footprints (Brizga et al., 2017). As a result, composite indices (e.g., the SDG index) are positively correlated with high per capita ecological footprints (Wackernagel et al., 2017) and high import rates of biomass (Freudenberger et al., 2010).

Furthermore, there are significant consumption footprint differences within and between countries. Globally, the wealthiest 10 per cent of people are responsible for nearly half the world’s CO2 emissions, against 12 per cent of emissions by the poorest half of the world’s population (Chancel, 2022). Moreover, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework has been criticized as the costs of conservation are likely to have disproportionate negative impacts on LMICs, while benefits are distributed globally (Waldron et al., 2022). This is despite the fact that biodiversity loss and climate change have been mainly driven by consumptions demands in HICs. Expanding protected areas in ways that are not equitable and inclusive, especially in the most biodiverse parts of the world, is likely to affect rural communities in the poorest countries of the world, raising concerns of so-called green colonialism and land-grabbing for conservation (Collins et al., 2021; Henry et al., 2022).

Issues of global justice when it comes to the costs and benefits of action as well as responsibilities are aspects that are not sufficiently considered in the global conservation agenda nor easily monitored. For example, the implementation of climate adaptation actions (SDG13), particularly in ocean and coastal and mountain ecosystems, is having negative impacts on gender equality (SDG5) (Roy et al., 2022). As another example, additional protected areas might be ineffective if poorly implemented and managed (Meng et al., 2023). These findings suggest that the current monitoring frameworks lack a consistent consideration of complex interactions between different sustainability dimensions and fall short in considering global justice aspects. This includes goals both within and between frameworks (e.g., between different SDGs or between the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement) as well as other policy silos (e.g., international trade and economic development). Although there is mixed evidence regarding the potential of increased policy coherence by itself for achieving sustainable development (Chapter 4), based on evidence in this chapter, effective implementation of policy frameworks clearly requires a more careful consideration of trade-offs and synergies between different goals. This is in alignment with the need for improved ‘nexus governance’ which is further outlined in Chapter 4. As part of this move towards nexus governance, a stronger consideration of justice issues within indicator frameworks, including the rights of IPLC, is crucial given this chapter’s findings about the unequal burdens that negative declines in nexus elements are having on them, and would support a more substantial and equitable realization of the global policy frameworks.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.6.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Critical reflections on the implementation of monitoring of global policy frameworks" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6DD56Y4T>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/79EDXQSK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/79QXTL2J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CPS29S86>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/G6XGVW7R>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MLPPYVRZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MMH8A7CE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MYQVYQ53>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NEXS8CPR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RGF5RSG9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RUFQ9CMN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TNN5MYAP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VYVIYMBB> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Multiple socio-economic drivers can influence future food systems, including population growth, economic growth, dietary preference and food-related technology development (Hinz et al., 2020; Humpenöder et al., 2022; Tallis et al., 2018). Various food-related policies, such as free trade policies, may regulate production, food safety and other food system components on multiple scales (Alexander et al., 2019; von Braun et al., 2021). Furthermore, changes in environmental conditions, such as temperature, precipitation, rising carbon dioxide levels and nutrient and hydrological cycles can influence food production (Doelman et al., 2022; Engström et al., 2016; Hinz et al., 2020).

BAU scenarios for food systems often estimate a steady increase in food demand, supply and agricultural land-use under these socio-economic and ecological drivers. Total food demand under “middle of the road” scenarios (e.g., Shared Socioeconomic Pathway 2 (SSP2), or the FAO’s baseline scenario) is projected to increase by 51-98 per cent between 2005 and 2050 (Alexandratos et al., 2012; Valin et al., 2014; Van Dijk et al., 2021). The demand for animal-based items may increase faster than for plant-based items, resulting in an increased share of livestock products to total food demand (Alexandratos et al., 2012; Valin et al., 2014). The total amount of food loss and waste may also increase with BAU scenarios (Bijl et al., 2017; Lopez Barrera & Hertel, 2021). On the production side, the total production amounts for crops and livestock, agricultural land-use areas, and land-use intensity may increase under BAU assumptions, consistent with the changes in the demand side (Alexandratos et al., 2012; Popp et al., 2017).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Impacts of multiple drivers on food" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2VGTPI8V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8XYUT7JE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AGEA3IAB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CTMT6PWA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D9Z6PGMU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/G9QRYPNH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HW5MVL23>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LLMA6P9U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MQ25ZEWV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NBI5DTI3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P6GWI5GJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PX3N5XNR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VDGRSLU6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YHYNUVG8> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Various authors have started to define the concept of “nexus governance” e.g., (Newell et al., 2019; Opejin et al., 2020; Urbinatti et al., 2020). Nexus governance is widely seen as involving public, private and civil society actors and organizations coordinating activities across policy domains, governance levels and geographical boundaries (Leck et al., 2015). The concept of nexus governance broadly highlights innovative and holistic perspectives to identify, analyze and manage interdependencies through an interdisciplinary approach with consideration of how sectors and policies interact (Koulouri & Mouraviev, 2019). However, there is no current consensus on what nexus governance should or does encompass (see Table 4.3).

Given the current lack of agreement and consensus, there is considerable room for more systematic and clear definitions of nexus governance. For example, none of the definitions in Table 4.3 emerge from systematic reviews or intergovernmental assessment processes. Several authors have also pointed to persistent gaps in the existing nexus governance literature, including “(i) conditions for cross-sector coordination and collaboration; ii) dynamics that influence the nexus beyond cross-sector interactions; and (iii) political and cognitive factors as determinants of policy change” (Weitz et al., 2017: p.165). Furthermore, much of the existing literature on nexus governance focuses on what isn’t working, e.g., governance failures as a result of fragmented, reactive, exclusive and opaque systems (Lebel et al., 2020), while there has been significantly less work on empirical examples of nexus governance and on positive examples of successes.

Nexus governance can also be related to transformative governance, a recent approach that refers to strategies, actions and roles of key actors implemented at different spatial scales (from local to global) aimed at enabling transformative change, either for governing transformative change or transforming governance to open up avenues for transformative change (Visseren-Hamakers et al., 2021; Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022). In line with the IPBES Transformative Change Assessment, this assessment defines transformative change as fundamental, system-wide shifts across views, structures and practices. When such shifts address the underlying causes of biodiversity loss and nature’s decline, transformative change contributes to a just and sustainable world. Transformative governance thus refers to an alteration of existing configurations of views, structures and practices to enable effective and just biodiversity governance. Actions include integrative governance that revises decision-making processes, an inclusive governance that creates ownership and deliberation among stakeholders, a global and multi-level governance that addresses global interdependencies, as well as an adaptive governance that continuously evaluates and revises regulatory and holds responsible actors accountable for addressing biodiversity related issues (IPBES Transformative Change Assessment). Yet it is currently unclear to what extent nexus governance might contribute to transformative change while also addressing nexus-related sustainability problems more generally (Pascual et al., 2022; Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022).

Given these limitations, a systematic review of existing policy and governance literature has been undertaken to identify gaps and highlight empirical examples of where governance that focuses on interconnections among two or more nexus elements is happening (see section 4.3). Building on the review, section 4.5 proposes a more comprehensive definition and operationalization of the concept of nexus governance and provides case study examples of it in real-world practice. This includes examples of scaling nexus governance (as introduced in section 4.4). This more comprehensive approach to nexus governance includes a deeper and more systematic assessment of enablers and barriers; enhanced understanding of the key actors and arenas of engagement involved and/or excluded in the governance of nexus interactions; and examination of the key cross cutting issues of equity, justice and intersectionality. This section also discusses how to bring nexus governance into alignment with transformative governance in terms of aiming for more transformational changes. Section 4.6 then highlights the important role of decision-support tools to address some of the complexities involved in governing nexus interactions.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Existing definitions of nexus governance and their challenges" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5TW9ZCPM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6ENBGNXL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8FN88U35>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8QMHIYF7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8XYUT7JE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AM8WFBYS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ANBXHGR2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/G9HY7JPV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GI8IKWM8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/K7BLXTLT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VWHUENAG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y8V3BWV9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YUAWBQAW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YY44FQRR> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """There are multiple actors involved in governance and decision-making for the nexus and the implementation of diverse response options across sectors and scales (see also Chapter 5). Chapter 1 has identified the categories of actors likely to be of importance (Chapter 1, section 1.3.3, Table 1.2) and highlighting the scale at which they operate. This chapter analyses actors’ participation in governance approaches and implementation of policy and sociopolitical options, including through a systematic review of the literature (section 4.3).

Actors seek to influence public decisions and enable action to address societal aspirations, needs and concerns (Kooiman, 2003). The main actors described in the Chapter 1 categorization can be divided into three different (but not mutually exclusive) groups according to their level of influence or power and affectedness in relation to response options and governance across the nexus (Chevalier & Buckles, 2008; Kelemen et al., 2022). This includes:

Actors with influence: actors and institutions who influence decision-making processes related to the nexus and therefore have an impact on those who implement decisions. This also includes institutions/actors that can act as lobbyists or as watchdogs to foster accountability through advocacy.

Affected actors: people, including communities and marginalized groups who are directly involved in (and dependent on) the implementation of nexus-related decisions, and have their own stakes and interests (outlined in more detail in Chapters 5, 6 and 7).

Key players: people and institutions who can both influence and become affected by decisions.

There are clear differences in the level of influence that different actors can exert depending on their social groups, sector or scale of intervention (Alonso Roldán et al., 2015; Felipe-Lucia et al., 2015). Understanding asymmetries in power and influence, information and capacities among these actors is therefore critical for considering who benefits or is burdened by decisions and who has both the power and/or the responsibility to make changes linked to nexus interlinkages, and the drivers that act on these interlinkages (M. S. Reed et al., 2009; Vallet et al., 2019). Power can be exerted through formal and informal institutions, as key actors from government, global and regional institutions, knowledge communities, financing institutions, media and the arts, civil society and the private sector seek to influence the trajectory of development. Interactions and relationships between governance actors are foundational to understanding the power asymmetries that affect governance across the nexus (Bodin, 2017; Bodin & Crona, 2009; Bréthaut et al., 2019; Crona & Bodin, 2010; Srigiri & Dombrowsky, 2022; Stein et al., 2018; Weitz et al., 2017). For example, Box 4.2 presents a case study from Brazil emphasizing how actors affected by environmental challenges can self-organize in networks, build collective strategies and capacity and therefore become key players in governance. This highlights the importance of the relative capacities of actors.

To understand how capacities may function as either enablers or barriers to effective nexus governance, it is necessary to understand the context in which these capacities operate. Such a context is defined not only by the actors and institutions themselves, but also the physical, social and political spaces (referred to here as the “arenas of engagement”, see section 4.5) in which these actors and institutions engage with each other and with the nexus. section 4.5.1 provides a more complete discussion of actors and their corresponding arenas of engagement to elucidate the role of context in determining governance of nexus interactions. This is followed by a fuller discussion of capacities in section 4.5.5.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actors and their roles governing the nexus" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4.1.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/47DBSULV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C75YXG4W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D87NZRHD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FXL9WYAV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GZ4RBZ9B>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HUQ323V7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/M3Z5NW9G>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NWNPNPAL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RXWCGYVT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TR5VAVVW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U3Z7F5EN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VSB3QV4Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XKCV5UHZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YME6CN3S> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scaling down, often used interchangeably as downscaling, primarily emphasizes the vertical devolution of responsibility and accountability from higher-level institutions and actors to lower levels to support and improve the local policy and decision-making process (M. G. Reed & Bruyneel, 2010). By devolving authority to the local level, scaling down recognizes and respects the local capacity to identify and address specific challenges and opportunities at the local level (Barakat & Kapisazovic, 2003; Lawford, 2019). Furthermore, scaling down is closely intertwined with geographical scale, emphasizing the materialization and localization of response options from larger scales (e.g., international or national) to the local level (also referred to as down-zoning) (Lampinen et al., 2019; Taylor et al., 2013), including down to the household level (Itayi et al., 2021). It also underscores the importance of downsizing and tailoring broad response options or criteria to better align with the local context (J. Liu, Hull, et al., 2018) (see Box 4.6)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.4.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scaling down" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5.3.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3EV268VW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/46GWLQ5M>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5SQVT4US>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9KVJKCGW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9UK2IWBJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DXZAIU3Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KFHJV32Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LPD8A34N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NIKJ2RSV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PVW54SVE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QMRSPLB2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/X64X9FXF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/X9V4CU3F> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """In seeking to achieve the three dimensions of equity, consideration of rights-based approaches, such as a human-rights based approach, can be useful (Box 4.11). They can facilitate a more equitable acknowledgement of the diverse values, needs and rights of marginalized actors, and can intersect with nexus challenges and governance in numerous ways. Participation guaranteed by human-rights based approaches and which makes use of safeguards like free, prior and informed consent (FPIC) can strengthen representation and effective engagement of key actor groups, supporting their agency to effectively assert their voices in policy and decision-making processes. For example, locally led climate change adaptation response options that involve Indigenous Peoples, local communities, women and youth are more likely to have positive outcomes for both people and the ecosystems on which they depend (Asugeni et al., 2019; Global Centre on Adaptation & Climate and Development Knowledge Network, 2023; Nalau et al., 2018; Westoby et al., 2020). This can also lead to virtuous cycles: for example, when IPLC (and elders and women in particular) are afforded meaningful participation and representation as key actors with distinct knowledge and value systems, this can aid in passing these knowledges, practices and values onto younger generations, as well as leading to the co-design of innovative response options (COD-ILK, 2023; IPBES, 2023b). Moreover, establishing leadership roles can create the potential for these actors, including IPLC, to grow their adaptive capacity that will in turn improve their ability to address future environmental issues (Kampanje & Kampanje, 2022).

This is particularly the case for women, including Indigenous women, who are disproportionately impacted by climate change, food, water and health insecurity and biodiversity loss. Ensuring their inclusion in the development and implementation of any response option to address these issues can ensure that benefits and burdens are distributed equally. Options for deepening gender transformative actions move beyond identifying women’s rights to exploring the gendered roles of men and women, as well as the acknowledgement of LGBTIQA+ actors in nexus governance (Carthy & Landesman, 2023). By adopting a holistic gender-transformative approach (see also section 4.5.3.3 on intersectionality) which focuses on the economic, political, ecological and cultural underlying causes of vulnerability of different groups, co-designed interventions to address the root causes of vulnerability can transform power relations that have been shaped by unequal patriarchal and discriminatory norms and practices. In turn, this can facilitate overcoming barriers to the visibility and voice of marginalized actors and result in a better recognition of the importance of tailored approaches to address the differentiated needs of actors in a system (Humayra et al., 2024; TEBTEBBA & ELATIA, 2022). Thus, a holistic gender-transformative approach goes beyond the symptoms of inequality to redress gender inequalities, remove structural barriers, such as unequal roles and rights of diverse gender identities and empower disadvantaged actors, and is increasingly being called for by multiple donors funding nexus related work (MacArthur et al., 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Operationalizing equity and justice" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3SLRL6QY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4L7RT3H7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5ARLHEV7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6R5MXRE6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B5MX36ND>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BFEIIB6X>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EEVRNTQY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/G9QRYPNH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NFWQI8YF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PA2LPK93>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R56SCWLA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WJC3TNIU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z77GMNRS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZRFMQX3R> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """A diverse set of actors are associated with water, some unique to the sector (United Nations, 2015) (Table 5.2.1). The examples of actors in this section are described in general terms that can be applied to different geographical areas and countries with their own cultural, social and economic characteristics.

Global and regional institutions: These actors can be multi-national organizations with a broad interest in ensuring clean water for food and health (e.g., United Nations, World Health Organization). These organizations may elaborate on resolutions (e.g., on the human right to access to clean water and sanitation), which are implemented at the national level through the enactment of local regulations and implementation of public policies. River basins are often transboundary; thus, they require multiple governments and are often managed with international organizations such as water basin commissions (Fasel et al., 2016). Mechanisms such as UN-Oceans are also fundamental in their role as facilitators and coordination between water actors and other elements like biodiversity.

National governments: Government actors can enact regulations and policies that allow (or curtail) the use of water by actors in the other elements, such as through ministries or departments that can enact regulations across local/provincial boundaries. However, in some instances there will not be just one ministry or department but several that oversee different aspects of water.

Sub-national and local governments and municipalities: These are similar to national governments but are often focused on one body of water (or a series of connected bodies of water). Some basins can encompass large systems and can also be water bodies within a province or district. Water boards with multiple actors is a common approach to address complex water problems (Bos et al., 2015; Nesheim et al., 2021).

Knowledge and educational communities: These actors focus on understanding complex topics that can inform policy decisions linked to water, disseminating findings to policymakers and providing networks to build from existing knowledge and technology (Davies et al., 2021). These actors can also integrate multiple water-related disciplines (e.g., hydrology, limnology, ecology) to address the complexity of knowledge required for the nexus approach, thus overcoming disciplinary silos (Ozment et al., 2015). Professional societies focused on water issues are also a valuable part of scientific institutions. Scientists are key actors because they develop new knowledge on processes in the natural world and socioeconomies, often producing actionable knowledge that contributes to the improvement of the quality of life of society.

Civil society organizations: This is a very wide class of social actors with characteristics which vary across regions, cultures, societies and scales. Some programs focus on biodiversity conservation (e.g., International Union of Conservation of Nature, The Nature Conservancy) or clean drinking water whereas others may focus on other elements that influence water, including health and food systems (Newell et al., 2019).

Community-based organizations: These actors have an interest in water issues at the local level and may be lake associations or community collectives that may focus on certain water issues, such as reducing pollution, improving water quality and access, regulating local catch or harvest of fishes. Although these actors may focus on local issues, they can also be a part of a larger organization (e.g., river keeper alliance).

IPLC: Water provides spiritual connections and is often integral to IPLC origins. The context in which IPLC understand ‘water is life’ concepts may be very different to that of other actors. In New Zealand, the Maori people view water as having its own rights because water is its own being (Stewart-Harawira, 2020). This approach relates to how water is used, as in some cases ILK acts as a guardian of ecosystems and for implementing higher levels of sustainability. In Côte d’Ivoire, certain watercourses are sacred and inaccessible to everyone without the authorization of IPLC managers. IPLC also collaborate with researchers, ideally from the inception of the scientific study, to jointly produce and provide information (Lara et al., 2021).

Private sector and business organizations: These actors have a broad range of interests both within and outside the water sector and are influential across multiple spatial scales. Large businesses such as human health systems, insurers, water companies and financial institutions have a direct linkage to clean water and sanitation, whereas other actors use water for manufacturing (Hoekstra et al., 2019). Smaller businesses operate at a local scale but can include commercial fishers and farmers among others. However, crop production can also be tied to large agribusiness (Galeano, 2012), which may have a stronger influence than local farmers. Dam regulators may be public or private entities that operates a dam for multiple uses.

Irrigation associations also oversee water allocations and could be from the private sector or community organizations. Consumers influence the private sector when buying bottled water and paying for public water services.

Financing institutions: Financial institutions can allocate funds to nexus governance and the implementation of specific response options. For example, using financial instruments for agri-environmental payments may benefit water through helping to reduce erosion and protect water quality. Biodiversity financing may also benefit water, such as paying forest owners to protect watersheds and recharge aquifers (Muñoz-Piña et al., 2008) (Chapter 6, section 6.2.4.2).

Media and the arts. Media outlets and the arts disseminate information to the public and can raise awareness of water-related concerns. For example, in the U.S., the Water Desk approach summarizes water- and agricultural-related issues in specific river basins (e.g., (Mississippi River Basin Ag & Water Desk, n.d.; The Water Desk, n.d.).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key institutions and actors" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.8> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2C4CD38A>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2RK5XP8B>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BIA5WZDG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FK9PCWEE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HN2M2LP4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HQWEQIKH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MSKGRZDT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NQSDPR2S>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NXDDJAH8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TI7Y949X>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VPPZHZHA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XVKUNNTV> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Working across political boundaries to balance water needs across countries and sectors can have global significance (Figure 5.2.5) with 153 countries having territory within at least one of the 286 transboundary river and lake basins and 592 transboundary aquifer systems (UN & UNESCO, 2021). Transboundary cooperation is mainly supported by cooperative water agreements, which cut across several sectors, including water resources, irrigation, hydropower, environment, biodiversity, agriculture, fisheries and others. For example, the Convention on the Protection and Use of Transboundary Watercourses and International Lakes (Water Convention), adopted in Helsinki in 1992, is an international environmental agreement wherein the parties take actions to prevent, control and reduce transboundary impacts on biodiversity and other nexus elements, use transboundary waters reasonably and equitably and ensure their sustainable management (UNECE, 2018).

Successful transboundary cooperation can be enabled by a functional water cooperation mechanism that regulates transboundary water management and diversifies transboundary water governance responsibilities, sharing benefits within and outside transboundary river basins and making systemic efforts to pursue transboundary water management in a sustainable, equitable and integrative manner (Grünwald et al., 2020). Cooperation on transboundary waters cuts across several nexus elements (Figure 5.2.3). Examples include cooperation between several Balkan countries which share the Dinaric Karst Aquifer System, which has enabled national and regional management approaches for ecosystem protection (UN & UNESCO, 2021). The Permanent Okavango Basin River Commission’s livelihood and socioeconomic development thematic programme has enabled cooperation between Angola, Botswana and Namibia, and demonstrates ways in which basin-wide strategic development programmes can address underlying drivers of poverty (Permanent Okavango Basin River Water Commission, 2019). The 2012 Great Lakes Water Quality Agreement has provided a framework for common action by Canada and the United States of America to reduce “chemicals of mutual concern” that threaten human health and the environment (Government of Canada, 2012). Countries of the lower Mekong River basin have adopted the Mekong Climate Change Adaptation Strategy and Action Plan (Mekong River Commission, 2018) to coordinate climate action. Transboundary cooperation on adaptation and mitigation of climate change can also bring additional benefits, such as conflict prevention, socioeconomic development and human well-being (UN & UNESCO, 2021).

Cost- and benefit-sharing between riparian states can solve water pollution issues and support sharing of benefits from collaboration related to hydropower, flood control, monitoring and facility use (Ansink & Houba, 2014; Dinar, 2006; UN Water, 2008). Transboundary management can facilitate “win-win” opportunities enabling financial and water transfers, cross-border water storage, river basin restoration, pollution control and wastewater management (Arjoon et al., 2014; Bark et al., 2014; Ghosh et al., 2017; Roebeling et al., 2014; L. Yuan et al., 2023; Zeferino et al., 2014). Data from 101 of 153 countries indicated that Europe and North America have the fullest coverage of operational arrangements (covering 90 per cent or more of their transboundary river and lake basin area), followed by sub-Saharan Africa (18 of 42 countries) (UN & UNESCO, 2021). The SDG target 6.5 is closely aligned with this response option, with benefits contributing to several SDGs (UN & UNESCO, 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Transboundary water cooperation (W08)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/863R5XCY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ELVVW6AS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N5W54F83>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SSJBGQ3B> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Several knowledge gaps were found in the course of assessing the food-related response options. Many of the key knowledge gaps for food systems are closely linked:

1) Synergies and trade-offs across both response options and nexus elements. Food system actors rarely adopt single response options but apply and adapt multiple response options while seeking impact across nexus elements. This contrasts with most scientific evidence, which assesses single response options and one or few nexus elements. More generally, there is a knowledge gap around how to promote the alignment of different interest groups, industries and policymaking bodies to minimise trade-offs and maximise synergies.

2) Engagement, implementation and behavioural change. There is a lack of sufficient knowledge on how to engage all relevant actors, integrate action across actors and sectors, implement response options and translate knowledge into useful forms. Food system response options are contingent on behavioural change but major gaps remain in understanding effective behavioural change mechanisms to move production, consumption and purchasing decisions toward options that have more nexus-wide benefits.

3) Cross-scale issues and international trade. Identifying and managing trade-offs while leveraging synergies is relatively well documented at landscapes scales, but not at national and international scales. How international trade affects the magnitude and distribution of the costs and benefits of food production, and the efficacy of response options remains a critical gap, particularly with the potential of leakage and the displacement of negative nexus impacts to other regions.

4) Justice implications of response options are largely missing from the literature, including impacts of change on communities, notably food producing communities, but also the justice dimensions of food over-consumption and displaced environmental impacts on populations least responsible for that impact. There is also a lack of knowledge on gender input to climate change mitigation and adaptation policies (section 5.3.3.14). Issues of equity beyond gender and youth are not systematically incorporated into research and innovation, and <10 per cent of 1.3 million agri-food research publications reported any social inclusion or human development outcomes (CoSAI, 2021). There is a need to address specific questions around how to ensure the benefits and costs of yield-increasing (sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) or nature-restoring response options (sections 5.3.3.1 and 5.3.3.2) are equitably distributed, notably across countries and in terms of economics, gender and IPLC.

5) Unbiased knowledge. There are geographic and sociocultural biases in the availability of evidence, with most evidence from high-income countries. Literature that focuses on smallholder farming systems is more representative of the Global South and has high relevance. While known to be important, diverse and adapted to local environments, ILK food systems are poorly documented. There are also biases in which world view and knowledge system is acknowledged as valid evidence, with ILK systems frequently neglected or marginalized.

6) Clear terminology. Terms such as agroecology, diversification, regenerative, conservation tillage, ecological or sustainable intensification, and organic are used varyingly as knowledge systems, sets of practices, specific practices and outcome measures. This limits the effectiveness of evidence synthesis and can have unintended sociopolitical implications such as the promotion of a biased or suboptimal suite of interventions.

7) Microbiomes as drivers of human and soil metabolism. While much focus has been given to macro-biodiversity there is growing recognition that microbiomes play key roles in metabolic processes, notably in soils and in and on humans. Soil and gut microbiomes are gaining increasing attention thanks to the emergence of increasingly accurate microbial tracing technologies. While soil and human microbiomes share some overlap if individuals are exposed to soils at an early age, the microbiomes remain quite distinct. However, there is growing evidence that modern practices, both in food production and food consumption have similar effects in altering the diversity, composition and function of soil and gut microbiomes with effects on both soil and human health. Low input diversity (crop diversity/food diversity); regular exposure to biocides (pesticides, herbicides, antibiotics); and high turnover inputs (nitrogen fertilizer/sugar consumption) have all been correlated with important changes leading to lower microbiome diversity in soils and human guts. To what degree this loss impacts important soil and human health functions, including possible soil/human gut microbiome interrelations, remains poorly understood (see also Chapter 5.4, Box 5.4.6).

To overcome these knowledge gaps, food systems research would benefit from the co-development of research and implementation plans with a range of food system actors. In addition, it should focus on achieving change, rather than just documenting unsatisfactory conditions (B. A. Williams et al., 2020). Broadening evidence reviews and recognizing diverse knowledge systems (particularly place-based ILK systems), clearly identifying responsibilities, and fostering collaboration and open participation will all be needed to increase fairness, justice and impact in food system change (Duncan et al., 2022; Webb et al., 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/263AXB3P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6RKXBRI3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/72N6ZQXJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7N3SBZLZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/A7BLPJTX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JK5VWMBS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMJ22R7V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PWHNEMB7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QAGLZ9FC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R5BCNRZJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SEKF3JYY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VB26UB8G>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W6MHQXSH> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Climate action unfolds in a polycentric arena featuring a range of actors, including global and regional institutions, national governments, local governments and municipalities, knowledge and educational communities, civil society organizations, community-based organizations, IPLC, private sector and business organizations, science-policy interfaces, financing institutions, and media and the arts (see section 5.5.2). These actors either influence climate action or are affected by it or play both roles (Figure 5.5.14). Actors operate at multiple scales (local to global), introducing intricate governance dynamics (Schipper et al., 2022; Singh & Chudasama, 2021b). Global and regional institutions often operate in a political sphere where public and private actors interact across national borders and political jurisdictions (Andonova et al., 2009; Singh & Chudasama, 2021a). Global and regional institutions and national governments delineate governance terrains via an ensemble of economic, financial, legal and regulatory instruments, which employ a mix of coercion and control, voluntarism and frameworks and goal-setting. Such complexities accentuate the asymmetric power dynamics pivotal to effective climate action. While transcending international borders, global and regional institutions (Table 5.5.1) amplify their influence through dialogues with other stakeholders (Andonova et al., 2009; Singh & Chudasama, 2021a). Their adeptness at connecting diverse actors stimulates harmonized and impactful climate action and often sets a constructive course for cross-sectoral solutions for nexus issues.

The governance landscape for climate action encompasses a diverse range of formal and informal mechanisms. Both are pivotal in addressing climate change effectively and are integral for achieving balance across biodiversity, water, food, health and human well-being. Informal governance models often manifest through grassroots initiatives and community-led actions. In these non-institutionalized settings, IPLC contribute significantly to climate action through their traditional knowledge and sustainable practices (Lestarini et al., 2018; Melaku et al., 2023; Sarmiento et al., 2018). More traditional governance mechanisms have been the bedrock of international climate action. Foremost among these is the Conference of the Parties (COP) of the UNFCCC, in which all parties to UNFCCC are represented, and which establishes goals and actions to implement the convention. Yet, as Jordan et al. (2015) articulate, the limitations of these centralized structures become apparent when grappling with the complexity of climate action.

As a response to these limitations, polycentric governance has emerged as a compelling alternative, characterised by multiple, overlapping centres of authority (Jordan et al., 2015). Polycentric governance encourages experimentation and fosters a culture of innovation, sharing this in common with nexus governance. Both engender policy diffusion and cross-scale interactions, which are essential for an integrated climate response rooted in principles of equity, justice and sustainable development (Reckien et al., 2018; Schipper et al., 2022). However, the fluidity in governance structures often results in the absence of a central authority to manage the design, implementation and evaluation of climate responses (Robinson et al., 2022). This has been the source of much attention to polycentricity in climate governance, as challenges such as fragmented networks and divergent goals among NGOs, IPLC and the private sector persist (Oubenal et al., 2017; Stanišić et al., 2021; Zinngrebe et al., 2020). Coordinated financial instruments, regional platforms for collaboration and networking mechanisms are prerequisite to bridge these gaps.

Figure 5.5.13 shows the role of actors in the 15 climate action response options assessed in section 5.5.3. This nuanced portrayal underscores the complexity and multi-dimensionality of climate governance, where different actors play distinct roles in shaping climate action outcomes. For instance, global and regional institutions consistently emerge as influential actors, reflecting their pivotal role in setting international agendas, facilitating cooperation, and providing essential resources and frameworks for action. National governments, categorized frequently as key players, are responsible for influencing and being affected by climate policies, indicating their critical position in implementing and adapting global strategies to local contexts.

Financing institutions show a mixed level of involvement, underscoring the necessity for stronger financial commitments and innovative funding mechanisms to support climate change response options. The varied participation of media and the arts highlights the potential for these sectors to enhance public awareness and engagement, though their influence remains underutilized in some areas.

Figure 5.5.14 shows the classification of the response options as an action or management (i.e., a response option that is directly implemented on the ground), a policy instrument that guides action and/or an enabler that facilitates or improves action, management or policy. Chapter 4, Table 4.4 describes the policy categories used in Figure 5.5.14.

Table 5.5.15 identifies gaps and opportunities for enhancing the effectiveness of climate action. For instance, there is a need for enhanced financial commitments from financing institutions and increased public engagement through media and the arts. National governments can improve their influence by fostering cross-sector collaboration, investing in capacity-building, and strengthening international cooperation. By recognizing the strengths and weaknesses of each actor, policymakers and stakeholders can better strategize collaborative efforts, optimize resource allocation, and drive more inclusive and impactful climate initiatives. This comprehensive understanding is crucial for advancing integrated approaches that align global aspirations with local realities, ultimately fostering a more resilient and sustainable future.

Notes
7. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actors and institutions in the complex space of climate action" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2HGMZBR4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6TLGR8XQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7DYHZXD8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AY8W7T4U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DA7E3L4U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/E8H57W6Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FFT24JN6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IWBDB7UP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KMZRGLSG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LYJUS3AW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MICPJ2KI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PFZRXW3Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PIFEX4EJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TKTIARG7> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The nexus between climate action and the interconnected spheres of biodiversity, water, food, health and human well-being can be tackled with a multitude of legal, financial, rights-based and socio-cultural policies and procedures. This section presents key policies and measures that have been suggested, implemented or discussed in the context of global frameworks such as the Paris Agreement, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the SDGs (CBD, 2023; UNFCCC, 2015).

The primary legal instruments that have emerged include the nationally determined contributions under the Paris Agreement, and the preparation of national-level climate and biodiversity action plans (Table 5.5.15). These plans serve not only as a medium of implementation of global and national targets, but also for monitoring, reporting and transparency in achieving the targets. Approximately two-thirds of nationally determined contributions recognize ecosystem vulnerabilities to climate change, with 63 per cent explicitly stating ecosystem protection as a key impetus for adaptation strategies (Seddon et al., 2019).

Nearly 43 per cent of nationally determined contributions cover food value chain considerations, mainly from least developed countries and small island developing states (Wieben, 2019). Nonetheless, an evident gap persists in comprehensively incorporating food, water and health systems within nationally determined contributions. For example, there is no requirement to specify how climate change mitigation actions might negatively affect biodiversity (Pörtner et al., 2021). Addressing this gap is a priority, especially when global targets favour greater synergy between sectors. This is evident from the the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets such as reducing global food waste under Target 16, advancing health under Target 12 and water sustainability under Target 11 (CBD, 2023), which further reinforce SDGs 2, 3 and 6, and are critical for climate change mitigation, adaptation and resilience. Among many national-level planning processes, national adaptation planning mandated under the UNFCCC can potentially support responses that maximize synergies with food, water, health and biodiversity. However, many national adaptation programmes of action lack integration with biodiversity impacts of some actions, such as hydropower (Pörtner et al., 2021). Therefore, integration of nexus elements into legal and regulatory measures will help build understanding of the overlaps and trade-offs, and their effective management.

In addition to legal and regulatory measures, economic and financial measures play a critical role in advancing the implementation of response options (Table 5.5.15). Policies like Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation and the role of conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks in developing countries (REDD+), for example, target the enhancement of forest carbon stocks (C12) in developing countries through financial incentives (UNFCCC, 2008) and can contribute to the livelihoods of communities (Ball & Makala, 2014). Furthermore, the Paris Agreement underscores the multiplicity of forest-related benefits, extending beyond carbon sequestration to encompass food, water and health-related gains (UNFCCC, 2015). More recently, the UNFCCC and Convention on Biological Diversity have paid attention to the use of ecosystem and/or nature-based solutions (nature-based solutions) (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019; UNFCCC, 2022) considering it as a multi-benefit approach for climate change and biodiversity. Although there are significant overlaps between EbA and nature-based solutions, the latter covers joint mitigation and adaptation options more holistically (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019; Pörtner et al., 2021). But relevant social and environmental safeguards need to be present for implementation of nature-based solutions (UNFCCC, 2022) to improve on synergies and reduce any trade-offs associated with its implementation (see section 5.5.3.14, C14, Urban nature-based solutions).

Rights-based and customary paradigms are particularly salient in securing the success of numerous response options through elevated levels of community engagement, which fosters accountability, compliance and active stewardship (Doswald & Osti, 2011). Community-based participatory approaches can spearhead implementation of C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change (section 5.5.3.12); B03/C11, Agroecology (section 5.5.3.11; Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3); C02/F03, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3) and F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.5.3.15; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11), catalysing the achievement of sustainability approaches (H. Gupta et al., 2022). Table 5.5.15 shows the policies and procedures with their potential for synergistic interlinkages with different nexus elements. These policies have been chosen based on their potential to impact multiple sectors, but the scale represents the extent to which this impact can be realized.

Governing climate actions and managing trade-offs between climate change mitigation, adaptation and their nexus interlinkages with biodiversity, water, food and health systems requires the right enabling conditions. Policies and procedures can act as enablers, but also barriers. In addition to policy effectiveness, other enablers and barriers include institutional capacities, financial and economic, behaviour and lifestyle, technology and material endowments (see Chapter 4, Table 4.6). Figure 5.5.15 presents a comprehensive overview of various enablers influencing climate change response options, highlighting their roles and impacts. Policy design and implementation emerge as consistently strong enablers across multiple response options, underscoring the necessity for robust policy frameworks to support climate actions such as C01, Increased soil organic carbon (section 5.5.3.1) and C02/F03, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3). While generally supportive, equity and diversity exhibit mixed effectiveness in certain response options like C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (section 5.5.3.4), indicating the need for targeted equity measures to enhance inclusivity in climate strategies. Institutional capacities vary significantly, with strong institutional frameworks being pivotal for the success of options like C02/F03, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3) and C03, integrated multitrophic aquaculture (section 5.5.3.3), emphasizing the importance of capacity-building. Financial and economic considerations frequently surface as critical enablers or barriers, exemplified by strong financial support for C01, Increased soil organic carbon (section 5.5.3.1) and C03, Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (section 5.5.3.3), and contrasted by economic barriers to C02/F03, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3), highlighting the necessity for tailored financial mechanisms. Behaviour and lifestyle enablers generally support sustainable practices, though mixed conditions in some areas indicate challenges in driving behavioural change. Technological advancement is crucial, as evidenced by its role as an enabler in many response options, reflecting the need for ongoing innovation and dissemination of climate technologies. Material endowments, or the availability of natural and material resources, play a variable role, being a strong enabler for C03, Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (section 5.5.3.3) and C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (section 5.5.3.4) but a barrier for C05, Offshore wind power (section 5.5.3.5), highlighting resource-specific challenges. Indigenous and local knowledge generally supports climate response options, particularly in enhancing soil organic carbon (section 5.5.3.1, C01) and C02/F03, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3), though its role remains neutral in some areas, suggesting opportunities to better integrate this knowledge into broader climate strategies.

Both actors (Figure 5.5.13) and enablers (Figure 5.5.15) play complementary roles in the success of climate change response options. While actors provide the necessary governance, resources and engagement, enablers offer the structural support needed to implement and sustain these actions. Effective climate strategies benefit from a holistic approach that leverages the strengths of both actors and enablers, ensuring coordinated and impactful action. Strengthening the roles of both actors and enablers is crucial to creating a resilient and sustainable framework for addressing climate change. Thus, it is vital to have integrated efforts in governance, resource allocation and structural support to enhance the effectiveness of climate response actions.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Policies and procedures for effective climate action in the context of the nexus" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Executive summary

Response options are widely available and feasible across the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change (well established) {5.6.2}. Expert teams in the five subchapters identified and assessed a total of 71 response options. This provides evidence-based support to policymakers, communities and other actors to understand the impacts of these response options both within their own element and across all nexus elements. Response options can be clustered into ten general categories: conserve and halt conversion of remaining intact ecosystems, restore natural and semi-natural ecosystems, manage ecosystem functions in human-dominated ecosystems, consume sustainably, reduce pollution and waste, integrate planning and governance, manage risk, ensure rights and equity, align financing, and an ‘others’ category. These categories represent broad types of actions that are relevant across nexus elements and involve similar types of actors.

All the response options presented and assessed in Chapter 5 have positive impacts on at least one other element of the nexus (well established) {5.6.3}. The number and strength of these impacts varies, from benefits to all other elements to a mix of positive and negative impacts. Nevertheless, all response options assessed here represent promising ways to advance holistic, integrated pathways to sustainable futures (established but incomplete) {5.6.3}. Eleven response options were independently identified as promising by two or more subchapters; these shared response options are viable options for collaborations among actors in multiple elements that often act independently (established but incomplete) {5.6.3}.

Response options commonly interact with each other, either by facilitating impacts through synergies or impeding effectiveness through trade-offs (established but incomplete) {5.6.4, 5.6.7}. By acknowledging and managing these synergies and trade-offs, actors can improve the impacts of their actions and the likelihood that they will achieve the desired outcomes. Bundling or sequencing several response options is a promising approach to coordinate implementation to manage synergies and trade-offs. However, current management and governance approaches are typically designed and implemented in isolation, without considering interconnections among them (well established) {5.6.4}.

Response options are not pursued in a vacuum; broader enabling conditions can influence the likelihood of success and of nexus-wide benefits (established but incomplete) {5.6.5}. Policy design and implementation are the most common enablers for the response options assessed, while financial and material resources are the most common barriers {5.6.5.1}. Attributes of the response option itself can also influence success and impact. Response options that more strongly advance equity, for example, tend to have greater benefits across nexus elements. In contrast, nexus-wide impacts are not related to technical feasibility or economic costs, suggesting that when chosen carefully and in keeping with contextual needs, impactful response options are not necessarily more difficult or costly (established but incomplete) {5.6.5.2}.

Response options assessed in Chapter 5 are well aligned with the goals or targets of existing global policy frameworks, and as such can contribute to the transformative changes they seek to create (well established) {5.6.7}. All response options individually support at least two (and up to 10) Sustainable Development Goals, at least one (and up to 16) targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and one or two of the long-term global goals (climate change mitigation and adaptation) of the Paris Agreement. In addition to supporting goals individually, many response options contribute strongly to all three global policy frameworks and can therefore serve as mechanisms to integrate and align efforts across them (established but incomplete) {5.6.7}.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Options for delivering sustainable approaches – synthesis" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2.5.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/77V4UWTF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9KPND88D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B8X6GYCZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HE25NYEN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JFR5SJRY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MNAZHZH3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N3UCT6EM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QCTU2JMS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R4W9TT4N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/T76UPRBM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U8RPWZBH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/X56KUDBB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZAEU92RS> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """A key challenge for the finance system is the contrast between the increasing scale of risks associated with the declining state of nature (Bank for International Settlements & Banque de France, 2020; Kraemer & Volz, 2022) and the multiple benefits provided by a nexus approach, and the fact that only a small proportion of these benefits can be monetized without changes in both culture and the institutional enabling environment (Chenet, 2019; Dasgupta, 2021a; UNEP, 2015; van der Hoff & Zwieten, 2022) (Figure 6.6). This adds to the pressure to make nature-related markets work (Nature Finance, 2023) but also to align financial interests with sustainability goals to remove the negative externalities associated with mainstream economic activity (Fortt, 2021; G20 Italian Presidency, 2021). However, choices around investment in nature are not limited only by the enabling environment in connected markets, but broader behaviours around taxation, spending, lending and borrowing which are likely to be influenced by international economic institutions as well as sovereign decisions (Bößner et al., 2020).

Another challenge relates to the interconnections between inequity and the decline in nexus elements, which the operations of finance systems may exacerbate. For example, if the economic impacts of a decline in the state of nature are concentrated in lower income countries (Johnson et al., 2021), such countries not only already face a higher cost of borrowing, but could also see this increase as a result of weaker economic performance (M. Agarwala et al., 2022) with consequent ramifications for the sustainability of debt repayments (Kraemer & Volz, 2022) and fiscal space to spend on protecting nexus elements.

This has been further exacerbated following the COVID-19 pandemic. According to the 2022 Financing for Sustainable Development Report (UN, 2022), global public debt showed rapid increases in 2021 from already unsustainably high levels, reaching around 99 per cent of GDP in some countries in 2021. Gross public debt in developed countries, which remained unchanged between 2012 and 2019, surged 18 percentage points reaching 122 per cent of GDP by the end of 2021. In many developing countries, the pandemic and other related factors, including rising interest costs and slow growth, have hastened debt vulnerabilities, leading to increases in public debt by 11 and 15 percentage points of GDP in small island developing states and middle-income countries, respectively, between 2012 and 2019 (UN, 2022). Similarly, least developed countries and other low-income countries experienced a 9 percentage points increase during the same period. About 60 per cent of least developed countries and other low-income countries are reported to be at high risk of debt burden (UN, 2022), and this is expected to worsen with added challenges related to climate change and loss of biodiversity (M. Agarwala et al., 2022; Kraemer & Volz, 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Challenges for the financial system and institutions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/33QPE3I3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3VERSKDT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/45UG2IHS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5YHMF8D2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BQHJZQJW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C23GCRKC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DUVS7NTA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F8T3TAML>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NSBLEJGN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RU9ATTU3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/S3NRAI73>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/T3IWIEQM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UIATREGI> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Scenarios aim to broaden our understanding of how different paradigms might evolve to support sustainability and well-being, providing a framework for exploring how ecological values may be integrated into decision-making processes. The six nexus scenario archetypes in Chapter 3 (section 3.7.1) provide insight into economic characteristics and their consequences for financial decision-making. For instance, scenarios that follow a business-as-usual approach typically show declines across nexus elements (i.e., nexus scenario archetypes food first and nature overexploitation). However, when improvements are noted across nexus elements, they are generally linked to economic and lifestyle and consumption changes that reduce environmental pressures and material use (e.g., nexus scenario archetypes nature-positive nexus, balanced nexus and biodiversity first).

The Nature Futures Framework (NFF) developed by the IPBES task force on scenarios and models offers additional insights in how economic and trade systems that prioritize environmental and societal well-being could be shaped (Durán et al., 2023; Pereira et al., 2020). The NFF illustrates diverse, non-exclusive ways people, institutions and cultures might relate to and value nature: nature for nature (emphasizing the intrinsic value of nature), nature for society (emphasizing instrumental values such as those associated with nature's contributions to people), and nature as culture/ one with nature (emphasizing relational values between people and nature) (Figure 6.7).

The NFF also provides insights into the economic, financial and trade implications of varying conservation and growth scenarios. For example, the "green growth to post-growth" gradient in Figure 6.7 examines the transition from an economy that aims for sustainable growth within 'safe' environmental limits, where GDP growth is still pursued (UNEP, 2011), to one where ecological sustainability and social well-being are prioritized over economic growth. This green growth gradient aligns with the concepts of doughnut economics, planetary boundaries and the steady state economy, all of which restrict growth within strict environmental limits (Durán et al., 2023). In the post-growth gradient, economic and trade systems evolve to de-emphasize GDP as the primary success metric, instead focusing on alternative indicators that reflect environmental health and societal well-being, such as those proposed in the “well-being economy” (Dixson-Declève et al., 2022). Post-growth narratives also prioritize technological advancements optimizing efficiency of consumption and transitions from circular to steady-state economies (Schulz & Bailey, 2014). This shift may lead to more integrated approaches to societal sustainability, reducing negative trade-offs between environmental preservation and societal needs (D’Alessandro et al., 2020; Slameršak et al., 2024) and aligning economic practices with societal values and well-being (Paulson & Büchs, 2022).

The "half Earth to whole Earth" gradient in Figure 6.7 examines the economic effects of conservation strategies ranging from strict protection to integrated approaches with land use policies and sectors such as agriculture and real estate. The "land sharing to land sparing" gradient explores agricultural and land use strategies that integrate conservation into productive landscapes or separate intensive agriculture from high-conservation areas, influencing global supply chains and agricultural trade policies. These scenarios implicitly reflect the diffusion of innovative financing and policy instruments, especially natural capital accounting, to integrate ecological values into economic decision-making and redistribute the economic and social benefits of conservation and sustainability derived from ecosystem services. They also respect local and indigenous knowledge systems, reflecting community-specific cultures and values as shown in the narrative of the “Reciprocal Stewardship” NFF scenario from (Durán et al., 2023).

The gradients also envision adjustments in global trade to minimize environmental impacts or to optimize trade systems to support ecological principles, provisioning services and local production strategies. Other scenario narratives have also championed sustainability and equity, advocating for self-sufficiency and bolstering local and regional trade (Fioramonti, 2024). For example, some green growth scenarios suggest more regional and modular trade, particularly in agriculture and energy systems, as seen in the “sustainability” scenario of the shared socioeconomic pathways (SSP1) developed for the IPCC (van Vuuren et al., 2017). This approach aligns with economic paradigms such as the blue-green economy, which focuses on the sustainable use of land and ocean resources. While these scenarios typically assume that substantial investments and advancements can account for and separate economic growth, trade and finance from environmental impacts, effectively realizing this decoupling remains a significant challenge (Antal & Van Den Bergh, 2016; Hickel & Kallis, 2020; Otero et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future scenarios on financial systems and economic models" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3VTH3FT7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CEIWVIQX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NY7KGFAU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMJ22R7V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SM3Y3G57>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UDAUREGA> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Chapter 4 describes the need for engaging with the nexus challenges of complexity, values, financing, scaling and governance as critical to supporting sustainable and just outcomes (Chapter 1, section 1.1.2; Chapter 4, section 4.3). This may require transforming prevailing practice and interactions within diverse arenas of engagement, as interactions between governance actors in these arenas advance or constrain nexus governance efforts (Chapter 4, section 4.5.4). This section builds on Chapter 4 to assess what characterizes the types of interactions between diverse civil society, government, science, IPLC and private sector actors that enable economic and financial choices and actions that could cumulatively transform development trajectories towards sustainable and just futures (see Figures 6.8, 6.9, 6.10).

“Arenas of engagement” are described as “the settings, places and spaces in which societal actors interact to influence the nature and course of development” (E. L. F. Schipper et al., 2022) (see also Chapter 4, section 4.5.1).

The ways diverse actors interact in these economic and financial, political, ecological, socio-cultural, knowledge-technology and community arenas can either generate change or entrench business-as-usual development pathways. These arenas can be temporary or enduring in nature, with interactions spanning local to global scales. Economic and financial arenas in particular are wide ranging and can include local markets for forest products, village-level planning committees, community forestry, conservation or rural development project meetings, corporate boardrooms, organized criminal networks, biodiversity policy and planning processes, negotiation meetings in international environmental agreements and conventions, and international forums for trade and public finance decisions (Adeniyi, 2023; Bakhtary et al., 2020; S. Chaudhary et al., 2018; Nelson et al., 2021) (see also chapter case study boxes).

Key actors interacting in economic and financial arenas include not only economists, financiers, bankers and others who hold formal positions in economics and finance but also diverse actors in civil society and from IPLC, government and science (see section 6.1 and Figure 6.8). The nature of engagement by these diverse actors shapes resistance or receptivity to the system transitions necessary to improve development outcomes for the nexus. For example, interactions across science, policy and politics by critical action intellectuals, along with social movements, macroeconomic processes or technological breakthroughs, can drive transformation in environmental governance to address climate change, biodiversity loss, social inequality and livelihood insecurity (Ojha et al., 2022). The quality of interactions between governance actors in economic and financial arenas plays an especially important role in opening-up or closing-down biodiversity and sustainability prospects because of the predominant influence of economics and finance in societal decision-making. For example, privilege and injustice in participation and decision-making processes can lead to uneven outcomes (S. Chaudhary et al., 2018). More transformative economic and financial response options would catalyze and support decision-making that addresses trade-offs across nexus elements and better promotes synergies between them, as well as shifting system goals towards sustainability and equity objectives beyond financial returns (section 6.2, Figure 6.8, Figure 6.11).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enabling transformative response options and change" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-A3> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A7> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Current economic and financial systems invest $7 trillion per year in activities that damage biodiversity and other nexus elements. Dominant economic systems can result in unsustainable and inequitable economic growth and prioritize only a limited set of nature’s contributions to people (e.g., water and food) while not accounting for diverse values of nature. Consequently, more than half of the world’s population is living in areas experiencing the highest impacts from declines in biodiversity, in water availability and quality, and in food security, and from increases in health risks and negative effects of climate change. These burdens disproportionately affect developing countries, including small island developing States, Indigenous Peoples and local communities, as well as those in vulnerable situations in higher-income countries. Despite these pressures, Indigenous Peoples and local communities successfully conserve biodiversity and sustainably manage other nexus elements using their knowledge and practices, supporting arguments for their recognition as rights holders and their increased inclusion and participation in decision-making. Efforts to improve the status of the nexus elements (e.g., environmental regulations) have been partially successful. However, they are unlikely to be fully effective without more concerted efforts to address interlinkages among the nexus elements and their direct and indirect drivers. Governance can also be improved through inclusion of a wider range of actors and values, with a particular focus on equity, alongside economic and financial reforms." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM-A3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Societal, economic and policy decisions that prioritize short-term benefits and financial returns for a small number of people while ignoring negative impacts on biodiversity and other nexus elements lead to unequal outcomes for human well-being. Existing governance approaches have often failed to account for and address these negative impacts in degrading nature, with the negative impacts disproportionately affecting the well-being of some as compared with others {A6, A7}." .

