    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Governance for health in the nexus requires health actors to engage in promoting health co-benefits across nexus elements. Such governance roles for health sector actors may range from providing basic services (e.g., ensuring school canteen options), supporting (e.g., addressing health disparities through cross-sectoral policy advocacy) or engaging in bilateral or trilateral partnerships (e.g., food safety legislation and enforcement) to leading delivery of a core mandate (e.g., adequate water and energy supplies to health facilities). Collaborative and distributed leadership, developing the capacity of the health sector to work with and learn from other sectors, and developing a shared problem perspective can constitute a way forward, including in low- to middle-income country contexts (e.g., Rasanathan et al., 2017).

Figure 5.4.15 categorizes actors in terms of their roles in the 15 response options presented in this subchapter, based on the assessed evidence for these response options. Global and regional institutions are influencer actors across response options, except for H02, Intercultural health services (section 5.4.3.2) and H24, the One Health Approach (section 5.4.3.14), where these actors are key players. As influencers, these actors play a major role in influencing decision-making. National governments are either influencers or key players, depending on the specific response option concerned. They are key players for H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (section 5.4.3.6); H07, Pollution prevention (section 5.4.3.7); H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration and conservation for health (section 5.4.3.8); H09, Urban green infrastructure (section 5.4.3.9) and H10, Forest conservation for health (section 5.4.3.10). National Governments influence decision-making and implementation and are also affected by the decisions taken. For all the other response options, they are influencers, including, for instance, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (section 5.4.3.4) and H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (section 5.4.3.11).

In the case of both global and regional institutions and national Governments, their role in decision-making and influencing outcomes is pervasive across health response options. Local governments and municipalities are key players for response options, except in the case of H01, Universal health coverage (section 5.4.3.1) and H05, Nature on prescription (section 5.4.3.5) where they act primarily as influencers on decision-making, but largely are not seen to be impacted themselves by decisions because of their influencing roles. Barring a few, for most response options, knowledge and educational communities, civil society, community-based, private sector and business organizations are key players, who engage to a varying extent in decision-making, and in turn are affected by these decisions. IPLC are key players for most response options. They are affected actors, directly involved in implementation and at the same time having their own stakes and interests, as in the case of implementation of H01, Universal health coverage (section 5.4.3.1). Actors in the science-policy interface and financing institutions are primarily influencers, except for a few response options. Actors in the media and arts domain are invariably influencer actors, wherever evidence of their engagement is available. In certain cases, as for the response options H15, Integrated health education (section 5.4.3.15); H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (section 5.4.3.12) and H02, Intercultural health services (section 5.4.3.2), there is lack of data and a need for knowledge creation on the role of certain types of actors. Similarly, there is a gap in understanding the engagement of IPLC in promoting H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare (section 5.4.3.3), although IPLC actors have an influencer role in H13, Health impact assessments (section 5.4.3.13) and H14, the One Health Approach (section 5.4.3.14).

Figure 5.4.16 maps the actor categories shown in Table 5.4.1 against the 15 response options. A minimum of five actor categories are engaged in each response option, while a much higher number of actor types are involved in some, up to a maximum of 14 for H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (section 5.4.3.11). Furthermore, all actor categories are engaged with at least three response options, while three actor categories are involved in the governance of ten or more response options. National, sub-national and local government bodies; national and sub-national NGOs; health professional and regulatory associations; and community and civil society-based organizations are among the most engaged actors. There is scope for improving engagement of IPLC, regional institutions, and financial and insurance institutions across several of the health response options.

Key actors in health are also spread across the other nexus elements (Figure 5.4.16B). Actors from health and biodiversity are required for all the 15 response options. Actors from all five nexus elements are required for implementing seven of the response options: H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare (section 5.4.3.3); H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (section 5.4.3.6); H10, Forest conservation for health (section 5.4.3.10); H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (section 5.4.3.11); H13, Health Impact Assessments (section 5.4.3.13) and H14, the One Health approach (section 5.4.3.14).

Substantial differences in health status, access to healthcare services and their utilization prevail across regions (Vos et al., 2020; WHO and World Bank, 2021), and these influence the choice, operationalization, implementation and outcomes of nexus response options. Distributional inequities, across and within countries, and the growing inequality within countries is a global issue (Permanyer & Smits, 2020). Failure to provide health services and inequitable exposure to environmental health risks such as pollution, are long-standing challenges and linked to systemic issues associated with the existing global economic paradigm that perpetuates poverty and concentrates wealth and power in a minority (see Chapter 6). Thus, while the foundations of health-related response options may be a function of the health sector (i.e., provision of healthcare, addressing health risks, promoting health and well-being), key barriers to implementation are outside the health sector influence. To advance health in the nexus, different aspects of governance, including regulatory, institutional and procedural aspects, can build on some of the pre-existing arrangements.

Collaborations between actors in health and the other nexus elements, and the international agreements and frameworks that have resulted from these, constitute important steps in developing shared aims, actions and processes that enable more integrative governance (Willetts et al., 2023). Health has traditionally engaged with the other nexus elements in individual sector-specific ways, including on food safety and antimicrobial resistance (e.g., Codex Alimentarius, the FAO/WHO Food Standards Programme) and safe water (e.g., WASH, with UN-Water). The inclusion of health as a priority in the Nationally Determined Contributions submitted for the Paris Agreement, in National Adaptation Plans, in the IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, in the development of country specific Health National Adaptation Plans, and in the recognition of the need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from healthcare (WHO, 2023b) demonstrates major progress on climate change and health.

The WHO has collaborated with the Convention on Biological Diversity (e.g., (e.g., Romanelli et al., 2015) to jointly identify public health and biodiversity conservation evidence and priorities. The adoption in 2021 by the Conference of the Parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity of a Global Action Plan for Biodiversity and Health and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (CBD, 2023) are key subsequent developments. The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets 9 to 13 address people’s needs through sustainable use and benefit-sharing of biodiversity-related determinants of health. However, it is likely these will predominantly focus on the environment or conservation agenda if they do not directly consider the health priorities of addressing disease burden or health coverage, or harmonization with health concepts and data (Willetts et al., 2022). This subchapter, and the Nexus Assessment, contribute as a whole to addressing these challenges and progressing on health and biodiversity.

The health sector has promoted healthy environments for people, particularly within neighbourhoods and cities and through whole-of-government health approaches for many decades (WHO, 1986; WHO, 2023e). For example, cities, villages and islands, as well as institutions, have been systematically addressed as ‘health settings’ by holistically engaging government and community actors. These include the long established European Healthy City network (de Leeuw, 2012; WHO, 2023e) and ‘Healthy Islands Pacific,’ which has provided a unifying vision of health, culture and environment across multiple nations (despite reporting only against its health metrics) (WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2020). These well-established models for enhancing health within a physical landscape have rarely audited their impact on the environment or biodiversity explicitly (or on the health of other species). Health considerations in policy concerning food, water, biodiversity and climate change are made possible through ‘Health in All Policies.’ This is a whole-of-government approach to protecting health across multiple sectors that has been embraced by governments in several countries, including Australia, Denmark, Finland, the Netherlands (Kingdom of the) and the United States of America, who now have considerable experience in multi-sector health policy and decision-making (Delany et al., 2014; Van Vliet-Brown et al., 2018). These health-promoting forms of governance have complemented regulation of environmental health risks (section 5.4.1) and are adaptive to changing and expanding evidence across the determinants of health, including ecological and cultural determinants. Health promotion also embraces equity, Indigenous voices and knowledges and planetary health (Hancock & the IUHPE’s Global Working Group on Waiora Planetary Health, 2021).

Indigenous health governance is embedded in self-determination, partnerships and relationships, and respect for Indigenous knowledge on health and well-being. Sustainable practices that preserve traditional lands, waterways and natural resources are essential for maintaining the physical, spiritual and cultural identity of these communities. Effective Indigenous health governance prioritizes community engagement, traditional knowledge systems, and the integration of Indigenous values and practices into healthcare delivery. Collaborative decision-making processes that involve elders, healers and community leaders can ensure that health policies and programs are culturally safe (Nursing Council of New Zealand, 2011), relevant and responsive to the specific needs of each Indigenous group. Institutional recognition through creation of Indigenous controlled health organizations through to international organizations, such as the WHO Global Centre for Traditional Medicine and the United Nations Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues, has catalysed greater representation for IPLC at all levels of governance and health service planning (Crooks et al., 2023; O’Neil et al., 2016).

Progressing nexus approaches can be considered in terms of scaling up, out, down and deep (Chapter 4, section 4.4). Scaling up refers to impacting laws and policy such has occurred for the Quadripartite that supports H14, the One Health approach (section 5.4.3.14) that now influences national and regional governance. Scaling out refers to reaching greater numbers of people. The transferability of health nexus response options to other locations and populations requires attention to the unique nature of each group and place, and further data gathering. Even a globally relevant response option such as F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.15) with H06, Reduced meat overconsumption (section 5.4.3.6) requires adjusting for nutritional status of populations, women of reproductive age or pastoral communities with traditional high meat diets. Scaling down is also important; for example, scaling down the environmental footprint of the health sector (i.e., section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare) while not reducing its functions is important for future health. Scaling deep refers to change of cultural beliefs or norms to transform narratives and approaches such as reframing health through H15, Integrated health education for health professionals and actors (section 5.4.3.15) and by engaging with the arts and other cultural world views to build empathy with nature (Figure 5.4.2; Box 5.4.1).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actors involved in health governance" .

