    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Soil organic carbon (SOC) content can be increased through management interventions in croplands, grazing lands and managed forests that either increase carbon inputs to the soil, cause the carbon to persist longer in the soil, or reduce losses from the soil by decreasing soil disturbance or placing carbon at deeper layers of the soil (P. Smith, 2016). Carbon inputs can occur through traditional forms such as litter and manure, but also through newer technologies such as biochar (P. Smith, 2016).

Increasing SOC content is a response option that can be applied globally on all managed land (FAO & ITPS, 2015) (Figure 5.5.5). It is beneficial across all nexus elements, with very large potentials for climate change mitigation potential (0.4–8.6 Gt CO2eq/yr) and adaptation (up to 3200 million people potentially benefiting) (P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.3). However, soil carbon sinks, like all biological carbon sinks, are time-limited (due to sink saturation) and reversible (through, for example, poor management or extreme events such as fire) and could be impacted by climate change (C. Jones et al., 2005).

For water, well-managed soils with high SOC content have high infiltration capacity that increases green water (available for plants to enhance crop production and nature) and regulates blue water (fewer floods) (Figure 5.5.3). Soils also function as a filter for pollutants, improving water quality (Cheng et al., 2021; Keesstra et al., 2021). For food and health, higher SOC levels also give rise to higher crop yields (Qiao et al., 2022) which could alleviate the burden of undernutrition in some areas (with a subsequent strong positive impact on health), though there is limited evidence on its impact on food access or the nutritional value of food. For biodiversity, soil organic matter is a headline indicator of soil health. With around 120 thousand km² of land lost to degradation every year (IPBES, 2018), management practices that increase SOC would help increase the area in better condition, thereby improving soil health and biodiversity. Since increasing SOC content is practiced only on managed land, it has no impact on the area of land protected or other aspects of native biodiversity, but practices that lead to increased soil organic matter also tend to improve soil biodiversity (P. Smith et al., 2015). Increased SOC also improves ecological functioning, since soils higher in SOC function better than degraded soils with lower levels (De Deyn & Kooistra, 2021). Additionally, it also improves ecological resilience as soils higher in SOC are more resilient to drought and floods and less prone to soil erosion (Lal, 2015).

Practices to increase SOC levels, which increase carbon inputs or decrease carbon losses, are already widely implemented through best management practices in agriculture (e.g., conservation agriculture) and forestry (TRL 9: Babiker et al., 2022) (Figure 5.5.5). There are no known social acceptability issues with implementing this response option and it aligns strongly with policies to improve soil and water quality, to combat land degradation and desertification, to increase food production and to tackle climate change (P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.4).

Increasing SOC is a relatively low-cost option (P. Smith, 2016) though monitoring, reporting and verification (MRV) costs can be high (P. Smith, Soussana, et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.4). Since it leads to higher yields and improves resilience to drought and erosion (Lal, 2015), it enhances economic benefits and reduces risk. In terms of equity, many IPLC management practices are known to result in high carbon levels and biodiverse soils (McElwee, 2021). For example, traditional homestead and landscape-level interventions in the Andaman and Nicobar Islands of India focusing on biodiversity, including the use of over 7500 plant species, contributed to SOC improvement through the enhancement of ecosystem diversity and resilience (Ichikawa, 2018). When combined with other response options (e.g., B03/C11, Agroecology; see Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.11), it has high transformative change potential (Figure 5.5.4).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Increase soil organic carbon (C01)" .

