        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4A4ZSHSB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/66IQ5DIM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/72C9F9VH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9BU9NY99>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B8P2EKIL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BNBILNEE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CBFLQ7U8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DK5AJUEK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/E8TUMGLW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FRR6JNGI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GLLMJVW9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GRKGVWG7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GWY3U7BI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/H36JRWTQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N3UW6PVL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QLRZIWA2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VHF4VFY4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WH7GUZF9> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Land and sea planning (hereafter ‘planning’) is a technical-political-administrative process aimed to configure the use of terrestrial and marine territory in the long-term, considering potential and limitations of the area, aspirations of people and sustainable development objectives (Joanna, 2017; Massiris Cabeza, 2005). Marine spatial planning is now a key process within the current United Nations Decade of Ocean Research (Ehler, 2020; Kidd et al., 2020;

Rivers et al., 2022). Planning is typically manifested through public policies, with a focus on developing medium- and long-term strategic plans and balancing trade-offs between multiple different uses of the natural environment (Lombard et al., 2019). The state plays a crucial role as a guarantor of territorial sustainability against multiple development pressures, within a process designed to be highly participatory and equitable, incorporating multi-stakeholder consultations and other tools (Sanabria Perez, 2014; Zaucha & Gee, 2019).

A key outcome of planning is limiting accelerated urbanization by respecting natural and cultivated areas (Alfasi et al., 2012; Gurran et al., 2015). Thus, planning also conserves biodiversity and water, mitigates climate change and supports food production through agriculture, aquaculture and fisheries (Lombard et al., 2019; Rivers et al., 2022). Well implemented spatial plans also enhance human health and well-being through providing recreational access to natural environments (Day & Dobbs, 2013), and more recently meeting sociocultural needs (Saunders et al., 2019).

Ninety examples of implementation of land planning examined showed regional differences. In Latin America, planning emphasized social and cultural aspects, serving as a tool for communities to protect their identities and rights linked to the territory (Asher & Ojeda, 2009). Elsewhere, land planning tends to be more technical, focusing on zoning of uses and achieving pre-established objectives, with less consideration of social and cultural aspects. However, recent sea planning cases now include ILK and transparent processes to reduce user conflicts, with ecosystem service models for multi-objective planning, especially in data poor high-value sectors, and strategic mapping products for different stakeholders (Lombard et al., 2019). However, the complexity of coordination and costs in the ocean are challenging. For example, seafloor mapping is intensive and expensive, leaving most of the planet unmapped (Rivers et al., 2022). Additionally, while land planning often occurs at local scales, sea planning typically spans national and sometimes transnational scales.

Planning has high transformative potential, particularly in changing structures, values and practices, by fostering innovative participatory processes at the local level, and promoting social cohesion through collaborative building of prospective scenarios for a common territory (Guardamagna & Cueto, 2015).

The main challenges in implementing these large-scale plans include the complexity of coordinating policy across multilevel governments, insufficient citizen participation and representation of local interests, power imbalances among stakeholders, inadequate institutional capacities and insufficient funding for monitoring and enforcement over time (Flannery et al., 2019). Implementation failures due to these challenges can lead to medium- and long-term loss of biodiversity and transformation of ecosystems (e.g. Hailu et al., 2023; Shen et al., 2019). Conversely, positive impacts on ecological and cultural aspects of territories occur when these processes are carefully evaluated and adjusted (e.g. Gurran et al., 2015; Peskett et al., 2023; Short et al., 2023).

Notes
7. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Land and sea planning (B12)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2E8MWEQY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/56YSTV8U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/72EDTPF3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AS5555UD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/E88XJAGR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FYNHQHZ8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HCFNR7FB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IZMSWXDH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MYJQTHFJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q8Y8A8YT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TIQUPJSU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U69F436I>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VDEPAUY6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W8H2YPSJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WBAIRUHK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y2UK8BN2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y4L27UEH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z7K6NKVT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZUSRHGXC> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The 14 assessed biodiversity response options all have the potential to benefit at least four nexus elements, with only two not positively impacting all five elements (Figure 5.1.16). They involve several actors at all levels with different arrangements for interaction (see section 5.1.4).

The nexus-wide outcomes vary across the biodiversity response options. Some response options are highly multifunctional providing benefits to several nexus elements (e.g., section 5.1.3.4, B04 Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes; section 5.1.3.9, B09 Integrated landscape and seascape approaches; section 5.1.3.12, B012 Land and sea planning), while others are targeted to fewer nexus elements in addition to biodiversity (e.g., section 5.1.3.2, B02, Urban nature-based solutions; section 5.1.3.5, B05, Forest landscape restoration; section 5.1.3.6, B06, Restoration of coastal and marine systems). Some response options can comprise trade-offs especially those reducing production intensity (e.g., section 5.1.3.1, B01, Area-based conservation; section 5.1.3.3, B03, Agroecology) or changing land use (e.g., section 5.1.3.5, B05, Forest landscape restoration; section 5.1.3.8, B08, Rewilding). Overall, more trade-offs were reported with food and to a lesser extent with health, water and climate change.

Biodiversity response options primarily target biodiversity protection to maintain current state, management of its use and extraction, and restoration of degraded structure and function. In addition, they primarily benefit water for ecosystems and people and climate change adaptation and mitigation, although quantitative evidence for climate change mitigation by specific response option is incomplete (Figure 5.1.2). Several response options address both climate change mitigation and adaptation: B03, Agroecology (section 5.1.3.3); B04, Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes (section 5.1.3.4) and B08, Rewilding (section 5.1.3.8). Other response options favour either mitigation (B05, Forest landscape restoration, section 5.1.3.5) or adaptation (B02, Urban nature-based solutions, section 5.1.3.2).

Response options for biodiversity have substantial co-benefits for health, especially in terms of well-being, with B03, Agroecology (section 5.1.3.3) and B14, Reconnecting people with nature (section 5.1.3.14) showing the greatest benefits. There are also some potential trade-offs with disease vectors when pathogens, parasites or predators are favoured, for example, when B02, Urban nature-based solutions (section 5.1.3.2), B05, Forest restoration (section 5.1.3.5) or B08, Rewilding (section 5.1.3.8) favour harmful biota in proximity to vulnerable human populations (Pineda-Pinto, Frantzeskaki, & Nygaard, 2022; Wudu et al., 2023).

Some response options include food as a primary target, such as B03, Agroecology (section 5.1.3.3). B04, Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes (section 5.1.3.4) and B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (section 5.1.3.9). However, trade-offs with food can occur in the case of B05, Forest landscape restoration (section 5.1.3.5) (Paterson & Bryan, 2012) or B08, Rewilding (section 5.1.3.8) (Duckett et al., 2022) when food production is reduced in favour of return to natural systems, and during transitions to agroecology (Ponisio et al., 2015).

The varying performance and trade-offs associated with the response options can be overcome by combining response options in bundles or sequences at the landscape to regional scale (Chapter 5.6, section 5.6.5). This complementarity between response options, where implementation of a response option can strengthen or enable the performance of other options, is recognized in B09, integrated landscape and seascape approaches (section 5.1.3.9), B12, land and sea planning (section 5.1.3.12), and in some B10, Rights-based approaches in landscapes and seascapes managed by IPLC (section 5.1.3.10).

For example, an integrated landscape approach and/or land planning can deal with the trade-offs with food for B05, Forest restoration (section 5.1.3.5) or B08, Rewilding (section 5.1.3.8) by combining these actions with others that have a positive impact on food production, such as sustainable intensification (response options F03 in Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3 and C02 in Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.2), fostering synergies in the landscape. In addition, when deforested land used for agricultural crops or pastures reverts to natural forest the restored biodiversity may provide a wide array of nature’s contributions to people, supporting rural economies and livelihoods (Chazdon, Lindenmayer, et al., 2020). Furthermore, it may increase resilience to climate variability, and when integrated with B03, Agroecology (section 5.1.3.3), complementarities among crop rotations may offset yield losses when considering longer-term (e.g. decadal) production, especially under climate change (Bezner Kerr et al., 2023; S. K. Jones et al., 2023). Thus, while forest restoration and rewilding are inherently associated with decreased food production where there is competition for land, they can contribute to increasing the provision of, and income from, other essential foods, products and medicine (game, fish and seafood, wild foods and medicinal plants), particularly to local people (Bharucha & Pretty, 2010; Chamberlain et al., 2020; Haq et al., 2023; Meinhold et al., 2022; V. Thomas et al., 2022; Wiersum, 2017).

Bundling response options can also deliver reciprocal benefits for mental health and the conservation or restoration of natural spaces. For example, protected areas (e.g., B01, Area-based conservation (section 5.1.3.1), B02, Urban nature-based solutions (section 5.1.3.2) or B08, Rewilding (section 5.1.3.8) combined with adequate land-use configuration (e.g., B12, Land and sea planning (section 5.1.3.12)) can be an enabler for B14, Reconnecting people with nature (section 5.1.3.14) by overcoming inequities in physical access to nature and reducing the footprint of travelling to natural areas. In turn, reconnection with nature can lead to behavioural change that benefits biodiversity conservation, restoration and sustainable use (Ives et al., 2018; L. B. Miller et al., 2022).

Procedural equity is an enabling condition for biodiversity response options to have nexus-wide benefits and positive socioeconomic impacts. Negative outcomes, if reported, tend to fall either in the lower categories of participation or in cases where there was no information on the participation level (Figure 5.1.17). Higher levels of participation were associated with a lower proportion of trade-offs for nexus elements. Conversely, inadequate institutional capacity is one of the strongest barriers to implementation of response options that may lead to negative impacts across all elements of the nexus, as was reported in some cases of B12, Land and sea planning (section 5.1.3.12; Figure 5.1.13). Legal instruments that recognize the legitimacy of governance mechanisms based on customary systems and traditional knowledge and social and information-based instruments enable other response options by boosting equity and driving social and behavioural changes (B10, Rights-based approaches (section 5.1.3.10); B14, Reconnecting people with nature (section 5.1.3.14)).

Finally, social acceptability and technological feasibility are also enabling factors in the implementation of biodiversity response options, while economic cost is the most frequent barrier in the examined examples of implementation of response options.

Besides these trends in enablers and barriers, successful response options require a mix of top-down and bottom-up processes. This includes tailoring response option implementation to context, taking into account the local state of enabling conditions and the incidence of direct and indirect drivers that are addressed by the biodiversity response options (Figure 5.1.18). The most desirable mix of top-down and bottom-up processes varies across response options and places, with the best combination depending on context. Exemplary cases, for example, for B04, Ecosystem-based climate adaptation in rural landscapes (section 5.1.3.4) or B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (section 5.1.3.9; Box 5.1.4), highlight successful processes and their combinations of actors, institutions and sociopolitical options. Knowledge synthesis and sharing of successes and failures according to standardized criteria in databases will support scaling out and scaling up of such exemplary cases. Capacity strengthening and transformative social learning across actors and institutions are also essential to support these knowledge exchange and scaling processes, and to build capacity for multi-actor governance (Chapter 7, section 7.4.1). Knowledge exchange and networking across on-the-ground actions that are locally led and managed by IPLC and private actors is essential for their scaling out to more numerous locations. The scaling out of successful response options will support mainstreaming biodiversity into all sectors of society. The roles of intermediary institutions and change leaders in communities also need to be considered, along with regulations and institutionalization (scaling up) and behavioural change processes (scaling deep) (Chapter 4, section 4.4).

The drivers behind biodiversity degradation are complex and multifaceted, with direct and indirect factors playing significant roles (Chapter 2). Analysis shows that the selected response options for addressing biodiversity concerns align with global assessments of threats, such as those outlined previously (Balvanera et al., 2019). Among the direct drivers targeted by the biodiversity response options, habitat conversion and degradation emerge as strongly predominant, accounting for 48-65 per cent of cases across regions (Figure 5.1.18). This is followed by climate change, pollution and exploitation of wild populations, each with similar levels. Species introductions are addressed to a much lesser extent. There is more regional variation in how the response options target the indirect drivers that underpin the direct drivers (Chapter 2, Figure 2.8). The top four – demography, institutional, technology and economic – comprise 80 per cent of the indirect drivers addressed globally, with some regional variation in their representation across the database.

In line with the drivers addressed by the biodiversity response options, each response option contributes to the achievement of several SDGs (Figure 5.1.19), exemplifying different and complementary actions to support sustainable development though biodiversity conservation, restoration and sustainable use. The response options contribute to between three and nine SDGs. More response options support those SDGs that are directly related to the nexus elements (e.g., biodiversity – SDGs 14 and 15, water – SDG6, food – SDG2 and climate change – SDG13), probably reflecting the selection criteria. The exception is health (SDG3) since most response options contribute indirectly to health and well-being, through multiple nature’s contributions to people or people connecting with nature.

The response options also strongly contribute to the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, supporting between 4 and 15 targets. In particular, response options support targets 1 to 3, 11, 14 and 22 as these align with nexus approaches that aim to mainstream biodiversity into other human actions and decision-making through conservation, restoration and sustainable use of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people, with equity as the main enabler. The other targets are addressed by fewer response options as their specificity increases. For example, targets 7, 8 and 10 are addressed by response options focusing on reducing pollution, climate change and sustainable production, respectively. Some targets are addressed by only one or two response options each, but when bundled in complementarity ways can contribute to many more targets. For example, B10, Rights-based approaches (section 5.1.3.10), supports targets 13 and 23; B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (section 5.1.3.9), B11, Multilateral environmental agreements (section 5.1.3.11) and B13, Natural capital accounting (section 5.1.3.13) together address targets 15, 18 and 19; and adding B14, Reconnecting people with nature (section 5.1.3.14) also addresses targets 16 and 20. There is no single response option that supports all targets, although some have broad effects, particularly B01, Area-based conservation (section 5.1.3.1), B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (section 5.1.3.9) and B11, Multilateral environmental agreements (section 5.1.3.11).

In summary, the biodiversity response options span a range from conservation to sustainable use, local to global action, and on-the-ground action to legal instruments, highlighting the necessary of multiple approaches to ensure biodiversity and other nexus elements move closer to a more sustainable future. Real world implementation often combines several of these response options and relies on a broad diversity of local to global actors, including IPLC, with complementary roles for all sectors to meet nexus-wide objectives.

Notes
11. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2355H72M>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2GRFEP3P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/82PMU3CB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8P6PW6RB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8YQ74R4R>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/92JSTGHN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9T26HUUY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F9XRN5QQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GXFBKDKC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/M9RUFM9M>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NPEX9XEP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NWPS9CYC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P8A6984D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P9DCVMQW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XBGFPCSD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XZ49GQXU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YBPXHH4Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZCF5ASHL> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Reducing overconsumption of red and processed meat is a globally applicable response option wherever overconsumption takes place (Figure 5.4.7). Currently, health recommendations vary between 98-500g (cooked) lean red meat/week for adults and little or no processed meat (WHO, 2023c). The associated burden of disease and recommended intake continue to be revised (Murray, 2022; WHO, 2023c) with variation between studies related to specific health risks and reference populations (e.g., Afshin et al., 2019; González et al., 2020; Papier et al., 2021). Moderating consumption of red meat has synergies with other nexus elements, particularly climate change and biodiversity, with few trade-offs (Figure 5.4.5). The response option recognises that undernutrition associated with underconsumption (including for meat-associated iron, B12 and zinc, and protein) persists in many populations (WHO, 2023c).

The highest overconsumption of red meat occurs in high-income Australasia, North America, western Europe and southern and tropical Latin America, with 2.1 to 2.8 times optimal intake for reduced all-cause mortality (Afshin et al., 2019).et al., 2019) (see also Chapter 2, section 2.3.1.2 and 2.3.2.1). The highest overconsumption of processed meat occurs in high-income North America, Asia Pacific, western and eastern Europe and southern Latin America, with 4 to 13 times optimal intake (Afshin et al., 2019). This response option has the potential to reduce approximately 4.9 million disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) and 155,000 deaths from diets high in red and processed meat globally per year. When combined with increased intake of plant-based foods (especially whole grains, fruits, nuts and seeds), there is a further global potential to reduce approximately 197 million DALYs and 7 million deaths per year from diets low in these food items (Afshin et al., 2019). This aspect of healthy diets is linked to response options discussed in other subchapters (F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets, Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.15).

Reducing meat overconsumption improves the environmental performance of food production (Mbow et al., 2019; Poore & Nemecek, 2018; Springmann et al., 2018; Tilman & Clark, 2014) and reduces greenhouse gas emissions (Mbow et al., 2019). The mean land use needed to produce 100 g of protein is highest for lamb/mutton (185 m²year) and beef from beef herds (164 m²year) (Poore & Nemecek, 2018). Similarly, the mean greenhouse gas emissions to produce 100 g of protein also are highest for beef from beef herds (50 kgCO2eq) and for lamb/mutton (20 kgCO2eq). In comparison, a diet without these two red meat types, and accounting for increased consumption of plant-based foods, would reduce global land use by 46 per cent (from 4,130 to 2,210 million ha). In addition, it would reduce terrestrial acidification by 6 per cent (from 89.0 to 83.4 MtSO2eq) and eutrophication by 9 per cent (from 64.7 to 58.8 Mt phosphate eq) (Poore & Nemecek, 2018). In principle, freed-up land could be managed in part to improve its condition and level of native biodiversity (see Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.7, B07, Restoration of inland water systems) and in part for sustainable intensification of plant-based food production (see B03/C11, Agroecology in Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.11; C02/F03, Sustainable intensification in Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.2). This response option would reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 20 per cent (from 13.65 to 10.98 GtCO2eq) (Poore & Nemecek, 2018).

There are several countries in which the response option is included in national priorities and it is endorsed by 21 cities worldwide (Federal Ministry of Food and Agriculture, 2023; Ministry of Health, 2020; Plant Based Treaty, 2021). While there is broad international support for this response option (European Commission, 2020; WHO, 2019b), its social acceptability is variable, depending on several factors, including frequency of meat consumption, attitude toward environmental protection and concern about animal welfare (T. A. Kenny et al., 2023) (Figure 5.4.6). Compared with the cost of current diets in upper-middle-income to high-income countries, nutritionally balanced and predominantly plant-based dietary patterns are up to 22-34 per cent lower in cost on average (Springmann et al., 2021). Such a diet change has high transformative change potential, given its high potential to facilitate innovations (e.g., disruptive technology like cultured meat), transform structures across the food chain, and lead to new views that promote a sustainable future. There is growing interest in animal products produced through systems that enhance carbon sequestration (see Chapter 5.3, section 5.4.3.5, F05, Ecological intensification – rangelands) and produced locally with greater connection between consumers and producers (Box 5.4.7; see also Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.12, F12, City region food systems). Sustainable management of fish and game, particularly for IPLC are also an important resource for sustainable healthy diets (Box 5.4.2).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reduce meat overconsumption (H06)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2JBGM8SC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3FI8GM5C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/64Y7Q286>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9KLYKL34>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B998KG5F>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BB9TQNAY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C66Y55Y5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CGUVC8PV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GDDDMVM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MJWKX5DT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MKRLWBG3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NDYV49ZY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PFZRXW3Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PKAM3M4J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q7Q7SL9T>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TIQUPJSU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TJMFJYMV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XV8XQ8GX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YQ2GVNIE> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Addressing the multi-dimensional challenges posed by climate change requires a nuanced understanding of the complex interrelationships among diverse nexus elements. While some climate change response options can deliver mutual benefits across the nexus, such as water, energy and food security (see Figure 5.5.3 and section 5.5.3), others may have unintended consequences, impeding aspects of sustainable development (Figure 5.5.4).

Failures to account for nexus interactions have serious consequences in the climate sector. Response options designed solely for climate change mitigation, such as large-scale afforestation with non-native species in ecologically incongruent regions, tend to have more pronounced negative impacts. These may lead to resource competition or ecological imbalances. In contrast, strategies devised with a comprehensive nexus approach can maximize synergies. For instance, F11/C15,Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.5.3.15; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11) can reduce GHG emissions, alleviate pressure on land and water resources, and lower public health costs associated with poor nutrition, thereby acting as an enabler for other response options to be implemented successfully. When conflicts arise within the nexus, tailored management strategies, such as incorporating new woodlands into sustainably managed landscapes in ways that benefit local populations, can mitigate adverse effects.

Some response options enable the successful implementation of others, highlighting that bundling and sequencing of response options can help deliver transformative change. For example, the reduced pressure on land arising from shifting to F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.5.3.15; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11) in overconsuming regions and/or through C02/F02, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3) can enable more sustainable forms of farming such as B03/C11, Agroecology (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3; section 5.5.3.11). This in turn can free up land for other land-based climate change response options, such as reforestation, as part of C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change (section 5.5.3.12); C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (section 5.5.3.4) and land-based renewable energy, such as C06, Solar photovoltaics on land (section 5.5.3.6).

The rapid transformation, through systems transitions, of multiple sectors including energy, land-use, urban planning, industrial activities and societal behaviours, is necessary for holistic climate change mitigation, adaptation and sustainable development. Achieving this systemic shift necessitates harmonizing various enabling conditions – financial, social, economic, institutional and political. The governance of these sectors is influenced not just by formal institutions but also by a plethora of actors and networks, both of which can either facilitate or obstruct effective solutions.

The potential for transformative change is often optimized when multiple response options are integrated. For example, the additional land demands that might arise from employing B03/C11, Agroecology (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3; section 5.5.3.11) can be offset by a parallel shift to F11/C15, Sustainable heathy diets (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.11; section 5.5.3.15) and F10, Reducing food loss and waste (Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.10). Cities that epitomize low-carbon resilience through implementing integrated B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.2; section 5.5.3.14) are pivotal in driving these interlinked positive outcomes.

Implementation of all response options supports the achievement of international policy frameworks, such as the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement (Figure 5.5.4; Figure 5.5.16). All response options support the delivery of at least two SDGs with some contributing to eight and all contribute to at least two of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, with some contributing to up to fourteen, and all contribute towards the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for climate change mitigation and adaptation. Examples of response options contributing to the implementation of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework are C01, Increased soil organic carbon (section 5.5.3.1); C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (section 5.5.3.4); C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (section 5.5.3.7), (IPBES, 2018; P. Smith et al., 2022) and C10, Global cooperation for finance and technology (section 5.5.3.10) (CBD, 2023; Dempsey et al., 2022; Deutz et al., 2020). Others, despite their importance for climate action, e.g., C09, Multi-hazard early warning systems (section 5.5.3.9), have limited representation in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, potentially due to the framing of biodiversity challenges (Scienseed, 2016). Similar response options contribute to multiple SDGs, i.e., C01, Increase soil organic carbon (section 5.5.3.1) (P. Smith et al., 2019, 2021); C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (section 5.5.3.4) which contributes positively to 12 of the 17 SDGs (P. Smith et al., 2019) and C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (section 5.5.3.7). In addition, several response options are closely aligned with the United Nations Food Systems pathways, such as C01, Increased soil organic carbon (section 5.5.3.1) (Qiao et al., 2022); C02/F02, Sustainable intensification (section 5.5.3.2; Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3) (FAO, 2022b; Pretty et al., 2018); C03, Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (section 5.5.3.3) and B03/C11, Agroecology (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3; section 5.5.3.11) (Bezner Kerr et al., 2023; HLPE, 2019).

Implementing individual, bundles or sequences of response options is enabled by nexus governance. Consideration of ethics, values and world views is fundamental to such governance as it dictates the inclusivity and effectiveness of climate strategies. Equity and justice are not just normative principles but operational necessities for resilient and sustainable community well-being. ILK contributes to this by offering contextual, nuanced strategies that simultaneously address climate action and broader sustainable development goals, such as biodiversity conservation and food security. The weaving of ILK into scientific frameworks enriches climate policy toolkits, embedding equity and inclusivity into the resulting strategies.

To strengthen climate actions effectively, creating strong policies through governance systems that connect and improve the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and health across levels from local to global is essential. Effective nexus governance for climate is most likely realized through a hybrid model, which combines the structured predictability of formal mechanisms with the adaptive capacity of informal systems and networks of actors. This complementarity contributes to the resilience and robustness of climate strategies, fulfilling broader environmental and human well-being objectives.

When strategically aligned with nexus elements, integrating diverse options for climate actions presents an unparalleled opportunity to drive transformative change. Through coordinated efforts, inclusive governance and the empowerment of local communities, resilience, equity and sustainability can be fostered across all nexus dimensions. By embracing holistic and innovative approaches, the global community can effectively navigate the complexities of climate action, ensuring a balanced and equitable future for all. This comprehensive approach addresses the immediate impacts of climate change and lays the groundwork for long-term sustainability and resilience. Collaboration of policymakers, communities and other stakeholders leverages scientific insights and local knowledge to implement effective and equitable climate change response options. The successful realization of these goals will require sustained commitment, robust governance frameworks and adaptive capacity to respond to emerging challenges and opportunities. As society moves forward, the lessons learned and best practices from diverse contexts must be shared and scaled to foster a global response that is both inclusive and impactful.

Notes
2. The data management report describing the Chapter 5 response option scoring criteria and process is available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.
8. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.4.3.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch7> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3W4ZAMWV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8NMK4GPF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AKQTLF93>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BLV3C8LA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DFX9SPHY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DGSUHRLB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HSRIX2XR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LAWRD5Q6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MGFCYIGY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MT32FQDY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MVIGL6MC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NML8T6W5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RGNQW7D9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RM9J8N9Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SFGQ52LE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/STNTPILP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VBDPCPNK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y8RGCQM6> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Technology, in particular digital technology, is having a significant impact on improving understanding of the natural environment and has untapped potential in understanding the complex interlinkages in nexus assessments. For example, recent advances in areas such as computational infrastructure, cloud computing and artificial intelligence (AI) are allowing the realization of data-intensive science at scale (Hey & Trefethen, 2020). These have created new opportunities, as well as highlighted continuing technology gaps, particularly those related to modelling and monitoring.

Technology is having a major impact on all areas of scientific investigation starting with the acquisition of data. There has been a dramatic increase in the availability of remote sensing data from satellites, e.g., by 2023, Copernicus was delivering 16 terabytes of high-quality data every day through its Sentinel satellites. There is also a significant increase in data from the deployment of unmanned aerial vehicles (drones), including the potential to deploy such technologies on demand. This remote sensing capability is matched by a similar dramatic increase in the deployment of in situ monitoring networks as science takes advantage of innovations around the Internet of Things. This is also supplemented by the rise of participatory monitoring/citizen science (Fraisl et al., 2022) (see section 7.3.8.2), and the ability to mine data from the internet and social media (Gibert et al., 2018). There are also other important areas of innovations including the use of eDNA (the identification of DNA from environmental samples from water, soil or air), and automated biodiversity monitoring combining AI with vision and/or acoustic data (Besson et al., 2022; Van Klink et al., 2022). As discussed in section 7.3.8.2, steps towards integrated monitoring frameworks, taking advantage of all these elements in a coordinated manner, are particularly important for nexus assessments, but remain now at incipient stages.

There have been similar advances in computational infrastructure. In terms of High-Performance Computing (HPC), technology is on the cusp of delivering exascale supercomputers, capable of executing 10¹⁸ floating point operations per second (exaFLOPS), offering the potential for more complex modelling (P. Neumann et al., 2019). Such computing power could improve integration of models across nexus elements, increase the resolution of models and/or understand sensitivities and uncertainties. The emergence of cloud computing is also having a profound impact on science, delivering on-demand computational and storage capabilities combined with the rapid innovations around cloud services at different levels. As an example, recent advances in data lake and data warehouse services offer innovative approaches to the storage of highly heterogeneous datasets (Grossman, 2019) that could be highly valuable for supporting cross-scale and integrated approaches for management across nexus elements.

Building on the above, there is now a strong emphasis on the role of data science in making sense of environmental data. It is now recognized that there is a need for tailored techniques given the complexities of environmental data (Blair et al., 2019; Zammit-Mangion et al., 2023), with a strong emphasis on integrating data across scales. Research is needed to develop new techniques that understand the spatio-temporal nature of data in this field, and the importance of extreme events. Artificial intelligence is also having a profound impact in this area, offering a range of powerful techniques to analyse and understand complex data sources (Zhong et al., 2021). There have been significant advances in the use of AI-based computer vision techniques to analyse and interpret visual imagery with application in areas such as land cover classification and species identification. Similar AI techniques can also be used with acoustic data, with the potential to also combine image and acoustic data, e.g., in biodiversity monitoring as discussed above. There has also been huge interest in generative AI, including Large Language Models, with interest in how such techniques can be applied in science (Birhane et al., 2023).

Finally, there is growing interest in the role of AI in modelling and prediction. For example, the AI 4 Soil Health project is using AI to monitor and predict soil health, recognizing the importance of healthy soils in capturing carbon, improving yields, reducing flooding and boosting biodiversity. Researchers from Google DeepMind recently developed GraphCast, a trained AI model for medium-range weather forecasting, that is both faster and more accurate compared to industry standard process models (Lam et al., 2023). Given the efficiency of such models, it is also possible to couple multiple models in integrated modelling platforms allowing richer analyses of complex systems and their interactions, an area of high potential for nexus approaches and assessments (Harrison et al., 2023). There is also untapped potential in developing environmental digital twins that achieve a synergy between process and data understanding whereby process models can be improved over time following discovery of patterns, insights and new interactions from data (e.g., modelling as a learning process (Blair & Henrys, 2023). For example, the BioDT project is developing use cases that investigate systemic issues such as species response to climate change and species interaction with each other and with humans (Trantas et al., 2023). Similarly, the Destination Earth (DestinE) project is developing a highly accurate model of the Earth and investigating how the resultant digital twin can address use cases such as climate change and flood risk mitigation/adaptation (Nativi et al., 2021).

Underpinning this, one major gap remains the need for open science and FAIR data practices, and importantly to ensure findability, accessibility, interoperability and reuse of data across disciplines (particularly important for nexus approaches given the need to integrate socioeconomic, environmental and health-related data) (G. Boulton, 2012; Wilkinson et al., 2016). Despite some emerging positive examples, this remains a major barrier to ambitious, cross-disciplinary science. The Australian Research Data Commons (ARDC) offers one example of what can be achieved in this area with its emphasis on thematic research data commons with intrinsic support for interoperability across the fields of health, earth and environment and humanities and social science (including data from Indigenous research), all underpinned by a common cloud infrastructure.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.4.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Digital technology gaps and links to monitoring" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2UMGE4HV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5NA8JCPV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5Q3IETIY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/84CBXPKG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C66Y55Y5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D2F5IKH8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D8TTH8SE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FJG5UQUQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FKH6HXUJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FSUUHN4T>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HSG3S8UH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N5NTZ8SY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NVA7X9GN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SGFTNUPC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/THD6PRF8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VEVYZ422> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """This section synthesizes the scenario literature on climate change and its interactions with the other nexus elements, particularly with respect to climate action needed to meet the long-term global goals of the Paris Agreement. It is structured into three sections focusing on: (i) scenarios of climate change impacts; (ii) scenarios of climate change mitigation; and (iii) scenarios of climate change adaptation.

Many scenario studies have been undertaken on climate change impacts, adaptation and mitigation (IPCC, 2022a); out of the scenarios in the structured review, an assessment of potential future nexus interactions was included in 112 scenarios. Those studies predominantly relied on exploratory (rather than target-seeking) scenarios, about half were of global scope opposed to national or sub-national scope, and all were based on researcher-designed (rather than participatory) scenarios with no inclusion of ILK.

Climate change impacts, mitigation and adaptation actions are tightly intertwined with many dimensions of sustainable development (van Soest et al., 2019) and biodiversity conservation (Arneth et al., 2020; IPCC, 2022a; Pörtner et al., 2023; P. Smith et al., 2022) and, hence, with the other nexus elements. It is likely that exposure to multi-sector risks (such as in the energy, transport and land-use sectors) will double under the 1.5°C and 2°C climate goals relative to pre-industrial levels, and double again in a 2°C to 3°C world (Byers et al., 2018; IPCC, 2022a), with delayed actions increasing long-term cost to the economy and human society substantially (Bertram et al., 2018; Hoegh-Guldberg et al., 2018; Zhao et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate-oriented scenarios and their nexus interactions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.9> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/23ZEAK2Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3TV95ZSH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/585NNNPV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7DY4927K>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7EDMVXX9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/A7BW7N5P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AQRXAFTU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CPW5J3E3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EHBP26WW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F2EXSZ4U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HCD8IUFN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JYDQB692>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LLC9JKZW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R3ABYEAF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SCL3IDWT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UNZ33QD7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VLRZ8ZAM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WUGBRVYW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y7ELEQ8K>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZPWYXFER> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Groundwater, which accounts for approximately 99 per cent of all liquid freshwater on Earth (UN Water, 2022), is threatened by high rates of groundwater storage depletion in several parts of the globe (Bierkens & Wada, 2019), as well as pollution and salinization in coastal areas (Lapworth et al., 2022). However, 2.6 billion people live in river basins where groundwater is needed due to the lack of surface water (Stewart-Koster et al., 2023). Effective groundwater management increases welfare; however, several factors, e.g., economic, hydrologic and agronomic, may lead to situations where the benefits are negligible (Edwards, 2016; Knapp & Olson, 1995; Koundouri, 2004).

New forms of decentralized governance specific to groundwater are emerging in response to severe groundwater deficit in various parts of the world, aided by the advent of deep tubewell groundwater lifting technology, state-led incentives for its adoption and de facto groundwater regulatory regimes (Birkenholtz, 2015). It is well established that groundwater governance is best conceptualized as a process and not as a set of descriptive managerial policies. This encompasses the biophysical conditions of the resource and interactions between groundwater users and the resource, and between those between users, communities and the state (Closas & Villholth, 2020). Integral elements of groundwater governance include: updating knowledge on groundwater resources (including cases where these resources are present as ‘fossil-water’) (De Jong et al., 2020; Jasechko et al., 2017); policy and planning at aquifer level while also setting national groundwater visions; management to regulate removing groundwater from aquifers as well as to address the effects on ecosystems, surface waters, land subsidence and other effects; and ensuring availability of tailored finances (UN Water, 2022). Recognition of the significance of institutions, improved availability and access to groundwater information and science, robustness of civil society, and supportive economic and regulatory frameworks are key enablers of effective groundwater governance (Varady et al., 2016). For example, in India, participatory forms of groundwater management based on ‘aquifer-based, common pool resource’ approaches are being used to address rapid declines in groundwater resources in several parts of the country (Kulkarni et al., 2015).

Groundwater management can also deliver potential climate change mitigation benefits without detrimental effects to biodiversity and food (Figure 5.2.3), however, the role of governance in such projects is yet to be systematically investigated. The potential of saline aquifer formations, onshore and offshore, to store carbon dioxide has been explored (Bachu, 2015; Bachu et al., 2007; Bradshaw et al., 2007; Goodman et al., 2016), however, the energy footprints of such measures have not been considered in such estimates. Projects in the North Sea and Canada have demonstrated the potential of large geological storage of carbon dioxide in saline aquifers (i.e., 1 Mt of CO2 per year) (Government of Alberta, 2019). Adaptation to climate change-induced water stress is increasingly employing groundwater-based adaptations, such as managed aquifer recharge (Dillon et al., 2020).

Groundwater is linked to at least 53 targets in SDGs 6, 12, 13 and 15 of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development (Figure 5.2.4) (UN Water, 2022) While in most of the cases there exists reinforcing linkages between achieving the target and trends or aspirations regarding groundwater, conflicts also exist for a few such as SDG Target 7.1 (access to reliable and affordable energy), which may negatively affect groundwater quality (Guppy et al., 2018).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Groundwater governance (W09)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
