    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/47T9ZBXM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4LK8W8K7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6X2QXCKN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/77YKECUB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9GWXR3GB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BKFIR9P7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BLB3SZWJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CIJ9GT4D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GLZGY9DW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IN65CWGZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IR4U956K>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JWPERHFI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KRAWA4KY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N966DHRI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PPYQK58C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q62Y2QJY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q922WVRN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XJ9KMAL4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y9Q5KGVX> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Healthcare providers and the health sector broadly are addressing the need for urgent action to safeguard health by reducing their carbon and environmental footprints. This is being done by changing practices (including increased prevention and community care to reduce the need for hospitalisation), and materials, energy sources and procurement across supply chains (Andrieu et al., 2023). Over sixty countries pledged action to achieve net-zero sustainable healthcare following the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change COP26 Health Programme and more than 68,000 hospitals and health centres in 81 countries are members of Healthcare Without Harm’s Global Green and Healthy Hospitals network (Romanello et al., 2021; Wise, 2021).

The impact of transforming healthcare can be estimated as a reduction of 2.26 × 10⁻⁴ excess deaths from heat, per ton of greenhouse gas emissions avoided per year (Bressler, 2021). Healthcare carbon footprints range from 1.51 t/greenhouse gas/per capita in resource-intensive healthcare in the United States of America to 0.06 t/greenhouse gas/per capita in India (Pichler et al., 2019). Reducing all impacts across the nexus would have even greater impact (Figure 5.4.5).

Health harms from air and water pollution (Lenzen et al., 2020) and plastic and biomedical waste (Wei & Davis, 2021) are also targeted. Water conservation and protection from contamination with pharmaceuticals (in concert with efforts to reduce waste and pollution from pharmaceutical production and prescribing (Ademollo et al., 2008; Daughton, 2003) is being addressed in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland (NHS England, 2022), United States of America (Hu et al., 2022), Australia (Wyns & Carino, 2022) and Europe (Haines & Scheelbeek, 2020). Food and catering are a major area of healthcare resource use (Ostertag et al., 2021) and shifts to providing menus based on locally produced, plant based sustainable healthy diets ( see section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Box 5.4.7) with reduced food (and all other) waste are increasingly being adopted by hospitals (Carino et al., 2020; Rinninella et al., 2023). All of these changes benefit biodiversity indirectly but ‘greening’ of hospitals and land owned and operated by health sector or organizations is also occurring in recognition of the benefits of nature-based therapies and prescribing (section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on prescription) and the opportunity to increase and improve the area available for biodiversity (Box 5.4.5).

These initiatives have broad social acceptability and are potentially transformative. They can be enabled by more regulatory approaches by national and subnational institutions (Figure 5.4.5). For example, hospitals in New York are required by law to offer plant-based meals so some have made it the default menu (Storz, 2020). This option strongly supports implementation of the Paris Agreement (Figure 5.4.6). Equitable, global progress to net zero and sustainable healthcare require leadership from high-income countries for innovation and decarbonization. Economic risks to low-income countries from restructuring are possible, raising some concerns regarding distributional equity (Andrieu et al., 2023; Romanello et al., 2021). In many low-lying island states and exposed vulnerable populations, the priority is in developing climate-resilient health services (Corvalan et al., 2020). Transforming the health sector may catalyse other sectors to do the same, and increase public awareness and support. Reported savings are predominantly from investment in disease prevention, energy and waste reduction (NHS England, 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Net-zero sustainable healthcare (H03)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.4.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3E4LAP4Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4P2SBNTH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/66SBR6CB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6V68KTFS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/776J3MFL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7KHHH3IY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AWXUW864>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AWZPCHBJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CP3WCXAV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EJ4YAJXJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FZAJNETM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GK5VDCCC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KDJIIIUY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MSSG4TN2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PCAKDEQM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QCTU2JMS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UBW8J5PM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UYQ8R3UR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WSKM5JG7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZKZEUDXC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/NXS24-ch5-4300> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Moving from multi-sectoral to integrated governance is facilitated by the SDGs, where SDG3 aims to “ensure healthy lives and promote health for all at all ages” actioned in synergy with other goals, or with them conceptualized as determinants of health (Figure 5.4.17) (WHO Regional Office for South-East Asia, 2017). However, direct evidence on the benefits of advancing human health through integrative approaches, particularly from low- and middle-income countries, is limited. These countries are falling short of reaching targets on most SDGs including health (UN Economic and Social Council, 2023) and meeting each goal and target separately dominates global efforts (United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs, 2023). Other countries have taken integrative approaches; for example, the Welsh Government’s Well-being of Future Generations (Wales) Act (2015) has, through legislation, required all new policy and development to address the SDGs and to ensure that the needs of the present are met without compromising future generations – thus supporting intergenerational health equity (Green et al., 2020). Measuring health and well-being across multiple domains has replaced gross domestic product as the metric of success in some locations. For example, New Zealand produces a ‘Well-being Budget’ (Sindall et al., 2021). Such developments create regulatory and legislative frameworks that can transform decision-making processes and accountability.

Nevertheless, the governance arrangements for integration across multiple sectors, including health, have few good examples, with the exception of H14, the One Health approach (section 5.4.3.14). This offers a new governance structure to enable better integration through the Quadripartite Alliance of One Health: WHO, FAO, WOAH and UNEP (Chapter 1, Boxes 1.4, 5.4.12 and 5.4.14). The One Health approach has gained significant momentum for its potential to operationalize “living in harmony with nature” including the reduction of disease risks. The Global Action Plan for Biodiversity and Health and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework promote One Health. In addition, several countries have declarations and commitments towards adoption of a One Health approach. The Quadripartite Alliance for One Health 2022-2026 Joint Plan of Action (FAO et al., 2022) facilitates the establishment of structures and mechanisms for enabling integrated governance and implementation of One Health. Recent developments on supportive legislation and regulatory mechanisms include ongoing discussions for incorporating a One Health article under an International Pandemic Agreement/Treaty, and the One Health principle being enshrined within the founding regulation of the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention(European Union, 2012). The latter mandates the agency to use a One Health approach. Furthermore, the European Parliament in its final report about the COVID-19 pandemic included 13 One Health recommendations, among which is a call for the establishment of a European cross-agency task force dedicated to the One Health approach to advance transdisciplinary research and cross-sectoral scientific advice (Amand-Eeckhout, 2023).

Collaborative models under One Health promote a multi-disciplinary approach and address issues of knowledge, skills and competencies including soft skills for successful collaborations (R. Dasgupta et al., 2021). Nexus governance domains for the health sector can be characterised as a fabric where horizontal (informal trust-based relationships) and vertical (formal and official relationships) threads intersect (Nyatanyi et al., 2017). In planning for health emergencies, for example, it is recognised that climate change, biodiversity loss and land-use change are important drivers, and accordingly effective and enforceable legislation, investment, capacity-building and integration of other sectors and professionals beyond health is required (Elnaiem et al., 2023). Processes of governance for health in the nexus include putting in place appropriate mechanisms that allow for risk management and provide for checks and balances, including oversight, availability of finance and other resources, and capacities. Leadership development capacities in One Health approaches, often identified as a key need, covering systems thinking and promoting values and ethics require prioritization through in-service programmes for One Health professionals and university curricula (Frankson et al., 2016). For example, early analysis suggests that governance mechanisms for the World Bank’s pandemic fund need to enable funding for response financing for outbreaks and link this with health security frameworks and instruments (Boyce et al., 2023).

In alignment with global targets, key governance actions include investments in public sector institutions and capacities of public servants within the health sector to identify trade-offs and synergies, leverage private sector actors and increase funding in order to enable nexus decision-making. Similarly, designing national enabling frameworks that recognise the role of sub-national governments through empowering them and providing them with human and financial resources would strengthen the implementation of the response options. Fiscal decentralization is important, and along with better quality governance in terms of control of corruption, government effectiveness, regulatory quality and accountability, can improve health sector outcomes (Nakatani et al., 2022). Strengthening learning in health systems has been recognized as a key means of advancing health through multisectoral governance and for scaling and addressing broader health goals (Sheikh et al., 2022; Sheikh & Abimbola, 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supporting response option implementation through international frameworks" .

