<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7IKDNS8C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7SNNSST7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/893TRFLF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BBT53YMD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BVPHZ2RY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C3EBGFZ9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CM76Q9IF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IQ8Q26WG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JSTK7LGE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N48T3WWH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PWASE6D3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q46JAJA8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RJECSEIZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/S7BVCFLZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SWAL4K46>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U8PSDBIG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/V48VCEHJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WTACHK7J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YNEHIAPF> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Coastal and marine nature-based solutions, including coral, seagrass and mangrove restoration, aim to protect, conserve and restore ecosystems while enhancing human quality of life (Riisager-Simonsen et al., 2022). These actions aim to restore habitats essential for economies and livelihoods, and reduce vulnerabilities against natural disasters for communities relying primarily on nature’s contributions to people (Bayraktarov et al., 2016; Giffin et al., 2021; Greiner et al., 2013; Westoby et al., 2020). Seagrass, mangrove, algae and shallow and deep coral reef restoration promote habitat integrity, connectivity and resilience, benefitting intraspecific genetic variation, biodiversity and ecosystem functioning and, thereby, food security and health (Acreman et al., 2020; Bayraktarov et al., 2016; Des Roches et al., 2021; Greiner et al., 2013; R. Hughes & Stachowicz, 2004; Montoya Maya et al., 2016;

NOAA, 2023; Riisager-Simonsen et al., 2022; Sinclair et al., 2021; Westoby et al., 2020). Restoring seagrasses, seaweeds and mangroves also helps mitigate climate change through “blue carbon” sequestration (Eger et al., 2022; Greiner et al., 2013; Thorhaug et al., 2017). Restoration projects at the local scale are long-term processes that can take decades and require balancing of conservation goals, human needs and ecological values with other strategies to promote synergies at different scales (Westoby et al., 2020).

Restoration efforts are facilitated by building and supporting trust and coordination in multi-stakeholder collaboration. This requires addressing concerns, breaking down social barriers, respecting traditional customs, implementing systematic planning and ensuring monitoring, financing and equal decision-making among scientists, traditional owners (e.g., IPLC), the private sector and governmental and non-governmental bodies (Bayraktarov et al., 2016; Greiner et al., 2013; Z. Liu et al., 2016; Sinclair et al., 2021). This collaboration also brings additional benefits. For example, actively involving local communities and volunteers in restoration increases awareness of their ecosystem dependency. It also increases coordination between local stakeholders, with traditional knowledge, and national stakeholders in the implementation of restoration actions while reducing costs (Bayraktarov et al., 2016; Greiner et al., 2013; Z. Liu et al., 2016; Sinclair et al., 2021). Such coordination exists, for example, in Australia, where coastal ecosystem management is a national environmental concern and coordination of stakeholder networks is implemented (Sinclair et al., 2021).

New approaches for restoring marine and coastal ecosystems are needed, as this process requires long-term commitment due to the timescale of species growth and succession and impacts of global drivers such as climate change. Projects are often short-term and only measure success in terms of species survival, hindering the measurement of the full and long-term outcomes of restoration for ecosystem functioning and socioeconomic benefits for stakeholders, such as cost savings in managing for flood risk (Bayraktarov et al., 2016; M. Y. Hein et al., 2017; Z. Liu et al., 2016; Reguero et al., 2021; Sinclair et al., 2021; Westoby et al., 2020). Implementing metrics to evaluate cost-effectiveness, documenting the causes of failures and assessing impacts on society’s inclusion (e.g., cultural, educational, gender and health) across governance levels is a critical need (Bayraktarov et al., 2016; Greiner et al., 2013; T. Liu et al., 2016; Westoby et al., 2020).

Ethical considerations in ecological restoration also need to be addressed by governments and scientists. These include: the higher cost of restoration versus preserving ecosystems; matching actions to drivers of ecological degradation; careful selection of techniques and species used; identifying sites based on factors of ecological degradation; minimum ecological requirements for restoration; and evaluating small-scale actions before large-scale implementation (Bayraktarov et al., 2016; Z. Liu et al., 2016; Rogers et al., 2015; Sinclair et al., 2021; Westoby et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restoration of coastal and marine systems (B06)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
