        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WXEHTLBG> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Increasing knowledge to improve inland fisheries assessments and management is a response option that can be applied globally on all managed inland waters (Figure 5.2.5) (Funge-Smith, 2018). It also furthers equity initiatives and aligns with international framework targets through increased catch that can reduce poverty, while also increase economic stability and improved status in the community (Lynch et al., 2020). However, results of the knowledge generated from this option, how that knowledge can be used and how practitioners choose to implement it will affect its impact (FAO, 2021). Therefore, there is a high-level of context-dependence inherent in this response option.

Well-managed fisheries are dependent on suitable water quality, as this directly affects fish populations and communities and fisheries' health. However, there is high uncertainty on the valuation of ecosystem services such as water quality (Keeler et al., 2012). Therefore, the lack of appropriate data and the lack of understanding of the effects of water quality changes on fish and fisheries remains unresolved (Bunnell et al., 2021). Evidence on how the physiology of a fish responds to changes in temperature, water quality and other stressors would help gain a better understanding on how to manage these fisheries in the future (Y. Chen et al., 2016; Whitney et al., 2016). Therefore, increased knowledge around fisheries management is likely to benefit water for both humans and ecosystems (Figure 5.2.3) (Funge-Smith, 2018).

Inland fisheries have an important role in sustainably feeding growing human populations, as they deliver quality nutrition to some of the world’s most vulnerable populations in a manner that is both accessible and affordable (Funge-Smith, 2018). They help meet SDGs 2, 6 and 15 and can play a role in cost-efficient water quality management (Jůza et al., 2019). Inland fisheries catch represents ~12 per cent of global fishery production, however, values may be considerably higher given data and reporting limitations. Thus, further assessments would improve understanding of inland fisheries as a source of food to support nutritional security and overall well-being (FAO, 2021). For other aspects of health, there is a need for additional information on well-being (Figure 5.2.3).

Implementing fisheries management plans can positively impact freshwater biodiversity, which can serve as an indicator of ecosystem health (Funge-Smith, 2018). In general, the level of knowledge on freshwater biodiversity (i.e., species richness, endemism, production, level of endangerment and value) is poor or out of date (FAO, 2021; Funge-Smith, 2018; Taylor & Bartley, 2016). Increased knowledge would allow countries to assess the status of their ecosystems to predict future impacts and quantify the effects of management on fisheries for their future needs in food security, livelihoods, recreation and investment (FAO, 2021). There is limited evidence on the transformative change potential for this response option alone (Figure 5.2.4), though when combined with others, it may have high transformative potential and may be considered a cross-cutting option for addressing interactions across the nexus.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sustainable inland fisheries (W05)" .

