        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/I5K87ZKT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IDGJE3RL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LRJ3SE3Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P9D5CXMH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PJDWHGY4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QV7H68U3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SWAL4K46>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UK8796WN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UYA4NVAJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WTACHK7J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z3Y33EKX> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Short‑lived climate pollutants are highly reactive compounds frequently co-emitted with CO2 in combustion processes for energy production or transport (Naik et al., 2021). They include aerosols, such as particulate matter (PM) or black carbon, sulphates (SOx), nitrates, ammonium (NH3), and ozone (O3) precursors such as nitrogen oxides (NOx) or volatile organic carbon.

Short‑lived climate pollutants persist in the atmosphere from a few hours to a couple of months. Their climate effect predominantly occurs in the first two decades after emission (or formation) and consists of both warming and cooling, with potential changes in cloud formation and precipitation patterns (Bellouin et al., 2016; Bony et al., 2015; Naik et al., 2021). SLCPs are also the main contributors to air pollution, damaging human health and negatively influencing land and ocean ecosystem productivity with implications for food security, water quality and biodiversity (Figures 5.5.3 and 5.5.6) (Silva et al., 2016; Stohl et al., 2015; Y. Xie et al., 2018).

Emission sources and atmospheric concentrations of short‑lived climate pollutants are highly heterogenous. The largest surface concentrations of particulate matter occur in Southern Asia, Eastern Asia and the Middle East, where the dominant anthropogenic emission sources are biomass and coal fuel-based cooking and heating, and, especially in Eastern Asia, energy and industry (Naik et al., 2021) (Figure 5.5.5). At a global scale, road transport emissions from gasoline and diesel vehicles are the dominant contributor to surface ozone concentrations in populated areas (Silva et al., 2016; Unger et al., 2020). Aviation is mostly responsible for emissions of NOx, aerosols and formation of persistent condensation trails (contrails) (Lee et al., 2021), while shipping is an important polluting factor (mainly SOx, NOx, and PM) in coastal areas along the major trade routes (Jonson et al., 2020; H. Liu et al., 2016).

The type of short‑lived climate pollutant and impacts to be prioritized reflect the local context. For example, emissions from the residential and commercial sectors (fossil and biomass use for cooking and heating) and the energy sector (fossil fuel production, distribution and combustion) contribute the most to human exposure to PM, and mitigation measures in each region primarily target the main local source of pollution (i.e., the residential sector in Southern Asia and Africa, or the industry and energy sector in Central and Eastern Asia). Alternatively, emissions from energy and land transportation sectors contribute the most to ozone exposure and are relevant within cities affected by poor air quality. Short‑lived climate pollutant emission reduction strategies include measures such as energy efficiency, zero-emissions vehicles, reduced use of private vehicles, efficient and clean stoves for heating and cooking, soot filters, flue gas scrubbers and cleaner industrial processes. The interactions with nexus elements are multiple and complex (Table 5.5.6; Figure 5.5.3).

The evident and quantifiable health benefits of short‑lived climate pollutant abatement favor their social acceptance, but proper instruments should ensure a just transition, particularly when addressing the energy and transport sectors in poorer urban areas (Benmarhnia et al., 2014; Thompson et al., 2016; Vandyck et al., 2018) (Figure 5.5.4). Co-benefits for air quality and human health of short‑lived climate pollutant mitigation can offset the costs of climate measures (Harmsen et al., 2020; M. Li et al., 2018; Saari et al., 2015). The transformation of the energy system included in nationally determined contribution pledges following the Paris Agreement can reduce local air pollution and globally avoid 71 to 99,000 premature deaths annually by 2030 (1.5 million by 2050 under stricter mitigation scenarios), thereby offsetting the costs of climate policies (Vandyck et al., 2018).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reduce short-lived climate pollutants (C08)" .

