    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Agroecology is an integrated approach that simultaneously applies ecological and social concepts and principles to the design and management of food and agricultural systems (FAO, 2018; HLPE, 2019). Other sustainable farming approaches closely related to agroecology include ecological intensification (Kleijn et al., 2019) (see Chapter 5.3, sections 5.3.3.4 (F04, Ecological intensification - croplands) and 5.3.3.5 (F05, Ecological intensification - rangelands)) and regenerative agriculture (Giller et al., 2021), which vary more in their social, cultural and political focus than in their ecological and agricultural foundations (Tittonell et al., 2022; Wezel et al., 2015). They all utilize the functions of non-domesticated biota and genetic diversity in crops and livestock species to support sustainable production by restoring carbon, soil nutrients and water dynamics, controlling weeds, pest and diseases, and supplying pollination (Ewert et al., 2023). Some other sustainable farming approaches, like sustainable intensification, benefit biodiversity and ecological processes without necessarily relying on them for production; they focus mainly on reducing environmental impact by increasing input efficiency (Garnett et al., 2013) (see sustainable intensification response options F03 in Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.3 and C02 in Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.2). Agroecology is supported by many farming practices, such as agroforestry (see Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.11 (C11, Agroecology)), crop and pasture rotation, intercropping and cover crops, among others (Bezner Kerr et al., 2023; HLPE, 2019).

Agroecology is implemented worldwide to address the negative impacts of conventional or industrial agriculture and especially trade-offs between food, biodiversity, climate change and people (Pörtner et al., 2021). Agroecology is highlighted in the FAO’s 10 Elements of Agroecology framework (FAO, 2018) (see also Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.1 and Box 5.3.1), and is reflected in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework under target 10 on sustainable production systems (DeClerck et al., 2023; Wanger et al., 2020). The implementation of agroecology is framed by specific policies: while it is highly regulated in the European Union, in other regions such as North America policies are lagging, while they are expanding slowly in the Global South (Ewert et al., 2023).

Agroecology benefits multiple nexus elements by increasing their synergies and reducing trade-offs. For example, agroforestry is one of the current options for agriculture with the greatest synergistic benefits for nature’s contributions to people, with positive impacts on food production, managed and wild biodiversity, water and climate change (McElwee et al., 2020; Plieninger et al., 2020; van Noordwijk et al., 2020) (see Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.11 (C11, Agroecology). Regarding trade-offs, it is generally considered that agroecology has lower yields than conventional agriculture (Ewert et al., 2023). However, this might be explained by confounding agroecology with organic agriculture (Seufert et al., 2012). Recent studies nevertheless suggest that better management practices might reduce yield gaps in comparison to conventional agriculture (MacLaren et al., 2022). Moreover, even with lower yields, reduced production costs contribute to higher profitability (Van Der Ploeg et al., 2019).

In many local communities globally, agroecology builds on knowledge (e.g., traditional ecological knowledge), practices (e.g., rotational and shifting farming) and world views (e.g., plural valuation) of IPLC, embedded in regionally-specific sociocultural frameworks and institutions, generating positive impacts on the different dimensions of equity (Mier Y Terán Giménez Cacho et al., 2018). Its implementation is commonly supported by market instruments such as certification and short food supply chains, underpinned by sociocultural changes in consumer preferences (Ewert et al., 2023). The scaling out and up (see Chapter 4, section 4.4) of agroecology faces challenges such as power struggles with agribusiness, uncertainties regarding yield gaps with conventional agriculture, the necessity for farmer skill development, shifts in values among farmers and consumers, ageing farmers, land tenure issues and path dependencies in infrastructure or financial commitments (Tittonell et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Agroecology (B03)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4PCILRM7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9BEFUQJ5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GP2DEPVG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GZVA84GT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PNA7Z2HZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/S22ATK46>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZVZR37F7> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Food systems are remarkable in their diversity, which remains one of the greatest joys of the human experience as the source of cultural identity and cross-cultural exchange. The tension between urgently needing to secure enough food within planetary boundaries for approximately 10 billion people by 2050, and food production and consumption as a deeply humanistic, ecological and cultural act is a real, driving concern about oversimplifying scalable response options (FAO et al., 2020). The large productivity gains of the green revolution contributed to global food security; however, its impacts on small-holder systems, family farms, sustainable healthy diets and the loss of a diversity of culturally rich production systems, and concomitant negative environmental impacts including loss of agrobiodiversity among others, has been transformative (Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005).

Plausible response options assessed in the subchapter were identified from the catalogue of national pathways for shaping sustainable and equitable food systems submitted by countries for the 2020-2023 United Nations Food System Summit (Figure 5.3.2). In developing their pathways, countries drew on actions identified by the scientific community which produced more than 76 science briefs, nearly 1700 food system dialogues with 110,000 individuals from civil society, and surveys led by five action tracks yielding over 6,000 individual responses on candidate response options. The identified actions in the pathways cover broad themes, all with strong linkages to food systems, that include, but are not limited to, equity and fairness, economic and finance issues, use of technologies, partnerships and synergies, and policy and regulatory frameworks.

All response options identified in the United Nations Food Systems Summit pathways are important, but they have varying degrees of impact on the nexus elements. The expert opinion of the author team was used to identify a subset of the response options that impact multiple nexus elements. This emphasized the options associated with action tracks 1, 2, and 3: access to safe and nutritious foods, sustainable consumption and nature-positive food production, respectively. In addition, eleven solutions were proposed by United Nations Food Systems Summit under the action track on livelihoods and equity. These cover essential procedural options which underpin the successful implementation of response options. From these, gender, ILK and local food systems are emphasized in this chapter. The fifth United Nations Food Systems Summit action track focused on resilience and included an important list of cross-cutting actions. However, many of the resilience solutions have indirect impacts on nexus elements. Countries have struggled to articulate important nexus interactions in their pathways (Figure 5.3.2).

This iterative process yielded 16 response options that are assessed in this subchapter. Borrowing from conservation (Arlidge et al., 2018), these can be categorized into five major groups of actions (Table 5.3.3): (1) avoiding the conversion of remaining terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems of high ecological integrity; (2) restoring degraded lands and waters either with the aim of increasing contributions to climate change mitigation, biodiversity conservation or food production; (3) regenerating the environmental performance of food by intensifying ecological interactions in production systems, notably managing soil communities to improve soil health, increase long-term belowground carbon capture and storage, and increasing aboveground carbon such as with production of perennials; (4) reducing soil, water and air pollution impacts from food systems; (5) transforming to just food systems through alignment on healthy, culturally diverse diets, urban-rural food systems, including local participation and access to natural resources and land (notably by women, youth and Indigenous communities) and ensuring that public spending on food generates positive impacts across nexus elements.

Critical aspects of each response option are summarized in this section based on the synthesis of evidence and examples of implementation (Figures 5.2.3, 5.2.4 and 5.2.5). Each response option has been analysed and scored based on assessed evidence against 10 common criteria (Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1) related to its potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, its feasibility and breadth of applicability, its impact on equity and its potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks. Table 5.3.4 provides brief descriptions of the 16 food response options; the response options are described in more detail in sections 5.3.3.1 to 5.3.3.16. Of the 16 response options assessed, four focal response options were selected for more detailed consideration: F06, Ecological intensification – aquatic foods (section 5.3.3.6); F11, Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11); F12, City region food systems (section 5.3.3.12) and F14, Foster gender-transformative approaches (section 5.3.3.13). These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action.

Assessment of the 16 response options shows that the majority have strong impacts across nexus elements though these impacts can vary significantly (Figure 5.3.3). However, the success of the response options is strongly dependent on contextual factors and implementation effort, with social, political and economic dimensions being essential for successful implementation (Figure 5.3.4). The economic assessment of the response options was the most challenging aspect to assess considering that hidden costs are often poorly factored or not attributed to inconsistencies between social, political and economic dimensions of implementation efforts.

Notes
2. The data management report describing the Chapter 5 response option scoring criteria and process is available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for food" .

