        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CE98RWWC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DCBET8K7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EPW8W9HN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/H9MFVE78>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HUNBQ3JF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/I9N34ZXT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JHCASUJT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KKZBBB5Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L4L4QV5W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PUD8ESXJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RGDLKJKW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TCMP76VW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VQ4WMHA9> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Rangelands cover a greater area than any other terrestrial food system (Figure 5.3.5). Ecological intensification of rangelands can make them more productive while also providing benefits to other nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). This can be achieved through a variety of mechanisms, including regenerative grazing (modifying the intensity and duration of grazing to mimic natural systems), pyric herbivory (integration of fire and grazing in a shifting mosaic of patches), silvopasture (a form of agroforestry, involving adding trees to grazed lands (Box 5.3.6) and fence-free herding.

Ecological intensification has neutral to positive effects on food production from rangelands. Silvopasture increases yields of forage and wood products by increasing the production area of each (Pent, 2020), while regulating microclimate through shade (Zeppetello et al., 2022), reducing heat stress and providing shelter for livestock and additional fodder. Pyric herbivory can maintain livestock production levels (Limb et al., 2011).

Rangeland ecological intensification benefits biodiversity even more strongly. Regenerative grazing results in higher soil biome and grass diversity than continuous grazing (Morris, 2021), combining fire with grazing benefits rare species (Fuhlendorf et al., 2009) and grassland bird communities (Davis et al., 2016) while managing invasive species (McMillan et al., 2022; Sherrill et al., 2022). Fence-free herding reduces fragmentation in rangelands, allowing wildlife to access heterogeneous resources critical in arid environments (Hobbs et al., 2008; J. O. Ogutu et al., 2014).

While silvopasture capacity to increase soil and aboveground carbon is well documented (M. M. Smith et al., 2022), the climate change mitigation potential of grasslands is variable. Regenerative grazing can increase soil carbon compared to continuous grazing (Byrnes et al., 2018), but can take 5-20 years to take hold (Bartley et al., 2023). Optimizing grazing intensities across all grasslands globally could increase soil carbon sequestration potential by 148 to 699 MtCO2e year⁻¹(Y. Bai & Cotrufo, 2022). In Mediterranean environments, grazing has been used to adapt to climate change by reducing fuel-loads and fire risk notably around cities (Perevolotsky & Seligman, 1998). Rangeland ecological intensification has a limited but generally positive effect on water for ecosystems and humans. Pyric herbivory can enhance water retention (West et al., 2016) and silvopasture can increase water infiltration (M. M. Smith et al., 2022).

Ecological intensification is often practiced by IPLC (Connolly et al., 2021) and is threatened by a variety of policies, including land privatization and sedentarization schemes (López-i-Gelats et al., 2016). Economic costs and savings vary widely depending on where and how it is implemented (Figure 5.3.6). Diversified systems tend to be more economically stable but often require up front capital and specific capacity-building to facilitate transition.

Rangeland ecological intensification is aligned with multiple SDGs (S. L. R. Wood et al., 2018) and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework target 10 on sustainable production (Figure 5.3.4). Ecological intensification of rangelands can help facilitate halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (section 5.3.3.1) and restoring soil health (section 5.3.3.2). It will be most effective when combined with reforming public spending (section 5.3.3.13) and promoting ILK (section 5.3.3.15) in recognition of the importance of Indigenous pastoralist practices. Transformative change will require combining ecological intensification of rangelands with shifting to sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11) to reduce overall demand for meat to allow more sustainable stocking rates.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological intensification – rangelands (F05)" .

