        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KBUA8CFI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KHJLFN6C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MHKMEDT8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PIQ3T64Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QG28F85T>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UCER27SB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UV3GMV9V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WBBYIKKH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XAIEBZX4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z3EAKT9H>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZN8T4Z5J> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Feeding the world while reducing nutrient pollution can be applied at the global scale (Figure 5.3.5), requiring nutrient use efficiencies between 60 and 77 per cent. Reductions can be prioritized in regions where intensive agriculture is pervasive with increased nutrient use in regions where productivity remains low (Schulte-Uebbing et al., 2022). Reducing nutrient use is beneficial to overall environmental performance but can result in lower yields, although the evidence for this relationship is weak (Bijay-Singh & Craswell, 2021; Nadeem et al., 2022).

Reducing nutrient pollution has strong benefits for reducing the risk of contamination of surface water used for human consumption, preserving water quality for humans and ecosystems, and decreasing overall environmental exposure to algal blooms (Jarvie et al., 2018; Liu et al., 2021; Rastogi et al., 2015) (Figure 5.3.3). It also benefits human health as cyanobacterial blooms and the accumulation of several cyanotoxins in water bodies pose a severe risk to global public health and aquatic organisms (Rastogi et al., 2015). Algal blooms have been associated with several water-borne diseases (e.g., cholera) (Epstein, 1993) and other morbidities including neurological disorders (Schleyer & Vardi, 2020).

Reducing nutrient pollution has positive outcomes for native biodiversity and ecological functions, although evidence is weak. Actions such as improved nutrient use efficiency, precision application and creating effective buffers between agricultural systems and adjacent aquatic systems could have net positive impacts on terrestrial and aquatic biodiversity, while simultaneously creating habitat and connectivity (Asbjornsen et al., 2014; L. J. Cole et al., 2020; Fremier et al., 2015). Reducing nutrient pollution has minimal impacts on climate change, as the reduction in nutrient application to reduce pollution in intensive agricultural areas is nearly equal to the increases required to close yield gaps in areas where productivity is low (Schulte-Uebbing et al., 2022). Nevertheless, 15 per cent of global fossil fuel use is for creating nutrient inputs for food (GAFF, 2023).

Public opinion is generally supportive of reducing nutrient pollution. However, farmers’ perspectives may be less supportive due to tension between nutrients as an important agricultural input and a pollutant (Kanter et al., 2020; Rauh & Hughes, 2023) (Figure 5.3.4). Reduced nutrient use is associated with reduced yield risks, but also with improved nutrient use efficiencies for which investment in advanced technologies is required (Finger et al., 2019; Mizik, 2023; Rossi et al., 2023). Normative tools providing a locally adaptable framework and a voluntary set of practices to guide different stakeholders directly or indirectly involved with fertilizer use and management can play a significant role in reducing nutrient pollution (e.g., the International Code of Conduct for the Sustainable Use and Management of Fertilizers (FAO, 2019a).

Several other response options also support reductions in nutrient pollution with generally positive outcomes, including F10, Reduce food loss and waste (section 5.3.3.10); F11, Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11) and improving nutrient recycling and agricultural production practices (see F04, Ecological intensification – croplands (section 5.3.3.4) and F05, Ecological intensification – rangelands (section 5.3.3.5). All these options have an impact on reducing both nitrogen and phosphorus pollution (Springmann et al., 2018). However, implementation at scale is very costly and often limited (S. Kumar et al., 2020; Mizik, 2023) (Figure 5.3.5). The efficacy of actions is often dependent on agricultural systems, climate, soil types, topography and the nutrient management approaches employed (Liu et al., 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reduce nutrient pollution (F07)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2MH99PGM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2Q3YPW53>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3CBIZWMX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3FI8GM5C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3ZSET8LP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4KSMUXAV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6K4ANPKI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7EX92JKG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7M2UFHXP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9NVXAXUC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9VCPPWCC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/A4ZSDW3S>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C66Y55Y5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IQEZUMGU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L7SM7ANN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LZPW6FEY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N388LLIR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NBGFIGVC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NDYV49ZY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R2SXZIEV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SAH3XF6H>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WMVJ3PRD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XWTHMPE4> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """A huge quantity of carbon is stored in peatlands and wetlands (e.g., 500–850 Pg C, Turetsky et al., 2015), but around 12 per cent of global peatlands have been drained and degraded (UNEP, 2022). Restoration and reduced conversion of peatlands and non-coastal wetlands involves rewetting by blocking drainage channels and can include other interventions such as erosion prevention by stabilizing eroded peats through physical means (mounding or adding netting) and revegetation (IUCN, 2016). Restoration can be applied anywhere that peatlands / wetlands have been degraded (Figure 5.5.5). Peatlands occur on all continents and cover 3-4 per cent on the Earth’s land surface (UNEP, 2022). The global mitigation potential for reducing greenhouse gas emissions and increasing carbon storage through peatland / wetland restoration is 0.6-2.0 GtCO2e/yr, but there are no global estimates of adaptation potential (P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020; P. Smith et al., 2022) (Figure 5.5.3).

Degraded peatlands store less water than intact peatlands. Peatland restoration upstream in catchments is used as a nature-based solution for flood control, e.g., managing flood / highwater peaks downstream after heavy rainfall. The storage and regulation of water flow in wetlands may also buffer against the effects of drought (Munang, Thiaw, Alverson, Liu, et al., 2013; Munang, Thiaw, Alverson, Mumba, et al., 2013). While tropical peats in South-East Asia have been substantially drained and cleared for non-food commodities (e.g., pulp and paper, and oil palm), large areas of tropical peatlands have been cleared for food production, and their restoration could displace food production and damage local food supply in these areas. The same is true for cultivated northern peatlands (Miettinen et al., 2016). At a local level, while peatland restoration on land used for agriculture could displace food production, the impacts to regional and global food security would be minimal (Humpenöder et al., 2018) (Figure 5.5.3).

For health, rewetting of formerly drained areas in regions where vector-borne diseases (from organisms which have part of their lifecycle in water) are endemic, could lead to a greater disease burden, but can improve public health through decreased fire-caused pollutants locally and distributed more widely (Dale & Connelly, 2012; Griscom et al., 2017) (Figure 5.5.3). Large-scale changes in the water balance of peatlands and type of vegetation influence evaporation, which has a cooling effect on the local or regional climate. This can influence local or regional weather cycles. Rewetting drained peatlands can reduce heat, and reduced heat has a positive impact on public health (Sadeghi et al., 2022).

Peatlands in good condition support a range of specialist and unique species, so peatland/wetland restoration will benefit biodiversity (UNEP, 2022) (Figure 5.5.3). Globally, only a small percentage of peatlands are protected and, in many regions, there is no protection at all (UNEP, 2022). Hence, protection of peatlands / wetlands will improve the area of land protected, which enhances habitat connectivity and reduces fragmentation of intact peatlands, making them more resilient to, for example, the threat of erosion (IUCN, 2016). Drained and degraded peatlands lose many peatland functions, including carbon and water storage, so peatland / wetland restoration also restores these ecological functions and a range of NCP (P. Smith et al., 2019).

Restoration and reduced conversion of peatlands/wetlands is at TRL 8-9 (Babiker et al., 2022) (Figure 5.5.5), but there could be public acceptance issues around restoring wetlands in areas where vector-borne diseases are endemic due to health concerns (Dale & Connelly, 2012) (Figure 5.5.4). It aligns strongly with policies to improve soil and water quality, to combat land degradation and desertification and to tackle climate change (P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020). Costs can be relatively high. Humpenöder et al. (2020) use one-time costs of $7,000 ha⁻¹ (2005 market exchange rate) for the initial rewetting, and recurring costs of $200 ha⁻¹ yr⁻¹ for maintenance, based on an indicative cost estimate of £200–10,000 ha⁻¹ for rewetting, and £25–400 ha⁻¹ yr⁻¹ for maintenance (Glenk & Martin-Ortega, 2018; Moxey & Moran, 2014). Monitoring, reporting and verification costs, and opportunity costs associated with lost agricultural production, may be high. Direct economic benefits are limited, though carbon finance has provided income for peatland / wetland protection / restoration projects (Joosten, 2012). In terms of equity, it provides benefits to IPLC (e.g. Suwarno et al., 2016), and can enable inclusive and participatory processes (e.g. Tan et al., 2023). When combined with other response options it could be transformative (Figure 5.5.4).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wetland conservation and restoration (C04)" .

