    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Offshore wind energy is becoming a widely used solution for the decarbonization of the energy system. Its mitigation potential has been estimated at a maximum of 10 GtCO2/yr by 2050 and from 0.3-0.8 ºC by 2100 (Barthelmie & Pryor, 2021; Pek, 2020). Offshore wind farms employ fixed-foundation or floating turbines. The latter can be placed in deeper waters and are less impactful, but knowledge gaps exist (Dorrell et al., 2022).

Offshore wind farms negative environmental impacts, such as noise, contaminants, electromagnetic fields, and risk of collision and entanglement, with particularly detrimental effects on migrating birds and bats, seabirds, marine mammals, reptiles and on seabed habitats and sedimentary processes, and they could also facilitate species invasions (Dannheim et al., 2020; Galparsoro et al., 2022; Lloret et al., 2022; Saidur et al., 2011; Vaissière et al., 2014); though the same is true of fossil fuel infrastructure. Although biodiversity and water quality are negatively affected, these impacts are low when compared with those of other energy systems (Figure 5.5.3).

Offshore wind farms can have positive impacts. The exclusion of trawling can contribute to biodiversity and conservation and even augment neighbouring fisheries (spillover effects). OWF also create artificial hard substrates that increase biodiversity (Ashley et al., 2014; Boutin et al., 2023; Degraer et al., 2020; Hammar et al., 2016), which increases carbon assimilation (Mavraki et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.4). Combining offshore wind farms with integrated multitrophic aquaculture (C03; section 5.5.3.3) is currently being explored (Banach et al., 2020; Buck et al., 2018; C.-T. Huang et al., 2022; Ingle et al., 2022; van den Burg et al., 2020), as an option to enhance food production.

Optimal siting of offshore wind farms should minimize their negative effects (outside bird migration corridors) and maximize their positive benefits (homogeneous soft-bottoms) (Mavraki et al., 2020). Floating turbines can be used where possible. Ecosystem-based management principles are encouraged (Stelzenmüller et al., 2022; WWF, 2014) to avoid the not-in-my-backyard effect as seen in the Mediterranean Sea (Lloret et al., 2022) and minimize the detrimental effects on well-being.

While operating offshore wind farms can be regarded as a renewable energy, the construction of the turbines and decommissioning phases currently require fossil fuels. Despite the drop in costs of this technology (Wiser et al., 2021), there is a risk that large parts of the globe lag behind unless transitional costs in developing countries are addressed (Figure 5.5.4). Recycling and circular (at least spiral) economy principles would help to improve sustainability, e.g., replacing critical components by common and/or renewable materials, reusing rare earth elements (Valero et al., 2018) or increasing the lifespan of turbines. Deep-sea mining to meet this demand, thereby impacting pristine marine areas, could have adverse consequences for biodiversity (Levin et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Offshore wind power (C05)" .

