        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SU6NWXMX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U5GM9FXH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VZ5I8Y2L>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YWRYCURP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZBW9JIFB> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The Sixth IPCC Assessment Report underscores the direct effects of climate change on extreme events and associated hazards that threaten both human health and ecosystems, particularly the most vulnerable populations living in fragile environments (IAP, 2023; IPCC, 2021) (Figures 5.5.6 and 5.5.7). Multi-hazard early warning systems are designed to anticipate, predict and generate warnings for individual or multiple hazards, allowing communities and administrations to prepare, respond and recover effectively and thus build resilience against socio-economic and environmental changes (Fakhruddin et al., 2021; ILO, 2017; UNDRR, 2007; UNISDR, 2015). However, the one-way flow of information is limiting in addressing multi-dimensional risk communication that influences local response attributed to various factors such as trust, knowledge, heuristics, experience or socio-cultural contexts (Khan et al., 2017). Integrated risk communication with multi-hazards early warning systems thus ensures that communities receive, understand and act on impending hazard alerts and that they are adapted to local circumstances and needs (ILO, 2022).

By providing timely warnings for multiple hazards across the globe, such as thunderstorms, flooding, cyclones, heatwaves, water scarcity, food security, earthquakes and diseases, multi-hazard early warning systems and risk communication aid both key stakeholders and communities and thus impact the nexus elements (N. Liu et al., 2022; R. Newell, 2023; Prabhakar, 2012; WMO, 2023) (Figure 5.5.3). Multi-hazard early warning systems collect and process data then communicate and coordinate through assigned authorities of a jurisdiction. This process necessitates collaborative actions from related stakeholders. For example, in healthcare, multi-hazard early warning systems monitor heat, water-borne and vector-borne diseases in changing ecological scenarios that affect agriculture, health and cities (AASSA, 2021; EASAC, 2019; IAP, 2023; NASAC et al., 2022; WHO, 2018). However, the adoption of warning systems varies across regions and hazards, with only half of global countries accessing early warnings. Regions such as Africa and small island developing states especially need support (NASAC et al., 2022; UNDRR & WMO, 2022). A recent initiative from the United Nations aims for “early warning for all” by 2027, highlighting the commitment to regional and social equality (UNDRR, 2022). Multi-hazards early warning systems are also integral to the Sendai Framework of Disaster Risk Reduction (2015-2030) (UNISDR, 2015).

The incorporation of ILK with multi-hazards early warning systems and risk communication could further increase their effectiveness through enhanced participation of IPLC (Fakhruddin et al., 2021; ILO, 2017; UNISDR, 2015) (Figure 5.5.4). The ongoing efforts to mainstream traditional knowledge of risk communication are irregular and tend to vary across countries and regions (Commonwealth of Australia, 2021; Ministry for the Environment, 2022; Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change., 2022; NACCC, 2007; NAPCC, 2008).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Multi-hazard early warning systems (C09)" .

