        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZKZEUDXC> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Natural capital accounting is designed as an economic and financial approach to account for the value of natural assets, aiming to shift current economic and market dynamics towards sustainable nature use by identifying, valuing and possibly pricing natural assets. It introduces financial incentives or disincentives for the activities of economic actors, thus addressing interactions between nexus elements in an economy (Kelemen et al., 2022). As an emerging framework at national and regional levels, natural capital accounting seeks to integrate nature’s values into public and business accounts, potentially laying the groundwork for new fiscal systems and biodiversity payments (Dasgupta, 2021; European Commission. Statistical Office of the European Union., 2020; IPBES, 2022a; Kelemen et al., 2022; United Nations Environment Programme, 2022a) (see also Chapter 6, section 6.2.6.1).

Natural capital accounting measures the resources consumed by economic entities and their impacts on ecosystem services in either or both physical and monetary terms. This measurement aims to incorporate benefits of nature historically excluded from markets and treated as externalities, thereby informing economic decision-making (Bagstad et al., 2021; United Nations et al., 2017). However, integrating natural capital accounting into actionable steps presents challenges, including the balance between the system's complexity and its usability for decision-makers. Often, national policies simplify these accounts to biophysical measures, e.g., areas or tons, and thus neglect sociocultural values (United Nations Statistics Division, 2023).

Natural capital accounting enriches traditional accounting frameworks by incorporating natural capital data, enabling the analysis of trade-offs among natural, built and human capital within standard economic indicators such as gross domestic product for informed decision-making (La Notte & Rhodes, 2020; Vallecillo et al., 2019). The System of Environmental Economic Accounting framework, developed by the United Nations, represents the most advanced natural capital information system, expanding national accounts to include ecosystem accounting (Figure 5.1.9) (United Nations et al., 2021a). This accounting framework facilitates the establishment of environmental goals, policy design, progress monitoring and evaluation, covering sustainability issues from biodiversity to climate change, and is recognized in international commitments such as the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the SDGs (L. Hein, Bagstad, et al., 2020).

Natural capital accounting addresses sustainable development and sustainable use of natural resources in general by enabling the accounting of the non-market benefits of nature, but it is not specifically linked to one particular nexus element. Rather, a broad variety of topics that involve nexus elements and interactions can be addressed using the natural capital accounting concept, such as land use planning, green economic growth or designing policy measures (taxes, regulations) (L. Hein, Bagstad, et al., 2020; Ruijs et al., 2019). In developing nations, the focus is mainly on energy and water accounts due to data challenges, while developed countries prioritize accounts on material flows, environmental taxes and subsidies, especially concerning energy. This contrast reflects differing environmental priorities, implementation rates and the focus of account compilation, as well as disparities in funding and access to technical assistance (Pirmana et al., 2019).

The effectiveness of natural capital accounting in shaping real-world economic policies requires further refinement and closer integration with economic models (Bagstad et al., 2021; Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, 2020). Additionally, criticisms of the System of Environmental Economic Accounting framework for its top-down approach and exclusion of embracing traditional and biocentric knowledge underscores the need for methodological enhancements. Adopting an integrated valuation of ecosystem services as a methodological enhancement could more accurately reflect previously excluded knowledge and overlooked sociocultural values and IPLC perspectives (Gómez-Baggethun et al., 2014; Pascual et al., 2017).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Natural capital accounting (B13)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3.14> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/35DTX6BS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4PZZ2VJI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5V6S2442>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7ZPFG3EX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8LQLPRGM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8LY4VI47>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AS5555UD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C39FWMSY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C8ESCDSD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/E3N2DBCW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F45PRYVF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FLJ95366>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LJ3BIUCF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LZCDYHD9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RUN6SPD3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/T5QPCDSM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TR8PBPH9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U69F436I>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U7H5GYEQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U7JDLFDV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UCU976PR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UNJ8Q8S7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z6N2JEB2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZNR4IM8G> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Reconnecting people with nature encompasses the idea of restoring or fostering a deep connection between humans and natural environments. In current societies, many people have become increasingly disconnected from nature due to urbanization, technology and lifestyles (Soga & Gaston, 2016). This disconnection has negative impacts on mental and physical health, particularly among people in Western societies and cities, and on environmental awareness and conservation efforts (Barragan-Jason et al., 2023).

Efforts to reconnect people with nature include outdoor education programs, physical activity in nature, green space management or gardening in urban areas, nature-based spiritual practices or therapies, sustainable living practices, nature-based arts and crafts, and environmental advocacy (Coventry et al., 2021; Ives et al., 2018) (Table 5.1.8). These efforts can be supported by government agencies developing green spaces in cities, environmental NGOs facilitating nature-based experience, healthcare providers promoting nature-based therapies, or educational institutions integrating outdoor learning into curricula (Barragan-Jason et al., 2022).

Most activities to reconnect people with nature contribute to diverse types of connections and have multiple effects on the nexus elements (Figure 5.1.9). For example, community gardens reconnect people with nature through material (e.g., food) and experiential connections (sensory) and contribute to cognitive (knowledge, inspiration) or emotional connections (feelings, appreciation) (Rigolon et al., 2021). Among the various types of knowledge that can be mobilized when reconnecting with nature, ILK is crucial because it offers cultural perspectives and practices that can foster a deeper understanding and appreciation of the natural world (Burgos-Ayala et al., 2020) (Box 5.1.6).

As evidenced from 1990 case studies synthesized in 23 reviews and meta-analyses, reconnecting people with nature yields numerous benefits for the nexus elements: direct benefits on health, food and biodiversity, as well as indirect benefits for water and climate change. However, scientific evidence is often geographically and socially biased towards Western societies and urban populations. While physical and mental health, cognitive performance and pro-environmental behaviours garner substantial attention, social and spiritual benefits remain underexplored, despite their significant potential implications (Keniger et al., 2013).

Reconnecting people with nature has well-established positive impacts on physical and mental health (Barragan-Jason et al., 2022). Physical connections improve health by boosting activity levels and providing additional benefits such as lowered blood pressure, cortisol reduction, enhanced immune function and faster recovery from illness or surgery (Twohig-Bennett & Jones, 2018). Mental health benefits derive from both improved physical health (Ohrnberger et al., 2017) and direct exposure to nature. For example, activities such as walking or running in natural settings can alleviate anxiety, anger or sadness (Bowler et al., 2010), while natural sounds such as water or bird songs can reduce stress and enhance positive moods (Buxton et al., 2021). Effective nature-based interventions such as gardening, green exercise and nature therapy further improve mood and reduce anxiety (Coventry et al., 2021). However, connections with nature can also have negative physical impacts (e.g., animal attacks) (Soga & Gaston, 2022), as well as psychological impacts (e.g., eco-anxiety, Coffey et al., 2021).

The need to reconnect people with nature incentivizes nature protection and habitat restoration, with positive impacts on biodiversity, water and climate change. For example, urban green spaces designed for enhancing well-being can also benefit urban biodiversity, water regulation, carbon sequestration and climate change adaptation (Barragan-Jason et al., 2022). However, these benefits can have adverse effects, including the overuse of water or pesticides in community gardens (Raneng et al., 2023), the carbon footprint of travelling to natural areas and degradation due to overcrowding in protected areas (Birendra, 2022). Reconnecting people with nature can also directly benefit food production and access to healthy food. Community gardens enhance access to fresh produce and promote values of food sharing and cooking (M. T. Garcia et al., 2018). Nature-based therapies may involve preparing food from natural products (Rigolon et al., 2021), whereas hikes in natural areas offer opportunities for foraging wild foods (Schulp et al., 2014).

Reconnecting people with nature influences attitudes and behaviours, leading to pro-environmental actions that indirectly benefit biodiversity, water resources and climate change (L. B. Miller et al., 2022). Individuals feeling connected to nature are more inclined to use climate-friendly transportation, renewable energies and eco-friendly products, to care for animals, to support local biodiversity protection initiatives, or to volunteer for conservation (Mackay & Schmitt, 2019). Furthermore, the physical and mental health benefits derived from connecting with nature can enhance people's capacity to adapt to climate change (Wamsler & Brink, 2018).

Reconnecting people with nature is considered economically efficient, as it leads to health improvements that can reduce the high economic and social costs associated with physical and mental health problems (Coventry et al., 2021; Kotera et al., 2022). Strengthening physical and psychological connections with nature can be accomplished at a moderate cost through targeted interventions, such as developing urban greenspaces, greening schoolyards or facilitating access to nature (e.g., transportation, information, education) and to nature-based mindfulness activities (Barragan-Jason et al., 2022).

It can also reduce environmental injustice by improving equitable access to green spaces and natural areas for all communities (Rigolon et al., 2021). Such initiatives must address the widespread inequities in physical access to nature, especially in cities, which stem from spatially-skewed distribution of green spaces among socioeconomic groups, transportation costs and lack of resources (Y. Sun et al., 2022). Additionally, environmental education to promote psychological connections to nature can reduce existing inequities in childhood experiences or education about nature.

Reconnecting people with nature is considered a leverage for transformation towards sustainability, particularly through psychological connections, which are more likely to provide systemic and deep societal changes than material or experiential connections (Ives et al., 2018). However, there are major barriers to overcome such as existing land-use configurations, including urbanization, which has led to the loss of natural experiences and increased distance from green areas. Other major barriers are modern lifestyles dominated by technology and screen time, which reduce motivation and opportunities for engaging with the natural world (L. R. Larson et al., 2019).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reconnecting people with nature (B14)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.15> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5WDQ66RX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6QAYKKGF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6UYJUWLG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/79UNJPCS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/85D6BZR9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AMDXTL5Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AS7B9TH7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EPZCX5CM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EWLFYXKV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GJLMFZ23>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HTKP8L4Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KYKTNY6Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LIVNUXRA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LT473QSY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MZ4CTFCD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N62K35EF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RU322ADH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VE42ULHY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VYU2KSEL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WU2PX3XB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WUW37KYM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YLX39UUF> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Community water management is based on the premise that water challenges at the local level can be overcome if communities collectively develop their own behavioural choices and build social capital regarding the beneficial effects of collective action (Syabri et al., 2014). Placing communities at the heart of water management solutions, community water management recognizes the potential for local solutions to emerge from human networks, social interactions and local knowledge (Dean et al., 2016), which also result in benefits for biodiversity (Figure 5.2.3). Collective action to manage water commons may also include setting contextualized incentive-based rules, thus providing flexibility in contrast with a common and control-based approach setting specific parameters such as irrigation technology mandates (Allen & Smith, 2023). These actions, especially in rural settings, are relevant globally but implemented locally (Abeywardana et al., 2018; Krishan, 2011; Ostrom, 1990; Rodriguez De San Miguel et al., 2015).

Nexus approaches can support community water management in various ways (Boxes 5.2.8 and 5.2.9). For example, modern small-scale decentralized hydro-power based energy solutions in select low income countries have the potential for co-benefits in food security, income, health, the empowerment of women and resource conservation when synergies between these solutions and elements of the nexus are considered in programme development and implementation (Guta et al., 2017). Nexus approaches have also been used to provide insights into the linkages between sustainable energy projects and the food and water sectors to avoid trade-offs and enhance development outcomes and impacts of energy projects (Karan et al., 2018; Perrone et al., 2011; Terrapon-Pfaff et al., 2018).

Since the 1970s, water user associations have emerged to improve coverage and efficiency in low-performing state-managed irrigation systems as well as to improve small-scale community-based and farmer-led irrigation systems (Mollinga et al., 2007; Playán et al., 2018), and rural drinking water (Bisung et al., 2014; Hasan et al., 2020; Madrigal-Ballestero et al., 2013) with varying success. Through visioning and prioritization, knowledge-sharing and story-telling, the capacity of communities to act as valued environmental stewards increased (Reeves & Bonney, 2023). Similarly, collective action can be targeted at adaptation to water scarcity, including those triggered by climate change (Murtinho, 2016), yet demonstration of the effectiveness is limited. Community-based water stewardship around locally-led adaptation, when supported with inclusion, diverse and democratized knowledge and equity and justice could provide an important opportunity for greater climate-resilient development (Rahman et al., 2022).

Community-led initiatives are considered technically feasible and socially acceptable (Figures 5.2.5 and 5.2.6). They already contribute at a local scale to several SDGs, and even to synergies between different goals (Henfrey et al., 2023). In several cases, these initiatives have pre-empted many aspirations of the 2030 Agenda to integrate diverse environmental and social goals (Henfrey, 2018).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Community water management (W15)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
