        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XAUYI45J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YJ38QDG5> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The idea that biodiversity (or nature) is the foundation for the human economy and society is a core principle of the sustainable development paradigm and is well established in the scientific literature on ‘strong’ sustainability (Godin et al., 2022; Wu, 2013). The ‘ecosystem approach’ as defined under the Convention for Biological Diversity (CBD, 2000) established early recognition of this, as a “strategy for the integrated management of land, water and living resources that promotes conservation and sustainable use in an equitable way”. As understanding of the value and breadth of ecosystem services (Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005) and nature’s contributions to people (Díaz et al., 2018; IPBES, 2019a) has widened, so has incorporation of the concept into multiple policy frameworks.

Awareness of the multiple ways in which natural systems can support climate change mitigation and adaptation (Bayulken et al., 2021; Riisager-Simonsen et al., 2022; N.

Seddon et al., 2021) has increased rapidly under varied labels such as ‘natural climate solutions’ (Portner et al. 2021). The term ‘nature-based solutions’ (nature-based solutions) has recently come to be used as a catch-all terminology for these actions, triggering effort to identify standards determining what may count as a nature-based solutions, notably by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Global Standard for nature-based solutions (Box 5.1.1) (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019; IUCN, 2020a, 2020b), as well as a resolution adopted by the United Nations Environment Assembly (UNEP/EA.5/Res.5, 2022) (Figure 5.1.1). The UNEA definition of nature-based solutions was adopted as: “Nature-based solutions are actions to protect, conserve, restore, sustainably use and manage natural or modified terrestrial, freshwater, coastal and marine ecosystems which address social, economic and environmental challenges effectively and adaptively, while simultaneously providing human well-being, ecosystem services, resilience and biodiversity benefits” (UNEP/EA.5/Res.5, 2022). By this definition, nature-based solutions may address multiple challenges and in myriad contexts, but importantly they are (i) framed in relation to “social, economic and environmental challenges” or the full breadth of the sustainable development paradigm, and (ii) simultaneously provide for “human well-being, ecosystem services, resilience and biodiversity benefits” i.e., cannot be associated with declines in any of these four domains.

Nature-based solutions thus range across many levels of intervention within ecosystems, and in line with the nexus approach are based on a systemic, multi-goal approach to their contributions to resolving threats and impacts on nature and people (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019; Eggermont et al., 2015; N. Seddon et al., 2021). nature-based solutions rely on biodiversity and natural processes integrated within broader ecosystems (including populated ones, such as rural landscapes and cities (IPBES 2019)) rather than isolated, simplified or engineered systems with narrowly-focused outcomes, limited functions and resilience (Eggermont et al., 2015). They bring together established ecosystem-based approaches, such as ‘ecosystem services’, ‘green-blue infrastructure’, ‘ecological engineering’, ‘ecosystem-based management’, ‘natural capital’ and ‘ecosystem-based adaptation to climate change’.

Initiatives that are detrimental to biodiversity, such as narrowly designed climate change mitigation interventions, do not qualify as nature-based solutions according to the UNEA definition (N. Seddon, 2022; Smith et al., 2022). A range of interventions previously labelled as nature-based solutions do not meet UNEA or IUCN criteria, as a positive benefit in one area (e.g., carbon sequestration from monoculture planting of fast-growing exotic trees) directly results in significant harm in other areas (e.g., native biodiversity, ecological and social resilience and provisioning of multiple nature’s contributions to people to adjacent residents). Many such actions are driven by financial or other interests disconnected from the location of implementation of the action, thus with low accountability for impacts that violate multiple values, equity or human rights, and thus should not be considered nature-based solutions (N. Seddon, 2022). Furthermore, nature-based solutions or other actions for offsetting and adapting to climate change or biological invasions, for example, do not lessen the priority of addressing the primary drivers of biodiversity loss (Pörtner et al., 2021; N. Seddon et al., 2020). In response to concerns about nature-based solutions raised by countries and civil society, in particular from the Global South, issues of transparency and inclusion of multiple actors within nature-based solutions require addressing (Melanidis & Hagerman, 2022; Mercado et al., 2024; Scolobig et al., 2023).

There is growing evidence that implementation of nature-based solutions following quality standards (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019) “is in harmony with the concept of ecosystem-based approaches” (UNEA, 2022), and could increasingly become incorporated into multilateral frameworks meeting multiple linked objectives. As such, nature-based solutions provide an umbrella for a wide range of response options that have the potential to address nexus challenges. Through their explicit focus on the full spectrum from natural and semi-natural ecosystems to working lands and cities, and on multiple societal challenges, nature-based solutions complement classic biodiversity and area-based approaches towards conservation and sustainable utilization of natural resources, and indeed some such actions (e.g., locally managed areas) may be considered as nature-based solutions for sustaining wild-caught food, pollination and other nature’s contributions to people (see section 5.1.3).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Biodiversity as a foundation for other nexus elements" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3.11> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
