    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The management of aquatic invasive alien species (invasive alien species) to conserve freshwater biodiversity requires an understanding of the ecology of invasive alien species populations and their interactions within and across trophic levels of fishes (Conrad et al., 2016). Invasive alien species cover a wide range of taxonomic groups (macrophytes, phytoplankton, zooplankton, benthic invertebrates and fish) across different trophic guilds (primary producers, filter collectors, omnivores and predators) and habitats (rivers, lakes, and estuaries), with strong and pervasive negative impacts on native communities and aquatic habitat functioning (Gallardo et al., 2016). Economic wealth affects the risk of invasive alien species because increases in economic growth may encourage sectors to benefit from alien species (IPBES, 2023b). For example, invasive Blue Catfish in the Chesapeake Bay in the eastern United States of America create recreational fisheries and economic benefits but also are detrimental to native fish populations (Paukert et al., 2021). The main invasion vectors differ between high-income countries (imports of pets and plants) and low-income countries (shipping of goods and people), with the latter tending to have more reactive national policies. Countries with high incidence risk for invasive alien species tend to have proactive capacities, yet there are only a few examples of strong policies even in those countries with high incidence (Early et al., 2016) (Box 5.2.5). Furthermore, species interactions with native species might hinder the ‘ideal’ timing and success for this response option (IPBES, 2023b; Lampert et al., 2014; Santos et al., 2009).

The literature review focused on reducing pathways for aquatic invasive alien species as these are one of the top five priorities for conserving freshwater biodiversity (Dudgeon et al., 2006; Gallardo et al., 2016) and addressing biodiversity loss (IPBES, 2019). The geographic scope of aquatic invasive alien species is well established as global (Cuthbert et al., 2021; Early et al., 2016; Gallardo et al., 2016; Pyšek et al., 2020), but with interventions at local levels (Figure 5.2.4) along the invasion pathways from source to establishment and persistence (Kühn & Klotz, 2007). These interventions are well established with mostly positive outcomes that can help ‘bend the curve’ on freshwater biodiversity loss (Tickner et al., 2020). The temporal scale is variable (Figure 5.2.5) and depends on the specific aquatic invasive alien species (e.g., Masson et al., 2016). Most studies suggest that invasive alien species have variable impacts on water, such as changing turbidity, which affects water quality, or increasing water filtration and purity (Pejchar & Mooney, 2009). Managing aquatic invasive alien species could result in better water saving outcomes, but this has not been quantified.

Managing invasive alien species often focuses on removals to control the species/taxa but can also include prevention, but both measures have mixed success (IPBES, 2023a). Prevention of invasive species is more cost effective than actual removals or control once a species is present (IPBES, 2023b). However, prevention is complex as some aquatic invasive alien species contribute to food security by providing foods to local communities and economic benefit (Figure 5.2.3) (Lee & Gordon, 2006; Paukert et al., 2021). Some invasive alien species also outcompete native species which could also provide food security (Britton, 2023). Aquatic invasive alien species can have co-benefits for climate change adaptation and may be carbon sinks or sources (Pejchar & Mooney, 2009). However, knowledge is still inconclusive because of the variety of aquatic invasive alien species that need to be assessed and the range of conditions where they occur. There are also health benefits, such as invasive alien species contribution to disease regulation and recreational activities (Cambray, 2003), but also health disbenefits, such as invasive alien species acting as new vectors of disease (Pejchar & Mooney, 2009).

Management of aquatic invasive alien species is contested. Although they provide services and livelihoods (e.g., food, materials, tourism, etc.) that have economic benefits (Larson et al., 2011), those benefits typically do not mitigate their negative impacts, given that the total cost of alien species effects on humans is estimated to be over $423 billion (IPBES, 2023a). Acceptance of invasive alien species management is variable and depends on the local cost and benefits, which are often unresolved (Hanley & Roberts, 2019). In addition, many smallholders, fishers and communities already manage aquatic invasive alien species (Reo & Ogden, 2018) using traditional knowledge and experience (Magellan, 2019).

Overall, the management of aquatic invasive alien species is well established in many political priorities (Figure 5.2.4) and can be technically feasible (Figure 5.2.5), but feasibility depends on context (IPBES, 2023a). In connected systems such as rivers, eradication can be ineffective (IPBES, 2023a). However, managing aquatic alien species aligns with global goals for climate smart food production, biodiversity and food and water security (Hussner et al., 2017). The implementation of management interventions for aquatic invasive alien species is linked to many global frameworks (e.g., target 7 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework; UN Food System Pathways 2;

SDG15) and has medium transformative change potential with the right economic investment (Dasgupta, 2021), but evidence is incomplete.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage alien species (W11)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3.14> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
