        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AC48SJKN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B9MWGBLA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CRJF83IB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EBWQGIE2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EZWA5RJN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/FGGZNS9Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HINIUZ37>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LDGNHK24>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NDYV49ZY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PJNMSJ99>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UEH5MWEE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UX4RBRHR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VNSJ4IJD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XXQ6WX7A>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y2E2RV6A>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z8UFRSA2> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Women play key roles in food systems (FAO, 2020b) ranging from producing, processing, distributing, marketing and consuming (Funge-Smith & Bennett, 2019). Gender-based discrimination and human rights violations are major causes of poverty and food and nutrition insecurity (N. J. Bennett et al., 2021; FAO, 2023c). Gender often interacts with other forms of discrimination, such as socioeconomic class, age, ethnicity and disability, requiring intersectional analyses and policy responses (Motta, 2021) (see Chapter 4, Box 4.12).

Responses to address this situation include strengthening women’s access to and control of assets, notably land; better inclusion in value chains, including better access to markets and improving labour conditions and legislation (Ros-Tonen et al., 2019) and greater women’s (economic) empowerment (Njuki et al., 2023; Bryan et al., 2023) (Figure 5.3.14). However, relatively few studies assess the effectiveness of these responses and even fewer address gender equity in urban or aquatic food contexts.

Reviews show mixed impacts on nexus elements of increased tenure security for women (Figure 5.3.4). Studies in Uganda and Rwanda demonstrate positive impacts on soil conservation, notably among female-headed households (Ali et al., 2014; Deininger & Ali, 2008). Meinzen-Dick et al. (2019) found weaker evidence of impacts on natural resource management, food security, technology adoption or productivity but strong impacts on bargaining power and decision-making on consumption and (human) capital investment. These mixed findings may relate to how tenure is institutionalized and experienced (Winters et al., 2018), highlighting the need to distinguish legal security and contextual or de facto security (Asaaga et al., 2020; Ghebru, 2019). This option is more effective when bundled with other response options, such as access to credit, information and government services for women (Meinzen-Dick et al., 2019).

Women face many hidden costs related to market access and integration, which are systematically overlooked by public and private sector actors and institutions (Pouw et al., 2020; Noack & Pouw, 2015). Such costs are multi-dimensional (economic, social and/or political), creating obstacles for marginalized food entrepreneurs to professionalize and upscale their businesses. They influence risk perceptions, time and resource allocation, and business investment decisions. Innovations supporting inclusive business are only effective when applicable to specific problems and contexts, affordable and accessible to all. Better market access and integration does not automatically improve nutrition security. A review of thirteen projects in Africa and Asia, aimed at improving market access and integration, shows that this is due to a focus on cash crop production and prioritizing business investments and other costs such as health care over buying high quality food (Pouw et al., 2020).

Economic inclusion programming – including social safety nets, livelihoods, job and financial inclusion programs – strengthens women’s economic opportunities, income, and asset ownership and supports shifts in gender norms, such as increased mobility (Andrews et al., 2021). Cash or in-kind transfers combined with high-quality complementary programming, such as agricultural extension produce significant improvements in agriculture and nutrition outcomes (PIM, 2021).

Generally, efforts to improve gender equity often adopt a narrow focus on women, overlooking men or gender relations (Lawless et al., 2021). This risks putting the blame, or burden for change, on women (Hicks et al., 2022; Bank et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Foster gender-transformative approaches (F14)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2GRFEP3P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3J5TV4WY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5VMSAYMH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5XZ49SGP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/68MWHQST>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/88PWAV2T>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AC48SJKN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BCPLU5XE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DVZSI26D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EKWV3G26>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GA84YZLN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/K2MWB44Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KK2IUR3W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KMZRGLSG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MFAHFIYS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MG6ZTBYX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MQ9M5BAI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TKTIARG7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TNUSNDW4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W9W6CNUJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WU4I5QFC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z9N9CD4G>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZCABALIT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZLLSP2YV> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Ecological Intensification of aquatic foods focuses on enhancing the ecological processes within aquatic ecosystems, such as nutrient cycling, habitat structure maintenance, and trophic interactions, to increase the productivity and resilience of freshwater and marine food sources. Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems is a response option that can be applied across much of the world’s marine and freshwater systems (Figure 5.3.5), with consistently positive impacts on other nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). Benefits are greatest when combined with halting the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity, both aquatic and terrestrial (section 5.3.3.1), and reducing demand through reduced food loss and waste (section 5.3.3.10) and shifting to sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11). It involves a range of approaches in both wild caught and aquaculture systems (Table 5.3.7).

Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems could have significant positive impacts on food production, access to food and the environmental performance of food. It could increase aquatic food production by 36-74 per cent (~21-44 million tonnes) by 2050 (Costello et al., 2020; Naylor et al., 2021; Springmann et al., 2018), potentially benefiting more than 800 million people who depend on aquatic ecosystems (FAO, 2020b). This is particularly important as demand for aquatic foods is projected to double by 2050 (Naylor et al., 2021).

Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems includes strategies such as coral reef restoration (Mellin et al., 2022), mangrove restoration (Sievers et al., 2023), seagrass restoration, and wetlands and riparian zone restoration. These provide the triple benefit of biodiversity protection, fisheries productivity and securing marine carbon stocks (Sala et al., 2021). The restoration and avoided conversion of coral reefs, mangroves, seagrass beds, wetlands and riparian zones could deliver 0.3–3.1 GtCO2e yr⁻¹ in total (IPCC, 2019). Mangrove forests alone store ~10.8 billion tons of carbon (Ahmed & Thompson, 2019; Kauffman et al., 2017), which can be secured through ecological intensification of food production alongside halting conversion of these ecosystems (section 5.3.3.1). Approximately 650 million hectares of ocean could support seaweed production. If used for ruminant feed, this could mitigate approximately 2.6 GtCO2e yr−1 of greenhouse gas emissions (Spillias et al., 2023). As a substitute in the human diet, global seaweed production could spare up to 110 million hectares of land (Spillias et al., 2023). Collectively, kelp forests sequester 4.91 megatons of carbon from the atmosphere per year (Eger et al., 2023).

Approximately 52 per cent (~1.9 million ha) of deforested mangroves can be attributed to coastal aquaculture, with shrimp farming accounting for over 70 per cent of the loss (Ahmed & Thompson, 2019). In some cases, restoration could displace food production and damage local food supply (IPCC, 2019), pushing some actors to adopt more efficient but potentially harmful practices (Free et al., 2022). The potential economic gains from some conservation strategies might be optimistic and do not necessarily trickle down to benefit the poor and most vulnerable groups (Cohen et al., 2019) (Figure 5.3.4).

Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems through sustainable fisheries management practices, such as quotas, limiting bycatch, using low-impact gear and promoting offshore fisheries, can result in improved health, climate, biodiversity and food security outcomes (Costello et al., 2020; Free et al., 2022). Implementing these measures can positively affect biodiversity by enhancing conservation efforts and increasing the productivity of aquatic ecosystems (BFA, 2021; IPCC, 2019; Sala et al., 2021).

By addressing micronutrient deficiencies, ecological intensification of aquatic food systems could play a significant role in preventing several non-communicable diseases (Golden et al., 2021; Hicks et al., 2019). Approximately 2.3 billion people have diets lacking at least one micronutrient, which accounts for over 1 million premature deaths annually (Global Nutrition Report: The State of Global Nutrition, 2021). Strategies to reduce pollution from marine litter (Gallo et al., 2020), microplastics (Zhang et al., 2020), pesticides, fertilizers and heavy metals in aquatic and freshwater systems can reduce human health risks.

The intensification of aquatic food production calls for holistic policymaking and effective governance systems (Golden et al., 2021). This typically involves integrating various considerations for aquatic foods across policy systems and engaging a diverse set of governance actors (Crona et al., 2023) and strengthening collective action (Tigchelaar et al., 2022) (Table 5.3.8). This approach could be transformative, as it necessitates fundamental changes to many of the institutional structures that currently govern food systems and sustainability (Figure 5.3.4).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological intensification – aquatic foods (F06)" .

