    ipbes:hasDescription """Ecosystem-based adaptation is increasingly incorporated into national and international biodiversity and climate policies to address climate change challenges and achieve the SDGs (IUCN, 2020b; Pörtner et al., 2021; N. Seddon, 2022; United Nations Environment Programme, 2022b, 2022a). Ecosystem-based climate change adaptation uses biodiversity and ecosystem services to reduce people’s vulnerability to climate change by facilitating adaption and building resilience to its adverse effects (Chausson et al., 2020; Cohen-Shacham et al., 2016; Colloff et al., 2020; Lavorel et al., 2015; Woroniecki et al., 2023). In rural landscapes the conservation, management and restoration of natural or biodiversity rich semi-natural ecosystems for climate change adaptation delivers multiple benefits to biodiversity and other nexus elements (Key et al., 2022). The management and restoration of forests, wetlands and coastal vegetation provides ecological disaster risk regulation, improves water quality, secures resilient food production, wood for energy and construction and can mitigate climate change. In some cases of forest restoration, it can however reduce food and water quantity (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2016; Pörtner et al., 2021; WWF, 2021). The conservation, restoration and extensive, often traditional, management of high biodiversity, semi-natural grasslands supports adaptation by delivering climate-resilient livestock production, wild foods and medicines, sequestering soil carbon and contributing multiple social and cultural benefits (Bardgett et al., 2021; Lavorel et al., 2015).

While implementation of ecosystem-based adaptation is often led by local public institutions (e.g., municipalities or local governments), multiple models of governance often coexist within individual regions. These have varying levels of co-design and participation, and inclusion of multiple knowledge systems and world views, especially ILK so that Indigenous and traditional values and knowledge are represented (Dubo et al., 2023; Pascual et al., 2022). In Japan, wetland restoration for geese populations and flood risk management was led by a conservation NGO with government support, which has now scaled out to multiple sites across the region by proving benefits to farmers initially reticent regarding yield loss (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2016). In Germany, governance of nature-based flood management and mitigation spans four governance models: top-down government, government-led co-design, public and citizen cooperation and initiatives, and citizen-led governance (Zingraff-Hamed et al., 2021). Although fewer reported cases involve rights-based instruments and customary norms or social and cultural options, consideration of multiple values and world views alongside knowledge systems (including ILK) are essential to nature-based transformative adaptation of rural landscapes (Colloff et al., 2020; Fedele et al., 2020). Transformative adaptation is defined as systemic changes to societal paradigms, visions, goals, rules and knowledge in response to actual or anticipated changes in driver variables that fundamentally change the properties of a social-ecological system (IPBES, 2019b). Three critical mechanisms for nature-based adaptation and transformation in rural landscapes directly relate to empowerment and multi-stakeholder co-production: (i) traditional management of multifunctionality, especially at the landscape level; (ii) collective management and governance; and (iii) construction of values and uses for nature’s contributions to adaptation (Donatti et al., 2020; N. A. Kirk & Cradock-Henry, 2022; Lavorel et al., 2020; Werners et al., 2021). Scaling ecosystem-based adaptation is a pressing challenge, requiring the amplification of local successful projects to multiple locations (scaling out) and scaling up to required changes in knowledge, governance and their interactions (Colloff et al., 2021; Dubo et al., 2023; United Nations Environment Programme, 2022b).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes (B04)" .

