    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """In many ways, because all people must eat to survive and they make choices on what to eat when, all people everywhere are key actors. Too many people however are challenged in making food choices based on impacts across the nexus with over half of the world’s population struggling in one form or another to access healthy diets. Food producers are particularly important food system actors, though the number of individuals engaged in food production typically becomes smaller as countries develop economically. National actors are particularly important, developing policies, incentives and rules that determine what foods are produced, how, and whom amongst producer groups are rewarded. There is a significant private sector role in food, which spans food production, including inputs and technologies for food production, harvesting, and processing, through to retail and restaurants, including small scale food vendors. A diverse portfolio of food system actors is presented in Table 5.3.1 and are further described in this section.

Government actors and policymakers: This includes officials operating at scales from communities to countries to globally. Beyond agricultural or food safety policies, public health, environmental and financial decision-making play critical roles in the availability, affordability, quality and environmental impact of food. However, food policy is rarely coordinated across ministries or departments, which can result in policies being fragmented or siloed, and can drive antagonisms and incoherencies between policies (European Commission. Directorate General for Research and Innovation., 2022; Phulkerd et al., 2022; Sonnino et al., 2019).

Companies and corporations: This includes a diversity of actors along the food value chain, including medium and large corporations as suppliers of agricultural technologies (e.g., seed, fertilizers, plant protection, mechanical tools, fuel and technologies), food processors, beverages industry and retailers. There is a high level of concentration among corporate actors, notably in globalized and industrial food systems where they exert power over other actors (Clapp, 2018, 2021), including producers, consumers and even policymakers through lobbying activities.

Financial institutions and insurance: National and transnational insurance companies, international financial institutions and national banks, private banks and equity funds, and corporations and civil society organizations providing micro-credits, such as One Acre Fund, play important roles. States also play an important role in connecting private capital to agriculture (Martin & Clapp, 2015). The increased financialization of agriculture is related to the increased globalization of agriculture and value chains, and within globalized value chains, the need for capitalization and risk management (Anseeuw et al., 2017). Insurance companies can also be considered as food systems actors by giving clear indications of risky practices and technologies (Möhring et al., 2020).

Food producers: This category includes peasants, farmers, fishers and fish farmers, ranchers and pastoralists, farm workers, gatherers, Indigenous Peoples, and other groups of individuals and communities engaged in food production for their own consumption, sharing, exchange, salary or sale. This category embraces a diversity of types of producers, linked to different production systems (e.g., large, medium or small-scale; industrial, organic or agroecological; peasant farming; industrial or artisanal fisheries; Indigenous food systems). Producers can participate in local, regional or global food chains. Their management practices may result in different enablers and barriers to the maintenance or regeneration of environmental functions. In particular, small-scale farms provide environmental, social and economic functions and services and are relevant contributors to global food security with higher levels of biodiversity than larger farms (Herrero et al., 2017; Ricciardi et al., 2021). While the largest 1 per cent farms (>50ha) operate more than 70 per cent of land, very small farms (<2ha) produce 35 per cent of the world’s food from 12 per cent of agricultural land (Lowder et al., 2021). However, there are regional differences: large farms dominate production in North and South America, and Australia and New Zealand (contributing 75-100 per cent of cereal, livestock, and fruit production). Small farms (≤20 ha) produce >75 per cent of most foods in sub-Saharan Africa, southeast Asia, south Asia and China. Very small farms are particularly important in sub-Saharan Africa, southeast Asia and south Asia, where they contribute approximately 30 per cent of most foods (Herrero et al., 2017).

Post-production: This category includes diverse and complex set of actors involved in storing, distributing, processing, packaging and preparing raw materials and/or food products from the producers. Some large companies operate in this activity as part of large corporations, and hence could also be incorporated in the companies and corporations actor category. A large percentage of post-production also takes place in the informal sphere, particularly, but not only, in lower middle-income countries but also in high income countries. Women play a particularly important role and are overrepresented in the informal sector (Njuki et al., 2023).

Consumers: Everyone on the planet – specifically when purchasing or choosing food – is an consumer whose daily choices can have a major collective impact on all nexus elements and their sustainability. This is obviously an incredibly diverse group of actors, with a vast range of interests, capacities and flexibility in their consumption habits, all influenced by a complex interaction of intrinsic and extrinsic factors. One of the unique features of healthy and sustainable food consumption is that consumer choice and individual agency have impact so long as food environments within which actors engage include affordable and available options.

Indigenous Peoples and local communities: Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) contribute to the endurance, provision, maintenance, protection and surveillance of local foods and biodiversity, and collectively contribute to positive regional and global impacts on sustainability (Brondízio et al., 2021). Greater inclusion of ILK-derived perspectives of respect for intrinsic values of nature, socionatural equilibrium and ILK for present and future generations have important potential in shifting values towards a nexus framing of food (Grey & Patel, 2015) and fostering food system transformation (G. Kennedy et al., 2021). This group is highly impacted by current global change, and particularly for land conversion for industrial use, including commercial agriculture (C. M. Kennedy et al., 2023).

Non-governmental organizations, civil society and grassroot actors: This group of actors operate at a multitude of scales, have diverse goals and aim to shape food systems through campaigning, collaborating and educating. Significant overlap may exist between this and other groups; for example, organized consumer groups or farmer collectives advocating for ethical food production.

Researchers, educators, extension services and knowledge brokers: These actors help shape companies, producers’ and consumers’ decisions through information and persuasion. This is a diverse group of actors, both publicly and privately funded, with some operating mainly at local scales, while others collaborate more internationally. There is an increasing emphasis within science but also among other actors and institutions on the need for inter- and trans-disciplinarity. The latter involves the co-creation of knowledge and the development and dissemination of technology with a range of stakeholders – sometimes including IPLC (Chambers et al., 2022; Van Ewijk & Ros-Tonen, 2021).

The global food system is complex and dynamic, and involves a diverse range of actors, with different narratives, values and interests in food, as well as different motives, power and action capacity (Jacobi et al., 2018). Their interactions create and shape a diversity of local food systems (Gaitán-Cremaschi et al., 2018; IOM (Institute of Medicine) & NRC (National Research Council), 2015). Acknowledging these interactions across the diversity of actors, objectives and interests is critical for understanding the response options available and who benefits from them, and for developing effective policymaking for the food system (SAPEA, 2020).

Different narratives on food shape critical perspectives and assumptions of actors, although the boundaries between narratives and the different categories of actors that adopt them are blurred (Table 5.3.1). Food as a commodity highlights food as a tradable good with a production focus. This narrative is linked to the industrial food system and considered to be the dominant discourse regarding the valuation of food and the current configuration of agri-food networks. Food as a human right focuses on the social dimensions of food aimed at providing access to healthy, nutritious food but also to the positive cultural values associated with food, such as identity, taste and pleasure. This framing places emphasis on access to means of production, sustainability and no human exploitation, with the state as the main guarantor of the right (SAPEA, 2020; UNCTAD, 2013). Food as a common good emphasises sustainability. Food is framed as having multiple dimensions, both social and environmental, with all being equally and properly valued and requiring different governance structures and institutions that promote food democracy and strong participation of citizen-consumers through social organizations, social movements and IPLC (Vivero-Pol et al., 2018). Food as identity and culture puts the focus on the territorial diversity in food cultures, farming styles, ecosystems, climates and soils associated with the production and consumption of food (SAPEA, 2020). Regionalization is promoted as a reaction to globalisation of food, with production re-embedded into local contexts and associated with rurality and socially-constructed, value-laden concepts of tradition, authenticity and health (Conneely & Mahon, 2015). Finally, food as human’s closest link to nature highlights the complex relationship between humans and nature and considers that the act of eating provides an ideal object to reflect and reconsider the human-nature relationship and to establish more sustainable ways of living (SAPEA, 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key actors and institutions" .

