    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Forests are critical for human, animal and ecosystem health for their role in removing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and storing it in exchange for oxygen (see also Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.12, C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change). Their loss through removal and fire is a source of direct harm to human health, including through extensive air pollution and respiratory disease (E. W. Butt et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2015; Palmer, 2022; Spracklen & Garcia-Carreras, 2015; Wolff et al., 2021). However, deforestation continues to take place at alarming rates (see also Chapter 2, sections 2.3.1, 2.4.1, 2.4.2, 2.4.4, 2.5.2.1 and 2.5.2.2). Promoting forest conservation is a response option that can be applied globally wherever forests exist (Figure 5.4.7). Benefits and impacts are from local to global scales, with 60 million people, many IPLC, living within and wholly dependent on forests (McFarlane et al., 2019).

Exposure to forests is also associated with positive health benefits in pregnancy and childhood (foetal growth, brain development, mental health in childhood, and prevention and reduction of childhood infectious diseases and malnutrition), adult life (mental and spiritual well-being and quality of life, and cardiometabolic health), and later stages of life (strong positive effects on cognitive decline and dementia, mental health and well-being, and longevity and mortality) (IUFRO, 2023). Forest conservation efforts enable these exposures and can also reduce malaria prevalence (Fornace et al., 2016; MacDonald & Mordecai, 2019) (Figure 5.4.5).

Water quality for both humans and ecosystems is improved by forest conservation, since an estimated 75 per cent of the world’s accessible freshwater comes from forested watersheds (Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005). Forest conservation provides forest-based foods and products to an estimated one billion people (Maricé & Spalding, 2022). It is well established that the benefits of avoiding and reducing deforestation and promoting restoration can be significant for biodiversity (IPBES, 2019b). It also contributes to climate change mitigation, with nine countries having reported 8.82 Gt of greenhouse gas emission reductions due to reduced rates of deforestation and forest degradation under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change framework for Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation and the role of conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks in developing countries (REDD+) as of January 2020 (IPBES, 2019b).

There is broad public support for forest preservation, and it aligns with international priorities that promote forest conservation as well as being included in many National Biodiversity Strategies and Action Plans (Figure 5.4.6). As the world has globalized, so has the space of debate on public affairs, including forest conservation, also shifted from the national to the global, prompting the emergence of a global civil society and global governance (Castells, 2008). Global public support for forest preservation is exemplified by concern for the Amazon rainforest. International pressure of public opinion led the Brazilian agribusiness sector to adopt the Soy Moratorium, a voluntary agreement that calls for large companies to refrain from buying soy originating from areas in the Amazon deforested after 2008 (IPBES, 2022b). Another example is the New York Declaration on Forests, which strives to halve deforestation by 2020 (not achieved) and end it by 2030 through global action (Katila et al., 2019). Actioning forest conservation to manage fire risk has contributed to management decisions that promote greater diversity and resilience of forests (e.g., D’Evelyn et al., 2022). Equitable access to the health benefits of forests and other conserved natural areas has been promoted specifically for health and well-being, particularly in high income countries (Box 5.4.9).

Forest conservation can be equitable when IPLC living in and around forests participate in decision-making (Figure 5.4.6). The protection of land by IPLC has positive outcomes for environmental, social and economic sustainability. Forests provide many economic benefits to people in terms of forest products (see section 5.4.3.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants) in addition to non-consumptive uses. Notably, in Africa, Asia and Latin America, the areas with lower deforestation rates are those in which Indigenous Peoples have secured rights over forest resources through community-based tenure (FAO & UNEP, 2020). Cases for Indigenous community conserved areas and territories in forests (and in other biomes) can be seen as conservation success stories, demonstrating how conservation practices that protect or restore local values and livelihoods are much more likely to be legitimized locally and actively supported over the long-term (see also Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.10, B10, Rights-based approaches). Support for traditional IPLC management of forest fire risk (e.g., Smith et al., 2021) has further potential to improve IPLC rights and equity as well as broader population health benefits.

Conserving forests is technically highly feasible (Figure 5.4.7) and can be highly transformative by enabling a shift to a broader and more inclusive world view of forests (Figure 5.4.6).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forest conservation for health (H10)" .

