        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QGLB2VTG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R2SXZIEV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RJ7UQLAU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/S2RB9A8Q>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SGFTNUPC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/V38AC724>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VS6QXS8B>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/NXS24-ch5-4933> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The adverse impacts of climate change on biodiversity, freshwater resources, food security and human health are well documented by Assessment Reports of Working Group II of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), most recently in 2022 (IPCC, 2022a). In addition to its direct impacts, climate change is also acknowledged as a ‘threat multiplier’ because it amplifies existing vulnerabilities in critical sectors, intensifying challenges such as biodiversity loss, water scarcity, acute food insecurity and health crises (IPCC, 2022a). These cascading impacts emerge from extensive human activities that significantly alter terrestrial and marine ecosystems, subsequently influencing climate variability (Ellis et al., 2021; IPCC, 2019).

Climate change both impacts and is impacted by the other elements of the nexus. For example, healthy ecosystems supporting abundant biodiversity have been shown to be more resilient to climate change (Shin et al., 2022) and to provide effective nature-based solutions for climate change mitigation (P. Smith et al., 2022). Freshwater resources are threatened by drought and excessive precipitation, with such extreme events becoming more prevalent under climate change (IPCC, 2022a), while perturbations to the water cycle have impacts on climate (IPCC, 2021). Similarly, food security is threatened by climatic change, including extreme weather events (IPCC, 2022a), while the global food system is responsible for one third of anthropogenic greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions (Crippa et al., 2021) and hence has large opportunities for climate change mitigation (Babiker et al., 2022). The healthcare sector in responsible for 5 per cent of global GHG emissions (Watts et al., 2021).

Response options to tackle climate change fall into two categories: adaptation and mitigation. According to the IPCC definition, adaptation is “the process of adjustment to actual or expected climate and its effects. In human systems, adaptation seeks to moderate or avoid harm or exploit beneficial opportunities. In some natural systems, human intervention may facilitate adjustment to expected climate and its effects” (IPCC, 2021). The IPCC definition of mitigation is: “A human intervention to reduce the sources or enhance the sinks of GHGs” (IPCC, 2021). “Climate action” comprises climate change adaptation and mitigation (IPCC, 2018). Response options can deliver adaptation, mitigation or both.

Global surface temperature will continue to increase unless deep reductions in fossil CO2 and other GHG emissions occur (IPCC, 2022b). Climate change urgently requires a variety of efficient mitigation measures aiming at preventing emissions from combustion of fossil fuels and deforestation, complemented with other actions aiming at reducing atmospheric concentrations of CO2 (IPCC, 2022c). This urgency calls for a rethinking of current policies and action plans, and, where appropriate, the design of new ones. This subchapter assesses such actions from the perspective of the nexus approach, demonstrating the interactions between climate change adaptation and mitigation measures and their ensuing effects on aspects of biodiversity, water, food, health and human well-being.

Drawing from Chapter 1, this chapter emphasizes that action to climate change is not a stand-alone endeavour. Instead, it requires an integrative alignment with broader ecological and social infrastructures. In this regard, sector-specific interventions often yield sub-optimal outcomes, exacerbating existing challenges like resource depletion and biodiversity loss (Aggestam et al., 2023; J. P. Newell et al., 2019; Tudose et al., 2021). Alternatively, nexus approaches augment the co-benefits of action to address climate change. For example, a transition to low-GHG energy sources mitigates climate change and improves air quality and public health (IPCC, 2023). However, potential trade-offs need to be avoided, especially those that could escalate resource competition or result in ecological degradation (Pörtner et al., 2021; P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020).

Nexus governance provides opportunities for integrated, polycentric governance (see Chapter 4, section 4.5). Within the context of addressing climate change, nexus governance aims for and enhances policy coherence, refines resource allocation and fosters collaborative synergy among multiple stakeholders (N. Dawson et al., 2018; Klein et al., 2015; Kroll et al., 2019). Considering the intrinsic interconnectivity of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change, the nexus approach provides a resilient and adaptive governance model (Behnassi & Gupta, 2022; Lindley et al., 2019). It transcends piecemeal solutions, offering a more unified, effective suite of strategies considering multi-sectoral drivers and dependencies (IPCC, 2023) and leading to the synergistic and equitable implementation of climate change adaptation and mitigation strategies, thereby maximizing positive outcomes across multiple nexus elements and contributing to both human well-being and ecological resilience.

As shown in Figure 5.5.1, this subchapter examines the key actors and institutions in climate change adaptation and mitigation (section 5.5.2), the response options for climate change adaptation and mitigation that embed a nexus approach (section 5.5.3) and the enabling conditions for the response options (section 5.5.4). This is followed by an overview of key knowledge gaps (section 5.5.5) and a chapter synthesis and conclusions (section 5.5.6).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of climate change within the nexus" .

