        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NASZCRU7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QAGLZ9FC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QTA76QPG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SWFP56VP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TIQUPJSU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VNP4BTC2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/X4ZFRRYS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZYR9N2EU> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Agroecology (see also Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3, B03, Agroecology) includes a variety of practices for diversification and more sustainable management of farmland, such as agroforestry, intercropping, crop and pasture rotation, silvopasture, organic amendments, cover crops and organic farming, among others (Bezner Kerr et al., 2023; HLPE, 2019). These options have the potential to co-deliver climate change adaptation and mitigation while promoting biodiversity conservation, livelihoods, health and equity (Aguilera et al., 2020; Bezner Kerr et al., 2022; Wezel et al., 2020). Multiple agroecology-based practices exist. For illustrative purposes, this response option primarily focuses on agroforestry, given its wide implementation potential (Table 5.5.5), and briefly refers to other practices (agricultural diversification and conservation agriculture) that are representative of different geographical contexts and nexus interactions, as discussed in the Box 5.5.1.

In agroforestry, trees and woody shrubs are integrated into agricultural land and have the potential to restore ecosystem functional integrity into intensively managed landscapes for securing critical NCP (Mohamed et al., 2022). It can be practiced in nearly all climatic zones and farming systems, but the main environmental benefits are usually in tropical and semi-arid areas (M. Chapman et al., 2020; Kuyah et al., 2019). Agroforestry has positive effects on all nexus elements, but some trade-offs can also occur (Figure 5.5.3, Table 5.5.7).

Barriers to the adoption of agroforestry are mostly connected to lack of knowledge, relatively high opportunity costs of establishment (e.g., potential for direct reduction of cultivated land), maintenance costs (e.g., the presence of trees can affect the types of equipment used for harvesting crops) and fragmented or missing actor networks (Staton et al., 2022; Zinngrebe et al., 2020). The modest establishment costs can be mitigated by considering that profits from monocrops tend to decrease over time from unsustainable soil use, while agroforestry can secure yields and diversified products (fruits, fuelwood and fodder), stabilizing incomes in the long-term (I. K. Dawson et al., 2014; Do et al., 2020; Rosenstock et al., 2019; Tschora & Cherubini, 2020). Social barriers can be overcome by building social capital among key actors and local communities (Ollinaho & Kröger, 2021; Raharilaza, 2021), so to gradually make agroforestry a mainstream practice of local farming (Zinngrebe et al., 2020). Direct involvement of gender diversity in the adoption of agroforestry can both facilitate its deployment and favour beneficial outcomes for women in rural communities (Kiptot et al., 2014). Overall, active engagement and participation of local communities, use of local tree species and legitimization of Indigenous ontologies are a prerequisite for realizing the multiple potential benefits of agroforestry systems, including equity and social justice (Ollinaho & Kröger, 2021) (Figure 5.5.4).

Agricultural diversification, such as mixed cropping or livestock systems, crop rotations, and intercropping, promotes plant variety and diversification of income in agricultural landscapes as a measure to enhance food system resilience, increase NCP supply (pollination, pest control, nutrient cycling) and reduce management inputs. An example is the Landrace maize diversity in the Indigenous polyculture milpa system in the Santa Marta Mountains, Mexico (San Vicente Tello & Jönsson, 2019). Within this system, each variety contributes with its special characteristics to support the others. The 15 variations of native maize have improved adaptation capacity to local climatic conditions, further promoting biodiversity and NCP in cropping systems.

Conservation agriculture includes practices such as reduced tillage, residue retention, cover crops and mulching. It aims to reduce the environmental impact of intensive farming and prevent losses in soil fertility, with additional benefits for soil stability by increasing protection from erosion, floods and droughts (Palm et al., 2014; Somasundaram et al., 2020; Xiao et al., 2021). It supports climate action by enhancing soil carbon storage, biodiversity and natural biological processes above and below ground, with effects on increased water and nutrient use efficiency that can secure yields (Miralles-Wilhelm, 2021). For example, cultivation of additional cover crops in winter favoured by warmer temperatures in cold regions is an option to increase soil carbon sequestration, reduce use of synthetic fertilizers (by using legume cover crops), promote climate change adaptation (by reducing soil erosion and adverse effects from extreme events) and secure additional income for farmers (if cash crops are planted) (Kaye and Quemada, 2017). The proportion of time under cover crops usually has stronger impact than crop diversity on soil microbial diversity, soil multifunctionality and yields (Garland et al., 2021). Increasing crop cover can thus enhance yields, soil functioning and habitat for soil microorganisms. No-tillage is particularly successful in arid regions, where evidence shows win-win outcomes in terms of increased soil carbon storage, crop yields (Sun et al., 2020) and improved water use efficiency (Zhong et al., 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Agroecology (C11)" .

