        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZETJR9ID> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The establishment of protected areas is one of the most widely used strategies among existing mechanisms for biodiversity conservation (Akamani, 2020; Dudley, 2008; Machlis et al., 1985; MacKinnon et al., 2020; Maxwell et al., 2020; Palomo et al., 2014). There is evidence that PAs continue to provide critical ecological and socioeconomic benefits around the world (Andam et al., 2008, 2010). Nonetheless, one key limitation of conventional conservation approaches, such as classic protected areas that rely on legal instruments and other government institutions to protect and manage defined areas primarily for biodiversity conservation to the exclusion of people (Brechin et al., 2003; Colwell et al., 2014; Terborgh, 2002), is that they may serve as a barrier to nexus approaches, whereas newer more rights-based approaches enable such interconnections (Table 5.1.1). Conceptual and governance limitations of conventional conservation that are not addressed in planning and implementation processes can prohibit more integrative and inclusive approaches (M. D. Barnes et al., 2018; J. E. M. Watson et al., 2018) (Table 5.1.2). These include assumptions that fail to recognize the complexity of social-ecological systems (Folke et al., 2016; R. Hobbs et al., 2010; R. J. Hobbs et al., 2010; Keune et al., 2022), pursuit of narrow sectoral goals without adequate consideration of trade-offs and synergies (Adger et al., 2011; Agrawal & Redford, 2009; Erinosho et al., 2022; Razzaque et al., 2019; I. J. Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022), and focus on area protected per se, rather than on effective and equitable safeguarding of the site’s important biodiversity (M. D. Barnes et al., 2018; Visconti et al., 2019). Other challenges for advancing the nexus approach include the dominance of the positivist tradition of science that relies on quantitative data and experimental approaches (Akamani, 2020; Cortner & Moote, 1999; Endter-Wada et al., 1998; I. J. Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022), and on top-down institutional mechanisms formed mostly at national and international levels that often fail to address requirements of good governance (Lockwood, 2010; Pickering et al., 2022; Smallwood et al., 2022) (see also Chapter 1, section 1.1.2).

As an alternative, a more integrative, inclusive, equitable, coordinated and adaptive nexus governance approach for biodiversity conservation is possible (Table 5.1.2, see also section 5.1.3.1, B01, Area-based conservation). Adaptive and holistic management, recognition of multiple knowledge systems (including explicit consideration of the role of Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK) and traditional institutions), and diverse institutional mechanisms that are nested across multiple scales comprise such nexus governance (see also Chapter 1, section 1.3.4; Chapter 4, sections 4.2 and 4.5). These approaches are increasingly being implemented globally, although challenges persist in some regions (Erinosho et al., 2022; Estrada et al., 2022; Razzaque et al., 2019) (sections 5.1.3, 5.1.5).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shortcomings of classic nature conservation" .

