        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZNZ69HQA> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Intercultural healthcare is an approach that aims to bridge the gap between Indigenous and Western (or biomedical) health systems, emphasizing mutual recognition and respect of different knowledges, the scope of these medical practices and the conditions that they treat (Torri, 2012). They are formal arrangements between health departments and traditional health practitioners to provide dual health services. Notable examples of biomedical health systems integrated with traditional medicine are found in India (with Ayurvedic practices) and in China (with traditional Chinese medicine) and are also found in New Zealand, Australia, Canada, Brazil, Suriname, Chile and Colombia, among other countries (Patwardhan et al., 2005).

Access to intercultural healthcare programs and services could benefit around 476.6 million Indigenous Peoples representing ∼6.2 per cent of the world’s population (Brondízio et al., 2021). It is estimated that approximately 80 per cent of the world's population, both Indigenous Peoples and other traditional and local communities, use traditional healthcare and medicinal plants (see section 5.4.3.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants). However, the best treatment for many diseases includes modern medical and surgical knowledge delivered with respect to cultural and traditional practice (WHO, 2023d). Recognition of cultural determinants of health are essential in reducing differences in health outcomes in IPLC in Australia (Australian Institute of Health and Welfare, 2022) and have been shown to improve community well-being, in Chile, Suriname and Colombia (O’Neill et al., 2007). As demonstrated in Brazil (R. S. Walker et al., 2015), recently contacted Indigenous populations are vulnerable to adverse effects of biomedical care.

Intercultural health services have a key role to play in the management of emerging risks, such as food insecurity, water insecurity and vector-borne disease (Hofmeijer et al., 2013). They significantly advance the goal of providing nutritious and culturally appropriate food, a critical component of overall well-being (Anderson et al., 2016; Diamond, 2018; Montenegro & Stephens, 2006; Popkin & Reardon, 2018). Noteworthy initiatives have been undertaken in Thailand, Canada and Colombia (Kuhnlein & Chotiboriboon, 2022; O’Neill et al., 2007) (Figures 5.4.5 and 5.4.6).

Holistic approaches to health that incorporate respect and care for nature (for example, Box 5.4.4) are features of many Indigenous health systems. These are undermined by loss of access by IPLC to their lands and resources and from the impacts of climate change and biodiversity loss. The therapeutic properties of treatments are also affected by these factors. As the productivity of agro-ecosystems is reduced, traditional medicines compete poorly with biomedical healthcare (Patwardhan et al., 2023).

A potential trade-off of intercultural health services involves the use of wild animal products as traditional medicine or as a health food. Species may be threatened so that biodiversity becomes a trade-off with health. In China, traditional and allopathic medical care systems are closely integrated, and large hospitals commonly use both wild plant and animal products for healthcare. Some of these practices, e.g., the butchering process or drinking raw blood, are potential pathways for zoonotic infection and therefore a trade-off with health itself (Gbogbo & Daniels, 2019; USAID, 2017; Wassenaar & Zou, 2020).

Intercultural health services are demonstrating effectiveness in providing cost-effective solutions (Figure 5.4.6). Traditional health services, often the first choice for various conditions, result in significant savings for biomedical health services (Ministerio de Salud (Chile), 2021; O’Neill et al., 2007). However, traditional healers often face a lack of professional recognition and receive no monetary compensation for their services, for example, traditional midwives in Bolivia and Guetemala collaborating with hospitals have no expenses covered (M. A. Martin & Gurven, 2022; O’Neill et al., 2007). However, in some places such as remote Australia, Canada and New Zealand healthcare systems support traditional healers (Asamoah et al., 2023; Hawthorne, 2020).

Intercultural health services are also key in promoting equity (Figure 5.4.6), particularly in access to healthcare (see section 5.4.3.1, H01, Universal health coverage) as community driven responses including Indigenous knowledge, go beyond conventional healthcare systems, with a strong emphasis on cultural relevance and Indigenous self-governance (O’Neill et al., 2007). Reorienting health services and governance systems based on the capabilities of Indigenous Peoples (Bockstael & Watene, 2016), strengthens control over resources ad land (Peña et al., 2020; Sterling et al., 2017).

IPLC’ traditional medical practices link cultural and biological diversity and recognizing these through intercultural health services has potential to be transformative (Figure 5.4.6). However, cultural values are undermined when these practices are decontextualized, commoditized or appropriated. Unsustainable demand for traditional medicines can lead to a negative effect on the nexus elements and on the IPLC who are legitimate holders of this knowledge (UNEP, 2023). Traditional medicines may be commercialized and distributed back to communities for profit, devoid of any communal benefits (Rose et al., 2012). Additionally, some rituals and practices, such as the consumption of specific plants or fungi, have gained popularity among outsiders for recreational purposes, inadvertently causing displeasure among spiritual entities and inducing scarcity for IPLC. Biopiracy threatens equitable distribution of benefits derived from ILK and biodiversity used to address Indigenous health issues. Brazil has addressed this by introducing new regulations focusing on access, benefit sharing, utilization or third-party transfer (Sirakaya, 2019).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Intercultural health services (H02)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2.3.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2NBSHK4T>,
