        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HTADS7VQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/I9HUKJZ9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ITYVQTUA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JVJRECSX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MDHFNH7I>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NRWCVM2Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P67DINVB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QPMC59EF>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RZBDDGN7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SGFTNUPC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TI23VSCG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/U62D8KJV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UDSCC32S>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VARSUVGL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W5QSNU4D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WAURURBF> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The assessments of future impacts of multiple drivers on health in the context of the nexus primarily focus on health aspects such as life expectancy. For example, the impact of linked changes in population, gross domestic product, climate change, water quality, dietary change and land-use on trends in average life expectancy in 2030, 2050 and 2100 was simulated by a global system model (Moallemi et al., 2022). Under the most optimistic Green Recovery scenario (SSP1-2.6), life expectancy improved for approximately 50 per cent of simulations at each time point, but these trends fell short of stated moderate targets of life expectancy increase (Moallemi et al., 2022).

Considerable recent attention has been paid to the COVID-19 pandemic and its relationship to nexus issues, as most pandemics are caused by pathogens of animal origin (Judson & Rabinowitz, 2021). Future changes in zoonoses and infectious disease risks are expected due to diverse indirect and direct drivers of change. Specifically, increases in the world population are projected to lead to increases in human animal contacts through land-use change, agricultural expansion at the fringes of natural ecosystems, trade and consumption in animals and animal products (both domestic and wild), and increased populations of farmed animals. For SARS-CoV-2, for example, substantial evidence points to a likely origin where the greatest diversity of SARS-related coronaviruses is found (Latinne et al., 2020), where contact among people and bats is common (H. Li et al., 2019; H.-Y. Li et al., 2020), and where human populations are expanding and encroaching into a rapidly changing landscape (Rulli et al., 2021). With increased animal farming, emerging diseases are expected to increase due to spillover between wildlife and farm animals (Morse et al., 2012), e.g. SARS-CoV-2 (Sikkema et al., 2022), and vice versa, with possible devastating effects (e.g. HPAI H5 (Verhagen et al., 2021).

Climate change will also shift ranges of people, wildlife, reservoirs and vectors, and with them the pathogens they harbour and temperature dependent transmissions risks. Scenarios of infectious diseases and zoonoses mostly consider climate change scenarios, which are applied to project the spatio-temporal distribution of different species in the transmission cycle (e.g., Samy et al., 2022) or direct occurrence of disease cases (e.g., (Messina et al., 2019; Tjaden et al., 2021). Human population density is mostly considered as a constant factor over time as spatially high-resolution projections of population are missing (Zhiwei Xu et al., 2020). Climate change adaptation/mitigation measures in the form of water catchments, urban green spaces or peatland restoration can result in possible habitats for disease vectors of arthropod-borne diseases. Melting glaciers and permafrost reactivate and release pathogens into aquatic and terrestrial environments immured for millennia as shown by recent outbreaks (e.g. Siberia, Russian Federation) (Yarzábal et al., 2021).

Another driver affecting health in futures-oriented studies is pollution from human activities. For example, for the marine realm, a warming climate can further influence the geographical distribution and availability of ocean pollutants though release of legacy pollutants (e.g., plastic waste, pharmaceuticals, toxic metals, fertilizer, pesticide and sewage constituents) previously trapped in ice and permafrost (Allen et al., 2019). Pollution-sourced microplastics could become problematic vectors of disease by transporting and sheltering marine pathogens. Also predicted is expansion in the geographic range of bacteria that thrive in warmer waters, such as the disease competent Vibrio species (Vezzulli et al., 2016). Human and veterinary pharmaceutical use continue to increase globally, because of population growth, rising per capita consumption and growing livestock. The antimicrobial resistance associated with the presence of antibiotics in the environment (e.g., in surface and sewage water) is likely to worsen (Wöhler et al., 2020). Interlinkages between drivers such as eutrophication, temperature and salinization can also act directly on the mosquito larval habitat, or with secondary soil salinity of drylands, resulting in large and often underestimated impacts on mosquitoes (e.g., Culex pipiens s.l. (Boerlijst et al., 2023); Aedes camptorhynchus (Jardine et al., 2008)).

Limited quantitative estimates of health impact are available for BAU scenarios, particularly in terms of linking climate change projections and health. Under current GHG mitigation policies, global average temperatures reach 1.5°C in the near-term and 2°C by the 2050s, leading to a doubling of multi-sector risks (Byers et al., 2018). Climate change is projected to have net negative effects on malnutrition, heat-related human mortality and morbidity, food-borne, water-borne and vector-borne diseases, and mental health challenges (IPCC, 2022a). Compared to BAU, scenarios designed to achieve ecological and health benefits are projected to substantially reduce mortality rates (Hamilton et al., 2021).

The impacts on health of combined changes in biodiversity, water, food and climate change have not been quantified, but are likely to be severe, even if major social or ecological discontinuities can be avoided. Quantitative models of global health generally depend on individual risk factors and socio-economic trends and do not yet account for interactions across the nexus. Conversely, models investigating impacts of global environmental changes on health typically do not account for socio-economic or health system changes (Weber et al., 2023).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Impacts of multiple drivers on health" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4QZW9KHJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/53ZKFHK4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/54I3CDTE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/6TBELPFD>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8TAR4DY7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AA5VRX8X>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D6N8LW3N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JZL93FTN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KE2HL87X>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KMZRGLSG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KQCQTVL2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KQWAWCK4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LFH666PG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LIE4YXPB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LPZK64RR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PU3YXQ9W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RCMSV5HL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TKTIARG7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UNG7FGCB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WAVV99A8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YLYK3LFL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z2CUP7W2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZJW8TQN3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Because many projects consider no more than one nexus element other than biodiversity from their onset, direct evidence of outcomes of biodiversity response options across multiple nexus elements is often limited in individual studies. Even for biodiversity, most evidence regards structure and not function. This could be improved by using and collecting data for priority indicators such as the Essential Biodiversity Variables (H. M. Pereira et al., 2013) and Essential Ecosystem Service Variables (Balvanera et al., 2017), and using international functional trait databases (e.g., Kattge et al., 2011; Soria et al., 2021). Furthermore, impacts on security of access to food, health and water are rarely reported as outcomes of biodiversity-focused response options.

The lack of comprehensive reporting across nexus elements creates a critical knowledge gap on trade-offs, with benefits being reported more frequently. There is also a lack of reporting of negative impacts to nexus elements and relevant actors. For example, trade-offs between benefits to climate change and biodiversity (e.g., in urban nature-based solutions) can be pieced together from disparate evidence sources but are not often addressed in individual studies or syntheses.

Underreporting of non-significant or negative findings is chronic in the literature (Marks-Anglin & Chen, 2020; Rosenthal, 1979), compounded by the much slower publication time for non-significant or negative (rather than positive) results (Koricheva & Kulinskaya, 2019). For example, published case studies of nature-based solutions most often report co-benefits with little mention of any negative effects. However, there is a rich ecosystem services literature covering disbenefits or negative nature’s contributions to people (Campagne et al., 2018). Cases of imperfect implementation or failures in the planning and implementation of nature-based solutions and other initiatives are also rarely reported in the literature or databases, although they provide critical information on how implementation processes influence environmental, social and economic outcomes and trade-offs.

A synthesis on social, economic and environmental goals of specific response options is lacking. The analysis presented here only summarizes the limited information available across case studies, rather than these being reported systematically for individual cases of implementation. The assessment of economic and social costs and benefits and distributional equity is a major knowledge gap for biodiversity response options that hinders their implementation and scaling (Dubo et al., 2023; Mirzabaev & Wuepper, 2023; United Nations Environment Programme, 2022b). Beyond a lack of availability and synthesis on financial costs, there is a chronic lack of information on total costs, including labour input by local communities and volunteers, and on markets.

Despite interest in monitoring embedded in international standards, such as for nature-based solutions (e.g., see Box 5.1.1) (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019), long-term monitoring and assessments of the effects of biodiversity response options on ecosystem functioning and services, socioeconomic benefits, and governance/decision-making processes (including the inclusion of stakeholders and IPLC) are still limited. Moreover, response options for biodiversity often require years to decades until environmental, social and economic benefits are fully delivered. Sociopolitical response options also frequently take many decades to undertake. There is a lack of consideration of complex systems dynamics and how they may reduce the effectiveness of area-based conservation or nature-based solutions initiatives (e.g., telecoupling, historical legacies) (Boillat et al., 2020; Carmenta et al., 2023). This also impedes broader and more nuanced assessment of their effectiveness, demanding new assessment methods along with longer-term monitoring efforts (e.g., Donatti et al., 2020).

Regular, consistent and locally sensitive monitoring and evaluation protocols and tools and leading examples for the effectiveness of adaptive co-management for response options have been proposed (e.g., Donatti et al., 2020; IUCN, 2020b; Nishi et al., 2021; Wyborn et al., 2021), but are not systematically applied. Further research is needed on inclusive, participatory and adaptive monitoring and evaluation mechanisms that cover all elements of the nexus, including how they can be incorporated into international monitoring and evaluation guidelines (see Chapter 7, section 7.3.8).

The literature focuses heavily on some types of instruments over others: for example, urban nature-based solutions were reported as almost exclusively delivered via legal and regulatory options despite evidence to suggest that they are more successful when delivered with local communities through rights-based approaches or social and cultural norms (Kabisch et al., 2022). Novel governance structures have evolved for biodiversity response options, especially area-based conservation and rights-based approaches (see section 5.1.4), but global assessments on the distribution and widespread use of these structures are lacking. In particular, community engagement, traditional institutional arrangements, bio-cultural and customary approaches, and the incorporation of traditional value systems of IPLC in the implementation of biodiversity response options are rarely reported. Likewise, there are very few reports of the role of IPLC cultural practices and innovation for response options.

All these knowledge gaps hinder the scaling out, up, deep and down of biodiversity response options (see Chapter 4, section 4.4). While some barriers and levers for scaling-out biodiversity response options, and especially nature-based solutions, are well identified (Dubo et al., 2023, and references therein; United Nations Environment Programme, 2022b), evidence on successful examples is lacking, e.g., for ecosystem-based adaptation. Scaling out of response options is further limited by the lack of knowledge on enabling conditions and institutional arrangements for promoting networking response options. Finally, while on the ground cases usually combine several response options (e.g., “policy mixes”) (Chan et al., 2020; C. Sandström et al., 2023), conceptual and methodological frameworks and evidence for describing their outcomes are needed.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps" .

