        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GAE44I5S>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IPWNJ9XU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JM3YYUY7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KHJLFN6C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MHKMEDT8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NQTZMVIR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PBK4L8QC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PGW5RY5P>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PLAW5JIE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PUDGREYU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RC98ML5U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UCCHCQB8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UHZV2LFR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UV3GMV9V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W9WR75DV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WY6RLGFZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XM8FQJXM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XV9YV847> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Reducing pesticide pollution can be applied globally but should be prioritized in geographic areas with large areas under intensive agriculture where pollution risks are high (FAO, 2023b) (Figure 5.3.5). Ten percent of the 3 million tonnes of pesticides used annually in agriculture remains in the upper soil horizons as residue, 7 per cent leaches into the aquifers and 0.1 per cent enters rivers and oceans (Maggi et al., 2019). These residues put 64 per cent of agricultural land at risk of pollution and 31 per cent at high risk of pollution (Tang et al., 2022). Biocides residues have a wide spectrum of half-life and cycle and can persist in the environment as pollutants over the medium to long-term (UNEP, 2001).

Reducing pesticide pollution has very high benefits for reducing surface and groundwater pollution (Maggi et al., 2019; Tang et al., 2022), preserving water quality for humans and ecosystems and decreasing overall environmental exposure to persistent pesticides (UNEP, 2001) (Figure 5.3.3). Reducing pesticide residues in the environment also benefits soil biodiversity that is essential for maintaining soil health and performance (Maggi & Tang, 2021; Relyea, 2005) – notably carbon storage (Bastida et al., 2021; Patel et al., 2022). However, in the absence of alternative pest management practices (section 5.3.3.4), reducing pesticide use may decrease yield protection efficiency and increase costs (Ahvo et al., 2023).

Decreasing pesticide transport via surface water and wind drift to non-treated land (Desmarteau et al., 2020; Maggi et al., 2023) can have positive effects on preserving native biodiversity in protected areas and the wider landscape. This can improve associated ecological functions, which in turn can preserve/improve ecosystem resilience capability (Beaumelle et al., 2023; Zikankuba et al., 2019). Riparian buffers are useful for reducing pesticide flow into freshwater systems, which have important climate and biodiversity co-benefits (Asbjornsen et al., 2014). However, similar to reducing nutrient pollution (section 5.3.3.7), there is currently no evidence of the impact of reducing pesticide pollution on climate change, although an estimated 15 per cent of global fossil fuel use is allocated to agricultural inputs including pesticides (GAFF, 2023).

The response option is highly beneficial to human health as pesticide residues are present in fresh and processed foods, leading to toxicological risks (Boobis et al., 2008). Documented evidence shows that reducing pesticide use decreases environmental loads of residues and potential human exposure to them (Maggi et al., 2021). This in turn decreases the associated burden of diseases (Landrigan et al., 2018), as well as pesticide application operators’ consciousness of exposure risk and associated mental well-being (Daghagh Yazd et al., 2019).

While social acceptability of the response option is high, alignment to political agendas is intermediate to low mainly due to industrial assets, intellectual property and trading balance in imports/exports of individual countries (Tang et al., 2022) (Figure 5.3.4). In contrast, technical feasibility is well developed by means of a number of alternatives including integrated pest management as organic farming (Baker et al., 2020), biological pest control (Lucchi & Benelli, 2018; T. Williams et al., 2013), precision farming (Finger et al., 2019; Shamshiri et al., 2018) and protected farming (Van Lenteren, 2000) (Figure 5.3.5), although there are some limitations on their application at scales and associated costs (Deguine et al., 2021). Normative tools providing a framework that guides government regulators, the private sector, civil society and other stakeholders on best practice in managing pesticides throughout their lifecycle can play a significant role in reducing pesticide pollution (FAO & WHO, 2014).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reduce pesticide pollution (F08)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C1> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/72D5XUXL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8WARM9S3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BBW7CSI2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DCPPL9R4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/DYXIMBHL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GDDDMVM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HHGL97U4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HZ2WYFXA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MKRLWBG3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PKAM3M4J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SGFTNUPC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TWFV9DHZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TX4W8T4U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W563S4UK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/X34D47EY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/NXS24-ch5-4933> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """An initial set of response options that address health issues and at least two additional nexus elements were identified by chapter authors based on expert opinion informed by searching the literature and major reports (e.g., WHO Environment, Climate Change and Health reports, IPBES assessments, the Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics (IPBES, 2020), National Biodiversity Strategies and Action Plans, World Organisation for Animal Health terrestrial and aquatic animal health codes, Health-Climate National Adaptation Plans (H-NAPS) and Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) reports, including the contribution of Working Group II to the Sixth Assessment Report of IPCC (Cissé et al., 2022; IPCC, 2022, 2023) and the report from the first ILK dialogue workshop for the Nexus Assessment (IPBES, 2022a). This produced 51 response options after removal of regional or thematic duplication (see Chapter 5.4, online Supplementary material 5.4.1).

Fifteen response options with strong nexus interlinkages were selected for assessment using a multi-criteria decision analysis (IPBES, 2018; Keune et al., 2013; Van Schoubroeck et al., 2019). The initial set of response options were categorized as equitable, nexus-supportive healthcare; addressing environmental risks to health; promoting nature’s benefits to health; and nexus-integrated healthcare (Figure 5.4.3) and ranked in sets to eliminate ranking thematically different options against each other. The categories approximate existing health and health-biodiversity frameworks (e.g., (IPBES, 2019a), (Marselle et al., 2021) and with special attention to IPLC inclusion. The selected response options were chosen as examples for which sufficient evidence for analysis currently exists. See Table 5.4.2 for brief descriptions of the 15 response options assessed.

Each response option was analysed and scored based on assessed evidence against 10 criteria (see Chapter 5.0, section 5.0.3 and Table 5.0.1) related to its potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, its feasibility and breadth of applicability, its impact on equity and its potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks. In addition, specific case studies are provided to illustrate response options. The geographical distribution of case studies is shown in Figure 5.4.4. A synthesis of the scoring is provided in Figure 5.4.5 (impacts of the response options on nexus elements), Figure 5.4.6 (health response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, and international frameworks) and Figure 5.4.7 (temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the health response options). Individual response options are described in sections 5.4.3.1 to 5.4.3.15.

From the 15 response options assessed, five focal response options are analyzed in greater detail: H02, Intercultural health services (section 5.4.3.2); H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (section 5.4.3.6); H09, Urban green infrastructure (section 5.4.3.9); H10, Forest conservation for health (section 5.4.3.10) and H13, Health impact assessment (section 5.4.3.13). These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action.

Notes
3. The complete scoring templates are included with the Chapter 5 data management report describing the response option scoring criteria and process; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.
4. The average scores visualized in the figure are included with the Chapter 5 data management report describing the response option scoring criteria and process; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.
2. The data management report describing the Chapter 5 response option scoring criteria and process is available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for health" .

