    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Inland water ecosystems, such as lakes, rivers, wetlands and other waterbodies are among the most degraded ecosystems globally. For example, more than 80 per cent of wetlands have been lost (Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005), leading to a decline in their associated nature’s contributions to people (Reid et al., 2019; Schallenberg et al., 2013). Inland water systems have been heavily modified and face multiple threats, including water withdrawal for human use; impacts of other natural resource uses, such as extraction of aggregates in mining and impacts on biodiversity from fisheries (Cooke et al., 2023); and flow modification driven by flood management, dams and channelization (Dudgeon et al., 2006). Thus, their restoration is critical for both nature and people. The restoration of inland waters can encompass diverse activities that lead to healthier and more natural flows, cleaner states and the sustainable use of biodiversity (Palmer & Ruhi, 2019). Activities such as dam removal, reduced pollution and responsible fisheries restore the health of inland water ecosystems by maintaining key ecohydrological processes of natural flooding and fluvial connectivity (Arthington et al., 2023; Thieme et al., 2023).

Inland water restoration requires a cohesive framework to guide the evaluation of freshwater nature’s contributions to people, their trade-offs and sustainable management that takes account of the multiple, sometimes conflicting, dimensions of human and biodiversity dependence (Dunham et al., 2018; Palmer & Ruhi, 2019; Vollmer et al., 2018). Besides contributing to the conservation of freshwater and terrestrial biodiversity, inland water restoration also interacts, largely positively, with the other nexus elements. It can support healthier wild-capture inland fisheries, which are crucial aquatic food systems for millions globally, especially in sub-Saharan Africa and Asia (Craig et al., 2017). Access to an affordable source of protein and micronutrients is crucial for the health of vulnerable groups (O’Meara et al., 2021). Preserving riparian habitat, especially in upper catchment areas (Abell et al., 2019), positively contributes to water security by improving water quality and can be facilitated by urban-rural payment schemes like payment for ecosystem services (Bremer et al., 2016). Healthy inland waters are also significant contributors to global carbon capture (Mitsch et al., 2013; Wohl et al., 2017) and hence can contribute to climate change mitigation. However, some trade-offs exist, such as the impact of irrigation for food production on surface waterbodies and groundwater (D’Odorico et al., 2018) or reservoir construction for the water security of dense urban areas (He et al., 2021).

The restoration of inland waters often poses a difficult governance challenge due to the presence of multiple diverse actors with sometimes conflicting objectives and power (Lynch et al., 2023). Some freshwater resource management strategies including Integrated Water Resources Management are viewed as top-down and may fail to recognize and account for the cultural and societal values of ecosystems (Gain et al., 2021; Ochoa-García & Rist, 2018; Schreier et al., 2014; N. Watson, 2014; Woodhouse & Muller, 2017). Community-based approaches are more inclusive (Karres et al., 2022) and customary ILK-based management systems of IPLC play a vital role for inland water conservation and restoration (Bielawski, 2021). While policies often account for inland waters as part of the terrestrial realm (Lynch et al., 2017), which can lead to underrepresentation or less relevant conservation and restoration efforts (Birnie-Gauvin et al., 2023; Reid et al., 2019), the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework mentions ‘inland waters’ specifically, including the importance of the contribution of ILK and IPLC customary systems to inland waters conservation and restoration.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restoration of inland water systems (B07)" .

