    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Sustainable intensification is a globally applicable response option (Figure 5.5.5) for increasing agricultural and livestock production efficiency while reducing environmental impact and avoiding land conversion. This can be achieved through a range of practices, including improved use of water, fertilizer and pesticides, improved crop varieties and protection of crop wild relatives, better management of soil health and pests, and the application of traditional and modern breeding techniques that take account of other concerns such as animal welfare (Castañeda-Álvarez et al., 2016; Garnett et al., 2013). Sustainable intensification also considers the social and economic dimensions of agriculture and livestock production, ensuring that all stakeholders benefit. It is achieved using three non-linear stages: first efficiency, to reduce waste and improve resource use within existing systems; second substitution, through the replacement of less efficient technologies and practices with alternatives; and third redesign, transforming agroecosystems to deliver sustainability across all dimensions (Cassman & Grassini, 2020; Pretty et al., 2018). According to estimates, around 163 million farms, comprising 29 per cent of global farms, have adopted SI practices on 453 million hectares of agricultural land, representing 9 per cent of the global total (FAO, 2022b; Pretty et al., 2018). SI can be implemented on all managed land, without favouring any vision or approach to agriculture.

Sustainable intensification has large potential for climate change mitigation, with an estimated global reduction of approximately ~ 0.9-1.05 Gt CO2e/yr (Corbeels et al., 2020; Haughey et al., 2023; P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020), and benefits adaptation for up to 3.2 billion people, reducing land pressure and enhancing production (P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.3). It also has positive impacts on the water, food and health nexus elements. Sustainable intensification can decrease water use in irrigated croplands to meet the annual domestic water needs of nearly 1.4 billion people (Brauman et al., 2013). It prioritizes not only current and future food production but also food’s diversity, types and nutritional value. Adoption of sustainable intensification practices enables farmers to enhance diets with crop diversity and increase overall food production (Bais-Moleman et al., 2019; Ngoma et al., 2023; Wilkus et al., 2022), reducing the burden of undernutrition. SI practices fulfil basic human needs, promote landscape resilience and contribute to well-being (Godfray, 2015; Rockström et al., 2017).

The impact of agriculture and livestock on biodiversity exhibits regional variations (Figure 5.5.3). It is estimated that nearly half the world’s land could be suitable for diversified systems to achieve sustainable intensification, but different regions benefit from various approaches depending on biophysical potential: expansion (e.g., Europe) or intensification (e.g., tropics), with some areas suited for both (e.g., West Africa) (Haughey et al., 2023; Kamau et al., 2023; Kehoe et al., 2017). Land clearing and habitat fragmentation pose significant threats to biodiversity, making a “land sparing” approach (like sustainable intensification) combined with “land sharing” (i.e., agroforestry) crucial to minimize biodiversity loss (Grass et al., 2019; Perfecto & Vandermeer, 2010; Tilman et al., 2017). In reducing the impact of agriculture and livestock on the environment sustainable intensification makes land available for nature; however, other factors related to governance, regulations, public policies and markets still must be considered to ensure the protection of nature.

Despite numerous advantages of sustainable intensification, its implementation carries the potential for two negative consequences: the rebound effect and leakage. The rebound effect occurs when increased productivity leads to increased cropland utilization. Leakage occurs when a policy intervention displaces deforestation-related activities to other regions. Evidence suggests that agricultural intensification can have a short-term rebound effect, particularly in middle-income countries (García et al., 2020). For example, a study in Brazil found that intensification led to temporary reductions in local deforestation, but crop intensification was associated with increased deforestation over longer periods (Garrett et al., 2018). This suggests that farmers may respond to increased productivity by expanding their cropland area. Related to leakage, a study in South America found that a decrease in beef imports from biomes with more stringent deforestation regulations was associated with an increase in beef imports from other countries (le Polain de Waroux et al., 2019). Interventions that limit production are likely to result in leakage unless demand is reduced or met by other means, indicating important trade-offs between land use, intensification and nature conservation. In other words, agricultural intensification can lead to land sparing for nature, but only if accompanied by policies to control cropland expansion, otherwise, the rebound effect and leakage may lead to increased land use and deforestation.

The transition of farming systems to sustainable intensification considers multiple elements linked together. A significant upfront investment in smallholder sustainable agriculture is necessary, but it will yield long-term economic returns, e.g., it is estimated that a transformative investment of $10-20 billion per year for 10-15 years is required to support smallholders in Africa adopt sustainable agricultural practices (Piemontese et al., 2022; Spiegal et al., 2018). Smallholder farmers need financial support from government, investors and development organizations to successfully adopt sustainable intensification practices (Figure 5.5.4). Studies highlight potential disparities in the distribution of well-being impacts from sustainable intensification. While some argue that better-off farmers benefit more, others suggest equal distribution over time as the gap narrows during diffusion and adoption (Kassie et al., 2015; Keil et al., 2019; Z. Wang et al., 2021). Adequate support and addressing barriers and trade-offs are essential to transition to sustainable intensification.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sustainable intensification (C02)" .

