    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Zoonotic diseases (see Chapter 2, sections 2.3.2.1 and 2.4.3 for a description and current trends) include those of pandemic potential such as COVID-19, with estimates from the WHO of approximately 0.8 billion cases worldwide by late-June 2023. Zoonotic diseases also include many of the diseases referred to as neglected tropical diseases that impact almost 1 billion of the poorest people globally (Malecela & Ducker, 2021). However, global funding and scientific research is lower for zoonotic neglected tropical diseases despite their impacts on disability-adjusted life years (Goarant et al., 2019; McIntyre et al., 2017).

Stopping biodiversity loss has been presented as the best prevention strategy to limit the risk of spillover at source and spread of emerging zoonotic diseases (IPBES, 2020; Roche et al., 2020), while reducing the costs of response to pandemics (Bernstein et al., 2022). However, the relationship with biodiversity is multidirectional and often highly disease-specific such that increased conservation by itself is not assured to prevent zoonotic diseases emergence (Morand, 2020). It is also dependent on context – countries with effective surveillance, coordinated health systems (including H01, Universal health coverage and primary healthcare (section 5.4.3.1)), and trust in government have experienced better responses to outbreaks (One Health High-Level Expert Panel (OHHLEP) et al., 2023).

Interactions across nexus elements have critical roles in this response option (Malecela & Ducker, 2021) but have not been fully assessed (Figure 5.4.5). Climate change is an important driver for zoonotic diseases spread (McIntyre et al., 2017; Romanello et al., 2022), and more than half of zoonotic infectious diseases can be aggravated by climate change (Mora et al., 2022). Climate change is also increasing direct threats on biodiversity including the wildlife hosts and vectors of diseases (Pörtner et al., 2021), stressing the interlinkages between health, biodiversity and climate (Atwoli et al., 2021; Malecela & Ducker, 2021).

Globally, water resources are under stress, increasing water insecurity and threatening freshwater biodiversity (Grill et al., 2019; Vörösmarty et al., 2010). The increasing number of dams (Mulligan et al., 2020) favours emerging water-and vector- borne diseases (Kibret et al., 2021; McFarlane et al., 2013; Sleigh et al., 2006; Ziegler et al., 2013). The protection of wetlands, which were historically linked with unsanitary living conditions, is seen as a key approach to reduce the spread of emerging infectious diseases such as avian flu (Wu et al., 2020).

Agricultural expansion has major negative impacts on biodiversity, climate change, pollution and invasive species (Crist et al., 2017; Zabel et al., 2019). Significant relationships between zoonotic disease outbreaks with expanding human demography, livestock, poultry, tree plantation and artificial land expansion suggest that changes to the global food system could reduce the risk of emerging infectious diseases (Morand, 2022) and contribute to many of the SDGs (M. Clark et al., 2022; Lajaunie & Morand, 2021) (Figure 5.4.6). The consumption of wildlife and wildlife farming are further zoonotic risks (IPBES, 2020). The definition of the prevention of spillover at source (OHHLEP et al., 2023) identifies factors to be addressed as using a nexus approach and taking into account the socioeconomic context and wildlife supply chain (Kock & Caceres-Escobar, 2022). This has the potential to address equity issues, e.g., for those involved in wildlife procurement or sale, and reduce economic costs through a more complex approach to prevention. This approach stresses that the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (i.e., target 5) (CBD, 2022) should explicitly implement the objective of spillover risk reduction (Caceres-Escobar et al., 2023).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Biodiversity management for zoonoses (H11)" .

