    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """This response options cluster includes (i) improving water accounting; (ii) mobilizing financial resources for investment in infrastructure, human capital and institutional capacities for integrated water management (including mobilization of private sector expertise in these areas); (iii) stimulating innovative financing systems with environmental and social safeguards and regulation, (iv) establishing enabling conditions for financial viability and creditworthiness of water sector, and (v) advancing climate actions through mitigation and adaptation actions in the water sector (Boelee et al., 2017; OECD, 2022; The World Bank, 2023).

Financing water infrastructure for integrated water management occurs from local to national scales (Figure 5.2.5), with examples from Europe (Berbel & Expósito, 2020; Borrego-Marín et al., 2016; Edens & Graveland, 2014; United Nations, 2012), North America (Lazurko & Venema, 2017) and the Global South (Garrick et al., 2020). However, evidence on their benefit to biodiversity is unresolved (e.g., reduction in freshwater biodiversity and ecosystem services; Russi et al., 2013). Market-based water quality trading programs are also a mechanism for using price signals in river-basin management (Abdalla et al., 2007; Corrales et al., 2013; Tabaichount et al., 2019). Water quality and pollution trading in some markets might create incentives for more sustainable food production as farmers reduce water pollution (Zapata et al., 2023), but this has not been the case in much of the United States of America (Gasper et al., 2012). A review of water quality trading practices found that the implementation of these programs is complex with institutional, regulatory, environmental, economic and social factors contributing to the success or failure of these programs (H. Liu & Brouwer, 2023). Water banking, or use of an intermediary acting between buyers and sellers of water rights to facilitate market transfers, is another promising response to drought and other water crises (Barbier, 2022; Montilla-López et al., 2016).

The benefits of water-related economic instruments for achieving nexus-wide benefits are generally unresolved (Figure 5.2.3). However, economic instruments have been shown to help in increasing irrigation water efficiency by reducing water demand. For example, increases in irrigation water price led farms to choose rainfed crops in northern Spain (Aldaya et al., 2023), and a 1-10 per cent increase in water pricing in Europe and the United States of America reduced water consumption by 10-48 per cent (Reynaud, 2015; Scheierling et al., 2006). Economic incentives also have been used to determine crop rotations that reduce water consumption (Hulugalle & Scott, 2008). Blended finance can also play a critical role in mobilizing commercial finance to achieve SDG6 (water and sanitation for all) (OECD, 2019) and hence have co-benefits for health (SDG3) (see Chapter 6, section 6.2).

At the same time, implementation of economic instruments is challenging due to the common pool nature of water use and their lack of fit with abatement pollution measures (i.e., for non-point source pollution where water pricing may not be tied to the polluter) and overall effectiveness in ensuring efficiency and equity (Albiac et al., 2020; de Andrade Resende Filho et al., 2015; Pigram, 1999). Recognizing multiple values of water (Grafton et al., 2023), including non-market water values, in water accounts and financing are key components of transformative pathways towards more sustainable water futures. For example, the impacts of water resource development on Australian Indigenous customary economies have been assessed using replacement cost methods (Jackson et al., 2014). Economics, pricing and financing can be transformational in global water allocation in terms of by whom, what, where and when water is used (Figure 5.2.5) (Grafton et al., 2020, 2023; United Nations, 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Finance for water infrastructure (W10)" .

