    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Indigenous Peoples and local communities’ (IPLC) food systems integrate cultural dimensions to food (FAO, Alliance of Biodiversity International, & CIAT, 2021). These include access to food from ancestral and structural assets, cosmological and web of life values, sovereignty and sustainable production, and integrative health (Table 5.3.10). However, these food systems face challenges related to land tenure problems (Rudel & Hernandez, 2017), gender issues (Olsen, 2017), social justice (Olivé, 2004), horizontal cooperation (Ghate et al., 2011) and the embracing of plural common vision (McGregor, 2016), which are important considerations in addressing trade-offs between the nexus elements. The future of IPLC food systems depends on the formalization of sustainable and sovereign land use and territory aiming at integrative health that encompasses public health actions, promotion of sustainable healthy diets and respect of traditional food diversity and production.

Indigenous and local knowledge provides a distinct understanding of the nexus in relation to the interlinkages among IPLC food systems, biodiversity conservation and human habitats (E. M. Gardner et al., 2022; Hill et al., 2020). These systems are diverse and imply multiple perspectives related to ethics, values and world views (P. Cole, 2017). Critical to IPLC food systems are land access and distribution (Mika et al., 2022) and an emphasis on community-based lifestyles (Masitera, 2020). IPLC food systems are highly adept at navigating sustainable futures, including adaptive responses to water scarcity (Stewart-Harawira, 2020) and climate change (Leal Filho et al., 2022) (Figure 5.3.3). ILK is used to recognize biodiversity benefits of small-scale production systems, wild foods or extensive rangeland systems (Garavito et al., 2021). Foods gathered or produced based on ILK underpin community health and nutrition (Hou & Bainbridge, 2023; Kapoor et al., 2022). Importantly, IPLC world views question contested values related to extractivism and overconsumption (Babidge, 2016) and offer novel ways of understanding people’s relationships with food, nature and its sustainable management (see Chapter 7, online Supplementary material 7.1).

Two case studies in this section highlight lessons from IPLC food systems. These reflect important aspects of Indigenous knowledge and values embedded within IPLC food systems. The first case study, in the Yucatan Peninsula, Mexico, describes the Mayan milpa that has sustained the population for more than 3,500 years until today (Box 5.3.14). The milpa produces food, while fostering identity and culture, offering complete and quality nutrition, and has allowed evolution with the productive diversity of home gardens (FAO, Alliance of Biodiversity International, & CIAT, 2021; Garutsa & Nekhwevha, 2019). In the second case study from Malaysian Borneo, the role of valuing regional food heritage and long-standing sustainable food production and foraging practices through the adoption of geographical indication and cultural festivals is highlighted (Khazanah Research Institute, 2018; Lugun (Datin), 2015) (Box 5.3.15). Shared elements that are important to facilitate successes seen in these case studies include respecting local food diversity and production systems, and actions to support IPLC rights for collective land tenure recognition, e.g., through cross-sectoral committees to formalise access and declare legal tenure of traditional territories. Two further case studies of Indigenous food systems are provided in the Chapter 5.3 online Supplementary material 5.3.3: Indigenous agricultural practices in Khambashe, South Africa and the traditional knowledge and sustainability of the Tikuna, Cocama and Yagua food system.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Indigenous food systems (F15)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D3> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D5> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The steps of the road map can be applied to specific challenges or opportunities related to the nexus elements and their interactions and can include characterizing direct and indirect drivers and their impacts, identifying and convening governance actors across sectors and scales, understanding interactions and interdependencies among nexus elements, co-creating visions and aligning values, identifying response options and their synergies and trade-offs, assessing enabling conditions and overcoming barriers to coordinated and integrated action, negotiating implementation, scaling, and monitoring and learning iteratively. These steps can be undertaken by a range of actors working together and depend on more inclusive and cross-scale actor cooperation. However, there are many barriers to collaborative action, such as intersectional and compounding systems of marginalization, especially for groups such as Indigenous Peoples and local communities, young people, the elderly, migrants and displaced people, women, and persons living with disabilities. Improving capacities for governance can strengthen awareness of the need for change, enhance the co-production of knowledge, help navigate trade-offs and assist in addressing injustices. Actions within the road map can be both incremental and transformative, and all can improve the current situation and help move towards just and sustainable futures." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM-D3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus governance approaches, decision-making and capacity-strengthening can be enhanced through a series of deliberative steps and actions, informed by diverse evidence. A road map for nexus action can be used by a wide range of actors in multiple sectors to identify problems and shared values in order to work collaboratively towards solutions to help achieve just and sustainable futures aligned with global policy frameworks. Tools and methods facilitating a holistic understanding of nexus elements can increase knowledge and improve cooperation and decision-making {D2, D4, D5}." .

