    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Wastewater pollution, including excessive fertilizer use, can be reduced by repositioning wastewater management from an end-of-pipe problem to a circular economy opportunity which treats wastewater as a renewable resource to be conserved and sustainably managed (Hdidou et al., 2022). Sustainable sanitation practices play a crucial role in addressing the interconnected challenges of water availability, biodiversity conservation and climate actions (Hutton & Chase, 2016).

Safe and appropriate wastewater management has potential co-benefits such as improved ecosystem health, human health and well-being, reduced dependence on synthetic fertilizers and economic opportunities (Figure 5.2.3) (UNEP, 2023). Actions include: (a) reducing the volume of wastewater produced; (b) preventing and reducing water contamination; and (c) sustainably managing wastewater for resource recovery and reuse. Several solutions exist for the collection and treatment of wastewater to recover resources (such as nutrients) of appropriate quality standards depending on intended end use (UNEP, 2023; WWAP, 2015). Access to adequate sanitation services and domestic wastewater treatment is a critical issue in several parts of Latin American, Asian and African countries. Hence, sanitation projects implemented in these regions are applying ecological sanitation approaches for the recovery of resources like biogas, struvite, carbon dioxide and clean water from domestic wastewater to ‘close the loop’ (Werkneh & Gebru, 2023).

Implementing such approaches has many benefits for the nexus elements. Wetlands, both natural and constructed, are a nature-based solution that can remove nutrients, reduce maintenance costs and energy consumption, and provide wastewater reuse and application of biosolids in agriculture (Amorim Junior et al., 2021; Hdidou et al., 2022; Oliveira et al., 2021). For example, plants from constructed wetlands can be used for biofuel and can reduce cooking fuel by up to 55 per cent (Avellán & Gremillion, 2019). Preventing contamination of drinking water sources, such as aquifers and surface waters, from wastewater is key to ensuring the quality of water resources, providing benefits to biodiversity and improving human health by reducing diseases such as diarrhoeal illness (Fewtrell et al., 2005). Improved sanitation also saves lives and facilitates economic growth (Murungi & van Dijk, 2014; Van Minh & Hung, 2011). Wastewater management is also pitched as an important component of One Health, especially for challenges such as antibiotic resistance (Gholizadeh et al., 2023; Rousham et al., 2018), and addressing marine pollution (Shuval, 2003).

Enhancing the sustainability of sanitation projects entails co-designing and co-managing facilities with communities to invoke a sense of ownership, educating users with proper hygiene maintenance and building capacities to regulate the flow and treatment of wastewater (Brands, 2014) as well as developing effective business models (Amoah et al., 2021; Taron et al., 2023). To address the existing institutional and financial fragmentation in the provision of sanitation, comprehensive and integrated policies supported by cooperative and participatory methods are needed (Loewenson et al., 2023). Closing the funding gap in sanitation calls for specialized cross-sectoral nodal entities or the convergence of efforts across ministries to produce significant sanitation results. For example, the African Water Facility supports governments in the implementation of several water supply and sanitation projects in sub-Saharan Africa (GBAD, 2020). The circular economy appears to be an effective model for sanitation because it focuses on the entire sanitation chain, which includes the provision of toilets, waste collection, treatment and transformation into by-products (Guerra-Rodríguez et al., 2020; Mallory et al., 2020; Smol et al., 2020).

Reusing water in agriculture can help with irrigation, increase agricultural yield and even extend the growing season, while also lowering the danger of drought for crops. Wastewater and agricultural inputs can be used for any kind of crop, depending on the treatment standard and the procedures followed (Hanjra et al., 2012). Farmers in low-income countries have cited the year-round availability of wastewater as a significant reason in favour of its reuse (Drechsel et al., 2010). Systems that assure wastewater treatment prior to discharge mitigate risks to ecosystems and nature’s contributions to people. These include enhancing freshwater quality and safety, which makes it more usable, and lowering pollution and eutrophication in food-producing ecosystems (Corcoran et al., 2010).

Significant amounts of nutrients can be recovered from wastewater and excreta; estimates suggest that all current fertilizer use in smallholder-dominated countries could theoretically be replaced with nutrients recovered from human excreta (Rosemarin et al., 2008) (Box 5.2.5). The 330 km³ of municipal wastewater generated every year worldwide could theoretically irrigate around 15 per cent of all currently irrigated crops (Mateo-Sagasta et al., 2015). The primary organic matter found in excreta and wastewater is composed of lipids, carbs and proteins, which can be used as a soil conditioner which would address declining soil quality. This is especially true if it is combined with food waste and agricultural leftovers (Lal, 2008). Additionally, improving soil organic matter content reduces the impacts of run-off and eutrophication issues by enhancing nutrient and water retention (Renwick et al., 2021).

Efficiently producing renewable energy from wastewater and excreta can also be a successful strategy for mitigating the effects of climate change (Hanjra et al., 2012). Enhancing sanitation and managing wastewater can significantly aid in mitigating climate change by decreasing emissions of carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide (Figure 5.2.3), as the waste sector contributes significantly to global emissions. Producing biogas is an effective approach to extract the energy from these waste streams and the most compatible with resource recovery (Werkneh & Gebru, 2023). It can also be an inexpensive and healthier substitute for wood and other biomass fuels used by low-income households (Rajendran et al., 2012). The estimated 330 km³ of municipal wastewater output could theoretically supply electricity for almost 130 million people based on an average yearly electricity usage of 3,500 kWh/household (Mateo-Sagasta et al., 2015). The controlled burning or incineration of waste streams is another method of extracting energy, which is prevalent in Sweden and Denmark; China is also making significant investments in the incineration of solid waste (Li et al., 2015). For example, changing the treatment design in a more traditional wastewater management system can cut carbon dioxide emissions by 35 per cent (Khiewwijit et al., 2015). Therefore, recovering energy from excreta, wastewater and other organic waste and making treatment systems efficient can aid in increasing access to contemporary energy while also reducing greenhouse gas emissions.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage wastewater (W12)" .

