    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Health interventions have been developed explicitly within integrated landscape and watershed management (Basuki et al., 2022; Jupiter et al., 2013; Thapa et al., 2022), integrated landscape-seascape approaches (Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.9, B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches) and ecosystem health (‘Ecohealth’) approaches (Horwitz & Finlayson, 2011; A. Jenkins et al., 2018; Morrison et al., 2012). These vary in focus, scale and the degree of integration with health departments or within broader health promotion (Nishi & Hashimoto, 2022) (see also section 5.4.5). They are specific, place-based responses, as illustrated in Box 5.4.10 featuring a successful and well documented example from Watershed Interventions for Systems Health in Fiji (WISH Fiji) (Jupiter et al., 2023).

Protected catchments provide clean water for people and the environment, including coastal fisheries (Figure 5.4.5) (see also Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.6, B06, Restoration of coastal and marine systems). Such catchments reduce the distribution and transmission of waterborne diseases, such as leptospirosis, typhoid and dengue which have increased incidence associated with flooding and extreme weather events (Nelson et al., 2022). Under-5 mortality involving diarrhoea particularly occurs in rural areas where most drinking water is sourced direct from (microbially polluted) local catchments (Prüss-Ustün, Wolf, et al., 2019). Restoration of vegetation to protect catchments has further benefits: enhanced carbon sequestration and the reduction of landslides, flooding and the impact of extreme weather events (Stosch et al., 2017). In addition, reducing nutrient and sediment pollution of coastal water protects marine health. Such pollution is a top-ranked pressure on coral reefs worldwide (Andrello et al., 2022).

Integration of human health into landscape and watershed management requires participatory planning; in cultural landscapes participatory planning has enabled IPLC cultural well-being to be addressed (Godden et al., 2023). Technical feasibility is established but requires adaptation to local contexts and issues (Figure 5.4.7). Assessing the economic cost and benefits of such an integrated approach depends on the timescale and sectors or externalities included (Figure 5.4.7). Integrating health within similar interventions such as ridge-to-reef (R. R. Carlson et al., 2019) and land-sea interactions (Cottrell et al., 2018) could be transformative as approximately 1.3 billion people live within 100 km of coastlines in the tropics (Figure 5.4.6).

Health impact assessment is an approach to assess the potential health effects of a policy, programme or project on a population (particularly on vulnerable or disadvantaged groups) that may follow international (WHO, 2023b) or other guidelines (e.g., (e.g., European Centre for Health Policy, 1999)). Stakeholder and community engagement, equity, ethical use of evidence, sustainable development and use across sectors are core features (WHO, 2023b). Health impact assessments help decision-makers make choices about alternatives and improvements to prevent disease or injury and to actively promote health. The approach recognizes the small extent to which health is considered within environmental impact assessments and emphasizes the importance of the learning process to envision a holistic approach to health and well-being (Cameron et al., 2011).

The community health impact assessments are an extension of the health impact assessment; they empower communities to conduct health impact assessments, to use the information and results gained, and to negotiate during public policymaking processes (Leuenberger et al., 2022; Pengkam, 2012). The community health impact assessment is an example of co-production of knowledge between a local community, scientists and policymakers, and a learning process that can lead to changes in unsustainable practices and foster increased understanding of health issues resulting from development projects and policies through collective attempts to find solutions. The community health impact assessment is an approach communities can use to define what means for a community to be healthy (WHO, 1999).

Community health impact assessment can benefit multiple nexus elements (Figure 5.4.5). The approach shows positive impacts on development projects on community health, well-being and livelihoods, including consideration of the health of their ecological systems. Community health impact assessment improves the consideration of health outcomes in development projects and policies on agriculture, water management, forest conservation, mining or tourism (Lajaunie & Morand, 2015). The methodology of health impact assessment, as used in Thailand (Box 5.4.11), is traditionally based on social norms and beliefs and consideration of natural resources. Implementations of health impact assessment have provided measurable pathways and outcomes for reducing the level of communicable and non-communicable diseases associated with development policies (Geneletti, 2016; Sheffield et al., 2014).

Health impact assessment is a powerful process for assessing health impacts in the face of climate change (Patz et al., 2008), such as the impacts of climate change on the health of the population of Vanuatu, which focused on potential adaptation strategies, actions and decision-making (Spickett et al., 2013). A health impact assessment conducted in the semi-arid Kolar district in India revealed positive health impacts on nutrition, sanitation and water quality, but identified negative health impacts on vector-borne and zoonotic diseases within watersheds identified for development projects (Pradyumna et al., 2021). Health impact assessments can be used to identify risks and challenges to health relating to food, for example by identifying risks to food production (Baskin-Graves et al., 2019) and choosing the most promising policy intervention to improve nutrition, as has happened in Fiji and Tonga (Snowdon et al., 2010).

Results of community health impact assessments (including processes, types of data collected, recommendations and outcomes) benefit from dissemination and critical assessment. Community health impact assessments should be associated and/or complemented by a strategic environmental assessment and environmental impact assessment in order to better influence decision-making on the integration of health while addressing biodiversity and ecosystem conservation (Slootweg, 2018). Health impact assessment, and by extension community health impact assessment, is a well-established, response option that can connects health impacts with all elements of the nexus and gives importance to the community's core values. Community health impact assessment applies various adaptive and inclusive tools, making the process highly policy relevant and high in distributional, inclusion and procedural equity (Figure 5.4.6).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Integrated watershed-health interventions (H12)" .

