    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Health promotion through contact with nature is supported by nature on prescription and nature-based health interventions when healthcare professionals refer individual patients or groups with a range of health conditions to activities in nature (Adewuyi et al., 2023; Nguyen et al., 2023).

There is increasing evidence that the natural environment promotes health through supporting physical activity, reducing stress and/or depression, prevention or treatment of obesity and diabetes, cooling during heat waves, and improved quality of life, well-being and social support (Marselle et al., 2021; Yang et al., 2021) (Figure 5.4.5). This includes blue (water) and green spaces (see also Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.2, B02, Urban nature-based solutions) as healing environments. Natural environments also enable beneficial contact with microbial biodiversity and associated health benefits (Box 5.4.6).

Research on health benefits of nature contact is not distributed evenly geographically. Mental health benefits are mainly reported in Europe and Asia, suggesting a bias (Beute et al., 2023). Risk of tick-borne diseases in some regions also highlights the existence of negative consequences (Heylen et al., 2019).

Nature-based health interventions can support biodiversity conservation when combined with increased nature connectedness and pro-environmental behaviour (E. A. Martin et al., 2019; White et al., 2020) but can also put pressure on biodiversity. This became more apparent in the lockdowns during the COVID pandemic (Ferguson et al., 2023). Nature on prescription can promote use of greenspace during heat waves, e.g., among vulnerable groups like the elderly (Arnberger et al., 2017; Kabisch et al., 2021). Health benefits of community gardening (Howarth et al., 2020; Koay & Dillon, 2020; Malberg Dyg et al., 2020) and care (or social) farms (Hassink et al., 2014; Moruzzo et al., 2019) are relevant to nature on prescription (Figure 5.4.9).

Nature on prescription is emerging as a complementary healthcare approach (Adewuyi et al., 2023; Buckley et al., 2020; Kondo, Oyekanmi, et al., 2020; Nguyen et al., 2023; Robinson & Breed, 2019), and can be applied globally (Figure 5.4.7). For example, nature on prescription is gaining interest from the healthcare sector (e.g., in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland (NHS, 2023) and Canada (PaRx, 2023)) but is far from common practice (Astell-Burt et al., 2023) and needs implementation and evaluation research (Buckley et al., 2020).

Access inequalities through gentrification (Anguelovski et al., 2022) and unequal accessibility to natural areas (Rigolon et al., 2018) can impact equity of nature on prescription (Figure 5.4.6). In terms of avoided healthcare costs, it has the potential to contribute significantly at a population level to address non-communicable diseases (A. C. Santos et al., 2023), as well as in decreasing the environmental footprint of the healthcare sector (section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare) (Andrieu et al., 2023; Lenzen et al., 2020; Pichler et al., 2019; Seppänen & Or, 2023). However, nature on prescription cost-effective studies are currently lacking (Hinde et al., 2021). Nature on prescription is potentially consistent with ILK, inclusive and participatory. There is limited evidence of transformative change potential for this response option alone, though when combined with other initiatives for example through nature connectedness, nature conservation, community garden activities and social prescription (Bickerdike et al., 2017) it has significant transformative potential (Figure 5.4.6).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nature on prescription (H05)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-D1> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D2> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Existing governance fails to address the complex, interconnected and interdependent challenges resulting from the pace and scale of environmental change and rising inequalities. Institutions that are fragmented and siloed and policies that are short-term, contradictory or non-inclusive undermine the achievement of global policy frameworks. Addressing the indirect drivers of environmental change, and the underlying values and behaviours influencing those drivers, is crucial for tackling declines in nature and its contributions to people and is integral for improving governance approaches. “Nexus governance approaches” provide more synergistic, holistic and transdisciplinary framings of problems and solutions, include more actors across multiple nexus interactions, emphasize explicit values such as equity and accountability, enable policy alignment, collaboration and integration and are experimental, adaptive and reflexive. Integrating these components of nexus governance and decision-making with multiple actors across sectors and scales can foster a whole-of-society approach, as enshrined in many global policy frameworks." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM-D1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Transforming current siloed modes of governance through more integrative, inclusive, equitable, accountable, coordinated and adaptive approaches enables successful implementation of response options to manage the nexus elements, and their associated direct and indirect drivers, in an integrated manner, with benefits for people and nature now and into the future {A1, A7, D1, D2}." .

