    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Efficient water use in agriculture (both water quantity and quality) can be implemented by planting drought resistant crops, optimizing fertilizer use, and practices such as conservation agriculture (Hobbs et al., 2008; Pittelkow et al., 2015) and can be used to alleviate water scarcity and food insecurity (Blum, 2005; Ruggiero et al., 2017; Sivamani et al., 2000; Tilman et al., 2001) and improve water quality, human health and biodiversity (Darch et al., 2014; Huang et al., 2017; Humbert et al., 2016; Jwaideh et al., 2022; Tilman et al., 2001). There is well established evidence to show that optimizing fertilizer application reduces chemicals in water and thus increases water quality, and the respective economic costs and benefits have been extensively studied (e.g., Garcia-Hernandez et al., 2022; Lötjönen & Ollikainen, 2019; Sihvonen et al., 2021). The benefits of implementation of this response option vary locally (Mbava et al., 2020; Molden et al., 2010), with the most pressing areas being water-scarce regions (Vishwanathan et al., 2021), where 25 per cent of the global population lives (WWAP, 2015). Efficient water use in crop production, which can be achieved through drought-resistant crops, is also one of the 10 science-based policy recommendations for agriculture (Garibaldi et al., 2019) (see Chapter 5.3, section 5.3.3.4). However, the temporal scale is short because crops are grown annually and fertilizer is often applied annually or more frequently (Figure 5.2.5). In contrast, long-term fertilizer application may have effects on human health that are not observed over shorter time periods (Chen et al., 2020).

Implementing this response option benefits water for both humans and ecosystems (Figure 5.2.3) (Evans & Sadler, 2008), as it reduces competition for freshwater supplies (Chouchane et al., 2020) and improves water quality (Darch et al., 2014). Drought resistant crops can decrease farmers’ risks and increase crop yields and farm income (Birthal et al., 2012; Martey et al., 2020), while also providing food security by using underutilized crop species (Icka & Damo, 2023). There are also health benefits from naturally drought resistant plants in their contribution to pharmaceutical compounds (Gechev et al., 2014), and it is possible that the drought resistance as well as the quantity and quality of such compounds can be enhanced with plant growth-promoting rhizobacteria (Vaghela & Gohel, 2023), or through fertilizer reduction that may reduce cancer and non-cancer disease risk by up to 21 per cent from changes in micronutrient (e.g., lead, zinc, copper) uptake (X.-X. Chen et al., 2020). The use of drought resistant crops can improve land management (e.g., Thierfelder et al., 2015) when aligned with conservation agriculture and land tenure rights (Kabubo-Mariara, 2007; Nguyen et al., 2023). Drought resistant crops can also reduce emissions of methane, and implementing optimal fertilizer application rates can reduce greenhouse gas emissions by up to 20 per cent by 2050 (Gao & Cabrera Serrenho, 2023). The use of inorganic nitrogen fertilizer can disproportionally affect uneducated and poor women and children through increased morbidity and mortality by absorbing toxins from rice paddies where the fertilizer is used (Farnworth et al., 2017), and lack of a free market means some countries may impose tariffs that create economic inequities (Filippelli, 2018). Reducing the impacts of agriculture on water quality and quantity can free up water to be used for other uses and is also more cost-effective as fertilizer application rates are reduced to minimize runoff of fertilizers into water sources.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Efficient water use in agriculture (W04)" .

