    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Ecological Intensification “entails the environmentally friendly replacement of anthropogenic inputs and/or enhancement of crop productivity, by including regulating and supporting ecosystem services management in agricultural practices” (Bommarco et al., 2013) (e.g., pollination, pest regulation, soil formation) and is the basis of agroecological practices (Box 5.3.3). Ecological intensification practices span traditional and modern innovations originating from local, Indigenous, farmer and scientific knowledge systems (Caron et al., 2014; Geertsema et al., 2016; Raj et al., 2021). Garibaldi et al. (2019) proposed ten science-based policy recommendations for ecological intensification of agriculture with recent work suggesting that minimum habitat per km²should be between 10-25 per cent (Mohamed et al., 2022):

1) Enhance above- and belowground species diversity 2) Reduce synthetic inputs 3) Enhance soil health 4) Maintain and/or restore natural and semi-natural habitat areas with recent values suggested to at least 10-25 per cent per km² (Mohamed et al., 2022)

5) Enhance habitat diversity 6) Implement a landscape design plan to integrate practices 7) Protect and efficiently use water resources 8) Evaluate agricultural productivity and ecosystem services over the long-term 9) Consider multiple outcome benefits 10) Facilitate participatory action, multiple knowledge systems and farmer training Ecological intensification of croplands can be applied across the 1,244 Mha of croplands globally (Potapov et al., 2022) (Figure 5.3.5). Ecological intensification can increase the water holding and infiltration capacity of soils, improving crop production, while also strongly mitigating drought impacts and modestly reducing flood impacts (Kane et al., 2021; Renwick et al., 2021). Increasing soil organic matter is a “no regrets” intervention (Bossio et al. 2020; Bradford et al., 2019) with multiple food, water and soil biodiversity benefits. It also has a positive impact on mitigating climate change globally although the scale of impact is less certain ranging between 0.9-1.85 GtCO2e year⁻¹ (Zomer et al., 2017).

Above ground, ecological intensification intercepts land-based runoff of nutrients (see section 5.3.3.7) and biocides (see section 5.3.3.9) before they enter waterways (Luke et al., 2019). It also creates habitat (Marshall & Moonen, 2002; Mkenda et al., 2019; Vickery et al., 2009) and habitat connectivity (Anderson et al., 2022) and provides pest regulation, pollination and nutrient interception and has the potential to create barriers to pest dispersal (Avelino et al., 2012). This reduces dependency on pesticides, which has positive impacts on human health and biodiversity (de Vries, 2021). Favouring cropping systems that more closely match the ecological function capacity of the biome they are located in has very strong climate change mitigation potential equal to 42-47 percent of the difference between current and potential biomass stocks (Erb et al., 2018).

Habitat elements can be used by natural pest regulating and pollinating species which are essential for fruit, nuts and legumes production (Ellis et al., 2015; Klein, 2006). Retaining >20 per cent habitat per km² is required to maintain pest regulation, pollination, pollution interception and soil erosion reduction; between 33-66 per cent of croplands are below this threshold globally (Mohamed et al., 2024).

When ecological intensification practices are adapted to local contexts they can provide increases in both environmental performance and long-term yield (Garbach et al., 2017; Tamburini et al., 2020), including in highly intensified conventional systems (Asbjornsen et al., 2014; Zaimes et al., 2004; Zemp et al., 2023). Trade-offs between yield, and one or more indicators of environmental performance, can arise and are best handled through combined application of local and scientific knowledge to adapt practices to local landscapes, capacities and contexts (Carmenta et al., 2020; Estrada-Carmona et al., 2014; Milder et al., 2014; Zanzanaini et al., 2017).

Ecological intensification is dependent on farmers for uptake but can be hampered by increased labour and material costs, loss of productive lands, or weed and disease spread (Figure 5.3.4). Subsidies, payments for ecosystem services or certification, supported by policy measures, are significant means of enabling farmers participation (section 5.3.3.13).

Embracing agroecological transitions towards sustainable food systems are supported by the development of effective and innovative policies, institutions and markets that enable and support transformative change (Tittonell, 2023). Visual narratives using the 10 Elements of Agroecology offer a flexible tool to support integrated policymaking and food system transformation (Box 5.3.5). The concept of visual narratives can be applied in food systems policy analysis to highlight promising entry points for policy interventions, identify opportunities for enhancing co-benefits through the nexus approach and help build policy packages to catalyse food systems transformation (FAO, 2023a).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological intensification – croplands (F04)" .

