    ipbes:hasDescription """Coastal blue carbon ecosystems (CBCE) are marine habitats composed of rooted vegetation (Nellemann et al., 2009), namely mangroves (Giri et al., 2011; Spalding et al., 2010), seagrasses (Duarte, 1991; Green & Short, 2003) and salt marshes (Mason et al., 2023). Some authors also include marine sediments, components of the biological carbon cycle (e.g., whales), calcifying organisms and macroalgae in blue carbon estimates. This response option focuses on mangroves, seagrasses and salt marshes, which are responsible for 45 per cent of the organic carbon burial in the ocean (Duarte et al., 2005, 2013), and the emerging macroalgae. CBCE have many impacts on climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and health (Figure 5.5.3, Table 5.5.10).

Mangrove forests support Indigenous institutions and cosmologies around the world, e.g., African Joola (Diatta et al., 2020), American Wayampi (Dugast, 2002), in Brunei (Islam & Yahya, 2017) or Indian festivals and celebrations (Jalais, 2014; Uddin et al., 2013). As such, mangroves sustain ILK (Walters et al., 2008) often by women (Chuku et al., 2022; Crow & Carney, 2013). Kelp forests are central to Canadian Haida Gwaii people, whose ethics and values have been adapted to support restoration and are aligned with ecosystem-based management principles (Eger et al., 2022). Kelp forests are also part of the traditional knowledge of the Kawésqar people of southern Chile (Friedlander et al., 2021). Saltmarshes are sacred places for the North American Gullah Geechee (Seabrook, 2012) and native Cajuns (Gramling & Hagelman, 2005). In contrast, seagrass meadows have limited societal recognition (Unsworth et al., 2019), although examples of local communities’ involvement exist in the Philippines (Quevedo et al., 2020), the Solomon Islands (Lauer & Aswani, 2010) or Zanzibar (de la Torre-Castro & Rönnbäck, 2004). They also play a role in preserving valuable submerged archaeological and historical heritage (Krause-Jensen et al., 2019) (Figure 5.5.4).

Restoring CBCE is at TRL 9 (Figure 5.5.5). However, there are issues involving land tenure and jurisdictional boundaries that should be considered within marine spatial planning (Cumming & Dobbs, 2020; H. D. Smith et al., 2011). Lack of monitoring, communication barriers and poor implementation have been reported for mangroves (M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2006; D. A. Friess et al., 2022; Su et al., 2021) and seagrasses (Herrera et al., 2023; Unsworth et al., 2019). Coastal wetlands are also often perceived as a source of pests and diseases, danger and odour (D. Friess et al., 2020). Seaweeds necessitate a different approach as they involve management of the fate of the carbon they produce and the locations in which carbon accumulates, which may be distant from the sites of production (e.g., in deep water) (Krause-Jensen et al., 2019; Lovelock & Duarte, 2019).

This response option aligns with several SDGs and with the United Nations Decade for Ocean Sciences and the United Nations Decade on Restoration (2021-2030) (Figure 5.5.4). Despite its limited carbon removal potential (3 per cent at its upper range) (Williamson & Gattuso, 2022), its high co-benefits makes CBCE a ‘low or no regret’ response option (Bindoff et al., 2019). This is illustrated by the Sine-Saloum delta in Senegal, which supports diverse livelihoods through its mangrove ecosystem (Box 5.5.3). However, the area faces environmental challenges and social issues, necessitating integrated management and community-based restoration projects to ensure sustainable development and climate change mitigation.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration (C13)" .

