    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """To be healthy, diets must meet four core principles driven by human biology and universal in their application. Specifically, healthy diets are adequate in essential nutrients, without excess, diverse in a variety of foods for various food groups, balanced in energy intake and sources, and moderate in the consumption of nutrients and foods associated with negative health outcomes.

Shifting to healthy diets includes both increasing access to the diversity of culturally relevant under-consumed foods while significantly reducing unhealthy over-consumption, notably of sugar, salt and fat including red-meat, impacting nearly 2 billion adults today (González et al., 2020; Poore & Nemecek, 2018; Swinburn et al., 2019) (see also Chapter 5.4, section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.15, C15, Sustainable healthy diets). Insufficient access to healthy diets is the most significant driver of premature mortality today affecting more than half of the global population and accounting for six of the top 11 risk factors driving the global burden of disease (Afshin et al., 2019).

A shift to healthy diets (Willett, 2019) alone represents highly significant reduction in environmental pressure including a 29 per cent reduction in food-based GHG emissions and a 5-9 per cent reduction in other environmental impacts including reduced nutrient pollution, blue water consumption and land allocated to production. Shifts to healthy diets that also focus on sustainability could result in a 56 per cent reduction in food-based GHG emissions, with a 6-22 per cent reduction in other environmental factors without compromising health (Kim et al., 2020; Springmann et al., 2018) (Figure 5.3.3).

Impacts on biodiversity (Kastner et al., 2012) and climate change are dependent on policies and actions that halt the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (section 5.3.3.1). There are weaker effects on aquatic biodiversity which are more dependent on sustainable intensification (section 5.3.3.3), ecological intensification (sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and reduced pollution (sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9). Shifts to healthy diets have weak impacts on water savings and may require additional freshwater for fruit, nut and vegetable production (Springmann et al., 2018). They also have weak effects on water quality, increasing dependencies on nutrient applications and pollution (sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.7).

Significant increases in diet-related non-communicable disease incidence have been observed in most countries, including where malnutrition is in decline (Afshin et al., 2019; Swinburn et al., 2019) (Chapter 2, section 2.4.1). Regionally adapted shifts to healthy diets would reduce 11 million premature deaths per year (Afshin et al., 2019; Springmann et al., 2018). Between food group diversity is particularly important to human health and nutrition (Lachat et al., 2018), while within food group diversity is important to individual and cultural food choices (Argumedo et al., 2020; Johns et al., 2013; Johns & Eyzaguirre, 2006; Khoury et al., 2014; Solomons, 2000) compatible with healthy diets. This emphasizes that crop and food diversity receive insufficient attention despite their significant impacts on nexus issues.

Current food production is incompatible with healthy diets with significant underproduction of major food groups (Kc et al., 2018; Willett, 2019). Alignment would require increases in fruit and vegetable production (Mason-D’Croz et al., 2019) of 8.72 per cent yr⁻¹(Kc et al., 2018), and nut production by 150 per cent by 2050 (Willett, 2019), zero to minimal growth in cereal production (Kc et al., 2018), and significant global reductions in the production of ruminant meat independent of changes in meat production practices (Springmann et al., 2018; Willett, 2019).

Shifts to healthy diets are amongst the most cost-effective food system response option available (Bodirsky, 2023; Ruggeri-Laderchi et al., 2024) (Figure 5.3.4). Diet-related non-communicable diseases currently cost $11 trillion in health costs alone; this is marginally lower than the economic value of food estimated at $12 trillion annually(Ruggeri Laderchi et al., 2024).

Several actions are possible. Aligning agricultural, trade and subsidy policies, as well as health policies and actions, to food-based dietary guidelines (Box 5.3.8) and public food procurement (Box 5.3.9) have significant potential (Damania et al., 2023; FAO, IFAD, UNICEF, WFP and WHO, 2022; Springmann & Freund, 2022; Valencia et al., 2019) (see also section 5.3.3.13). Consumer education and food health and sustainability labelling, alone or in combination, can guide consumer choice (Cecchini & Warin, 2016; Contreras-Manzano et al., 2022; Hawley et al., 2013; Julia & Hercberg, 2017; Taillie et al., 2021) while providing a common standard for food companies. However, these are insufficient as stand-alone interventions (Borgmeier & Westenhoefer, 2009). Healthy diets have been found to potentially contribute 70 per cent of the benefits of a comprehensive food system transformation through their direct impacts on health and their indirect impacts on production, including stemming pressures for land use change (section 5.3.3.1) and promoting biodiversity (sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.5).

Sustainable healthy diets are necessary to stay within the Paris Agreement long-term global goal to limit the increase in global average temperature to 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels (M. A. Clark et al., 2020) and GBF targets (Leclère et al., 2018). However, there is inadequate implementation of policies, programs and interventions – even those with proven efficacy – with only limited spending going to improving nutrition by national governments in their own countries and by international development organizations (Global Nutrition Report, 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sustainable healthy diets (F11)" .

