    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Reducing plastic pollution as a response option can be applied globally given the threat it poses to nexus elements notably, water (Barcelo & Pico, 2020; Borrelle et al., 2020; R. Kumar et al., 2021; Mbachu et al., 2021) (Figure 5.3.6). Growing demand for food and food convenience, while increasing food safety and reducing food loss and waste, has increased food packaging, including single-use plastics (Geyer, 2020; Ncube et al., 2021; A. T. Williams & Rangel-Buitrago, 2022). Plastics can accumulate and persist in the environment for several decades, requiring short to prolonged impact mitigation strategies (Yao et al., 2022).

Reducing plastic pollution has several impacts on the nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). It can reduce access to healthy food, and reductions in plastic packaging for perishable foods can lower their shelf life reducing availability and increasing food waste. These impacts may be higher than the detrimental impacts of plastics although there is currently low confidence and high uncertainty in these numbers (White & Lockyer, 2020).

Implementation of measures to reduce single-use plastics directly reduce plastic waste in water courses and hence improves water quality (Kiessling et al., 2023), which has positive impacts on wildlife (Puskic et al., 2020) and added benefits of reducing flood risk from blocked sewage systems. There is also good evidence that reducing plastic waste in soils improves soil quality (by reducing soil bulk density and improving soil porosity and hydraulic conductivity), resulting in higher yields (Koskei et al., 2021; Shah & Wu, 2020). For example, shifting from use of plastic mulch to biodegradable mulch in farming can reduce plastic residue accumulation in soils, with numerous environmental benefits including enhanced food production (Koskei et al., 2021; Shah & Wu, 2020).

A higher percentage of recycled plastic content could reduce health risks, resulting in ‘longer human life per tonne of plastic recycled’ (Deeney et al., 2022). In addition, reducing plastic pollution is associated with reduced exposure to carcinogenic or endocrine disrupting chemicals (Revel et al., 2018; M. Smith et al., 2018).

Reducing plastic pollution through substitutes and recycling are socially acceptable but there are geographical disparities and information gaps and the cost of alternatives can preclude change (Ciano et al., 2023; Filho et al., 2021; Oke et al., 2020) (Figure 5.3.4). Political will to reduce pollution from plastics broadly exists but implementation is often problematic. Bans of single-use plastics have not been effective in some countries, and subsidizing recycling often requires costly government intervention (Ogutu et al., 2023; Raab et al., 2023; Williams & Rangel-buitrago, 2022). There is limited evidence of increased costs to consumers from novel packaging materials (Ciano et al., 2023).

There are high production costs associated with shifting from use of plastics in the food system yet the economic, social and environmental performance of biodegradable plastics is relatively low and uncertain (Moshood et al., 2022). Depending on specific interventions, technical feasibility varies (Figure 5.3.5). Substitution of plastics (e.g., edible films and coatings) are still in less advanced stages and could create leakage to raw materials (e.g., wood and crops). Although policy-based options are better developed, such as those guided by biodegradable and compostable plastic product standards (FAO, 2021), they are weakly implemented or in a piecemeal manner at best (Shah & Wu, 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reduce plastic pollution (F09)" .

