        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WLN6GYLL> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Optimized management of existing forests, prevention of forest losses, reforestation, forest restoration and afforestation are among the main multi-purpose and cost-effective options for climate change mitigation via solutions based on nature (Kaarakka et al., 2021). Although forest carbon sequestration and storage are not a substitute for urgent reductions of fossil CO2 emissions (Anderson et al., 2019; Seddon et al., 2021), the conservation, restoration and sustainable management of forests offer contributions to achieve climate and biodiversity targets. For example, reduced deforestation and forest degradation plays an important role in climate change mitigation and adaptation. Likewise, improved and sustainable forest management (ISFM) maintains and enhances the social, economic, ecological and cultural values of forests for the benefit of present and future generations (Kaarakka et al., 2021; MacDicken et al., 2015). Reduced forest degradation, forest restoration and ISFM are strongly interconnected in terms of their objectives and approach.

The mitigation potential of reduced deforestation and forest degradation is high (up to 5.8 Gt CO2-eq/yr) (P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.3). ISFM also has a high mitigation potential as it maintains forest carbon sinks (estimates are up to about 2 GtCO2/yr) (Griscom et al., 2017; Roe et al., 2019), it makes forests more resilient to climate-driven disturbances and it temporarily stores carbon in long-lived products (Anderegg et al., 2020; Daigneault & Favero, 2021). Reforestation has a large mitigation potential (up to 10 GtCO2/yr, with realistic figures around 3 GtCO2/yr) (Roe et al., 2019). It can be implemented globally, but the highest benefits occur in the tropics where carbon sequestration rates and concentration of endangered species that live in the forests are high (Locatelli et al., 2015; Prudhomme et al., 2020) (Figure 5.5.5). In the tropics, biophysical effects of reforestation also reinforce the cooling contributions from CO2 removal, while in areas with seasonal snow cover, the cooling benefits of reforestation are reduced due to changes in surface albedo (Perugini et al., 2017; Windisch et al., 2021). In terms of forest restoration potential, global forest carbon storage is currently below its natural potential (with an estimated total deficit of 226 GtC). Since most of this potential lies in areas with existing forests where human footprint is low, effective ecosystem protection and management can promote forest restoration up to 139 GtC by allowing forests to recover to maturity (Mo et al., 2023).

All forest-based solutions for climate action have the potential to co-deliver across multiple nexus elements (Table 5.5.9; Figure 5.5.3; Box 5.5.2). Interactions are mostly positive, with the main potential trade-offs connected to land competition and food security. Forest projects should be conducted in light of the local context and community to be successful, and be developed together with measures that improve the efficiency in the agri-food sector at both local and global levels (e.g., closing yield gaps in developing countries, reducing beef consumption) to decrease pressure on land resources and reduce inequalities (Humpenöder et al., 2022).

Forest-based solutions are relatively inexpensive and may enable distributional, recognitional and procedural equity, provided that identities, knowledge, values and views of local communities are considered (Erbaugh et al., 2020; Lenzi et al., 2021) (Figure 5.5.4). There is evidence that reforestation programmes have helped to support local livelihoods and decrease poverty, but economic benefits are dependent on CO2 sequestration pricing schemes, support for nature conservation benefits and existence of national targets and/or private investments (Brauman et al., 2013; Heilmayr et al., 2020; Humpenöder et al., 2022; Ota et al., 2020). In cases where equity was not recognized or enhanced, the risks of suboptimal outcomes have been higher (Börner et al., 2020; Rantala et al., 2013; Schreckenberg et al., 2016).

Social acceptability of forest-based options is usually higher in developed countries, where forests are regulated via certified practices of management and various levels of protection, while it tends to be lower in developing countries and remote areas that are highly dependent on wood supply for heating or cooking (Paletto et al., 2013; Purnomo et al., 2005) (Figure 5.5.4). Participatory processes, education and transfer of rights to local communities can contribute to procedural equity and challenge power imbalances (Chandra & Mukhopadhyay, 2022; Karsenty & Salau, 2023; Nantongo et al., 2019; Sadowska, 2022). More generally, community forest management in developing countries is a viable model for sustainable forestry, carbon sequestration and livelihoods (Apipoonyanon et al., 2020; Charnley et al., 2022). These activities benefit from being implemented by prioritizing local communities’ rights to manage and restore forests (Erbaugh et al., 2020). The implementation may require financial and technological support to minimize trade-offs between socio-economic and environmental outcomes, with active consideration of gender dynamics (Karsenty & Salau, 2023; Sheppard et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forest-based practices to address climate change (C12)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-C2> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C10>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D2> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options that reduce competition for land or other resources can facilitate other response options in achieving positive outcomes across several nexus elements. For example, sustainable healthy diets, reduced food loss and waste, ecological intensification and sustainable intensification of agriculture and ecosystem restoration can be combined (i.e., bundled together), incentivized and driven by behaviour change to halt or reverse biodiversity loss, to reduce land conversion and water pollution, to improve human health, and to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Some response options are inherently similar to bundles in that they comprise multiple synergistic actions, such as Indigenous food systems that emerge from Indigenous and local knowledge and traditional practices and which are based on holistic world views. Response options designed and implemented in isolation, without considering interconnections among them, may result in fewer benefits across the nexus elements. Integrated planning and governance approaches, rights-based approaches and aligning finance can facilitate the bundling and sequencing of response options to achieve synergies or multiple co-benefits and address trade-offs and even achieve cost savings. Ensuring the full and effective participation of a wide range of actors, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities, in the co-design, coordination and implementation of bundles of response options can help to increase the magnitude and equity of benefits as well as to facilitate the emergence of new options from collaborative contexts." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM-C2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Response options can facilitate or impede each other, leading to potential synergies and trade-offs among them. The efficacy of response options in realizing nexus-wide benefits can be enhanced by implementing them together or sequentially, as some response options enable others or amplify their benefits {C6, C8, C10, D2}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5> ;
    ipbes:KeyMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-A1>,
