    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2Q3YPW53>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3FI8GM5C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/5VHZGQN3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7M2UFHXP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7N3SBZLZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9LID65R5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AY8W7T4U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C66Y55Y5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GDDTXC2Y>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GMHAQ4TA>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HYK9IDW5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IQN33KAG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IS68IDU4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/K9U2ASA7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/MICPJ2KI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PMJ22R7V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PY6MZ7CM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QQZHGWAV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R2SXZIEV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RJ7UQLAU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RT4279IS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W563S4UK> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """In chapter 18 of the IPCC’s Sixth Assessment Report, five domains are identified for rapid transitions to facilitate climate change mitigation, adaptation and sustainable development: (i) land, oceans and ecosystems; (ii) energy; (iii) settlement and infrastructure; (iv) industrial; and (v) societal systems (Schipper et al., 2022). These system transitions are used for organizing the response options that have been assessed in this subchapter.

Land, oceans and ecosystems transitions: Land, oceans and ecosystems are experiencing significant transitions driven by climate change, with implications for resilience and nature’s contributions to people (NCP) (IPBES, 2019).

Energy systems transitions: Energy systems require shifts towards low carbon (including renewable) energy sources, improved energy efficiency and carbon capture and storage to meet climate change mitigation objectives and abate emissions of CO2 from fossil fuels (IEA, 2019). Ongoing energy transitions respond to climatic and non-climatic considerations, with sustainable development priorities driving change (Clarke et al., 2022).

Settlement and infrastructure systems transitions: Urban and rural settlements and infrastructure play a crucial role in climate-resilient futures but face risks from climate stresses (K. Davidson et al., 2019). Enhancing urban resilience can be achieved through investments in disaster risk reduction, climate-resilient green infrastructure and updated building codes (Dodman et al., 2022). Integrating nature-based solutions and green infrastructure can enhance resilience and support economic development (Shaneyfelt et al., 2017).

Industrial systems transitions: Industrial systems transitions comprise dematerialization and decarbonization (Petrides et al., 2018; Worrell et al., 2016), supported by effective governance, enlightened policies, meticulous regulations and empowering corporate strategies (Singh & Chudasama, 2021b).

Social systems transitions: Societal systems transitions focus on changing behaviours, attitudes, values and consciousness across society (De Witt et al., 2016; Hayward, 2012; Schipper et al., 2022; Wolf et al., 2013).

To select a set of response options for assessment, a non-exhaustive long list of about 70 options, representative of the five system transitions, were drawn mainly from previous IPCC and IPBES reports (Babiker et al., 2022; IPCC, 2018;

McElwee et al., 2020; Pörtner et al., 2021; Schipper et al., 2022; P. Smith, Calvin, et al., 2020; P. Smith et al., 2022), supplemented by others identified by the chapter author team. The contribution of each response option to climate change adaptation or mitigation at the global scale and its contribution to the other nexus elements was assessed from the literature. Details of these response options, their spatial scale of implementation, the primary actors needed to implement them, the relative cost of implementation, and the contribution to mitigation (gigatonne carbon dioxide equivalent per year) and adaptation (millions of people who could benefit) at the global scale, and their contribution of other nexus elements: biodiversity, water, food and health (with supporting references) are available in data management report.

From the long list of about 70 response options, 15 response options (Tables 5.5.2 and 5.5.3) were selected to analyse in greater detail by scoring against the common criteria used by all subchapters of Chapter 5 (see Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1). Additional criteria for selection of the fifteen were to ensure a spread of case studies across the following dimensions: representation across the five system transitions, strong climate change adaptation or mitigation potential, coverage of different geographical regions, response options applicable to land/freshwater/ocean, examples from rural/urban, examples with a strong component on Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK), and with a focus on solutions-oriented examples that show nexus benefits.

Of the 15 response options assessed, five focal response options were selected or more detailed consideration: C11, Agroecology (section 5.5.3.11); C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change (section 5.5.3.12); C13, Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration (section 5.5.3.13); C14, Urban nature-based solutions (section 5.5.3.14) and C15, Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.5.3.15). These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action. These fifteen response options, including the five focal response options, are described in this section. Section 5.5.4 on nexus governance also delineates 21 distinct policy instruments and enablers for supporting the implementation of the response options.

Four of the fifteen response options (C01, C07, C09, C11) deliver exclusively positive outcomes for all elements of the nexus, with a further seven (C02, C03, C11, C12, C13, C14, C15) potentially delivering positive outcomes across all nexus elements (Figure 5.5.3). The remaining four (C04 Wetland restoration and conservation, C05 Offshore wind power, C06 Solar photovoltaics on land, C08 Reduce short lived climate pollutants) have negative impacts on at least one nexus element, though even for these response options, they have potential positive impacts on three or more nexus elements. For some of the potential negative impacts, methods of implementation or additional measures could be taken to minimize the risk of negative outcomes, as shown by cells with mixed outcomes (Figure 5.5.3).

Figure 5.5.4 provides scoring of the response options on political priorities and international frameworks, social acceptability, economic and equity implications and transformative change potential. All response options have the potential for high social acceptability, are well aligned with political priorities, and all are well aligned with political priorities for biodiversity, climate change and the SDGs (see section 5.5.6 for further discussion of alignment with global goals). All response options have potential gross and individual economic costs associated with them, although all also have potential for economic benefits, and all except two (C07, C08) have the potential to lower economic risks. In terms of equity, all response options have the potential to promote inclusion and/or recognition and procedural equity, and all either unequivocally support distributional equity (C01, C02, C06, C07, C09, C10, C11, C12, C13, C15), can support it if implemented well (C03, C04, C08, C14), with only C05 judged to mostly have a negative impact on distributional equity. All response options are judged to have positive transformative change potential (Figure 5.5.4).

Figure 5.5.5 illustrates temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the response options. Most response options are applicable all over the world, though some are limited to specific systems (e.g., urban nature-based solutions to urban areas, peatland restoration to where peats occur), but most are implemented at the micro- or meso-scales. Most response options are implemented over the short- to long-term, but five (C01, C02, C05, C11, C15) deliver benefits only in the medium- to long-term, nine (C03, C04, C06, C07, C09, C10, C12, C13, C14) over all timescales, with C08 delivering benefits only in the short- and medium-term. Most response options are already widely implemented (Technology Readiness Level, TRL = 7-9), with only two (C08, C11) having components at a lower TRL level, though some components of these response options are at TRL level 9 (Figure 5.5.5).

A detailed assessment of the response options, which led to the scoring in Figures 5.5.3, 5.5.4 and 5.5.5, is described in the following sections 5.5.3.1 to 5.5.3.15.

Notes
2. The data management report describing the Chapter 5 response option scoring criteria and process is available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.
3. The complete scoring templates are included with the Chapter 5 data management report describing the response option scoring criteria and process; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for climate change" .

