    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Biodiversity supports all the other elements of the nexus and good quality of life through nature’s contributions to people (IPBES, 2019b, 2020, 2022b; IPCC, 2019; Pörtner et al., 2021) (see Chapter 2). Policies, decisions and interventions from the biodiversity sector directly contribute to ecosystem state and associated biodiversity, and thereby to food, water, health and climate change. The conservation, restoration and sustainable use of ecosystems, species and genetic diversity contribute to multiple regulating nature’s contributions to people with benefits to other nexus elements. These include climate regulation, regulation of the quality and quantity of water, and regulation of hazards and extreme events (Bimrah et al., 2022; Gelcich et al., 2019; P. A. Harrison et al., 2014; Martín-López et al., 2019; Mori et al., 2017; Riis et al., 2020; Woodhead et al., 2019). Some ecosystem processes may be hazardous to human health and assets, e.g., fire spreading from flammable shrublands or forests into human settlements (Norman et al., 2021). Habitats, soil biodiversity and gene pools for wild relatives of domesticated species, and for species with yet unknown uses, are all essential to sustaining food provision, health and livelihoods (Jaradat, 2015; Reyes-García et al., 2015). Wild species can impact positively or negatively on human and domestic animal health (Campagne et al., 2018; Daszak et al., 2000; P. Pereira & Baró, 2022). Organisms both beneficial and harmful organisms to food and fibre production, and human and domestic animal health, such as pollinators, weeds, pathogens and fish nurseries depend on landscape and seascape connectivity (W. J. Allen et al., 2022; M. G. E. Mitchell et al., 2013). Furthermore, spatial spillovers of organisms and material or energy flows between protected and managed lands, inland waters, coastal and marine ecosystems or cities have critical consequences for water, food and health (W. J. Allen et al., 2022; Case et al., 2020; Di Lorenzo et al., 2020; Grass et al., 2019). From a relational perspective, nature supports social, cultural, spiritual and physical experiences, critically contributing to human physical and mental health. Nature also supports identities, especially the cultural and spiritual lives of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC), and the maintenance of traditional livelihoods of local communities (Daniel et al., 2012; Reyes-García et al., 2023).

Biodiversity, therefore, contributes across multiple policy objectives (Geijzendorffer et al., 2017; Jacobs et al., 2022). In particular, biodiversity is the direct target of SDG14 and SDG15, but it also underpins SDG 2, 3, 6, 11, 13 and indirectly contributes to most others (Blicharska et al., 2019) (Chapter 1, section 1.3.2). Addressing the biodiversity crisis is central to addressing nexus challenges (Chapter 1, section 1.1.2) and thereby important policy instruments, including the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (e.g., targets 1, 8, 10, 11 and 12) and the Paris Agreement. Outside these three frameworks, however, the biodiversity sector is often not central in policy and decision-making, including at the international level. This is primarily because the value of biodiversity is not comprehensively captured in economic decision-making (Dasgupta, 2021; Pascual, Balvanera, Anderson, et al., 2023) (Chapter 6, section 6.2). Additionally, the benefits of biodiversity conservation are often perceived as a matter for local governance and management, while in fact biodiversity benefits and the impacts of its degradation permeate every human activity and scale, particularly through supply of nature’s contributions to people (Díaz et al., 2019).

Taking a nexus approach can prompt biodiversity actors to considerably broaden their scope and engage in cross-sectoral interactions beyond the traditional focus on protected areas. This includes biodiversity actors considering all components of diversity (systemic, inter and intraspecific) across terrestrial, inland water, coastal and marine ecosystems, including those highly transformed by human activities such as agricultural lands, forest plantations, aquaculture or cities (Ellis, 2019; Hoban et al., 2021; Leadley et al., 2022). Moreover, nexus approaches focus attention on actions that reverse drivers of biodiversity loss, restore biodiversity and integrate planning across landscapes and seascapes beyond protected areas (Leclère et al., 2020). In addition, nexus approaches provide an opportunity for actors to embrace multiple values of nature to people (Pascual, Balvanera, & Christie, 2023). Implementing these changes requires diverse actors beyond public institutions (governmental and intergovernmental agencies) and non-governmental organizations (NGOs) that are dedicated to nature protection, including private sector organizations from other sectors (e.g., agriculture, forestry, fisheries, health) as well as customary institutions of IPLC.

Section 5.1.3 shows how these actors have broadened their scope in multiple response options.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Influence of biodiversity on the other nexus elements" .

