    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Urban nature-based solutions are a common response option to address multiple sustainable development goals in urbanized populated areas (Babí Almenar et al., 2021;

Carvalho et al., 2022; Goodwin et al., 2023; Kabisch et al., 2016, 2022; Kalantari et al., 2023; Raymond et al., 2017). Urban nature-based solutions range from urban forests to sustainable urban drainage systems (Escobedo et al., 2019; European Commission. Directorate General for Research and Innovation., 2021; Ruangpan et al., 2020), and are regarded as an “umbrella concept of established urban ecosystem-based approaches” (Kabisch et al., 2022, p. 1388; also: Babí Almenar et al., 2021; Bayulken et al., 2021; Frantzeskaki et al., 2019; Goodwin et al., 2023). However, implementation remains geographically concentrated in the Global North (Grace et al., 2021; Moosavi et al., 2021). Differences in infrastructure, attitudes towards public spaces and more decentralized governance in the Global South may limit the utility of conclusions from the academic literature (Diep et al., 2022; Goodwin et al., 2023; Kabisch et al., 2022; Keeler et al., 2019; Nassary et al., 2022; Wolff et al., 2022). This is even more salient considering a key factor for urban nature-based solutions implementation is attention to context (Kabisch et al., 2022; B. B. Lin et al., 2021).

To assess urban nature-based solutions implementation, 15 case studies, randomly selected among 118 published studies, were scored (Figure 5.1.5). These showed that implementation of urban nature-based solutions is currently overwhelmingly focused on providing protection from climate risks that are exacerbated in urban areas (e.g., disaster risk reduction) (WWF, 2021). Urban nature-based solutions are “explicitly solution-orientated” (Dorst et al., 2019), but their current implementation is often skewed to resolving a single issue. Most often this is climate change: urban nature-based solutions comprise half of all urban climate change adaptation interventions to date (Goodwin et al., 2023). This single issue focus is also seen for water (Acreman et al., 2021; Oral et al., 2020), health (H.-Y. Liu et al., 2021) and food (Carvalho et al., 2022). Rarely are other nexus elements considered holistically (epitomised by the prevalence of non-native plants in urban green infrastructure; e.g., Mata et al., 2021), with negative impacts almost never quantified (Keeler et al., 2019).

Urban nature-based solutions have a higher chance of success when the level of co-design and participation of those living in proximity is high (Bai et al., 2019; Kabisch et al., 2022). Socioenvironmental justice is a key consideration as nature-based solutions may not benefit all actors equally, potentially even displacing some (Kabisch et al., 2016). The level of co-design and participation is variable across cases, as is the integration of multiple world views: projects tend to focus on purely anthropocentric value, with a heavy bias towards academic knowledge systems (Pineda-Pinto, Frantzeskaki, Chandrabose, et al., 2022; Pineda-Pinto, Frantzeskaki, & Nygaard, 2022). This echoes calls for more research into effective, more inclusive participatory approaches than basic consultation or partnership (Bayulken et al., 2021; Carvalho et al., 2022; B. B. Lin et al., 2021; Puskás et al., 2021).

Despite the potential for urban nature-based solutions to redefine the relationship between people and nature, their governance and funding remains centralized. This underpins the often limited participation (e.g., Goodwin et al., 2023; Box 5.1.3) and merely reformist or incremental transformative change potential (Hoover et al., 2021). Despite monitoring and evaluation being highlighted as a key goal (e.g., Kabisch et al., 2022; Raymond et al., 2017), the uptake of urban nature-based solutions is currently compromised by its lack of effectiveness. Beneficial holistic outcomes are often implicitly assumed (Pineda-Pinto, Frantzeskaki, & Nygaard, 2022), consistent with holistic outcomes being rarely reported for nature-based solutions in general (Chausson et al., 2020).

When reported, implementation tends to flow through municipal planning and zoning instruments developed by local authorities, in some cases responding to regional or national policy. Finance for urban nature-based solutions is overwhelmingly provided by the public sector, with little to no examples of public-private partnerships (Toxopeus & Polzin, 2021). Urban nature-based solutions are occasionally supported by local community organizations, but even fewer cases involve rights-based instruments and customary norms or social and cultural options. However, their wide-scale adoption is still limited, with a need to include nature-based solutions in urban governance and planning agendas (Mendonça et al., 2021). Decision making is mostly hierarchical, with very few involving polycentric or inclusive governance, despite polycentric governance being identified as an enabler for urban nature-based solutions elsewhere (J. G. C. Martin et al., 2021).

Monitoring and evaluation is critical for documenting environmental impacts of nature-based solutions (Kabisch et al., 2022; Raymond et al., 2017), and frequency of measurement needs to be high as nature-based solutions are dynamic and uncertain (Chrysoulakis et al., 2021; Moosavi et al., 2021). Earth observation of nature-based solutions can be a key tool to assess changes in land cover, but ability to document urban metabolism modification (e.g., energy, water and carbon fluxes and balances) still remains underexplored (Chrysoulakis et al., 2021; Frantzeskaki et al., 2019).

Nevertheless, this analysis corroborates conclusions for nature-based solutions more broadly (although the majority of NbS studies take place in an urban land use; see H. I. Hanson et al., 2020) that more synergies than trade-offs are reported when considering other elements of the nexus: as little as 2 per cent of nature-based solutions studies report across a broad spectrum of outcomes, with many assuming implementation delivers multiple benefits (Chausson et al., 2020; Pineda-Pinto, Frantzeskaki, & Nygaard, 2022).

There is widespread agreement that urban nature-based solutions represent efficient solutions to facilitate adaptation to climate change and increase the resilience of cities. Yet, evidence is inconclusive on where or when urban nature-based solutions are effective, let alone how this effectiveness might extend into the future or to the surrounding landscape. Regional spillover impacts are rarely considered, despite the importance of considering these interactions (Haase, 2019; Pineda-Pinto, Frantzeskaki, & Nygaard, 2022). There is also little assessment of their future effectiveness: many interventions may not be effective in the future due to implementation that does not consider native or climate-adapted species (Chausson et al., 2020; Goodwin et al., 2023). Evidence on expected timeframes of interventions and transformative change potential is inconclusive. Cases are mostly medium term (decades) and involve the new implementation of existing solutions, or a continuation of traditional top-down power structures (Cousins, 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Urban nature-based solutions (B02)" .

