    ipbes:hasDescription """Sustainable intensification can be confused with other terms related to the sustainable production of food production (Box 5.3.3). IPBES defines sustainable intensification as “a process or system where agricultural yields are increased without adverse environmental impact and without the conversion of additional non-agricultural land” (IPBES, 2016, 2018).

Sustainable intensification can be applied globally on all arable and pastoral land due to yield gaps in many regions (Global Yield Gap and Water Productivity Atlas, n.d.) and has the potential to reduce environmental impacts while maintaining yields elsewhere (Halpern et al., 2022) (Figure 5.3.5). It can be achieved through a wide range of approaches with differing impacts and timescales (Table 5.3.6).

Sustainable intensification has positive impacts across nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). Yield-increasing response options (i.e., F02 Restore soil health (section 5.3.3.2); F03, Sustainable intensification (section 5.3.3.3); F04, Ecological intensification – croplands (section 5.3.3.4); F05 Ecological intensification – rangelands (section 5.3.3.5) and F06 Ecological intensification – aquatic foods (section 5.3.3.6)) are needed to achieve these nexus benefits even if consumption changes (e.g., F10, Reduce food loss and waste (section 5.3.10); F11, Sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.11)) are achieved (D. R. Williams et al., 2021). However, realizing benefits may require policy or economic mechanisms to avoid additional agricultural expansion (Desquilbet et al., 2017; Phalan et al., 2016). Sustainable intensification has limited the conversion of natural ecosystems avoiding up to 590 GtCO2e emissions from 1961-2005 (Burney et al., 2010). It is projected to reduce future emissions by 2-2.45 GtCO2e per year, largely through limiting agricultural expansion (M.A. Clark et al., 2020), and climate adaptation benefits achieved through sustainable intensification could impact up to 163 million people (P. Smith et al., 2020).

Limiting agricultural expansion has also benefited biodiversity (Balmford, 2021) and future sustainable intensification could have huge benefits for native biodiversity and the area available for protection (Tilman et al., 2017; D. R. Williams et al., 2021), as well as off-farm benefits on ecological function, connectivity and resilience, while on-farm impacts vary depending on the approach (Ekroos et al., 2016; R. E. Green et al., 2005; Tscharntke et al., 2012).

Sustainable intensification improves crop water-use efficiency, potentially reducing water use or producing more food for the same amount of water (Leakey et al., 2019; Wani et al., 2003). In livestock systems, it may increase blue and grey water use due to greater consumption of irrigated feeds (Mekonnen & Hoekstra, 2012). It also increases water quality by reducing nutrient run-off and soil erosion (Z.-L. Cui et al., 2018; Finger et al., 2019).

Sustainable intensification can have variable impacts on human health. Intensive livestock systems limit the probability of zoonotic disease spillover by reducing livestock-wildlife interactions, but may increase the probability of zoonotic disease take-off, antimicrobial resistance, foodborne diseases and air pollution (Gilbert et al., 2021; Post et al., 2020; Rohr et al., 2019). Interlinkages between sustainable intensification practices, air pollution and health are described in the case study in Box 5.3.4. Benefits through increased nutrition will only be realised if production is linked to sustainable healthy diets (section 5.3.3.11; Chapter 5.4, section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.15, C15, Sustainable healthy diets).

Sustainable intensification approaches vary in social acceptability (e.g., intensive livestock production may be unacceptable due to animal welfare concerns) and economic costs and benefits (Figure 5.3.4). Many approaches are at Technological Readiness Level 9 (Figure 5.3.5), with low direct costs, but the investment required may still be beyond the poorest farmers, or bring unacceptable risks from changing production techniques, raising equity concerns (Carter et al., 2017; Loos et al., 2014; Shuaibu & Nchake, 2021; Tscharntke et al., 2012; Vanlauwe et al., 2014) (Figure 5.3.4).

There is an apparent alignment of sustainable intensification approaches with multiple international frameworks (CBD, 2023; UN, 2023; UNFCCC, 2015; United Nations, 2015). Sustainable intensification stands in contrast to ecological intensification, as there is limited common understanding of scope and hypotheses underlying sustainability whereas there is a large body of research findings to be drawn on from the science of ecology (Petersen & Snapp, 2015). There is limited evidence of transformative change potential, in part due to contestation around its definition (Figure 5.3.4).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sustainable intensification (F03)" .

