    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Secure access to land is fundamental to sustainable food systems and to addressing the challenges caused by habitat and biodiversity loss, land and water degradation, and GHG emissions (Santpoort et al., 2021) (Figure 5.3.4). Unequal access to natural resources encompasses aspects of social, ethnic and gender inequalities, and reflects power differentials (N. J. Bennett et al., 2023; Meyfroidt et al., 2022) (Figure 5.3.4). Recognizing the importance of equitable access, the (FAO, 2022b) produced voluntary guidelines on the responsible governance of tenure of land, fisheries and forests, specifically addressing the needs of vulnerable and marginalized groups such as smallholders, IPLC and women.

The FAO guidelines emphasize the importance of local contexts, and the various mechanisms and institutions – formal and informal, individual and communal – through which natural resources are accessed (cf. Ostrom (2012); Peluso & Ribot (2020)), which need to be taken into account when designing tenure systems to secure access. Moving beyond titling and formal registration as the main mechanisms for securing access, the guidelines propose a wider range of policies and legislation, as well as advocacy programmes, to strengthen tenure security for vulnerable groups to promote more equitable and secure access to land, fisheries and forests for these groups. Here, the FAO recognizes that strengthening tenure security relates both to legal security and contextual or perceived security, and refers to the confidence that rights will be acknowledged and upheld by all other actors, including the state (Mwangi & Meinzen-Dick, 2009; Sjaastad & Bromley, 2000).

Formal registration and individual titling have long been a dominant response to address tenure insecurity (Tseng et al., 2021). Research, however, shows that for some marginalized groups, formal registration and titling may actually undermine tenure security – notably for women (Duncan et al., 2022; Feyertag et al., 2021; Singirankabo & Ertsen, 2020). Individual titling can, especially among the poor, be counterproductive, resulting in distress sales for low prices (Musyoka et al., 2021; Meinzen-Dick et al., 2019). Without specific provisions for vulnerable groups, national land laws often fail to protect the individual and collective rights of peasants, tenants, IPLC and other vulnerable groups, and without proper recourse mechanisms may even undermine their rights (Hall et al., 2013; Notess et al., 2018; Tura, 2017). Recognition of these flaws resulted in calls for fit-for-purpose land administration systems (R. Bennett & Alemie, 2016), and an increased focus on titling and registration communal lands – notably to safeguard IPLC tenure security (Notess et al., 2021). Examples of communal land registration and protection exist, for instance, in Brazil (Baines, 2022; Baragwanath & Bayi, 2020; Benzeev et al., 2023), Mozambique (Balas et al., 2021) and Scotland (Doyle, 2023). Registration, however, needs to be accessible, both in terms of costs as well as procedures and transparency of conditions (R. Bennett & Alemie, 2016; Notess et al., 2021). A review of 33 community land formalizations in fifteen countries (Notess et al., 2021) showed that this is quite often not the case. The review confirmed the importance of the intention by other actors – notably the state and companies – to acknowledge and uphold community rights. State actors often allowed companies to take shortcuts to land registration, undermining community rights. The FAO voluntary guidelines, revised in 2022, recognize the complexities, and hence, as mentioned above, propose a more localized and comprehensive approach to strengthen tenure security.

Reviews of the impacts of strengthening land tenure and access rights on the nexus elements report mixed findings (Figure 5.3.3). Regarding food security, a number of studies report positive impacts of increased security on agricultural productivity (Ali et al., 2014; Deininger et al., 2009; Higgins et al., 2018; Tseng et al., 2021). Increased security results are associated with better access to loans, and a willingness to invest in agriculture (Deininger & Jin, 2006; Reddy, A. A. et al., 2020). Others, however, have found mixed or little support for this positive relation with increased productivity (Bizoza & Opio-Omoding, 2021). They relate this to differences between de jure and de facto security, i.e., how individuals or groups perceive tenure security (Singirankabo & Ertsen, 2020). Reviews do not always clearly distinguish between different tenure security interventions. Furthermore, increased productivity does not always translate automatically in food or nutrition security (Pouw et al., 2020).

Supporting land and forest tenure results in mostly positive impacts on environmental outcomes, particularly for soil and (agro)biodiversity conservation (Higgins et al., 2018; Tseng et al., 2021). Impacts, however, vary according to the type of tenure intervention. A large-scale individual titling programme in the Brazilian Amazon resulted in an increase in deforestation, especially among medium and smallholders, and was connected to crop and cattle prices increases (Probst et al., 2020). This is consistent with the finding that strengthening individual tenure in combination with the promotion of market-based food production (framing food as a commodity) has negative impacts on soil and biodiversity conservation (Ranjan et al., 2022). Collective land titling, on the other hand, is more often associated with positive outcomes for biodiversity conservation (Vélez et al., 2020), though a study by Kraus and colleagues (Kraus et al., 2021) found that this depends on community capacities to enforce compliance with conservation measures vis-à-vis third parties. Hence, titling alone is not sufficient for positive environmental outcomes, it requires active community support and supporting legislation in other state sectors such as trade and environmental affairs (Kraus et al., 2021; Probst et al., 2020). Evidence of impacts on water quality is lacking, but positive impacts are found in sustainable watershed management (Katusiime & Schütt, 2020). A comprehensive review of tenure on human well-being reported positive outcomes in most cases (82 per cent) but found evidence of trade-offs between human well-being and environmental outcomes (Tseng et al., 2021).

Strengthening customary communal rights through legal recognition of these rights and local institutions, and/or communal titling, is especially important in forestry (Katila et al., 2020), fisheries (N. J. Bennett et al., 2023) and pastoralism (Flintan et al., 2021). While this is recognized by many governments, a review of forestry tenure reforms in 23 countries (Aggarwal et al., 2021) concluded that community tenure is less robust than the tenure of companies and smallholders, reducing possibilities for community livelihood and ecological benefits (see also (Vélez et al., 2020). Environmental and human well-being impacts of tenure security in pastoralism and fisheries are severely understudied. There is some evidence of pastoralists benefitting from rights-based approaches to biodiversity conservation in terms of access to grazing, but these come with trade-offs regarding biodiversity conservation (Flintan et al., 2021).

Several reviews stress the importance of bundling responses, such as combining strengthening tenure security with capacity-building, but this is less studied (Katila et al., 2020; Tseng et al., 2021).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.16" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Access to natural resources and land (F16)" .

