    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The biodiversity response options assessed in this subchapter involve more than seven (7.71) actors on average in their implementation (Figure 5.1.12, Figure 5.1.13) for the actor analysis methodology). The national government level predominantly engages in biodiversity response options with a leading role, along with global and regional institutions and civil society organizations, who are mainly involved in implementation (Figure 5.1.12, Figure 5.1.14). Civil society organizations usually play an enabling role bridging government, funding institutions and local actors (e.g., Figure 5.1.15D). Actors from the private sector and IPLC are the main targets and can have less power in this role. However, in several of the presented response options, the private sector and IPLC are key actors because they can decide if and how to implement the actions in their properties and territories (Edake et al., 2020; Gonzalez-Duarte, 2021; Karimova et al., 2022; Olsson et al., 2004; Quintas-Soriano et al., 2022; M. A. Samoilys et al., 2019; Tanentzap et al., 2023; United Nations Development Programme, 2012).

The interaction of these actors and their roles in the implementation of the biodiversity response options results in different collaboration networks (Figure 5.1.15), some of which are more inclusive and have greater horizontal cooperation for addressing complexity in ecosystem-based approaches (D. Armitage et al., 2020; D. R. Armitage et al., 2009; Folke et al., 2005), and thus reflect patterns of nexus governance. However, cooperation itself does not guarantee successful outcomes, as different patterns of collaboration are suited to different management challenges (Bodin et al., 2017; Bodin & Crona, 2009). Well-connected and heterogeneous networks of collaboration among actors from different categories and interests (Figure 5.1.15, C and D) have greater potential to address complex problems related to the nexus (A. Sandström & Rova, 2010). In contrast, heterogeneous networks centred around a lead actor (Figure 5.1.15, A and B) may also overcome complexity if the central actor takes a facilitation role to the collaboration process (Bodin et al., 2017; Provan & Kenis, 2007). These transboundary interactions among different actors are key for facilitating social learning and synergies, and for overcoming trade-offs among nexus elements (Alonso Roldán, 2021; Riggs et al., 2020; A. Sandström & Rova, 2010), as illustrated, for example, by the interactions among different agencies in Singapore to implement Green Plan 2030 (Figure 5.1.15F). Nonetheless, simple networks connecting grassroot organizations and enabling IPLC rights-based approaches (Figure 5.1.15E) may also be effective in achieving nexus-wide benefits. In addition, other enabling factors and context is critically important to collaboration structure, such as resource availability, time of the collaborations (Raab et al., 2015) and power relations.

Power asymmetries are a cross-cutting issue that can affect development and implementation of nexus approaches (Chapter 4, section 4.5.1.1), as these asymmetries are present within and among categories of actors and nexus elements. Within governments, power dynamics can arise between different agencies with opposed interests across nexus elements. For example, environmental agencies closely related to biodiversity often have interests in the management of all other nexus elements, though with different goals to the principal agencies overseeing them (e.g., agriculture or forestry ministries). These other more powerful agencies may thwart attempts at policy integration or policy mixes, diminishing the effectiveness of the response options for biodiversity conservation (R. M. Garcia & Burns, 2022; Wibowo & Giessen, 2015). Nonetheless, improved coordination among government agencies is crucial to avoid or manage for trade-offs (Jordan & Lenschow, 2010).

Power asymmetries among different actors can threaten the sustainability of nature’s contributions to people (Felipe-Lucia et al., 2015; Vallet et al., 2020). For example, in large scale forest restoration, big companies in the forestry sector, governments, NGOs and IPLC each see restoration through very different lenses and hold very different levels of power in society that informs outcomes of their interactions (Rai et al., 2018). Even in other types of forest management (e.g., community forestry and payments for ecosystem services for native forest conservation), power asymmetries between state agencies, big land owners, forest user groups and rural people produce unequal access in nature’s contributions to people and decision-making, sharing of authority and responsibility (Aguiar et al., 2018; Baynes et al., 2016; Cabrol & Cáceres, 2017). Power imbalances can also lead to social resistance of marginalized actors, which can become a barrier to response option implementation (Baynes et al., 2016; McGinlay et al., 2016). Possible solutions to these imbalances include power-sharing arrangements (e.g., for co-management) (De Pourcq et al., 2017; Plummer et al., 2017; A. Sandström & Rova, 2010); multi-stakeholder platforms for shared governance (Kusters et al., 2018; A. M. Larson et al., 2022); knowledge-coproduction processes to develop trust (Diep et al., 2022; Reyers et al., 2015); use of intermediaries and brokers (e.g., NGOs that mediate between governments and local people and provide capacity support) (N. Kirk et al., 2022; K. C. Nelson & De Jong, 2003); and dialogue or scenario-planning platforms so that different actors have space to discuss shared values, problems and to air grievances (Cusens et al., 2022; N. Dawson et al., 2018; López-Rodríguez et al., 2023). Rights-based approaches as a response option can also potentially empower IPLC to balance out power dynamics and allow them to secure access to nature’s contributions to people, such as through adaptive collaborative governance (McDougall & Ojha, 2021).

Notes
9. The data management report describing the actor analysis is available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13913230.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Interactions among biodiversity actors for nexus governance" .

