        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XCGMW863> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Soils are the basis for producing more than 95 per cent of the food society eats (FAO, 2017; Kraamwinkel et al., 2021). Soil erosion by water is estimated to annually reduce global agricultural food production by 33.7 million tonnes (Sartori et al., 2019). Reverting soil degradation is central to restoring the productive capacity of degraded lands (R. Lal, 2004; UNCCD, 2022) (Figure 5.3.5).

Healthy soils constitute the foundation of agriculture and a critical resource providing society with food, feed, fibre and clean water (Kibblewhite et al., 2008). Restoration of degraded soils and conservation of soil productivity in agriculture is associated with management practices that increase soil organic carbon content (FAO & ITPS, 2021; Paustian et al., 2016) (see also Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.1, (C01, Increase soil organic carbon)). Soil organic carbon influences key soil functions such as crop yield and biomass production; storage and regulation of soil carbon, nutrient and water; control of erosion; filtering of wastes and pollutants from water that may compromise access to healthy food and well-being; and providing habitat for soil biodiversity (Adhikari & Hartemink, 2016; R. Lal, 2016) (Figure 5.3.3).

International efforts led by the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification, the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change and the Convention on Biological Diversity, particularly its Plan of Action (2020-2030) for the International Initiative for the Conservation and Sustainable Use of Soil Biodiversity led by the Global Soil Partnership hosted at FAO, have triggered increased interest in soil restoration, particularly the restoration of soil functions driven by soil organic carbon and biodiversity (Cowie et al., 2018; Minasny et al., 2017; Van Der Putten et al., 2023) (Figure 5.3.4). Recent evidence highlights the significant impact that the presence of earthworms alone can have on global food production, with contributions estimated at 6.5 per cent of global cereal production and 2.3 per cent of legume production, corresponding to over 140 million metric tons annually (Fonte et al., 2023) (Figure 5.3.3).

Management practices that enhance perennial vegetation significantly contribute to enhancing soil organic carbon content (Bayala et al., 2020; Bossio et al., 2020; P. Smith et al., 2020). Agroforestry has been increasingly recognized and practiced as a multifunctional land management option that can simultaneously contribute to income, food security and nutrition, soil health and carbon restoration, the conservation of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people, and climate change adaptation and mitigation (Barrios et al., 2018, 2023; Mbow et al., 2014; Muchane et al, 2020; Nabuurs et al., 2022; Rosenstock et al., 2019; Terasaki Hart et al., 2023) (Figure 5.3.3).

Perennial ground cover through hedges, food-bearing shrubs and grass stripes are important means to broaden agroforestry options in agricultural landscapes. Food producing perennial options can be means to mediate the challenges posed by integration of trees in some environments where competition is high to produce sufficient food from limited land and water resources (Nyaga et al., 2015; Sida et al., 2018). Shrubs, vines and other semi-perennial crop integration can also address gender equity barriers as access to input and output markets and the right to grow trees is often limited for women farmers and women-headed households (Figure 5.3.4).

Agroforestry has high established benefits across the nexus of biodiversity, food, health, climate change, energy, land and natural resource management (Ickowitz et al., 2022; van Noordwijk et al., 2018) (Figure 5.3.4). Nevertheless, the success at scale of perennial revegetation efforts to recover soil health needs to be socially acceptable, economically feasible and in alignment with political priorities (UNCCD, 2020) (Figure 5.3.4). This is well illustrated by the Great Green Wall Initiative in Africa (Box 5.3.2) which has evolved to embrace the need to include multiple stakeholder concerns and respect traditional customs, implement systematic planning and monitoring, and secure financing and ensure equity in decision-making among stakeholders.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restore soil health (F02)" .

