    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Urban green infrastructure broadly defined as a network of natural, semi-natural and artificial green spaces that deliver multiple ecosystem services (European Commission, 2013), is a strategy to transform urban environments to be more healthy, liveable and biodiverse (WHO, 2016). Management of urban green and blue spaces affects the health and well-being of over 50 per cent of the global population (UN, 2019) (Figure 5.4.7). However, only 27 per cent global urban centres had adequate greenspace in 2021 (Romanello et al., 2022). This response option complements others (see B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions in Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.2 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.14) with a focus on urban and peri-urban forests and trees, which can be a cost-effective way to improve health outcomes and address multiple socioecological needs.

Urban green infrastructure provides multiple health benefits (Superior Health Council, 2021) (Figure 5.4.5). Green space metrics are associated with reduced cardiovascular, respiratory and heat-related mortality (Gascon et al., 2016; K. L. Wolf et al., 2020) and increased life expectancy (R. Connolly et al., 2023; Idrovo, 2011). Urban green infrastructure is consistently associated with better mental health, elevated mood levels and lower anxiety across life stages (K. O. Lee et al., 2023; K. L. Wolf et al., 2020). Increasing tree canopy could potentially reduce hundreds of premature deaths within a single urban area, valued at billions of dollars (Kondo, Mueller, et al., 2020).

Urban trees can increase biodiversity and ecosystem resilience (Morgenroth & Armstrong, 2012). High tree diversity areas support more animal diversity and are more resilient to climate change, pests and diseases than tree monocultures (Cowett & Bassuk, 2020). Publicly accessible fruit and nut trees also improve food access and reduce food insecurity (K. H. Clark & Nicholas, 2013). Urban green infrastructure also filters stormwater pollutants and helps maintain local water supplies (Kuehler et al., 2016), further benefitting health. Moreover, it can improve ecological connectivity (Munshi-South, 2012; Von Thaden et al., 2021), but knowledge gaps remain around which configurations maximize connectivity regionally (LaPoint et al., 2015) and how to minimize vector-borne disease risks (Heylen et al., 2019).

Estimates of climate change mitigation potential and urban green infrastructure outcomes generally (Chapter 5.1, Figure 5.1.5) are limited to specific cities and geographical regions (e.g., Quaranta et al., 2021). One estimate of urban greening mitigation potential globally is 1.7-3.6 GtCO2/yr (Lal et al., 2018), which likely represents the upper range. Other benefits may be more important than carbon sequestration. Moderate increases in tree cover improve climate change adaptation potential (Skelhorn et al., 2014) through improved air quality (Marando et al., 2016), flood risk mitigation (FEMA, 2022) and reduced heat island impacts (Cissé et al., 2022; Marando et al., 2019).

Urban green infrastructure is largely viewed as a low-cost intervention to address multiple environmental and human health benefits (Figure 5.4.6; Box 5.4.6; Box 5.4.8). Increasing tree cover is a goal frequently incorporated into municipal plans (e.g., Million Tree initiatives) and national and multi-national strategies. The return on investment for urban green infrastructure varies (Kroeger et al., 2018; Roy, 2018) but economic benefits typically outweigh costs (e.g., increased property values and reduced costs associated with storm water infrastructure, heat reduction, building energy use and healthcare (Nowak & Greenfield, 2018).

Equitable access to high quality green infrastructure is critical to reduce disparities and maximize health benefits and is a specific SDG target (11.7) (Figure 5.4.6). The WHO recommends all people reside within 300m of green space at least 1 hectare in size (WHO, 2017); the 3-30-300 rule recommends three visible trees from each home and at least 30 per cent tree cover in every neighbourhood (Konijnendijk, 2023). Yet, the more vulnerable often reside in areas with less and lower quality green space (Astell-Burt et al., 2014; Rigolon, 2016; Rigolon et al., 2018) and benefit less from associated ecosystem services (R. Connolly et al., 2023; Landry & Chakraborty, 2009; Schwarz et al., 2015). Those with lower socioeconomic status achieve proportionally greater health benefits from high-quality nearby natural areas (Rigolon et al., 2021). Therefore, addressing distributional equity would improve health and social outcomes and increase population-level benefits (E. Connolly et al., 2021; Marselle et al., 2021). Co-production and co-design processes (Hartigan et al., 2021) are increasingly used in urban green infrastructure planning but are not applied universally.

Urban green infrastructure has benefits across nexus elements and demonstrated implementation feasibility. Greenspace must be carefully managed to support local biodiversity while limiting disease transmission risks (see section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses), allergens, storm damage and fire. Thoughtful planning is also critical to achieve more equitable health outcomes (European Environment Agency, 2022). Nevertheless, urban green infrastructure can support structural and institutional transformation by enabling co-production and co-design of urban spaces and shifting views and values toward a more sustainable urban future (Webb et al., 2023) (Figure 5.4.6).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Urban green infrastructure (H09)" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/NXS24-KM-B1> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
