    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The global food system is essential for human survival, feeding societies and underpinning all the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). How food is produced, what foods are produced and consumed, where they are produced and how much is lost and wasted have enormous impacts on biodiversity, water, health and climate change. Food production accounts for upwards of 80 per cent of biodiversity loss, driven by land expansion, fragmentation, pollution, overexploitation and facilitating the spread of invasive alien species (Benton et al., 2021; IPBES, 2019b; Pinnegar et al., 2000; Tilman & Williams, 2020). The global food system is responsible for 80 per cent of water pollution (Schulte-Uebbing et al., 2022) and 70 per cent of freshwater withdrawal, with almost half of irrigation water coming from non-renewable and non-local sources (Rost et al., 2008). Over half of the world’s population today struggles to access healthy foods, significantly impacting global dietary health (FAO et al., 2020). Diet-related non-communicable diseases alone are estimated at $11 trillion per year, just shy of the $12 trillion value generated by food (Ruggeri-Laderchi et al., 2024). Current food system trajectories are unsustainable and would result in widespread biodiversity losses (Leclère et al., 2018, 2020; D. R. Williams et al., 2021) and preclude the achievement of the 1.5°C Paris Agreement long-term global goal even if all other fossil fuel uses were eliminated (M. A. Clark et al., 2020).

The global food system is also fundamentally reliant on the other nexus elements. Food production requires biodiversity (DeClerck et al., 2023a; DeClerck et al., 2021a). Changes in biophysical characteristics of agricultural systems through management practices influence the abundance, diversity and activity of organisms providing nature’s contributions to people such as pollination, pest and disease regulation and soil health (FAO, 2019c; FAO, ITPS, GSBI, CBD and EC, 2020; IPBES, 2019a) (Chapter 5.3, online Supplementary material 5.3.1). Resilient food production, with reduced use of pesticides, is dependent on pest regulating functions provided by biodiversity. Pollination services from biodiversity are particularly important for the production of a diversity of fruits, nuts and legumes which are globally under-consumed (Ellis et al., 2015). Genetic and species diversity of food crops (including wild relatives), animals and aquatic species underpins healthy diets, and resilient production system, with all three forms of malnutrition (underweight, hidden hunger and obesity) characterized by low dietary diversity (Afshin et al., 2019; FAO, 2019c; Khoury et al., 2014). Wild caught foods, primarily fish, are also vital sources of nutrition for many people, and utterly reliant on biodiversity. Microbiome diversity in both the human gut and in soils (Banerjee & Van Der Heijden, 2023) (Chapter 5.4, Box 5.4.6) drives nutrition function, which science is only beginning to better understand, including metabolic roles in soil carbon sequestration (Jansson & Hofmockel, 2020). Thus, agricultural biodiversity is foundational to crop-livestock systems, the diversity of wild lands and landscapes, and the resilience of rural communities. This is challenged by monocultures and intensified production systems. Transition pathways that enable diversified agriculture are urgently required (Rasmussen et al., 2024).

Food production requires water. Ensuring reliable and sustainable water supplies for food production underpins food security. While 80 per cent of agricultural lands are rainfed, accounting for 60 per cent of terrestrial food production, 70 per cent of freshwater consumption is dedicated to food production, primarily drawn from groundwater or blue water systems (FAO, 2020a; Ingrao et al., 2023). Green water, or the water from localized water cycles (including precipitation, evaporation and transpiration), is impacted by land use change, including deforestation, which can reduce localized water capture, evaporation and transpiration, while irrigation can increase soil water moisture, evaporation and transpiration (Wang-Erlandsson et al., 2022).

Food requires a healthy food producing and consuming community. Producer communities, including individuals employed in food processing and preparation, are often marginalized, isolated and threatened, yet their mental and physical health and well-being is a prerequisite for improving the role of food in the nexus (Lowder et al., 2016). Consumers, on the other hand, need to consume healthy diets comprised of diverse food groups that are adequate in essential nutrients and energy intake. Food producers are more directly confronted by climate and environmental risks. Ensuring a just transition (Lamine et al., 2019) requires important changes in enabling environments and growing recognition of food producer’s central role in Earth system stability and human health (Ahnström et al., 2009).

Food security requires a stable climate system (Mbow et al., 2019). Climate change interacts with water and food by increasing the frequency, severity and duration of severe water events – both drought and flooding – which threaten regional food security. Ensuring stable water supplies for food production primarily through canal and dam infrastructure have a positive impact on food production, but can have important negative impacts on biodiversity, health, mediated by waterborne diseases, access to lands and livelihoods (Beck et al., 2012).

Nature’s contributions to people emphasize the deep linkages between nature, food and people (DeClerck et al., 2016). Agroecosystems, including rangeland ecosystems, encompass nearly 40 per cent of the world’s ice-free lands (Ramankutty et al., 2018) and may be considered the world’s largest terrestrial ecosystem. Better management of biodiversity in food producing lands and waters can increase the provision of nature’s contributions to people that support the nexus (S. L. R. Wood et al., 2018) (Chapter 5.3, online Supplementary material 5.3.1). These include biodiversity nature’s contributions to people such as habitat creation and maintenance; water nature’s contributions to people such as regulation of freshwater quantity and quality; health nature’s contributions to people such as regulation of air quality, freshwater quality and extreme hazards or events; and climate change nature’s contributions to people such as climate regulation and regulation of ocean acidification. Food production is also vital to supporting identities, in particular the wild harvest of food which can be the primary source of culturally appropriate foods in ILK communities (Chapter 7, online Supplementary material 7.1). Food is a foundation of cultural identity for many, including in high income settings.

Pressures on and from the food system create the conditions for considering a global transition to food systems that ensure food security and nutrition for all while reversing environmental degradation and adapting to climate change, a consensus reached at the 2021 United Nations Food Systems Summit. Agroecology (agro-eco-logis), literally the study of ecological interactions in agricultural systems, has evolved to provide a holistic approach that simultaneously applies ecological, economic and social concepts and principles to the design and management of the whole food system to deliver more sustainable outcomes (Barrios et al., 2020; FAO, 2018; Gliessman, 2014; HLPE, 2019; Wezel et al., 2020) (see also response option B03/C11, Agroecology in Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.3 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.11). Food system transitions entail restructuring both the social and ecological dimensions of agroecosystems by and with stakeholders to improve and sustain rural livelihoods following agroecology principles (Tittonell, 2023). Recognizing that inherent complexity is commonly seen as a deterrent to action, the 197 members of the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) adopted a simplified analytical framework called the 10 Elements of Agroecology (Box 5.3.1) to facilitate understanding and use of a holistic approach to support decision-making during the design of differentiated paths for agriculture and food systems transformation (FAO, 2023a).

Addressing these challenges reveals vital opportunities for food system response options to positively affect the nexus elements. What food is consumed, how and where it is produced, in what quantities, and how much is lost and wasted present clear opportunities for transitioning to healthy, sustainable and just food systems. Sixteen major response options are assessed in this subchapter, along with their capacity to have positive impacts on global policy goals. Opportunities for synergies are identified, as well as challenges and trade-offs to be addressed and overcome. Ensuring that food’s land footprint leaves sufficient space for ecosystems of high ecological integrity to contribute to a stable Earth system, integrating biodiversity into food production systems and increasing biodiversity in healthy diets, all while celebrating the diversity of global food cultures, are particularly powerful biodiversity-based response options.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of food within the nexus" .

