    ipbes:hasDescription """Executive summary

Biodiversity supports all other nexus elements (water, food, health and climate change), particularly through the supply of nature’s contributions to people, yet policy and decision-making often neglect biodiversity or prioritize other elements (well established) {5.1.1}. Biodiversity is affected by many actions and decisions taken in other sectors but actors from these sectors rarely consult with biodiversity actors, nor consider their potential harmful impacts or synergies (well established) {5.1.1.2}. Response options identified in this subchapter aim to address this situation by mainstreaming biodiversity into other sectors. These response options primarily target biodiversity, but they also have considerable potential to benefit other nexus elements and thereby important policy instruments including the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Sustainable Development Goals (well established) {5.1.1.4}.

Promising response options for biodiversity comprise approaches around the protection, management, restoration and governance of nature and biodiversity (well established) {5.1.3}. Nine types of actions that comply with principles for ecosystem-based approaches were assessed: area-based conservation, urban nature-based solutions, agroecology, ecosystem-based climate adaptation in rural landscapes, forest landscape restoration, restoration of coastal and marine systems, restoration of inland water systems, rewilding, and integrated landscape and seascape approaches. These actions are shaped by, and have intricate interactions with, policy instruments and enabling conditions that help to support their effective implementation (well established) {5.1.3}. Other assessed response options are themselves enablers, including rights-based approaches, implementation of multilateral environmental agreements, land and sea planning, natural capital accounting and reconnecting people with nature. In addition to legal and economic instruments, these response options constitute social and information-based instruments required to transform the social, economic and political contexts to support nexus-wide benefits. These response options are often implemented together in bundles to increase their effectiveness and scope (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}.

Response options for biodiversity have proven technological feasibility and are already implemented globally, delivering positive outcomes for multiple nexus elements and addressing drivers of biodiversity degradation (well established) {5.1.3}. In addition to their benefits for biodiversity structure and function, assessed response options also benefit water supply and quality, and climate change adaptation and mitigation (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. Moreover, response options have substantial co-benefits for health (established but incomplete) {5.1.3}. Benefits to the food system vary depending on the response option, with several options specifically targeting food production, such as agroecology, ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes, and integrated landscape and seascape approaches {5.1.3.3, 5.1.3.4, 5.1.3.9}. Trade-offs with food can occur in some forest landscape restoration or rewilding actions (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.8}. Importantly, resolution of trade-offs between response options will be integral to successful implementation; however, trade-offs are chronically under-reported and constitute a major data gap (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.5}.

Complementarities across response options can enhance their efficacy and/or overcome trade-offs at landscape to regional scales (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. This complementarity is central to integrated landscape and seascape approaches, land and sea planning, and in some rights-based approaches in landscapes and seascapes managed by Indigenous Peoples and local communities in which several response options are bundled and implemented together (well established) {5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.10, 5.1.3.12, 5.1.6}. Integrated landscape and seascape management is a powerful approach for enabling coordinated action across multiple stakeholders that amplifies environmental, economic and social outcomes from diverse nature protection areas and nature-based solutions within landscapes and seascapes (well established) {5.1.3.9}. Land and sea planning, as a sociopolitical participatory process, can address trade-offs and encourage multifunctionality through public policy (well established) {5.1.3.12}. Land and sea planning also enables synergistic benefits for health from strategic implementation of response options across the landscape, such as area-based conservation, rewilding and reconnecting people with nature (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.14, 5.1.6}.

The success of response options in achieving nexus-wide benefits largely depends on their implementation, particularly in how enablers and barriers are managed (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Outcomes can range from close to business-as-usual or providing incremental improvements to significant transformative change. For example, equity and social acceptance are strong enablers of biodiversity response options if collaborative networks involving actors with different interests and mechanisms to address power asymmetries are in place; otherwise, these enablers become barriers to implementation and may lead to trade-offs with some nexus elements (well established) {5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Biodiversity response options in general show positive socioeconomic outcomes when implemented with high procedural equity (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. However, where response options have had lower procedural equity, low social acceptance has been a barrier to success (established but incomplete) {5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Examples of this include some area-based conservation and rewilding initiatives, which may not always be equitable, especially when driven by high-level institutional or private actors {5.1.4}. In general, biodiversity response options require locally tailored design and implementation (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6} as well as adequate monitoring that accounts for impacts on multiple elements of the nexus, although this is currently frequently missing or insufficient (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.5}. For example, the success of urban nature-based solutions in delivering on multiple nexus elements and social, cultural and economic goals varies widely depending on their local implementation (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.2}. Moreover, while there is almost universal agreement on the potential for urban nature-based solutions to benefit several nexus elements, few studies report comprehensively on these solutions, challenging their uptake (well established) {5.1.3.2}.

Response options for biodiversity rely on a broad diversity of local to global actors, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities, with complementary roles for all sectors to meet nexus-wide objectives (well established) {5.1.2, 5.1.4.1}. Actors from the public sector have usually led formally recognized conservation-oriented actions, while Indigenous Peoples and local communities and private sector actors have been the target of these actions. This has led to conflicts and calls for new ways of governing (well established) {5.1.1, 5.1.2, 5.1.4}.

Suggested improvements include rights-based approaches to biodiversity conservation, which promote equitable and sustainable conservation practices by effectively recognizing the rights of Indigenous Peoples and local communities, Indigenous lands and traditional territories, resource access rights, gender equality and social justice (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.10, 5.1.4.4}. Any actor can lead response options and bottom-up initiatives are valuable for social change, including recognizing existing biodiversity conservation actions undertaken by Indigenous Peoples and local communities and others (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.4}. The success of biodiversity response options often requires their implementation through participatory processes, as higher levels of participation are associated with a lower proportion of trade-offs for nexus elements; hence, strengthening and promoting procedural and recognition equity is important (established but incomplete) {5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Context determines which schemes of governance and modes of interactions among actors are more conducive to positive outcomes (established but incomplete) {5.1.4.1}. For instance, centralized networks are suitable to boost Indigenous Peoples and local communities involvement in marine area-based conservation or to facilitate the administration of urban biosphere reserves (established but incomplete) {5.1.4.1}. In contrast, interactions that are balanced across multiple actors (e.g., non-hierarchical) during implementation of integrated landscape and seascape approaches can favour social learning and overcome potential trade-offs and enhance synergies (established but incomplete) {5.1.4.1}.

One of the strongest barriers to implementation is lack of institutional capacities, because biodiversity response options need frequent interactions among actors, particularly coordination and negotiation with actors central to other elements of the nexus (well established) {5.1.4}. Biodiversity response options show medium to high transformative change potential, particularly for transforming structures and institutions; however, this requires overcoming institutional barriers through, for example, consolidated capacity-building among actors on transformative social learning (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.4}. Specifically, social and information-based instruments and other sociopolitical response options, such as rights-based approaches and reconnecting people with nature, enable biodiversity actors to conserve biodiversity though negotiations, transboundary cooperation, and through changes in behaviour, all of which influence and positively impact other elements of the nexus (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.4}.

The social and economic effectiveness of response options for biodiversity is poorly documented, which is a barrier to their scaling (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.5, 5.1.6}. Although information on financial and labour costs is not shared systematically, the economic costs of implementation tend to be lowest for response options that conserve socioecological systems and/or natural processes (e.g., protected areas, agroecology, integrated landscape and seascape approaches) compared to more intensive restoration or ecological engineering activities on land (e.g., urban nature-based solutions, forest landscape restoration) or on aquatic systems (e.g., restoration of coastal, marine and inland water systems) (established but incomplete) {5.1.3}. Overall, economic costs (ranging from up-front investments to longer-term maintenance) is a frequent barrier for the implementation of biodiversity response options (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.4}. Yet, biodiversity response options have multiple socioecological benefits, including the strengthening of institutions, participation, social cohesion and trust, and access to nature and nature’s contributions to people (established but incomplete) {5.1.3}. For example, rights-based approaches and appropriate implementation of land planning strive to ensure that biodiversity conservation efforts contribute to the well-being of both ecosystems and human communities in a cost-effective manner while strengthening institutions and navigating trade-offs and synergies with other societal goals (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.10, 5.1.3.12}. However, more systematic monitoring and reporting on these outcomes and processes is needed to understand what actions can increase the efficacy of response options, examine how barriers and enabling conditions can assist in scaling out response options globally and how ongoing learning to support institutional transformation can be scaled up (well established) {5.1.5}.

Biodiversity response options contribute to the achievement of multiple global policy frameworks, including the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and its Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. Each of the biodiversity response options addresses several Sustainable Development Goals, and more broadly the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, exemplifying different and complementary actions to support sustainable development though biodiversity conservation, restoration and sustainable use (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. The response options contribute to the achievement of three to nine Sustainable Development Goals, 4 to 15 Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and to both climate change mitigation and adaptation long-term global goals under the Paris Agreement. More response options support those Sustainable Development Goals that are directly related to the nexus elements (e.g., biodiversity – Goals 14 and 15, water – Goal 6, food – Goal 2 and climate change – Goal 13), while health (Goal 3) is supported indirectly through multiple nature’s contributions to people or people connecting with nature (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}.

Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets 1 to 3, 11, 14 and 22 are well supported by the response options, as these targets align with nexus approaches that aim to mainstream biodiversity into other human actions and decision-making enabled by equitable planning and implementation (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. There is no single response option that supports all Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, although some have broad effects, particularly area-based conservation, integrated landscape and seascape approaches, and multilateral environmental agreements. Bundling response options in complementary ways facilitates support across a wider range of goals and targets across international frameworks (established but incomplete) {5.1.6}.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Options for delivering sustainable approaches to biodiversity conservation, restoration and sustainable use" .

