    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """When agricultural products or processes have externalities, two public instruments for including those characteristics in the market price are subsidies (for positive externalities, e.g., soil erosion reduction) and taxes (for negative externalities, e.g., practices generating health or environmental problems). Nevertheless, many countries implement agricultural subsidies, mainly focused on commodity crops and fisheries, which are mostly harmful to human health and the environment as they promote unsustainable practices (FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021).

Most harmful subsidies influence international market costs and supply, distorting free trade rules and impacting small farmers in developing countries (DeBoe et al., 2020; FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021; Shetty & Winch, 2015; Swain, 2009; Vos et al., 2022). Three of them are particularly important: (1) subsidies coupled with production inputs and/or outputs that promote commodity production even when market prices are below production costs; (2) market price support acting as border measures (e.g., tariffs, licenses and quotas) that increase or decrease domestic prices; and (3) subsidies decoupled from production inputs or outputs that may or may not be associated with agri-environmental practices (Ash & Cox, 2022; DeBoe, 2020; DeBoe et al., 2020; FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021; Vos et al., 2022). Agricultural subsidies are estimated to be $540 billion per year and rise to $1.8 trillion per year by 2030 under a business-as-usual scenario (FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021). Implicit subsidies (in the form of hidden costs or negative externalities) of food systems are estimated to be at least $12.4 trillion per year (with a range of $5.3 trillion to $19.8 trillion) valued at 12.4 per cent of global gross domestic product (Damania et al., 2023; FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021; FOLU, 2019; Hendriks et al., 2021).

In the context of low and middle income countries, explicit and implicit agricultural subsidies ($4.42 person day⁻ 1) can exceed by up to 38 per cent the value of direct payments needed to bring families above the poverty level ($3.2 person day⁻¹) (World Bank, 2018). Other harmful subsidies include: fisheries with $35.4 and $83 billion per year in implicit and explicit subsidies, respectively (Damania et al., 2023); and fossil fuel subsidies valued at $1.3 and $5.7 trillion per year in explicit and implicit subsidies, respectively, which in the context of agriculture relates to subsidies benefiting reduced fuel, irrigation, refrigeration and fertilizer costs (Black et al., 2023; Timperley, 2021).

Harmful subsidies could be removed or redirected towards public goods, including those impacted by the nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). Several different options that consider a range of reforming or reducing of harmful subsidies have been recently analysed by referent institutions (i.e., International Food Policy Research Institute, World Bank, OECD) (Gautam et al., 2022; Henderson & Lankoski, 2019) and authors (Springmann & Freund, 2022) through global and farm-level models (Table 5.3.9). Two examples of models that compare impacts from reforming to different aims have provided an estimate of an increase in food production (crops 16 per cent, livestock 11 per cent, vegetables -2.5 to 19 per cent) and consumption (dairy products 16 per cent, fruits and vegetables 0.3 to 12 per cent and sugar 28 per cent), a reduction in agricultural prices (21 per cent) and in the costs of healthy diets (18 per cent), and a reduction in diet-related mortality and in GHG emissions, as well as positive impacts on biodiversity (restoration and avoided conversion) (Gautam et al., 2022; Springmann & Freund, 2022). A European farm-level model estimates multiple scenarios: one scenario shows the negative impact of market price support on habitat quality, GHG emissions and fertilizer use and another shows the positive impact of agri-environmental schemes on fertilizer use reduction (Henderson & Lankoski, 2019) (Table 5.3.9). Reforming harmful subsidies should be carefully planned and monitored as, for example, subsidies implemented to improve farmers’ livelihoods in low and lower-middle-income countries, may or may not help reduce socioeconomic and/or health problems (Lunduka et al., 2013; Mwale, 2023). Redeploying agricultural subsidies away from harmful production and towards healthy food have shown a return-on-investment of more than 200:1 in Mexico (Brero et al., 2023). Some countries have tested medium to long-term subsidies transitions (e.g., transforming coupled subsidies into decoupled subsidies), sometimes incentivizing environmental-friendly practices and generating interesting examples (DeBoe et al., 2020; FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021; Lele & Goswami, 2020; Vos et al., 2022) (see Chapter 5.3, online Supplementary material 5.3.2).

This response option is aligned with most SDGs and Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, notably target 18 (Figures 5.3.4 and 5.3.22). It is not directly dependant on other food systems response options but can influence all of them (Figure 5.3.21). Power imbalances among civil society, government, farmers and companies strongly influence the effectiveness and fairness of the response option.

A guide for the reforming process has been proposed with six key actions (FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021): (1) estimate current support; (2) estimate impacts; (3) design the plan; (4) estimate future impact; (5) review the strategy; and (6) monitor. Although finance helps, successful reforming also depends on other key issues (many not linked to the economy) such as ethics, community-based-decisions, cultural patterns, effective institutions and key complementary measures (Lele & Goswami, 2020; van Dijk et al., 2015) such as:

Policy instruments including regulatory instruments that directly impose environmental requirements on farmers (e.g., standards); hybrid instruments that combine environmental requirements with options for farmers to participate in other non-environmental policies (e.g., cross-compliance mechanisms) and economic instruments like tariffs, tradeable allowances, taxes and subsidies (e.g., agri-environmental payment schemes) (DeBoe, 2020).

Internalizing externalities on certain agricultural products, foods and beverages (Franck et al., 2013; Funke et al., 2022). Here positive examples exist for tackling non-communicable diseases (Teng et al., 2019; WHO, 2017; World Bank, 2020). Internalizing externalities through subsidies and taxes could change producer and/or consumer behaviour and have opposite effects to those generated by harmful subsidies such as price reduction of feed grains. Internalization could reduce the demand for unhealthy food, promoting healthy diets in populations over-consuming meat, while facilitating ecological restoration or the conversion of feed-producing lands to food-producing lands (Andreyeva et al., 2022; Cassidy et al., 2013; Charlebois et al., 2016; Kenny et al., 2023; Niebylski et al., 2015).

Improving relationships and interactions between rural producer communities and urban consumer communities through education, information and capacity-building regarding sustainable production and consumption and ecosystem conservation (Howley et al., 2015; Lastra-Bravo et al., 2015; Riley, 2016).

Considering research and development funding as subsidies (FAO, UNDP, et al., 2021). Green innovation with just 1 per cent of the value of farm output could increase productivity by 30 per cent and reduce GHG emissions. This could promote greater impacts of agroecological practices at scale, recognizing that investment in sustainable production practices pales in comparison to investment in conventional practices (Box 5.3.12) (Tittonell et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reform public spending (F13)" .

