    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Cities are major contributors to climate change, accounting for over 70 per cent of global CO2 emissions (Lwasa et al., 2022). Within a changing climate, complex socio-economic systems like urban areas require dynamic and flexible approaches to adapt to climate change impacts, reduce emissions and engage with different knowledge systems in order to respond to evolving climate needs (Dodman et al., 2022; Lwasa et al., 2022).

Urban nature-based solutions, in line with the agreed definition of nature-based solutions in UNEA Resolution 5/5, are aligned with targets 1 to 4, 7 to 8 and 10 to 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (CBD, 2023), the SDGs, particularly 3, 11, 13, as well as being relevant to the Paris Agreement (Figure 5.5.4). They have a high positive impact across the nexus, contributing to climate change adaptation and (to a lesser extent to mitigation), and to biodiversity, water, food, health and well-being benefits, and thus increasing overall urban climate resilience (Dodman et al., 2022; Goodwin et al., 2023; C. Tirado et al., 2022) (Figure 5.5.3) (see also Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.2; B02, Urban nature-based solutions; Chapter 5.2, section 5.2.3.12; W12, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure; Chapter 5.4, section 5.4.3.9, H09, Urban green infrastructure).

Urban nature-based solutions have very high potential for climate change adaptation, as 56 per cent of the global population (4.4 billion inhabitants) live in cities (World Bank, 2023). The main adaptation benefits include reduction of urban heat and prevention of the impacts of extreme weather events, such as heat waves, heavy rains and floods (Ferreira et al., 2022; Marando et al., 2022; Richards et al., 2022; Rojas et al., 2022). For example, rain gardens and management of living rivers and coasts can contribute to the prevention of river and coastal flooding and disaster risk reduction (Ferreira et al., 2022; Y. Huang et al., 2020).

Urban nature-based solutions have limited climate change mitigation potential as the extent of areas for implementation is restricted. Urban ecosystems (including biomass and soil) have a technical potential to sequester 1.7 to 3.6 GtCO2/yr (Lal et al., 2018). Additional measures or policies are needed to limit emissions and enhance the effectiveness of nature-based solutions (e.g., C10, Global cooperation in finance and technology, sections 5.5.3.10 and 5.5.4). For example, the development of connected green pathways, in combination with policies supporting public and/or active transportation (walking and cycling), can contribute to climate change adaptation by reducing urban heat, while also reducing urban transport GHG emissions, improving air quality and promoting physical and mental health (Rojas-Rueda et al., 2016; C. Tirado et al., 2022). Nexus approaches combining climate change adaptation and mitigation can help the additional 2.5 billion people predicted to live in cities by 2050 to be less exposed to heat waves and air pollution, and to reduce energy use, waste and related emissions (Dodman et al., 2022; Lwasa et al., 2022).

Urban nature-based solutions can improve biodiversity by supporting ecological function, connectivity and condition (Chatzimentor et al., 2020; Filazzola et al., 2019; L. Xie & Bulkeley, 2020), which increases climate resilience (Key et al., 2022). Agroecological urban and peri-urban gardens provide local fresh foods for urban residents, while contributing to climate change adaptation, promoting water storage and reuse of urban organic wastes, reducing urban energy use, and generating benefits to health and well-being by lowering temperatures (Dorr et al., 2023; Garcia et al., 2018; Hume et al., 2022; Puigdueta et al., 2021).(C. Tirado et al., 2022; J. Xu et al., 2023) Urban reforestation and the creation of green spaces are other options to mitigate urban heat impacts and reduce heat-related stress, air pollution, and mortality, while enhancing air quality, general physical health, and alleviating stress and mental health disorders (C. Tirado et al., 2022; J. Xu et al., 2023). In combination with non-nature-based solutions urban planning solutions, they can further contribute to positive health outcomes. For example, increasing green spaces, alongside increasing reflectivity of pavement surfaces in cities can substantially reduce urban heat island effects.

By integrating nature-based solutions in urban environments, cities can improve future liveability, particularly for the economically and socially most vulnerable (Kolokotsa et al., 2020). The effects on vulnerable groups and social acceptance vary across different nature-based solutions and countries (Sari et al., 2023) (Figure 5.5.4). If implemented effectively, considering equity and specific characteristics of the location, urban nature-based solutions can have a positive effect on vulnerable communities (Adu Boateng et al., 2023; W. Zhou et al., 2021). Otherwise, nature-based solutions might lead to negative impacts and cause resistance towards the response option (Choi et al., 2021). Integrating environmental justice in urban planning is critical for preventing negative effects such as widening inequity gaps (Langemeyer & Connolly, 2020). Implementation of urban nature-based solutions can lead to green gentrification by making the area more attractive and increasing property prices, thus displacing local communities (Anguelovski et al., 2022; Bockarjova et al., 2020). To avoid unintended consequences, the implementation of urban nature-based solutions can be accompanied by measures to ensure affordable housing and social infrastructure (Shokry et al., 2022).

For urban nature-based solutions to be effective, they must consider communities’ vulnerabilities, needs, identities (e.g., IPLC, gender, race, ethnicity), values and knowledge, and engage them in design and evaluation (Bremer et al., 2021; Calderón-Argelich et al., 2021; Kato-Huerta & Geneletti, 2022; Pineda-Pinto et al., 2022; Toxopeus et al., 2020; Wolff et al., 2022). Monitoring of urban nature-based solutions after implementation is also key to successfully addressing interactions with people and across the nexus elements (Kabisch et al., 2022; Raymond et al., 2017; van der Jagt et al., 2023).

Urban nature-based solutions are increasingly mainstreamed in national priorities and policies (Kabisch et al., 2016). The costs depend on the location where specific nature-based solutions are implemented (Van Zanten et al., 2023), and are higher in historical cities than in new ones. However, even high costs can be offset by benefits in the long-term (Horváthová et al., 2021; Van Zanten et al., 2023) (Figure 5.5.4), although there are challenges in accounting for their non-monetary benefits due to their long-term nature and nexus-wide influence. This response option is a promising option for transformative change and can enable shifts to more equitable and sustainable futures (Figure 5.5.4). However, its transformative change potential depends on governance and different systemic settings as noted above.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Urban nature-based solutions (C14)" .

