    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Halting the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity for food production is a response option that can be applied globally (Jacobson et al., 2019; Jung et al., 2020;

Riggio et al., 2020) and in the short-term, with long-term benefits (Figure 5.3.5). It has strong positive impacts across the nexus, although it has the potential for negative impacts on food production and access to food (Figure 5.3.3).

Food demand is projected to drive agricultural expansion and the loss of 2-10 million km² of ecosystems of high ecological integrity in coming decades (Alexandratos et al., 2012; Bajželj et al., 2014; Oakleaf et al., 2015; Tilman et al., 2011; Willett, 2019; B. A. Williams et al., 2020; D. R. Williams et al., 2021). Halting this conversion could have a negative impact on food production and access to food, particularly in the Global South and for the most marginalized in society (Alexandratos et al., 2012; Bajželj et al., 2014; Oakleaf et al., 2015; Tilman et al., 2011; Willett, 2019; B. A. Williams et al., 2020; D. R. Williams et al., 2021). However, this negative impact could be avoided by producing more food from existing agricultural and degraded land (sections 5.3.3.2 (F02), 5.3.3.4 (F04) and 5.3.3.5 (F05)), reducing demand by reducing food waste (section 5.3.3.10 (F10)) and shifting to sustainable healthy diets that require less land to support (section 5.3.3.11 (F11)) (Tilman et al., 2011; Willett, 2019; D. R. Williams et al., 2021).

Halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity has very large positive impacts on biodiversity and climate change. Intact ecosystems are irreplaceable for conserving native species (Balmford, 2021; Watson et al., 2018); ecosystem function and resilience (Eigenbrod et al., 2015; Forzieri et al., 2022; Watson et al., 2018); reducing or preventing fragmentation (Brennan et al., 2022; Crooks et al., 2017; Haddad et al., 2015); and carbon storage and sequestration (Friedlingstein et al., 2022; Molotoks et al., 2018; Roe et al., 2019, 2021; D. R. Williams et al., 2018).

Halting conversion of these ecosystems also benefits human health and well-being by reducing the probability of zoonotic disease transmission to people and livestock (Allen et al., 2017; Gottdenker et al., 2014; Rohr et al., 2019), reducing vulnerability to climate change and temperature extremes (Butt et al., 2023; IPCC, 2022) and benefiting mental, social and cultural well-being (IPBES, 2019a; Pascual et al., 2017). It also increases water provision by regulating streamflow, improving water quality and increasing precipitation, although there may be local-scale complexity in precipitation changes (Brauman et al., 2020; Hoek Van Dijke et al., 2022; Spracklen et al., 2018).

Multiple actions exist for halting conversion (Table 5.3.5). The most appropriate, effective and equitable actions will depend on social, economic, political and agricultural contexts. Halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity is at Technical Readiness Level 9 (Figure 5.3.6); direct costs are relatively low, but local opportunity costs may be very high if the land is needed to meet food demand (Armstrong, 2023; Balmford & Whitten, 2003; J. M. H. Green et al., 2018; Waldron et al., 2013, 2017, 2022) (Figure 5.3.4).

This response option is aligned strongly with multiple international frameworks (CBD, 2023; UN, 2023; UNFCCC, 2015; United Nations, 2015). Alignment with national and local priorities, social acceptability and impacts on equity and IPLC will depend on the action taken, scale of opportunity costs, their distribution and their mitigation. There is limited evidence of transformative change potential for this response option alone, but it has high transformative potential when combined with yield-increasing response options (Figure 5.3.4).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (F01)" .

