    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Health risks from pollution (air, water and soil) are influenced by anthropogenic actions including human induced pollution, dietary and consumption behaviour, unregulated economic growth, industrialization and demographics (P. Dasgupta et al., 2018; FAO & UNEP, 2021; Thompson & Darwish, 2019). Estimates indicate that 9 million premature deaths occur per year from pollution, of which 90 per cent are in lower-middle-income countries and among vulnerable populations, with variation in attributable sources of pollution (Fuller et al., 2022) and in disease burdens within regions and countries (WHO, 2019a; J. Wolf et al., 2023).

There are multiple benefits to other nexus elements by linking pollution prevention and health protection (Figure 5.4.5). Reductions in indoor and ambient air pollution, biomass consumption, and air-water-chemical pollution-induced habitat and biodiversity loss can enhance access to safe water and clean air, enhance sustainable food production and food and nutritional security (Willett et al., 2019; Woldetsadik et al., 2018; Zeng et al., 2019) and avoid the emergence of diseases (Aerts et al., 2018; Maquart et al., 2022; Marselle et al., 2021; Schmeller et al., 2018; Willetts et al., 2023). Several interconnections exist between climate change, biodiversity and pollution management for advancing human well-being (Akinnawo, 2023; Boudewijns et al., 2022; Li et al., 2021; Zahno et al., 2020). Examples include the sustainable use of medicinal plants and reducing pollution within the health and care sectors through reduced emissions and residues, promotion of clean energy, green infrastructure and aligned procurement policies (Green Deal, 2023; Lenzen et al., 2020) (WHO, 2023a). Reducing pollution was also assessed by other subchapters of Chapter 5 (see Chapter 5.2, section 5.2.3.12, W12, Reduce water pollution; Chapter 5.3, sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9 (F07, F08 and F09, reducing nutrient, pesticide and plastic pollution, respectively; Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.12, C12, Reduce short-lived climate pollutants).

Actions to benefit health and other nexus elements includes labelling of both nutritional and environmental outcomes for promoting healthy diets (De Bauw et al., 2021); command and control instruments such as bans (e.g., for plastic pollution); impact evaluations to trigger response options such as water pollution treatment for reducing morbidity (Luo et al., 2022) and air pollution reduction for increasing life expectancy in lower-middle-income countries (Abbasi-Kangevari et al., 2023); reducing harm from noise and air pollution by increased exposure to biodiversity (Marselle et al., 2021); and lowering risks of mortality and morbidity from air pollution reduction through green areas (Bloemsma et al., 2019; Crouse et al., 2019).

Solutions for pollution may come with economic costs, including those borne by individuals (e.g., through temporary job losses in response to changing technologies), public or private entities (e.g., losses from closure of thermal power plants), and governments (e.g., costs of funding transitions to net zero or changing land use in favour of conservation) (Figure 5.4.6). This deters action even where cost-effective technologies are available (e.g., improved cookstoves, clean cooking fuels, greening infrastructural interventions, tackling short-lived climate pollutants and waste treatment in healthcare and pharmaceutical sectors) (Eckelman et al., 2020; Lenzen et al., 2020; Speight, 2020).

The high social costs of pollution (e.g., lowered labour productivity, morbidity, mortality, opportunity costs from diversion of public resources, including finance and human resources to deal with the consequences for healthcare) and the fact that markets fail to convey these costs to society, calls for publicly supported actions for pollution prevention. The social costs of inaction are usually much higher than the costs of prevention (World Bank, 2016). While restoration is costlier than conservation for both ecosystems and human health (P. Dasgupta, 2021), there is evidence that the health co-benefits to humans and nature from climate change mitigation, inclusive of air pollution reduction, are greater than the costs of such policies (M. A. Martin et al., 2022). Reducing pollution and improving health, supported by a judicious mix of regulatory, financial and social mechanisms, and supported by value systems, can be used to realize the social gains, internalize externalities and manage costs. For example, efforts to minimize land and water pollution have been initiated as actions to enhance relational (nature for culture) values in parts of the Hindu Kush Himalaya (P. Dasgupta & Shakya, 2023).

Health actors can engage in evidence generation, management and co-creation of integrated public policies influencing funding, social behaviour and legal instruments to prevent pollution. Pollution reduction is key to achieving global goals and targets on health as enshrined in the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement (Baste & Watson, 2022) (Figure 5.4.6). Reducing air pollution has transformative potential reaching across nexus elements, with the most immediate and significant benefits and co-benefits accruing to human health (Roy, 2018) (Figure 5.4.11).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pollution prevention (H07)" .

