    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Rewilding is an open-ended restoration approach focused on the recovery of ecological processes and interactions. It is particularly relevant in the face of climate change, where uncertainties in ecosystem responses to interventions challenge prescriptions of specific end states in ecological restoration (Du Toit & Pettorelli, 2019).

Rewilding initiatives are predominantly local or sub-national, but their integration into national policies and multilevel environmental governance remains limited (Fayet et al., 2022; Jepson et al., 2018). Evidence from large-scale passive rewilding, however, demonstrates the potential for significant long-term social and cultural transformation (Calaboni et al., 2018; Li et al., 2019; Molin et al., 2017; Navarro & Pereira, 2015; Ramankutty et al., 2010; Silva et al., 2017; Tedesco et al., 2023).

Promising rewilding interventions include dam removal (Vahedifard et al., 2021), wetland and peatland restoration (Tanneberger et al., 2021), forest landscape restoration (Chazdon, Lindenmayer, et al., 2020; Stanturf & Mansourian, 2020) (see section 5.1.3.1, B05, Forest landscape restoration) and closer-to-nature forestry (Dandy & Wynne-Jones, 2019). Contentious issues include the return of large carnivores (e.g., Davoli et al., 2022; Rode et al., 2021) and the substitution of costly agri-environmental measures with rewilding practices such as natural grazing (e.g., Schou et al., 2021). These controversies may be addressed with intermediate approaches such as agricultural rewilding, which combines ecological restoration with limited agricultural production (M. S. Corson et al., 2022), alongside with cultural change in views and values of nature (see also section 5.1.3.14, B14, Reconnecting people with nature).

Rewilding can contribute to multiple nexus benefits (Carver et al., 2021; Perino et al., 2019; Root-Bernstein et al., 2017; Svenning, 2020). The well-documented biodiversity benefits of rewilding include habitat creation, restoration of ecological processes and improved connectivity (Eckerter et al., 2022; Garrido et al., 2019; Hart et al., 2023; Paillet et al., 2010), particularly in expanding the range and abundance of keystone wildlife species (Chapron et al., 2014; P. J. Seddon et al., 2014). However, mitigation of biological invasions needs more evidence (Derham et al., 2018; Mungi et al., 2023). The removal of dams and dikes, by enabling the up- and down-stream movement of aquatic wildlife and sediments and reconnecting rivers to their floodplains, benefits freshwater and marine biodiversity and water quantity and quality (Bednarek, 2001; Habel et al., 2020; Stanley & Doyle, 2003). These biodiversity benefits can support food security and health through provisioning of wild foods (Ingram et al., 2021) and other non-timber forest products (Wiersum, 2017).

Climate change benefits of rewilding are less documented and likely context-dependent (Fayet & Verburg, 2023). The rewetting of wetlands and peatlands, forest secondary succession, proforestation (the protection of existing forests, including degraded and secondary forests, towards full ecological maturity) and changes in trophic networks that increase soil carbon pools (Kreyling et al., 2021; Moomaw et al., 2019; Schwieger et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2023) are promising for climate change mitigation. Benefits for climate change adaptation through enhanced ecosystem and social resilience are also expected.

Rewilding can be implemented in diverse ways following guideline principles (Carver et al., 2021), but its social and economic impacts remain understudied. The involvement of local communities, particularly IPLC, and considerations of equity are central challenges (A. Martin et al., 2021; Mustonen et al., 2022). The environmental and economic costs and benefits of scaling out rewilding initiatives, and the development of novel policies and markets to enable it (Bateman & Balmford, 2023; Schulte to Bühne et al., 2022), present challenges for widespread adoption and may require cultural shifts (Mikołajczak et al., 2021; Van Meerbeek et al., 2019; Wynne-Jones, 2022) (see also section 5.1.3.14, B14, Reconnecting people with nature).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rewilding (B08)" .

