        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YVPKU33F>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZJW8TQN3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZVDG8AH5> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Integrated landscape and seascape approaches are comprehensive strategies to address the intricate interplay between socioeconomic and environmental objectives such as food production, local livelihoods, biodiversity conservation and climate action at landscape and seascape scales. Integrated landscape and seascape approaches encompass various frameworks including integrated landscape approaches (Reed et al., 2020), landscape stewardship (Bieling & Plieninger, 2017), foodscapes (Bossio et al., 2021), forest and landscape restoration (Chazdon et al., 2017), multifunctional scapes (Pörtner et al., 2021) and seascape approaches (Murphy et al., 2021). Integrated landscape and seascape approaches emphasize the integration of nexus elements across terrestrial, freshwater, coastal and marine ecosystems and leverage various ecosystem-based approaches to address societal challenges (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2019). While the terminology may vary, the core concept revolves around holistic, long-term and adaptive management of landscapes and seascapes to achieve socioecological objectives through collaboration among stakeholders from different sectors and social groups (Reed et al., 2021).

Integrated landscape and seascape approaches are a promising and well-practiced response option for the nexus as they operate across higher-order interactions (e.g., water-food-biodiversity, water-health-climate change) and place a particular emphasis on connectivity across socioecological land- and seascapes and the terrestrial, freshwater and marine realms (Nishi et al., 2022; Nishi & Yamazaki, 2020). Recent survey-based analysis suggests that highly integrated initiatives that include many sectors have an overall better performance for agriculture, nature conservation, livelihoods and effective governance in comparison to less integrated (siloed) initiatives which are biased towards conservation or agriculture (Carmenta et al., 2020). Integrated landscape and seascape approaches are implemented in all inhabited continents and include global scale programs such as the Satoyama Initiative, the UNESCO Man and Biosphere reserves and the FAO Globally Important Agriculture Heritage Systems, among others. Thirty integrated landscape and seascape approaches case studies were analysed in this subchapter: seven from Africa, twelve from the Americas, four from Asia and Oceania, and seven from Europe (Table 5.1.7). This quantitative analysis was complemented by conclusions from global syntheses (Carmenta et al., 2020; Denier et al., 2015; Duncan et al., 2021; García-Martín et al., 2016; Suit et al., 2021). These diverse examples demonstrate the effectiveness of ILSA in benefitting multiple elements of the nexus and sustainability dimensions (ecological, social, and economic). Relative cost-effectiveness and affordability are also features that make integrated landscape and seascape approaches an attractive nexus response option (Arts et al., 2017).

Integrated landscape and seascape approaches generally focus on food production and nature conservation as entry points, but also include and benefit water and climate change (Carmenta et al., 2020; Duncan et al., 2021; García-Martín et al., 2016; J. C. Milder et al., 2014; Obura et al., 2021), and health (Sakkhamduang et al., 2022), with most initiatives impacting three or more nexus elements. For example, the integrated landscape approach with IPLC engagement in decision making in Nagaland, India, strengthened and linked community conserved areas for conservation across three villages—Sukhai, Ghukhuyi, and Kivikhu—protecting 939 hectares (Edake et al., 2019). Hunting, tree felling, and destructive fishing were prohibited across the remaining forest and river landscapes. A capacity-building program reconnected the community with their forests, fostering conservation support through livelihood opportunities, such as ecotourism training, and involvement in biodiversity monitoring. The initiative revived the traditional practice of conserving community forests, secured by customary rights and reinforced by a joint management committee. Mobilization efforts and synergy-building enhanced nature’s contributions to people, improving productivity in shifting cultivation (jhum) areas. This outcome impacted all nexus elements though wildlife conservation and revival of wild populations, increased diversity; food from forest, restoration of food production; carbon storage in recovered/protected forest; traditional medicine secured and general wellbeing of community; enhanced watershed services of protected forest.

The benefits of initiatives aimed at sustainable food production (JWRC, 2012; Karimova et al., 2022; Sasaoka, 2010) for improved terrestrial and coastal water quality (Déja et al., 2019; Hsu et al., 2021; World Bank, 2021) and biodiversity conservation (Chape, 2015; Edake et al., 2020) are well documented. This is particularly visible at the landscape-seascape interface, where environmentally-friendly agricultural production activities upstream enhance plant and faunal diversity across the watershed and safeguard coastal and marine organisms and ecosystems (Guo et al., 2021; Hsu et al., 2021; Karimova & Lee, 2022; Tanaka et al., 2021; Turak et al., 2021). Synergies and trade-offs in relation to health and climate change (apart from disaster risk reduction) are much less reported in the literature. This may be explained by the difficulty of measuring and monitoring them within a short project span (two to three years) of most documented integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Nishi et al., 2021).

Policy instruments used within integrated landscape and seascape approaches include biodiversity-focused spatial planning, zoning regulations, habitat restoration subsidies, payments for ecosystem services, product certification and support for sustainable farming practices (Arts et al., 2017; Forestry Bureau (林務局), 2021). These instruments aim to incentivize sustainable land and resource management practices while addressing competing interests and values. While financial resources, technical capacities and infrastructure can pose challenges, evidence suggests that well-implemented integrated landscape and seascape approaches can achieve high social, economic and environmental effectiveness (Suit et al., 2021).

Integrated landscape and seascape approaches involve multiple actors including governments, IPLC, academia, private sector and other relevant stakeholders in a collaborative, adaptive, context-specific and scale-sensitive way, with attention to multiple knowledge types, values and objectives (García-Martín et al., 2016; Karimova & Lee, 2022; Suit et al., 2021). Participatory, collaborative and adaptive governance is essential for the success of integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Box 5.1.4). Inclusive governance mechanisms that involve IPLC play a crucial role in ensuring that integrated landscape and seascape approaches are human rights-based, socially acceptable and equitable (Adade Williams et al., 2020; Chiasson et al., 2019; Palang et al., 2019). However, governance arrangements need to go beyond consultation and actively involve stakeholders in co-management, decision-making and effectiveness evaluation processes. Moreover, intermediate actors such as non-governmental organizations, academia and media can play a critical role in bridging gaps between stakeholders and facilitating inclusive governance processes (Lee & Karimova, 2021; Rees Catalán, 2015).

Despite their advantages and increasing popularity, integrated landscape and seascape approaches are vulnerable to some implementation challenges, including setting and checking boundary and scale; degree of multi-stakeholder engagement and participation; identification of priority issues in the beginning and throughout the process; monitoring and evaluation of effectiveness; ability to be sustained over the long-term (continuity); and scaling up of experiences (Denier et al., 2015; Karimova et al., 2022; Nishi et al., 2021; Suit et al., 2021). More consistent and evidence-based documentation of integrated landscape and seascape approaches globally, supported by both quantitative and qualitative long-term data, presents a high opportunity for addressing these challenges, which in turn, is critical for scaling this response option (Angelstam et al., 2013; Sayer et al., 2017).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (B09)" .

