    ipbes:hasDescription """Rights-based approaches to conservation encompass the application of human rights principles to the ownership, governance, access and management of natural resources and biodiversity (Figure 5.1.7) (Witter & Satterfield, 2019) (see Chapter 4, Box 4.11). They also include the rights of nature, the rights of Mother Earth and rights of other non-human entities (Kenrick et al., 2023). This ensures that conservation-related laws/policies, plans and processes do not violate rights and promote human and natural rights as much as possible (HRBWG, 2022). Rights-based approaches can help address the multiple links between conservation and rights, recognizing that exclusionary approaches to conservation can violate rights; that rights are key to supporting conservation contributions of IPLC, women and other rights-holders; and that all people have rights to a healthy, sustainable environment (D. Boyd & Keene, 2021). Rights-based approaches refer to both collective and individual (HRBWG, 2022; H. D. Jonas et al., 2014) procedural rights, such as participation, access to information and Free Prior and Informed Consent (FPIC; McDermott et al., 2013; Raftopoulos & Short, 2019), as well as substantive rights, e.g., water, land, territory, food and cultural rights (C. Corson et al., 2020; Witter & Satterfield, 2014).

A number of concepts and mechanisms exist to support the implementation of rights-based approaches in conservation, such as co-management (N. Dawson et al., 2023); recognition and strengthening of customary and collective rights (Aswani, 2005; Memon & Kirk, 2012); the interdependence of biological and cultural diversity (Gavin et al., 2018; Sterling et al., 2017); ‘decolonization’ approaches (Domínguez & Luoma, 2020); and legal demarcation, titling and reparations for territories (Claridge & Kobei, 2023; Rist et al., 2019). Rights-based approaches generally go beyond inclusive and participatory conservation efforts; they are firmly grounded in recognizing rights. This includes, in particular, the leadership and rights of IPLC in decision-making, management, benefit-sharing and ownership of territories (Reyes-García et al., 2023; Tauli, 2022; Tauli-Corpuz et al., 2020; Tugendhat et al., 2023). Women’s rights are also central in rights-based approaches to conservation, particularly through seeking to promote gender equality and empowerment (Zweifel, 1997). Rights-based approaches recognize the unique contributions of women to conservation, ensure their rights to land and natural resources, and secure their rights to be free from gender-based violence (Castañeda Carney et al., 2020).

Rights-based approaches implementation involves both rights-holders (entities who have rights) and the duty-bearers responsible for upholding their rights (mainly States). There is also increasing recognition of non-state actors responsibilities for human rights, such as business (United Nations, 2011) and conservation organizations (H. D. Jonas et al., 2014). These sectors need to assume responsibilities and avoid causing or contributing to negative human rights impacts in their operations towards biodiversity conservation.

International and regional human rights instruments recognize many rights that can be (positively or negatively) impacted by conservation. This includes the UN Declaration of the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP), which has highlighted rights to, inter alia, self-determination, language, sociocultural identity, collective actions, lands, waters and territories, among other forms of rights, and notes that communities should not be deprived of these rights by displacement from protected areas or other conservation measures (Artelle, Zurba, Bhattacharyya, et al., 2019; Springer & Campese, 2011). Rights-based approaches can thus help avoid and remediate previous “fortress” models that led to disempowerment and displacement of many IPLC (Aini et al., 2023; Emini et al., 2023). International environmental law also increasingly recognizes related rights. The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, for example, includes rights-based approaches principles in several targets, including targets 21 and 22 that note that IPLC have procedural, lands, waters and resource rights, while target 23 discusses rights of women and girls to land and natural resources as well as “their full, equitable, meaningful and informed participation and leadership at all levels” of conservation action (CBD, 2023). Targets 1 and 3 also provide notice of safeguards of rights of IPLC in the expansion of spatial conservation.

The rights-based approach to biodiversity conservation needs to address a range of interconnected challenges. Expanding the notion of rights and access to consider the nexus between biodiversity and water, food, health and climate change (see Figure 5.1.8 and Chapter 4, Box 4.11) helps understanding of the interplay of actors involved and the political struggles that drive the sharing of benefits of nature among social groups and to nature itself (Campese et al., 2009).

There are many recommendations and road maps for applying rights-based approaches to conservation. These include ones focused on development (UNDP, 2006), conservation (D. Boyd & Keene, 2021; H. Jonas et al., n.d.; Rights and Resources Initiative, 2023), rights and leadership of IPLC (Forest Peoples Programme et al., 2020; ICCA Consortium, 2021; Stevens et al., 2017; Tauli-Corpuz et al., 2020; Tugendhat et al., 2023), governance of tenure (FAO, 2019) and related guidelines) and many decisions taken by the Conference of the Parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity, among others. Looking across these, successful rights-based approaches have in common fully recognizing and integrating rights and equity in policy, governance and management (including through strong safeguards and grievance mechanisms); recognizing and respecting rights-holders as leaders and partners; understanding and addressing inequities and their underlying causes, including in power relationships; and ensuring accountable action, including through monitoring of processes and outcomes and strengthening the capacity of rights-holders (to assert and claim their rights) and duty-bearers (to uphold their obligations).

Meta-reviews of rights-based approaches have strongly concluded that positive biodiversity outcomes are better achieved when Indigenous or local autonomy and control, based on local priorities and knowledge systems, form the basis for conservation actions (Chaplin-Kramer et al., 2023; N. M. Dawson et al., 2021; Loch & Riechers, 2021). Expansion of rights-based approaches therefore has the potential to deliver benefits to hundreds of millions of people worldwide whose rights to territories are not currently recognized and who are facing threats of dispossession and violence (Scheidel et al., 2020). IPLC recognition and rights contribute to climate change mitigation; for example, 30 per cent of the Amazon Basin’s above-ground forest carbon is estimated to be in Indigenous territories, which have been more successful than traditional protected areas in conserving these stocks (Alejo et al., 2021).

There are powerful examples of rights-based approaches where IPLC rights – including over their land and territories (e.g., through titling or other appropriate mechanism), governance, knowledge systems and management – are appropriately recognized and supported (Box 5.1.5). Secure rights – including tenure – can also help ensure sustained, effective outcomes. For example, in the Brazilian Amazon, IPLC territories with full enforceable property rights have shown a significant decrease in deforestation, whereas those with incomplete or less secure rights have not; causality has been clearly linked to the tenure processes through use of data sets of deforestation from before and after titling (Baragwanath & Bayi, 2020; Benzeev et al., 2023).

Despite the increasing successes, implementation of rights-based approaches continues to face challenges. State-governed and managed protected areas continue to be contested for their exclusionary boundaries and approaches, and gaps remain around grievance and restitution mechanisms so that rights-based approaches can be effectively enforced (Tauli-Corpuz et al., 2020). Power relationships among rights-holders, duty-bearers and other actors in rights-based approaches implementation can be complex and asymmetrical (C. Corson et al., 2020). For example, courts in different countries often interpret rights-based approaches in different ways and enforcement varies widely (Nikolakis & Hotte, 2020). Mismatches across scales, with higher levels retaining decision-making authority and paying only lip service to rights-based approaches, has also been a barrier to effectiveness (Gustafsson & Schilling-Vacaflor, 2022). These power imbalances can lead to marginalization, lack of meaningful participation and limited resource allocation for IPLC (Tauli-Corpuz et al., 2020). Rights-based approaches are also not always able to tackle interlinked nexus issues, particularly where external actors and pressures remain unchanged. For example, in New Zealand, granting of co-management of fishing rights to Māori communities was not enough to conserve lake resources, as upstream dairy farms continued to produce nitrogen runoff that could not be stopped by the rights-based approaches implemented downstream (Bodwitch et al., 2022).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rights-based approaches (B10)" .

