    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Growing awareness of the historical shortfalls of nature conservation has led to calls for transformative changes in governance to allow for mechanisms that are integrative across scales and sectors, based on comprehensive and adequate information, adaptive to change and inclusive of diverse values and interests (N. J. Bennett & Satterfield, 2018; Chaffin et al., 2016; Cooke et al., 2022; Razzaque et al., 2019). Emerging insights from the broader literature on the governance of socioecological systems, as well as ongoing transitions in the governance of land and water resources, suggest that moving towards nexus governance will require changes in current mechanisms for biodiversity governance (Chapter 4, section 4.5). This includes: recognition of the dynamic and complex interactions between humans and nature as social-ecological systems and the influence of drivers of change across multiple scales (Palomo et al., 2014; Weatherley-Singh et al., 2022; Zavaleta & Chapin, III, 2010); enhanced coordination of management goals across scales and sectors (Díaz et al., 2019; Zinngrebe et al., 2022); integration of diverse types and sources of knowledge (Akamani, 2021; E. M. Bennett et al., 2016; Lélé & Norgaard, 2005; Pickering et al., 2022; Tengö et al., 2017; I. J. Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022); development of diverse institutional structures that are nested across multiple scales; and facilitation of analytic deliberation processes for enhancing conflict management, as well as building capacities for adaptation and transformation (Akamani et al., 2016; Akamani & Wilson, 2011; Dietz et al., 2003; Pahl-Wostl, 2019; Razzaque et al., 2019; Valman et al., 2015; Weitz et al., 2017). These changes are transversal to multiple actions and policy instruments exemplified in the assessed biodiversity response options, and can ensure that they are complementary to achieve a comprehensive and sustainable approach to nexus challenges and even move towards transformative change (Figure 5.1.10). In addition to overcoming challenges in the transition process, the longer time frame required for realizing the benefits of transformative governance also deserves consideration in policy formulation and implementation processes (Meijaard et al., 2022) (see also Chapter 4, section 4.5.4.2).

In line with the discussions of both nexus governance and transformative change in biodiversity governance, all but three of the 14 response options presented in section 5.1.3 are implemented through multilevel governance and participatory processes, thus promoting procedural and recognition equity (Figure 5.1.3). However, some examples of implementation of response options have lower procedural equity, such as forest restoration or rewilding programs that are supported by funding from offsets, and some cases of area-based conservation in which the institutions involved in implementation do not fully address equity concerns (Akamani & Hall, 2019). The current emphasis on other effective conservation measures (OECMs) in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework is likely to improve these issues, as IPLC should be increasingly included in leadership roles (Estradivari et al., 2022b; Gurney et al., 2021; Maini et al., 2023). The current global policy context, with its strong focus on IPLC engagement across biodiversity and other nexus elements (CBD, 2023; IPBES, 2019a; United Nations, 2023), provides an enabling environment for area-based conservation approaches. In addition, in urban environments, effective monitoring and evaluation mechanisms, as well as networks for knowledge exchange, in the implementation of nature-based solutions that are locally led and managed by IPLC and other non-state actors are essential for their scaling out across larger geographic scales (i.e., mechanisms for collaboration as needed for nexus governance, see Chapter 4, section 4.5.4).

The examples examined (more than 2000 documented in 104 sources of information assessed5) show that while the implementation of biodiversity response options may promote various forms of social equity, the pre-existence of social equity provides a conducive environment for the successful implementation of these response options. In addition to equity, social acceptability and technological feasibility are also enabling factors in the implementation of biodiversity response options (Figure 5.1.3, 5.1.11). Conversely, the results show that in contexts with low equity, such as cases involving forest restoration where principles of forest landscape restoration are not applied, and in some urban nature-based solutions cases, low social acceptance can be a barrier to successful implementation (Elias et al., 2022; Osborne et al., 2021). To overcome some of these institutional barriers, it is important to recognize the legitimacy and efficacy of alternative governance mechanisms, such as those based on customary systems and ILK. Such long-lasting, self-organized local institutions can provide important lessons that are likely to improve governance mechanisms for nature and biodiversity worldwide (Artelle, Zurba, Bhattacharrya, et al., 2019). However, it is often difficult for IPLC to alter long-standing structures of inequity and the limited attention to rights in policy processes (Arney et al., 2023).

Economic cost is a frequent barrier across the examined examples of implementation of response options (i.e., funds are an enabling factor, Figure 5.1.11). For example, long-term funding is often limited in community-based area-based conservation approaches (Kawaka et al., 2017). Payment for ecosystem services can be used as a financing tool for different goals across multiple response options including area-based conservation, forest landscape restoration, and in combination with rights-based approaches to conservation for supporting IPLC (Alix-Garcia & Wolff, 2014). However, the existing literature indicates that payment for ecosystem services mechanisms is not a panacea for funding, but are complex tools that require multiple actors and intermediaries to work together and this complexity can reduce efficiencies (Wunder et al., 2018, 2020). Such mechanisms may also have mixed ecological outcomes that are context dependent (Börner et al., 2017; Ezzine-de-Blas et al., 2016; Samii et al., 2014). Moreover, they require attention to the sustainability of long-term financing as well as participant motivations (Akers & Yasué, 2019; K. W. Jones et al., 2020; Lliso et al., 2022), as they can induce trade-offs related to benefit distribution and equity (Blundo-Canto et al., 2018; Hu et al., 2023; Samii et al., 2014) and thus need to be designed carefully. Other financial enablers, such as repurposing subsidies (Box 5.1.7), can help support effective and sustainable implementation of biodiversity response options by freeing up additional funding (see Chapter 6, section 6.2.3.2).

In several examples of urban nature-based solutions, as in other response options, the most frequent barriers are limitations in institutional capacities and participation in governance (Figure 5.1.3, Figure 5.1.11). These institutional and capacity constraints limit the potential for transformative change to varying degrees across all response options (Figure 5.1.3). Overcoming these constraints will require implementation of decision-making processes that promote meaningful participation among actors for transformative social learning, improving upon mechanisms for facilitating cross-sectoral coordination, and utilization of negotiation and other dispute resolution mechanisms that promote procedural and substantive equity in governance processes. In this regard, different mechanisms for cross-scalar, cross-sector and cross-boundary governance, involving institutional structures and processes that transcend administrative boundaries, components of landscapes and seascapes or even sectoral boundaries, may be needed although are difficult to implement (Cockburn et al., 2020).

Notes
8. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus governance to support biodiversity response options" .

