    ipbes:hasDescription """Executive summary

Climate change threatens all nexus elements: biodiversity, water, food, health and human well-being (well established) {5.5.1}. Effective, rapid, sustained mitigation and accelerated implementation of adaptation actions have potential to benefit all nexus elements when combined with minimizing trade-offs and fostering synergistic outcomes (well established) {5.5.1}.

Rapid transitions in energy use and production, land and terrestrial and marine ecosystem management, urban areas, industrial activities and society are crucial for enabling climate change mitigation, adaptation and sustainable development (well established) {5.5.3}. Rapid transitions in energy use and production include the replacement of energy generated from fossil fuels with the rapid expansion of renewable energy, such as wind power and solar photovoltaics, improvements in efficiency and reductions in consumption. Transitions in land and terrestrial and marine ecosystem management, through response options such as sustainable intensification, agroecology, forest-based climate solutions, restoration of blue carbon ecosystems and integrated multitrophic aquaculture, can also support climate change mitigation and adaptation while benefiting the other nexus elements. Transitions in urban areas can be achieved through integrated urban nature-based solutions or in industrial activities through reducing short-lived climate pollutants and implementing a circular bioeconomy. Finally, societal transitions that can enable climate change mitigation and adaptation include shifts to sustainable healthy diets (well established) {5.5.3}. These transitions require interdisciplinary research, policy support and societal engagement {5.5.1, 5.5.4}. Fifteen response options that deliver climate change adaptation, mitigation or both through these system transitions have been assessed. Four out of the 15 response options contribute positively to all other nexus elements while in the remaining cases there are both positive and negative effects on nexus elements (well established) {5.5.3}. Those contributing positively to all nexus elements are increased soil organic carbon; circular bioeconomy; multi-hazards early warning systems and global cooperation for finance and technology {5.5.3} (Figure 5.5.3). The options that contribute positively across all nexus elements are most likely to have high transformative change potential and to bridge inequity gaps because they co-deliver solutions to a range of global challenges (well established) {5.5.3}.

Some response options enable the successful implementation of others, highlighting that bundling and sequencing of response options can help deliver transformative change (well established) {5.5.3, 5.5.6}. For example, the reduced pressure on land arising from shifting to sustainable heathy diets in overconsuming regions {5.5.3.15} and/or through sustainable intensification {5.5.3.2} can enable more sustainable forms of farming such as agroecology {5.5.3.11}. This can free up land for other land-based climate change response options, such as reforestation as a forest-based climate solution {5.5.3.12}, wetland restoration and conservation {5.5.3.4} and land-based renewable energy, e.g., solar photovoltaics on land {5.5.3.6, 5.5.6}. Although renewable energy options provide high climate change mitigation potential, they can have unavoidable negative effects on nexus elements, similar to fossil fuel infrastructure (well established). For example, offshore wind power can impact marine ecosystem structure and functioning, and associated fisheries, if poorly planned (well established) {5.5.3.5}. Integrating renewable energy production with energy efficiency measures or within existing agricultural and urban systems reduces such risks. Taking a holistic approach ensures sustainability and mitigates potential harm (well established) {5.5.4}.

Enabling transformative change towards a climate-resilient future requires enhancing synergies and managing trade-offs among climate change adaption, mitigation and other elements of the nexus (i.e., biodiversity, water, food and health) (well established) {5.5.4}. This can be enabled through participative, rights-based governance, innovations, partnerships and commitments to support global equity and climate justice in line with existing global agreements (well established) {5.5.4}. Successful implementation of response options requires capacity-building of local actors such as local governments and municipalities, civil society organizations, community-based organizations and Indigenous Peoples and local communities to overcome social, political, institutional, educational and economic barriers (well established) {5.5.4}. Investing in capacity-building facilitates widespread adoption and effective climate action. Regional differences and varying effectiveness in implementation exist among the promising response options. Involving local communities and considering all nexus elements from the start is necessary for appropriate contextualization. Implementation through participative, rights-based governance, innovations, partnerships and commitments, is most likely to secure co-benefits and manage trade-offs (established but incomplete) {5.5.4}.

Response options that combine climate change adaptation and mitigation offer greater benefits across the nexus, especially for vulnerable groups and Indigenous Peoples and local communities (well established) {5.5.3, 5.5.4}. Response options, such as agroecology {5.5.3.11} and reduced deforestation {5.5.3.12}, contribute to both climate change adaptation and mitigation by simultaneously contributing to vegetation carbon storage while increasing resilience to the adverse effects of changing climatic conditions on food products, people and nature (well established) {5.5.3.11, 5.5.3.12}. Mangrove forests can shelter economic activity during tropical cyclone exposure while supporting Indigenous cultural practices and management and supplying traditional and modern medicines around the world (well established) {5.5.3.13}. While solely mitigation-focused options are valuable, they may provide fewer positive effects on the other nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and health, as well as being more challenging for providing environmental justice. Prioritizing co-delivery of climate change adaptation and mitigation actions with other nexus elements maximises benefits and addresses the needs of vulnerable communities (well established) {5.5.4}.

Partnerships and commitments to finance and technology are vital for climate action, especially for developing nations (well established) {5.5.3.10}. The shortfall in achieving the $100 billion annual funding target for climate financing for developing countries, coupled with an acute imbalance between adaptation and mitigation finance, underscores the critical need for enhanced international commitments. Moreover, technological cooperation, crucial for equitable access and innovation, demands a strategic overhaul to facilitate low-carbon technology transfer and deployment (well established) {5.5.3.10}. Addressing these financial and technological gaps through increased contributions and integrated strategies is imperative for a sustainable global response to climate change (well established) {5.5.3.10}.

Effective governance for climate action is based on collaboration of state and non-state actors within a polycentric model consistent with nexus governance that relies on a mix of economic, financial, legal and regulatory instruments (well established) {5.5.4.2}. While progress has been made, the current state of polycentric governance arrangements remains in a formative stage, highlighting the imperative of advancing equity, justice and community resilience throughout the climate action process (well established) {5.5.4.1}. Polycentric governance encourages experimentation and fosters a culture of innovation, sharing this in common with nexus governance. This policy diffusion relies on cross-scale interactions, and thus attention to grassroots initiatives and community-led actions can help ensure locally appropriate governance and response options (well established) {5.5.4.4}.

Integrating rights, ethics, values and world views in policy decisions fosters collective responsibility and solidarity, ensuring that no one is left behind in pursuit of a climate-resilient future (well established) {5.5.4.3}. Policies that resonate with the public can inspire action and support a sustainable and equitable approach to addressing the climate crisis (established but incomplete) {5.5.4.3}. Equity and climate justice are essential for effective climate action and nexus benefits, particularly benefiting poor and vulnerable communities. Climate justice serves as a catalyst, tackling interconnected challenges such as poverty, hunger, energy poverty and gender inequality. Prioritizing equity, inclusion and just transition processes enable climate resilience through procedural, recognition and distributional justice (well established) {5.5.4.4}.

Integrating Indigenous and local knowledge in climate initiatives enhances climate change adaptation, mitigation and resilient systems, contributing to the conservation of water, forests, biodiversity, food security and health by supporting alternative world views and values systems (well established) {5.5.4.5}. Rights-based approaches and recognition of the land and water rights of Indigenous Peoples and local communities are critical for effective implementation of climate actions related to reforestation and other nature-based solutions. Meaningful participation and inclusive engagement processes are crucial, ensuring locally appropriate solutions and social acceptability (established but incomplete) {5.5.4.5}.

Prioritization of policies that address the interconnected challenges of climate change, biodiversity loss, land degradation, pollution, food insecurity, access to clean water and energy for all, and sustainable development delivers more effective climate action (well established) {5.5.4.2, 5.5.6}. Key considerations include integrating food systems and access to sustainable healthy diets in climate plans, strengthening land use governance, optimizing finance and building capacity for renewable energy production and to address the biodiversity and climate crises, and engaging in both formal and informal governance interactions (established but incomplete) {5.5.4.2}. By embracing these decisive measures, nations can advance their global climate objectives (nationally determined contributions) and the 2030 Agenda For Sustainable Development and its Sustainable Development Goals (established but incomplete) {5.5.4.2}. All response options for climate action examined are well aligned with political priorities for biodiversity, climate change, food systems and with the Sustainable Development Goals (well established) {5.5.6}. All response options examined support the delivery of at least two Sustainable Development Goals with some contributing to eight {5.5.3, 5.5.6} (Figures 5.5.4 and 5.5.19) and all contribute to at least two of the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, with some contributing to up to fourteen {5.5.6, 5.6} (Figure 5.5.18). All response options also contribute to the long-term global goals for mitigation and adaptation under the Paris Agreement.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Options for delivering sustainable biodiversity-related approaches to climate change, adaptation and mitigation, including relevant aspects of the energy system" .

