    ipbes:hasDescription """City region food systems address sustainability of urban food systems through integrated strategies (Vieira et al., 2018) that include social values (liveability), health (diets, pollution, mobility) and total environmental footprint (X. Bai et al., 2022). The city region focus provides the capacity to integrate impacts across supply chains, account for localized externalities and leverage proximate environmental and commercial relationships with adjacent rural communities. City region food systems borrow from the concepts of integrated landscape approaches (see also section 5.1.3.9, B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches) with the distinction that municipal governance provides an integrated policymaking space that is frequently missing in integrated landscape approaches (Estrada-Carmona et al., 2014). City governance, with its focus on enhancing liveability, demonstrates a heightened sensitivity to human well-being compared to governance at other scales (Oliveira et al., 2015). Moreover, this scale is better suited to tackle complex challenges, such as the need for integrated governance, territorial development and the ramifications of food, water, and environmental changes on human health (Ayambire et al., 2019; X. Bai et al., 2016; Blay-Palmer et al., 2018).

City region food systems emphasize both green spaces within city limits and in the proximate peri-urban spaces, largely rural, as uniquely adapted to providing nature-based solutions in spaces with dense human occupation (see also B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions in Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.2 and Chapter 5.5, section 5.5.3.14). Such nature-based solutions, implemented by rural farming communities, include, but are not limited to, improving air quality, food production, disaster risk mitigation, recreational space contributing to mental health, and regulating water quality and quantity (Lafortezza et al., 2018).

Proximity to peri-urban systems enables closed-loop material flows (Boudet et al., 2020) and fosters socioeconomic connections between rural producers and urban consumers, reducing the anonymity and marginalization of farmer communities (Daghagh Yazd et al., 2019; Gregoire, 2002). It enhances farmer responsibility for reducing degradation pressures and elevates recognition for their contributions to sustainability (Fremier et al., 2013; Lecegui et al., 2023). For example, pollution from food production affects dense urban populations directly (Bell et al., 2011; Schaider et al., 2019), incurring mitigation costs such as water treatment and health costs associated with respiratory ailments (Bray et al., 2019; Mittal et al., 2009) (Figure 5.3.3).

City region food systems have strong demands on, and incentives to improve, the management of water. A growing number of cities are recognizing and actively managing peri-urban agriculture to mitigate flood risks (Gunnell et al., 2019; Quagliolo et al., 2021) and to improve water quality while reducing treatment costs (Box 5.3.10). Cities concentrate food waste (Fattibene et al., 2020) with unique economic opportunities to reduce and transform waste (section 5.3.3.10), including waste water, to resource compost (Pai et al., 2019; Rashid & Shahzad, 2021), feed (Cheng & Lo, 2016; Fowles & Nansen, 2020) or energy (Curry & Pillay, 2012; Wong, 2022). Full recovery of wastewater could recover and reuse 13.4 per cent of global demand for nutrients (section 5.3.3.7) while decreasing pollution (Mateo-Sagasta et al., 2015; Qadir et al., 2020) though is increasingly challenged by chemical pollution of sewage sludge originating from urban sources (Delibacak et al., 2020; Mateo-Sagasta et al., 2015).

Making urban and peri-urban land available for food production (Perrin & Baysse-Lainé, 2020) generates localized employment and connects rural and urban citizens (Firth et al., 2011). Fully food-sovereign cities, however, may have reduced resilience to environmental change, such as climate variability, natural hazards, or local crop failures. By combining local production and consumption (i.e., short value chains) with trade, cities can mitigate risks, ensure a steady food supply, and address nexus challenges, thereby enhancing overall resilience (A. Wood et al., 2023).

City leaders shape food environments with zoning laws, influencing access to healthy foods across neighbourhoods, prohibiting advertising and sale of unhealthy foods around schools and parks, and increasing walkability (Barrera et al., 2016; Bridle-Fitzpatrick, 2015; Rundle et al., 2009). This creates greater capacity to allocate green space to low and no emission mobility (e.g., shaded bike paths and walkways), reducing GHG emissions and improving health.

Cities play a pivotal role in driving food system transformation, as evidenced by initiatives such as the Milan Urban Food Policy Pact (FAO, RUAF, et al., 2021; Milan Urban Food Policy Pact Secretariat, 2015; Tegoni & Licomati, 2017), the C40 Good Food City Network (C40 Cities, 2019), and the FAO Food for Cities program. Collectively these initiatives represent over 450 million inhabitants and over 621 urban food system practices and focus on five key actions: addressing food loss and waste (section 5.3.3.10), enhancing food governance, promoting social and economic equity (section 5.3.3.15), supporting sustainable food production (sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6), improving food supply and distribution, and advocating for sustainable healthy diets and nutrition (section 5.3.3.11) (Tegoni & Licomati, 2017).

Despite the common and growing evidence by cities investing in systemic solutions there is scant mention of urban food systems in the United Nations Food Systems Summit Pathways (23 of 127 countries). This signals an important knowledge and policy gap despite the importance of cities in achieving global goals (Kremen & Ostfeld, 2005) (Figure 5.3.4).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "City region food systems (F12)" .

