    ipbes:hasDescription """Area-based conservation with a nexus approach uses protected areas (Grorud-Colvert et al., 2021; IUCN, 2004; Meehan et al., 2020; Shafer, 2015), other effective conservation measures (OECMs) and other relevant area-based conservation strategies (Dudley et al., 2018; Grorud-Colvert et al., 2021) to conserve biodiversity across land and sea, including territories of IPLC (Chapters 1, 2). Based on a review of the literature, area-based conservation strategies were categorized into five types (Table 5.1.6), but analysis focused on the first four on which spatially explicit data were available. Overall, area-based conservation has a positive impact on biodiversity by increasing or maintaining area in good condition, enhancing ecological function and diversity, and reducing land and sea degradation (Figure 5.1.2) (e.g. Kawaka et al., 2017; Maini et al., 2023; Olsson et al., 2004; Soliku & Schraml, 2020).

Area-based conservation also positively impacts other nexus elements, particularly human health and well-being, but also climate change adaptation, food and water (Figure 5.1.2). For example, the implementation of ecosystem-based management approaches in Sweden and the United States of America has resulted in the enhanced provision of various nature’s contributions to people, including fish and wildlife habitat, water quality and recreational opportunities (DellaSala et al., 2015; Schultz et al., 2015; Spies et al., 2019). However, data on impacts of area-based conservation on water are limited except for wetlands (Hahn et al., 2006; Olsson et al., 2004). Evidence for climate change mitigation outcomes of area-based conservation are also less frequently reported (M. L. Barnes et al., 2022; DellaSala et al., 2015; Erinosho et al., 2022; Estradivari et al., 2022a), although a global study estimated that protect areas account for about one-fifth of the carbon sequestered by all land ecosystems annually (Melillo et al., 2016). Improved environmental performance of food production and access to healthy food through area-based conservation are more commonly reported from marine protected areas which contribute to improving fish populations within the protected area as well as across the ecosystem (Ban et al., 2023; Day & Dobbs, 2013, see Box 5.1.2). However, evidence on the impact of land-based AbC on food production is limited.

Governance mechanisms in area-based conservation increasingly rely on polycentric institutional arrangements, composed of multiple centres of decision-making authority with some degree of diversity and autonomy at each level (Lubell & Morrison, 2021; Morrison et al., 2019; Ostrom, 2010). Decision-making processes are also highly participatory nexus approaches to area-based conservation (Figure 5.1.3) (e.g., Estradivari et al., 2022b; Gavin et al., 2015; Gelcich et al., 2019; Kawaka et al., 2017; Lane, 2001), though with less evidence for positive impacts on vulnerable groups on land, largely due to reduced access to food and cultural needs. Participatory and multilevel governance offers opportunities for conflict management as well as mobilization of knowledge and resources across scales for transformative biodiversity governance (Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022).

Area-based conservation strategies also have positive economic impacts (Figure 5.1.3), as seen in participatory protected area governance in several countries (J. M. Huber et al., 2023), collaborative forest management in Ghana (Akamani & Hall, 2019) and ecosystem-based management of forests in the United States of America (Charnley, 2006). The implementation of area-based conservation strategies in Australia, Finland and Canada has also been shown to contribute to economic growth in local communities as well as increased revenue to local governments (Heagney et al., 2015; Orr, 2011; Selby et al., 2011). Moreover, it has been estimated that the Hoge Veluwe forest, one of the largest and most well-known protected areas in the Netherlands (Kingdom of the), generates economic benefits worth at least three times more than the per hectare-value generated by nearby agricultural land (L. Hein, 2011). However, examples of economic benefits from marine area-based conservation are still few and largely limited to income from tourism and improved fish catches (e.g., Day & Dobbs, 2013; Mann-Lang et al., 2021).

The majority of area-based conservation case studies had positive impacts on all aspects of equity (Figure 5.1.3) consistent with participatory and inclusive approaches to protected areas conservation where positive impacts are found to be strongly linked to genuine devolution of power and rights to the local level and involvement of diverse actors and multiple perspectives (Akamani, 2020; Cumming et al., 2015; J. M. Huber et al., 2023). Furthermore, most area-based conservation approaches utilize diverse types of knowledge (e.g., Olsson & Folke, 2001), and their management goals focus on addressing multiple values (e.g., Berkes et al., 2000; DellaSala et al., 2015; Houde, 2007).

Although new area-based conservation initiatives can have impact within five years (Kawaka et al., 2017), many have been in existence for several decades, e.g., Kakadu National Park since the 1970s (Lane, 2001), the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park since the 1980s (M. L. Barnes et al., 2022; McCook et al., 2010) and the Northwest Forest Plan (United States of America) since the 1990s (Spies et al., 2019). Notably, accrual of biodiversity benefits with area-based conservation improves over decades (Figure 5.1.4).

The need to embrace complexity, recognizing the effects of telecoupling, lags and legacies (see Chapter 1, section 1.1.2 and Chapter 4, section 4.5.4)), is not strongly evident in the area-based conservation case studies, although it is a key requirement of emerging resource governance regimes for managing across nexus elements (Folke et al., 2016; T. Hughes et al., 2018; Palomo et al., 2014; Rockström et al., 2009; Steffen et al., 2011).

Barriers to area-based conservation include increased fragmentation of responsibilities and lack of political will (M. L. Barnes et al., 2022), power imbalance among stakeholders (Olsson et al., 2007), inadequate funding (Kawaka et al., 2017; Morrison, 2017) and lack of technical resources and capacity within IPLC (M. A. Samoilys et al., 2019), while enablers include availability of funding, enabling legislation, leadership and bridging organizations.

Designing effective monitoring and evaluation in biodiversity conservation is challenging (Brörken et al., 2022) with only one third of area-based conservation cases having effective monitoring and evaluation. Coverage of nexus elements and integration of collaborative and adaptive planning processes in monitoring and evaluation are also relatively low. Of note, the Great Barrier Reef provides a strong example of quantitative biodiversity data for monitoring and evaluation (McCook et al., 2010).

AbC approaches vary in their potential to advance transformative governance (Razzaque et al., 2019; I. J. Visseren-Hamakers et al., 2021; I. J. Visseren-Hamakers & Kok, 2022) but have a high potential for transformative social and institutional change. Cultural change is less evident, a finding consistent with the literature (Landis, 1997).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Area-based conservation (B01)" .

