    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Forests and trees offer powerful tools for building inclusive, sustainable and resilient landscapes. They provide a multitude of benefits for people, including climate change mitigation and adaptation (IPCC, 2022), biodiversity habitat (Betts et al., 2017), water security (Calder et al., 2007; CIFOR, 2012; Ellison et al., 2017) and sources of food, energy, medicine and jobs (FAO, 2014, 2020). This forest-based bioeconomy plays a critical role in achieving climate neutrality (Grassi et al., 2021) and addressing nexus challenges (FAO, 2022; IUCN, 2021; Springgay et al., 2019). Forest landscape restoration goes beyond simply increasing tree cover (Ometto et al., 2023). It is a continuous process aiming to regain ecological functionality, improve human well-being and restore landscape functions in degraded areas (Chazdon, Gutierrez, et al., 2020; Guariguata, 2021; IUCN & WRI, 2014). This dynamic approach also aligns with existing nature-based solutions standards, contributing to the recovery of essential nature’s contributions to people (Sivadas, 2022).

While many actors are committed to forest landscape restoration (Slobodian et al., 2020), successful large-scale implementation remains challenging due to complex ecological, social, policy, market and institutional factors (C. Hanson et al., 2015). Payment for forest ecosystem services has been proposed as a financing mechanism (Slobodian et al., 2020), with examples such as China's large-scale payment for forest ecosystem services policies (Xu et al., 2006). However, evidence for payment for forest ecosystem services schemes delivering both social and ecological benefits across large landscapes remains limited (Li et al., 2019; Ota et al., 2020). The Restoration Opportunities Assessment Methodology, successfully implemented in Rwanda (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2016; IUCN, 2016), offers a promising path forward by building on existing policies and targets. However, documenting the economic and social success of forest landscape restoration globally is crucial, as addressing social critiques of inappropriate afforestation (Adade Williams et al., 2020; Reyes-García & Benyei, 2019) is vital for long-term success.

The complexity of forest landscape restoration as a socioecological nature-based solutions presents both challenges and opportunities (Stanturf et al., 2019). Spatial relationships within land-use systems can create trade-offs, such as increased crop raiding by wildlife due to forest proximity (Pfeifer et al., 2022) or unequal benefit distribution between local and external stakeholders (Platts et al., 2023). Such projects associated with agricultural land conversion can lead to social and ecological trade-offs for local communities due to direct losses in production, access restrictions, land tenure issues, justice and power asymmetries (Few, 2013; Few et al., 2017; Jindal et al., 2008; Lele, 2013; Sandbrook & Burgess, 2015). Extensive rangelands can synergize with recovered forests, enhancing biodiversity and pastoral livelihoods while addressing climate change and desertification concerns (Mansourian & Stephenson, 2023; Vetter, 2020). The risks and trade-offs depend on the activity’s nature, scale, agreed definitions, regulations and monitoring and adherence to key principles. Examples such as failed restoration projects in Northern Malawi highlight the importance of clear goals and community understanding (Höhl et al., 2020). Three key aspects for the success of forest landscape restoration are ecological and social balance, supportive policies, and monitoring and adaptation (IUCN, 2020c; Mansourian & Stephenson, 2023).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forest landscape restoration (B05)" .

