    ipbes:hasDescription """Traditional and complementary medicines usage is reported by 88 per cent of WHO member states (WHO, 2019c) and an estimated 4 billion people (IPBES, 2019a) across all countries critically rely on wild flora and fauna sourced medicines (Figure 5.4.7). Between 50,000-70,000 medicinal plants are used globally (Schippmann et al., 2006): 35,407 species have well-documented use (MPNS, 2023), 3,000 species are globally traded and 60-90 per cent are sourced from the wild with high risk of overextraction (Schindler, 2022). While IPLC hold extensive associated knowledge of health and nutrition (ECOSOC, 2023) inherently linked to their diverse socioecological systems, there is also historically codified traditional medicinal plant knowledge that is well integrated and regulated within national health systems (e.g., Ayurveda and Traditional Chinese Medicine (Cragg & Newman, 2013)) that is driving significant global demand for wild resources (Astutik et al., 2019). This highlights the need for comprehensive assessments on medicinal plant sourcing, conservation, trade, cultivation status and usage patterns within the health sector (Chen et al., 2016).

Promotion of sustainable harvest and use and conservation education among stakeholders is in progress. Strengthening the value chain and developing economic opportunities for dependent communities to facilitate integrated biodiversity planning can strengthen ecological stewardship among communities (Volenzo & Odiyo, 2020). Technological innovations for improving yield and quality of medicinal plants, identification of possible substitutes for endangered species and large-scale, in-situ and ex-situ conservation programs are also gaining recognition and application (Applequist et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2016; Kathe et al., 2010; Ved et al., 2020).

Use of traditional and complementary medicines contributes to accessible healthcare, especially in regions where it is less developed. Some phytochemicals and traditional therapies have a potential role in treating neglected tropical diseases such as leishmaniasis (Chouhan et al., 2014; Gutiérrez-Rebolledo et al., 2017; Iwu et al., 1994; Simoben et al., 2018), trypanosomiasis (Simoben et al., 2018), filariasis (Komoreng et al., 2017; Narahari et al., 2013, 2023), chikungunya (Bhakat & Soliman, 2015), helminthiasis (Cock et al., 2018; Romero-Benavides et al., 2017); malaria (Bekono et al., 2020; Willcox & Bodeker, 2004), non-communicable diseases (Howes et al., 2020; Xia et al., 2022) and mental well-being (Fajemiroye et al., 2016; Pferschy-Wenzig et al., 2022; Saki et al., 2014). Medicinal plants continue to be a source of bioactive leads and effective drug development and hold potential with novel methods (Cragg & Newman, 2013). Integrated research on potential health benefits, quality, safety and sustainable use need to be expanded (IPBES, 2022c) and the recently established WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre has a strategic plan to support evidence synthesis and research on these traditional medicinal practices globally. Implementation of free, prior and informed consent and appropriate mechanisms to assure equity for traditional knowledge holders in compliance with the Nagoya protocol requires ongoing attention (Figure 5.4.6).

Climate change and biodiversity loss have created significant impact on the present and future availability and quality of medicinal plants (Applequist et al., 2020; Borrell et al., 2020; Gupta et al., 2019). Climate migration can also reduce access to these resources (Pörtner et al., 2021). Medicinal plants can contribute to healthy food due to their rich sources of health-promoting ingredients (Jiang et al., 2021; Leakey et al., 2022). Agro-biodiversity systems such as homestead gardens can improve access to these plants (Mitra et al., 2020; Pörtner et al., 2021; M. Santos et al., 2022; Ved et al., 2020). A significant portion of medicinal plants are aquatic or semi-aquatic (Aasim et al., 2019; Arya et al., 2022; M. A. Butt et al., 2021); water stress can impact on availability of secondary metabolites (Netshiluvhi & Eloff, 2016; Noreen et al., 2022; Prinsloo & Nogemane, 2018), while contaminated water can lead to uptake of toxic substances (Sarma et al., 2011).

This response option can enhance nature’s contributions to people and conservation targets (Barata et al., 2016; Kassam et al., 2010; Kathe et al., 2010; Unnikrishnan & Suneetha, 2012; Ved et al., 2020), but may also result in unsustainable use (IPBES, 2022c; Sharma & Kala, 2018) (Figure 5.4.5). Medicinal plant and biofuel cultivation may have negative impacts on food production (Brahmanand et al., 2013). Conservation and sustainable use management (Barata et al., 2016; Chen et al., 2016; Sharma & Kala, 2018)), increased cultivation (Brinckmann et al., 2022; Falco et al., 2020) and landscape restoration programs (Prasad et al., 2023) show indications of positive impact on the status of medicinal plants as well as nexus elements. For example, the National Medicinal Plants Board of the Government of India supports in-situ conservation, research and education across the country with 110 medicinal plants conservation areas with ex-situ conservation within medicinal plant conservation parks (Ved et al., 2020). Seventy-two medicinal plant conservation development areas have more recently focused on community engagement and opportunities for alternative livelihoods, such as through enrichment of existing farming systems and integration of medicinal plants with joint forest management ((Biswas et al., 2017). As 90 per cent of medicinal plant species in India occur in forests, appropriate governance and multistakeholder partnerships between the Department of Forestry, research organizations and communities are key to successful implementation (Ved et al., 2020).""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sustainable use of medicinal plants (H04)" .

