    ipbes:hasDescription """Water governance approaches over the past two decades have been dominated by a characterization of water as an independent sector to be managed by technical criteria, legitimized by water scarcity narratives, and supported by governance modes based on environmental and economic efficiency criteria that were often independent of socioeconomic and development contexts (Woodhouse & Muller, 2017). Governance approaches differ in their underlying values (IPBES, 2022) and historically water has been framed as a resource in different ways: as a private good for which there is preference for technologies for accessing and managing water available to the highest bidder (market governance); as a public good that requires wise stewardship determining who gets how much and for what purpose (hierarchical governance); and as a common pool good that must be equally accessible to all including future generations and nature (network governance) (Chapter 4, section 4.3). A nexus governance approach by contrast encourages water governance to move from management as a standalone element and limited in scale by watersheds and river basins to that of “problemsheds” of context-specific and evidence-based policy-making (Conca & Weinthal, 2018; Mollinga, 2020; P. Sharma & Kumar, 2020; Stein & Jaspersen, 2019). This promotes interaction among pre-existing governance structures and scales (Benson et al., 2015) and the need to “break down the silos” (Cairns & Krzywoszynska, 2016) by expanding research and policy decisions beyond specialized knowledge and traditional sectoral governance structures (Urbinatti, Dalla Fontana, et al., 2020).

Water sector-related nexus governance touches on several themes such as water and basin governance; environmental and systems governance; risk and resource security governance; economic governance; global governance; urban governance; integrative and cooperative governance; and “epistemic” and transdisciplinary governance (Urbinatti, Benites-Lazaro, et al., 2020). Water sector-related nexus governance research has also spotlighted the need to manage for trade-offs (compromises involving negotiation) and to promote synergies (inter-connections that amplify benefits), which can be achieved by attention to intersectionality and inclusion of multiple stakeholders (Pahl-Wostl et al., 2021), to integrative and collaborative cross-scale action (Kurian, 2017; Kurian & Ardakanian, 2015), and by being informed and adaptive (see Chapter 1, section 1.3.4 and Chapter 4, section 4.5). These approaches can help to address the many wicked problems related to the water sector (Fallon et al., 2021; Pueppke et al., 2018; van Gevelt, 2020). For example, the European Union Water Framework Directive notes that reducing nutrients in water has been deemed a wicked problem that crosses political boundaries (Wiering et al., 2020) and for which there is no other option but shared nexus governance. The nexus governance features of integration; inclusion and participation; equitability and legitimacy; collaboration, coordination and connectivity; and adaptation, experimentation and innovation thus bear high relevance to the water sector (Srigiri & Dombrowsky, 2021; Yuan & Lo, 2022).

Nexus governance that includes the water sector requires a range of actors to engage across policy domains, governance levels (Bijl et al., 2018; Johnson & VanDeveer, 2017) and public, private and civic spheres (Klijn & Koppenjan, 2014; Koppenjan & Klijn, 2006; Pahl-Wostl, 2019; Stein & Jaspersen, 2019) (Chapter 1, section 1.3.3). This can be seen in the 15 response options presented in section 5.2.3, where a range of actors play a multitude of roles (from being influencers, affected, key actors (being both an influencer and affected) or being not involved) (Figure 5.2.6)(Chapter 4, section 4.2.6). Global and regional institutions, science and policy interfaces, and national governments serve as influencers across nearly all response options (barring W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (section 5.2.3.7) and W07, Rights of nature (section 5.2.3.7), as they provide the policy and programmatic environment as well as financial systems for implementing these options. Local governments and municipalities, civil society organizations, community-based organizations, private sector and businesses and IPLC have varied roles, depending on the response option. Media and arts can be an influential actor in five of the 15 response options (e.g., for W01, Inclusive water education (section 5.2.3.1) or W11 Manage alien species (section 5.2.3.11) but its role is often unknown or unclear for the other options. Therefore, knowledge gaps exist regarding the role media and the arts can play in the specific response options (for example see Anderson-Wilk, 2009; Antwi et al., 2022; Boyer et al., 2021; Onyenankeya & Salawu, 2018).

Each of the 15 response options can be classified as action or management (direct implementation on the ground), a policy instrument that guides action and/or an enabler that facilitates or improves action, management or policy (Figure 5.2.8). Of the 15 response options, 11 can be classified as policy instruments. Most commonly the response options are linked to legal and regulatory policy instruments with two of those considered direct management actions (W03, Dam operation (section 5.2.3.3) and W12, Reduce pollution (section 5.2.3.12). There were also social and cultural options (W06, Inclusive water management (section 5.2.3.6)) and rights-based options (W07, Rights of nature (section 5.2.2.7)) that are also classified as enablers. Of the 11 response options can be classified as policy instruments, 5 of them can be classified as more than one policy instrument typology (Figure 5.2.8). Of the four response options not classified as policy instruments, three were considered enablers (W01, Inclusive water education (section 5.2.3.1), W14, Address gendered burdens of water collection (section 5.2.3.14) and W15, Community water management (section 5.2.3.15).

Implementation of the 15 response options is influenced by a range of enablers (Figure 5.2.8) (see Chapter 4, section 4.2). For example, enablers of W15, Community water management (section 5.2.3.15) centre on contextualized understanding and strategic use of positive impacts of collective action and social capital (Suhardiman et al., 2017; Yu et al., 2016). Similarly, enhancing the sustainability of sanitation projects (i.e., W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (section 5.2.3.13)) entails co-designing and co-managing facilities with recipient communities to invoke a sense of ownership, educating facility users regarding proper hygiene maintenance and building capacities and infrastructures to regulate the flow and treatment of wastewater (Brands, 2014). A critical enabler of W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (section 5.2.3.2) is a collaborative and stakeholder-driven approach, involving the integration of several agencies and a coordinated use of water and cooperation between users (Galvez & Rojas, 2019). Effective W09, Groundwater governance of groundwater (section 5.2.3.9) is enabled by institutions that integrate stakeholders; policies and capital that support local, regional and global resource goals; legal systems with the capacity to create and implement laws effectively; and local knowledge, customary or cultural context, and scientific understanding of groundwater systems (FAO, 2016). Blending water diplomacy and nexus governance in a transboundary context to support W08, Transboundary water cooperation (section 5.2.3.8) has the potential to realize more balanced management across nexus elements based on shared understanding embedding politics in decision-making and promoting mutual gains (Pahl-Wostl, 2019; Salmoral et al., 2019). For technological solutions, such as the implementation of W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure design (section 5.2.3.13), a collaborative approach involving the public as well as private sector actors such as urban developers, civil engineers, landscape architects and ecologists is critical (Rashetnia et al., 2022; A. K. Sharma et al., 2016).

The 15 response options exhibit a range of governance challenges. Major barriers to W15, Community water management (section 5.2.3.15) are not sufficiently understood, including how deep sociopolitical divisions in a community can lead to a lack of social trust, elite capture of participatory processes, power heterogeneity imbalances at the micro-level and a lack of inclusive participation in decision-making (Reeves & Bonney, 2023; Shunglu et al., 2022). The impact of W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (section 5.2.3.2) has been constrained by a lack of clarity on the interventions, incompatibility between objectives such as efficiency and equity, and unclear conflict resolution mechanisms (Biswas, 2004; Giordano & Shah, 2014; Molle, 2008). Limited data and knowledge remain key impediments to W09, Groundwater governance (section 5.2.3.9) (UNESCO, 2021). Governance solutions for W11, Managing alien species (section 5.2.3.11) often need investments in other effective area-based conservation measures (OECMs) (Alves-Pinto et al., 2021; Laffoley et al., 2017) (see Chapter 5.1, section 5.1.3.1 (B01, Area-based conservation)).

W08, Transboundary water cooperation (section 5.2.3.8; Box 5.2.10), which cuts across several nexus elements faces a variety of obstacles because of the potential for competing uses for water and requires the involvement of actors from across nexus elements at multiple levels of planning and decision-making. However, managing water resources across political boundaries is part of the European Union Water Framework Directive (Carvalho et al., 2019), and the accelerator framework for ensuring transboundary cooperation coverage in all transboundary basins by 2030 shows the value of ‘bundling’ several response options together, along with enablers, to realize their overall impacts (UNESCO, 2021). Several response options themselves are bundles of responses; for example, integrated water infrastructure includes actions related to managing water storage inbuilt as well as natural ecosystems, joint management of surface water and groundwater resources and bundling of governance structures to address the challenges of siloed decision-making (GWP, 2021; World Bank, 2023). Depending on the context of the water challenge, the implementation of response options may also be enabled or enhanced through sequencing; for example, governance challenges that limit consideration of trade-offs and synergies may need to be overcome before interventions in technology or management are effective (Lombardi et al., 2011; Pahl-Wostl, 2007; Polaine et al., 2022). In addition, building capacities and learning systems may need to precede other interventions (Ibisch et al., 2016; Wehn De Montalvo & Alaerts, 2013). Response options such asW06, Inclusive water management (section 5.2.3.6), W15, Community water management (section 5.2.3.15) and W14, Addressing gendered burdens of water collection (section 5.2.3.14) may have generic applications across several water challenges as enablers.

Most of the response options (e.g., W02, Integrated water infrastructure (section 5.2.3.2); W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (section 5.2.3.4); W06, Inclusive water management (section 5.2.3.6); W10, Finance for water infrastructure (section 5.2.3.10) and W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (section 5.2.3.13)) can be scaled up with relevant enabling conditions. Scaling up solutions, such as W10, Finance for water infrastructure (section 5.2.3.10) is not merely about getting more funding to fill a financing gap, but making the best use of existing assets and finance, including timely asset management, capital expenditure planning, targeted allocation of public subsidies, and strengthening the enabling environment for investment (OECD, 2022; The World Bank, 2023).

A discussion on nexus governance for water cannot be concluded without reflecting on global water governance. The global water governance landscape is witnessing two opposing trends: of subsidiarity which pushes water governance to the lowest possible level and universality, which pushes water governance to the highest possible level (Gupta & Pahl-Wostl, 2013). A nexus framing of governance allows linkages across multiple sectors and scales and multiple entry points for governance. Learning can play a role by enabling a cosmopolitan perspective on how norms, values, principles and paradigms can help create and preserve water and nature’s contributions to people sustained by water (Gupta & Pahl-Wostl, 2013), alongside complementary mechanisms for bottom-up learning and experiences to scale to other places (Obura et al., 2021).

Notes
7. See the Chapter 5 data management report on assessing response option support for global policy frameworks; available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931483.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus governance to support water response options" .

