    ipbes:hasDescription """Perspectives related to water and its use and value have changed greatly over time, from the recognition of water as a vital resource and economic good, as reflected in the Dublin Principles in 1992, to water as a critical element determining the sustainability of living systems and biodiversity (International Conference on Water and the Environment, 1992; Ripl, 2003). Water is life, intrinsically connected with all living processes, human and non-human, terrestrial and aquatic, including biodiversity from animals and plants to microbes, and critical to the resilience of landscapes, communities and coupled human-nature systems (Rockström et al., 2014; Boltz et al., 2019). Yet, the opportunity costs of water use options and technologies have not been appropriately considered, especially in those regions that are water-scarce (Saleth & Dinar, 2000; Savenije & Van Der Zaag, 2002; WWF, 2023). The nexus approach has emerged from the realization of this interconnectedness and the awareness that anthropogenic interventions in one realm, if done without adequate sensitivity, can ripple through other sectors with negative consequences for humans, ecosystems and biodiversity (Muller, 2015). Nexus approaches related to water have evolved as an alternative to siloed approaches that consider the water sector in isolation from other sectors. These approaches favour a broad, multi-sector, multi-scale, multi-regional and multi-actor perspective, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) perspectives (Elliott, 2011), for solving global challenges mediated by water (J. Liu et al., 2018).

Water in the Earth system can be represented by stocks and flows of water across subsystems, and this holistic and dynamic perspective provides a conceptual framing that supports the identification and design of nexus response options to water challenges. Such a systemic view enables an understanding of how each response option acts on specific aspects of the water cycle, but also how response options might be interlinked and have cascading effects downstream or be affected by upstream response options. A more nuanced understanding of nexus interactions at different places within the water cycle is reflected by a shift in water management to precipitation-based water management that incorporates land-use and includes consideration of multiple scales (Falkenmark & Rockström, 2006, 2010); by increased understanding of telecoupled connections with different sectors (Du, Fang, et al., 2022; Du, Zhao, et al., 2022; J. Liu, 2017, 2017; J. Liu et al., 2018); by calculation of ‘water footprints’ reflecting on the type and quantity of water embedded in different products and trade cycles (Hoekstra et al., 2019; Hoekstra & Mekonnen, 2012; Hogeboom, 2020); and by increased recognition of the underpinning and integrating role of water vis-à-vis biodiversity and ecosystems (Kim et al., 2024). Within the Earth system sciences, for example, ecohydrology considers the relationships between hydrological and biological processes at different scales to improve water security, enhance biodiversity and further opportunities for sustainable development (Zalewski, 2000).

Water is found in many forms on Earth, from headwaters, creeks and streams, wetlands and lakes, to rivers and deltas flowing into the ocean as well as existing in many places as groundwater, invisible at the surface but fundamental to hydrological flows, ecological functions and human uses (Lynch et al., 2023; Rolls et al., 2018). These widely diverse water surfaces exhibit the environmental conditions that enable organisms to thrive (i.e., ranges of temperature, sediment and nutrients) and that enable limnological systems such as wetlands, peatlands, lakes, reservoirs, ponds, rivers, springs, streams and groundwater to host a diverse set of organisms and biodiversity adapted to unique, and sometimes extreme, environmental conditions (Bunn & Arthington, 2002; Daam et al., 2019). In many cases, this biodiversity is still unknown (Arthington et al., 2016; Collier et al., 2016). Yet, these aquatic ecosystems are faced with high dynamics, either due to natural flows of water or due to altered flows associated with human use (e.g., extraction, re-routing orpollution) making the biodiversity therein very vulnerable and threatened (Dudgeon et al., 2006; Lynch et al., 2023). This has led to repeated calls to enhance conservation and restoration efforts for freshwater biodiversity (Arthington, 2021; Arthington et al., 2023; Strayer & Dudgeon, 2010; Tickner et al., 2020). Ecosystem-based approaches have been developed to address this call, which aim for the integrated management of land, water and societies as components of ecosystems, while creating an incentive for conservation by making explicit the trade-offs in nature’s contributions to people associated with different resource-use options (Schoeman et al., 2014).

Water interacts with nature and society in diverse ways (Figure 5.2.1) and this has led to a variety of discourses, conceptual framings and entry points for dealing with water challenges depending on the actors and nexus elements involved in the development of response options. The ‘colours of water’ framework uses the analogy of colours as entry points to depict which flows of water have which uses, partitioning precipitation into green water (the moisture in the subsurface above the water table), brown water (which infiltrates into the soil and the deeper layers) and blue water (found in aquifers, wetlands, lakes and rivers, and water impoundments such as dams, but also in the ocean and other waterbodies) (Sood et al., 2014). Concepts such as grey water and black water allow the extent of pollution of water resources and the feasibility of recycling and alternate use to be factored into the framework. The coupling of the different colours of water, by explicitly linking quantity, flows and uses, provides a crucial systems perspective that is fundamental for understanding water system resilience (Falkenmark & Rockström, 2010; Rockström et al., 2014). This enables shocks to be examined such as those induced by salinization, conversion to alternate land uses, desertification, climate change, aquifer depletion, waterlogging and ecosystem collapse (including those induced and aggravated by climate change) (Falkenmark et al., 2019) and the system assessed for the potential of state shifts and tipping points.

The nature’s contributions to people framing provides a conceptualization of how human well-being interacts with finite natural resources such as water, and how changes in water availability and quality impact human well-being (Martin-Ortega et al., 2015). Freshwater biodiversity underpins several nature’s contributions to people, but many nexus elements, such as food production, energy and climate change have negative influences on marine and freshwater biodiversity (Kim et al., 2024). However, positive water-related impacts to and from biodiversity and nexus elements are also observed, for example, through integrated management of water landscapes, restoration and water quality improvements, protection of species and ecosystems, and urban blue infrastructures including nature-based solutions (Kim et al., 2024).

An important framing has been the water-energy-food nexus, receiving popularity since the 2011 World Economic Forum as a backdrop to a series of resource crises in the elements of food, energy and global finance (Mollinga, 2020) (see also Chapter 1, section 1.2.1). The water-energy-food nexus approach has been used to understand how the production and consumption of food, including wild foods, entails the consumption of water and energy. Water and energy footprints (Hogeboom, 2020) have been operationalized to indicate the sustainability of varied consumption styles across society (Melo et al., 2021; Nuwayhid & Mohtar, 2022; Purwanto et al., 2021). Other nexus approaches and framings include the idea of virtual water (Graham et al., 2020), i.e., water amounts that get transported globally in food products, affecting water availability in source locations. Interactions with health are also fundamental from a water perspective, particularly in the framing of the One Health initiative (World Wildlife Fund, 2021). Many diseases are water-borne and lack of sanitation and access to sufficient clean water is a major source of disease. For example, schistosomiasis, a parasitic infection most common in African countries with an estimated 200 million infections, can be transferred to humans through contaminated water (Chitsulo et al., 2000) (see Chapter 1, Box 1.2). Earlier nexus studies have primarily focused on the allocation of water to different sectors and users and to a lesser extent included an ecosystem perspective and have not explicitly addressed nature’s contributions to people (Hülsmann et al., 2019).

Technologies for water use and access vary among water actors and sectors. Traditional technologies, based on Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK) garnered over generations in specific hydro-ecological zones and societies (Hosseiny et al., 2021), have successfully addressed variations in water availability across space and time to serve human and environmental needs (Akpinar Ferrand & Cecunjanin, 2014; Fanteso & Yessoufou, 2022). Other technologies such as electric pumps and dams are widespread and used to make water accessible and distributed to many. Though important and convenient, many of these infrastructures have high negative impacts, such as groundwater depletion (Dangar et al., 2021; Scanlon et al., 2012, 2023), unregulated use (Shah, 2023), disruption of natural ecosystem processes (Palmer & Ruhi, 2019) and high fossil fuel energy footprints (Fujita & Fukumi, 2021). The use of technology without consideration of the nexus approach or social restrictions can have several deleterious impacts on water resources and other nexus elements. For example, in the Hindu Kush Himalayas, widespread drying of springs has been attributed to extensive water infrastructure development in fragile areas, loss of traditional water harvesting systems and ecological degradation (Verma & Jamwal, 2022), ultimately leading to water stress and abandonment of farming and settlements (Panwar, 2020; Rasul & Sharma, 2016). Such impacts may in turn reduce food production (Singh, 2021), increase energy expenditure (Shah, 2023), result in severe pollution (He et al., 2021), have negative impacts on biodiversity (González-Zeas et al., 2022) decrease downstream economic growth (Russ et al., 2022) and create social unrest (Dolan et al., 2021; Savelli et al., 2023; Tozer, 2023).

Water, as an integral part of nature, is associated with diverse values (intrinsic, instrumental, cultural and spiritual, and relational), which are held and expressed in equally diverse ways. The recently published IPBES Methodological Assessment Report on Diverse Values and Valuation of Nature (Values Assessment) calls for transformative change that shifts away from predominant values that currently over-emphasize short-term and individual material gains, to nurture sustainability-aligned values across society (Pascual, Balvanera, Anderson, et al., 2023; Pascual, Balvanera, & Christie, 2023). Power asymmetries, which determine the extent to which the values held by different actors are considered in decision-making, are apparent in the implementation of large development and infrastructure projects in the water sector. For example, dams are often proposed for their market-based instrumental values (including electricity to urban consumers, irrigation water for agriculture, mines and jobs), while the relational and instrumental values of those directly affected by the project (e.g., loss of farming and fishing livelihoods and ways of life, contamination due to acid mine drainage) are often excluded. Social movements have sought to shift this imbalance through resistance, litigation and protest (Hess, 2018; Punia & Singh, 2021).

Notes
2. The data management report describing the Chapter 5 response option scoring criteria and process is available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13931440.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of water within the nexus" .

