    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Executive summary

Health is core to human well-being and is undermined by deterioration of biodiversity (e.g., mental health impacts from lack of access to nature, increased emergence of zoonotic diseases), water (e.g., lack of water, polluted water), food (e.g., too little or too much food, unhealthy diet), and climate (e.g., deaths from heat, extreme weather events, loss of livelihoods and displacement from environmental degradation) (well established) {5.4.1, 5.4.5}. These health impacts disproportionately affect poor and marginalized people, particularly women and children, in the Global South (well established) {5.4.1}. Recognition and assessment of these impacts is critical. Addressing health inequity between or within populations enhances collective resilience in the face of the multiple socioenvironmental problems impacting people and the nexus (established but incomplete) {5.4.1, 5.4.6}. Health sector actors are well positioned to work with communities and other stakeholders to ensure good health, sustainable development and synergistic policy outcomes across the nexus (well established) {5.4.2, 5.4.3}.

The attainment of good health has consequences for other nexus elements (e.g., via resource use, material well-being and pollution generated, including through healthcare) (well established) {5.4.1, 5.4.3}. Increased health and well-being are associated with negative trade-offs for biodiversity and climate change globally, but uneven evidence means the magnitude, temporal scale and direction of impacts across the nexus are incompletely documented (well established) {5.4.1, 5.4.3}. Actors in the health sector can contribute to good health through advocacy and awareness-raising on the consequent adverse outcomes for other nexus elements and the need for greater investment in disease prevention, through action and research on causal and associative pathways between health and the other nexus elements and through data collection and record-keeping on the interlinked disease outcomes (established but incomplete) {5.4.2}.

Response options exist that support nexus-wide benefits within healthcare. These address environmental health risks, promote benefits to health from nature and include integrative approaches to healthcare and health education (well established) {5.4.1, 5.4.3}. In this subchapter, fifteen response options available to health actors have been selected from these categories. They are already implemented in different parts of the globe and embed concern for multiple nexus elements even where the evidence collection that documents this is incomplete. They have proven technological feasibility and high transformative potential. They can be selected, bundled or sequenced to address the disease burden or improve the health determinants of a population (established but incomplete) {5.4.3, 5.4.7}.

The health sector has an influential role and can provide leadership to make decisions and act in ways that address health equity and rights and support biodiversity, food, water and climate action goals (established but incomplete) {5.4.1, 5.4.3}. Providing all people with primary healthcare (addressed through education, provision of proper nutrition and safe water, maternal and child health, family planning and immunization) lays the foundation for health equity and a clean, healthy and sustainable environment (established but incomplete) {5.4.1, 5.4.3.1}. Addressing the environmental footprint of healthcare, and reducing its greenhouse gas emissions, reduces the impact of modern healthcare in high-income, high resource use countries and more than 60 countries have pledged to do this (well established) {5.4.3.3}. Supporting the knowledge and practice of Indigenous peoples and local communities can benefit the approximately 80 per cent of all people globally that solely use or co-utilize traditional health practices and medicines (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.2, 5.4.3.4}. Ensuring that global recommendations for pollution prevention and reduction are met can significantly decrease the burden of disease, particularly in low-income countries, with benefits across nexus elements (well established) {5.4.3.7}.

Sustainable healthy diets are a foundation for greater global health and for a liveable planet (well established) {2.5.2, 2.5.3, 5.3.1, 5.4.3, 5.5.1}. A shift from overconsumption, particularly in high- and middle-income countries to a healthy diet could prevent around 10 million premature deaths by 2040. The associated redistribution of food and reduced waste can help to eliminate the undernutrition of hundreds of millions of people primarily in low-income countries (well established) {2.5.3, 2.5.2, 5.4.3}. Transition to more plant-based diets, particularly in high- and middle-income countries, also benefits other nexus elements through the potential for more land for biodiversity, lower demands on water, reduced greenhouse emissions and a reduction in zoonotic disease risk (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.6}. Cultural well-being and health are also supported by traditional diets, including for Indigenous Peoples and local communities, although trade-offs may exist, such as for zoonotic disease risks (established but incomplete) {5.4.1} (Box 5.4.2). Health sector actors contribute towards a sustainable food system by promoting sustainable healthy diets and ensuring that the nutritional needs of women, children and vulnerable people are met.

Nature-based health interventions can help protect, restore and sustainably use natural resources while safeguarding health (established but incomplete) {5.4.3}. Diverse macro and microbiomes deliver benefits to mental, physical and social health and well-being and can be accessed through urban green infrastructure and other nature contact {5.4.3.5, 5.4.3.9} (Box 5.4.6; Box 5.4.8). Nature-based health interventions utilize these benefits and contribute cost-effectively to addressing non-communicable diseases, in particular, and to supporting diverse initiatives to increase urban nature (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.5, 5.4.3.9}. Health impacts of urban heat, air and water pollution, extreme weather risks and other impacts of climate change can be reduced with nature-based solutions (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.8, 5.4.3.9, 5.4.3.10}. New health actor partnerships, including with environmental managers and Indigenous Peoples and local communities, are required to enhance nature’s benefits to health (established but incomplete) {5.4.4}.

Health of humans and other species relies on healthy environments. Expanding the scope of ‘health’ to include optimal outcomes for other species and the environment benefits human health and well-being at local to planetary scales (well established) {5.4.1, 5.4.3, 5.4.5}. Enabling the right to clean, healthy and sustainable environments can reduce the global burden of disease by at least 24 per cent and provide benefits to other species (well established) {5.3.1}. For example, reducing the risk of zoonotic and many neglected tropical diseases at source {5.4.3.11}, waterborne disease within coastal catchments {5.4.3.12}, and pollution {5.4.3.7} and conserving biodiversity {5.4.3.4, 5.4.3.10} have benefits across the nexus (well established) {5.4.3}. Educators and institutions that train health actors to think and operate within the One Health, Planetary Health and other integrated approaches build the capacity to address problems beyond traditional silos and to value alternative world views (including those of Indigenous Peoples and local communities) to create better outcomes for people, nature and the planet (established but incomplete) {5.4.1; 5.4.3; 5.4.5}.

Governance approaches that engage health with other sectors exist but are yet to be fully inclusive of the nexus elements (well established) {5.4.4}. The opportunity for nexus governance is already underway, including with multi-sector international support (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.2; 5.4.3.14}. Choice, operationalization and implementation of response options fundamental for improving health requires multi-sector governance. Key enablers include collaborations with health and non-traditional health actors and institutions, changes in perceptions and behaviour, management of communications and learning systems for collaboration, capacity for nexus governance, creating legislation, use of multiple policy instruments and access to financial resources {5.4.4}. Incomplete knowledge or progress with the above are barriers to recognizing and addressing trade-offs, driving transformation at national and sub-national levels, and prudently scaling response options to avoid irreversible, ineffective pathways and to benefit from new insight regarding unforeseen complexities (established but incomplete) {5.4.5}.

Addressing health through a nexus approach supports achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for mitigation and adaptation (well established) {5.4.3; 5.4.4; 5.4.5}. The response options presented in this subchapter align strongly with key international frameworks because of their nexus approach. Collectively, they contribute to the achievement of all 17 of the Sustainable Development Goals, supporting them as the determinants of sustainable (multi-generational) health. All but one of the response options supports achieving multiple Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets through maintaining or improving health and well-being inclusively in harmony with nature to preserve the life-support functions that are nature’s contributions to people. They also contribute to reducing emissions associated with healthcare and the achievement of health, in line with the Paris Agreement.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Options for delivering sustainable approaches to health" .

