    ipbes:hasDescription """Executive summary

Food systems have an overwhelming influence across the nexus, as the environmental impact of food systems is larger than any other sector (well established) {5.3.1}. How food is produced, what foods are produced and consumed, where they are produced and how much is lost and wasted impact both nature and people (well established) {2.5.2.1, 5.3.1}. Despite significant potential of agriculture to contribute to positive health (well established) {5.3.3.11} and environmental outcomes (established but incomplete) {5.3.1, 5.3.3}, current agricultural practices and policies are largely responsible for the negative impacts of food (well established) {5.3.3, 5.3.4, 7.2.3}. Food production accounts for upwards of 80 percent of biodiversity loss, uses 70 per cent of available freshwater resources, occupies nearly 40 per cent of arable land, and accounts for approximately 30 per cent of greenhouse gas emissions (well established) {chapter 2, 5.3.1}. Response options for the food system aim to reduce environmental impacts while continuing to sustainably supply healthy diets to a growing population (well established) {chapters 2, 3, 5.3.1}. Sixteen food-oriented response options were assessed that address this aim through (i) avoiding conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity {5.3.3.1}; (ii) restoring degraded lands and soils {5.3.3.2}, (iii) regenerating ecological functions in production lands and waters {5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6}; (iv) reducing pollution and food waste {5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.3.3.10}; and (v) transforming food systems with attention to equity and inclusion of all peoples, with particular attention to marginalized, rural and Indigenous Peoples and local communities {5.3.3.11, 5.3.3.12. 5.3.3.13, 5.5.3.14, 5.3.3.15, 5.3.3.16}.

Food system response options have the potential to regenerate the environmental and food production functions of degraded arable lands and productive waters (established but incomplete) {5.3.3, 5.3.3.2, 5.3.3.6} and can help to achieve Land Degradation Neutrality (well established) {5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.6}. Ecological intensification underpins transition to greater use of agroecological practices which encourage maintaining space for biodiversity in food producing lands and waters (well established) {5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.3.4}. Such practices are essential for ensuring the habitat functions that support soil health and the nature’s contributions to people (e.g., pollination, pollution regulation and pest and disease regulation) that are required to produce healthy diets (well established) {5.3.3, 5.3.4}. This underscores that biodiversity provides an array of nature’s contributions to people that are in turn required for food production (established but incomplete) {5.3.4}. Collectively these response options have the capacity to impact food security feeding 10 billion people by 2050 (established but incomplete), keep emissions from food below 5 GtCO2e/yr (well established) or possibly make the food system climate neutral through increased emissions sequestration both above- and below-ground (unresolved), and increase water security locally (inconclusive) {5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.3.4}. Because of the high impact of food systems, actors within such systems are uniquely placed to minimize trade-offs with other nexus elements. The transformation of food systems has the potential to regenerate nature’s contributions to people and ecosystem integrity. Sustainable use of lands and waters allocated to food production are a determining factor not only in ensuring food security, but also in delivering on climate, water and environmental security (well established) {5.3.1).

Low dietary diversity is a common denominator in most forms of malnutrition (well established) {5.3.3}, and increasingly homogenous diets globally have contributed to this decline in diversity (well established) {5.3.1}. Aligning agricultural and environmental policies and practices to sustainable healthy diets can have significant nexus-wide benefits (well established) {5.3.3.11}. Shifts to healthy diets are inclusive of many cultural practices. Indigenous and local knowledge offer a wealth of food traditions compatible with health. Cultural identities are often closely tied to place and, hence, are important for maintaining the edible diversity adapted to and found in places where humans exist (well established) {5.3.3}. Some cultural habits and traditions, however, can be unhealthy and unsustainable. Tackling the triple burden of malnutrition – underweight, hidden hunger and obesity – constitutes a major food challenge (well established) {5.3.2, 5.3.3, 5.3.4} requiring reducing under-nutrition for nearly 2 billion people, while halting growing trends of unhealthy overconsumption for another 2 billion (well established) {5.3.3.11}. Increasing consumption of a diversity of whole grains, fruits, seeds, vegetables and fish has a positive impact on all forms of malnutrition (well established) {5.3.3.11}. Shifting to healthy diets would avert 11 million premature adult deaths per year, while improving the sustainability of other nexus elements compared to business-as-usual (well established) {5.3.3, 5.3.4}. This includes significant reductions in greenhouse gas emissions and land required for food, notably red meat, production (well established) {5.3.3, 5.3.4}. Inclusive co-creation of such response options enhances the relevance, credibility and legitimacy of actions to tackle food diversity (well established) {5.3.1, 5.3.6} (Box 5.3.16).

Sustainable use of lands and waters allocated to food production are a determining factor not only in ensuring food security and nutritional security, but also in delivering on water, climate and environmental security (well established) {5.3.1}. As global lands and waters are not uniform, their use and management need to be adapted to variable contexts (well established) {5.3.3}. While trade-offs between nexus elements are inevitable, food producers of all types are uniquely placed to minimize these trade-offs or even promote co-benefits, such as habitat connectivity, carbon capture or improved water quality, through ecological intensification practices {5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6}. Sustainable intensification increases production efficiency while minimizing pollution (well established) {5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8}, potentially sparing ecosystems of high ecological integrity from conversion (well established) {5.3.3.1}. This response option is more likely to be effective when sustainable production practices directly benefit farmers and food producers (established but incomplete) {5.3.3}, when consumers are oriented to health and sustainability (established but incomplete) {5.3.3}, and when policy provides enabling conditions (well established) {5.3.2, 5.3.3, 5.3.4, 5.3.3.13, 5.3.3.16}. Achieving this will require contextualized and integrated food system policies and investments consistent with healthy diets and environmental sustainability (well established) {5.3.5, 5.3.6}. There is significant evidence that such policies and investments are currently inconsistent (well established) {5.3.5, 5.3.6}.

Aligning response options to maximize synergies between them can lead to stronger and more enduring benefits than if each response option is implemented in isolation (well established) {5.3.6, 5.6.3}. Response options for food need to apply ecological, social and economic concepts and principles to the design, management and responsible governance of food (well established) {5.3.4}. The response options identified recognize the importance of responsible and effective governance mechanisms across scales as well as circular economies that reconnect producers and consumers, providing innovative solutions for living within planetary boundaries while ensuring the social foundation for inclusive and sustainable development (established but incomplete) {5.3.4, 5.3.5, 5.3.3.13, 5.3.3.16}. Indigenous and local knowledge and lived experiences are important as part of co-creation processes informing the design of response options adapted to particular contexts (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.15}. Response options are available, such as those associated with the enabling environment (e.g., responsible governance, incentives) and supporting diversification (e.g., sustainable and ecological intensification) (well established) {5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.3.3.7; 5.3.3.13, 5.3.3.16}. Co-created knowledge that draws on Indigenous and local knowledge alongside global scientific knowledge {5.3.3.15}, as well as inclusion of values that reflect gender and generational differences {5.3.3.14}, can nurture an enabling environment and sensitivity to context that is particularly adaptive when facing global environmental disturbances (drought, fire, floods, extreme weather) of increasing frequency and intensity (well established) {5.3.4}.

Response options originating within food systems provide unique leverage points for delivering multiple co-benefits with biodiversity, water, health and climate, and support the achievement of multiple policy goals, such as the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (well established) {5.3.3}. Many context-specific nexus response options are available, feasible and cost-effective that can shift food’s impact on other nexus elements positively {5.3.3}. The sixteen food-oriented response options assessed can safeguard remaining biodiversity by halting the expansion of agricultural land into remaining wilderness areas and ecosystems of high ecological integrity (target 1 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework) (well established) {5.3.3}, restoring the productive capacity of degraded lands and water (target 2) (well established) {5.3.3} and reducing pollution pressures on biodiversity, notably nutrient and biocide pollution (target 7) (well established) {5.3.3}. Sharing space for biodiversity in food producing lands and waters is essential for ensuring habitat functions that support species and underpin nature’s contributions to people that are required for food production, such as pollination, pest regulation and pollution regulation services (target 10) (well established) {5.3.3}. In addition, some response options are much more effective when aligned or implemented in combination than in isolation (for example, aligning across food-based dietary guidelines, agroecological production of diversified healthy food, innovations and alliances for healthy food and market and implementation rules such as for public procurement). Others enable such alignment, such as response options related to Indigenous and local knowledge and gender inclusion (well established) {5.3.6}.

Food can be a transformative intervention across nexus elements (well established) {5.3.5}. There is clear evidence that this potential is only attained with interventions spanning across multiple response options including production {5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6}, reduced pollution {5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9}, food loss and waste {5.3.3.10}, healthy and sustainable consumption {5.3.3.11, 5.3.3.12} and increased resource reutilization {5.3.3.10, 5.3.3.12} (well established). There is similarly clear evidence that the more difficult changes in individual and collective behaviours, notably reduced unhealthy and unsustainable overconsumption of red meat and sugar, are required for this potential to be achieved (well established) {5.3.3}. Costs of action can be high, but significantly less than the cost of inaction and can generate both savings and profits (well established) {5.3.3}. Food system actor motivation for transformation is mixed, with significant inequity in responsibilities and capacities to act (established but incomplete) {5.3.5}. This motivation can be enhanced by increasing investments that promote environmentally-sound technologies and expanding agricultural research systems and access to training, particularly in developing countries. In addition, it can be enhanced by the integration and alignment of policies both across aspects of the food system (from production to consumption) and across the different elements of the nexus (established but incomplete) {5.3.6}.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Options for delivering sustainable food systems" .

