    ipbes:Report ipbes:NXS24 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """A wide range of response options are available to actors in all five elements of the nexus, based on the evidence reviewed by the subchapters of chapter 5 (well established) {5.1, 5.2, 5.3 5.4, 5.5}. Response options are actions or policies that help advance governance and management of one or more elements of the nexus {5.0.1}. Expert teams identified a representative set of over 70 response options that involve a range of actors, spatial and temporal scales, and feasibility levels. These response options were assessed using thorough reviews of available evidence to inform scores for a set of common criteria. Criteria included potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, feasibility and breadth of applicability, impact on equity and potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks {5.0.3}. Many response options identified have multiple positive and synergistic effects across the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change (well established) {5.1.3, 5.2.3, 5.3.3, 5.4.3, 5.5.3}. Response options thus provide an opportunity to advance an integrated, collaborative approach to managing nexus elements holistically (established but incomplete) {5.1.4, 5.2.4, 5.3.4, 5.4.4, 5.5.4}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.0.

Context-tailored combinations of nature- and people-oriented conservation, sustainable use and restoration of ecosystems and their biodiversity are critical for achieving co-benefits with water, food, health and climate (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Response options for biodiversity have proven technological feasibility and are already implemented globally, delivering positive outcomes for multiple nexus elements and addressing drivers of biodiversity degradation (well established) {5.1.3}. Complementarities across response options can enhance their efficacy or overcome trade-offs at landscape to regional scales (well established) {5.1.3, 5.1.6}. For example, integrated landscape and seascape approaches combine a number of multi-stakeholder initiatives for area-based conservation alongside multiple restoration and sustainable use actions within a region, amplifying environmental, economic and social outcomes {5.1.3.9}. Land and sea planning also enables synergistic benefits for health from strategic implementation of response options, such as area-based conservation, rewilding and reconnecting people with nature (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.14, 5.1.6}. The success of response options in achieving nexus-wide benefits largely depends on their implementation (established but incomplete) {5.1.3, 5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Equity and social acceptance are strong enablers of biodiversity response options if collaborative networks involving actors with different interests and addressing power asymmetries are in place; otherwise, these enablers become barriers to implementation and may lead to trade-offs with other nexus elements (well established) {5.1.4, 5.1.6}. Moreover, biodiversity response options rely on a broad diversity of actors with complementary roles, thus appropriate governance mechanisms and collaboration opportunities can strengthen institutional capacities for coordination and negotiation (well established) {5.1.2, 5.1.4}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.1.

A range of technologically feasible, sociopolitically acceptable and equitable response options for actors in the water sector exist that benefit biodiversity, nature’s contributions to people and food, health and climate (well established) {5.2.3}. Two-thirds of the response options assessed have a positive impact on the state and function of biodiversity (established but incomplete) {5.2.3}. Yet, multiple human demands on water, such as through hydropower, urban infrastructure, and irrigation and drinking water, are often in conflict with conserving freshwater biodiversity. Response options can address these conflicts, such as by reducing pollution, managing invasive alien species and implementing sustainable inland fisheries, leading to improvements in ecological function and resilience and providing nexus-wide benefits. Aligning these options with the rights of nature approach has the potential for providing benefits for both biodiversity and people, provided the underpinning knowledge is inclusive {5.2.3.7} (established but incomplete). All water-related response options contribute to achieving at least one Sustainable Development Goal and Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework target. Feasible response options for water can be implemented across spatial and temporal scales but require a range of actors to engage across policy domains, governance levels and public, private and civic spheres, including water ministries and commissions across governments, regional institutions, Indigenous Peoples and Local Communities, water utilities, local lake associations, water-focused non-governmental organizations and scientific organizations (established but incomplete) {5.2.2, 5.2.3, 5.2.5}. This is enabled by shifting from single sector governance to nexus governance that engages across actor domains to address trade-offs and synergies (established but incomplete) {5.2.5}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.2.

Current food production and consumption patterns generate significant harm to human and environmental health (well established) {5.3.3}. Bundling and alignment of response options for food that seek synergies and address trade-offs leads to stronger and more enduring nexus-wide benefits (well established) {5.3.4, 5.3.5}. For example, agroecology provides a holistic approach, integrating across ecological, social and economic dimensions of the whole food system to deliver more sustainable outcomes (well established) {5.3.1}. Effective response options for food apply ecological, social and economic concepts and principles to the design, management and responsible governance of food (well established) (5.3.4}. Holistic combinations of multiple response options for food can include halting conversion of natural ecosystems and restoring soil health, sustainable and ecological intensification of production, reduced pollution, reduced food loss and waste, healthy and sustainable consumption, and increased resource reutilization (well established) {5.3.3}. Improving the enabling environment and adaptation to context are critical needs when combining response options to enable food systems to be sustainably reoriented to benefit nexus elements (well established) {5.3.3}. Changes in individual and collective behaviours, notably reduced unhealthy and unsustainable overconsumption, are fundamental components for this potential to be achieved (well established) {5.3.3}. Costs of action for some response options can be high but are cost-effective when compared to the costs of inaction, and many can generate savings and increase profits (well established) {5.3.3}. However, the motivation of actors and actions for transformation is mixed, with significant inequity in responsibilities and capacities to act (established but incomplete) {5.3.6}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.3.

Attainment of good health over the long-term requires attention to interlinkages with food, water, biodiversity and climate change, decision-supportive evidence and expansion of existing response options for the health sector to produce more nexus-oriented outcomes (well established) {5.4.1, 5.4.3}. Attainment of good health for all builds on respect and support for culturally diverse views on health and traditional medicines, while reducing burden of disease, unsustainable use of nature and zoonotic risk; decreasing environmental health risks such as pollution; lowering the environmental footprint of diets and healthcare including by shifting emphasis to prevention and primary care; and promoting nature’s benefits to health (well established) {5.4.3}. Existing response options are technically feasible and extensively support global policy frameworks such as the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and its Sustainable Development Goals, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement (well established) {5.4.6}. Indigenous Peoples’ and local communities’ values of health and nature and contemporary developments in health that promote optimal outcomes for humans, plants, animals and the environment (e.g., the One Health and other integrative approaches) enhance health actions that have nexus-wide benefits. Health governance for genuine cross-sectoral collaboration exists at international and many national scales and can be mobilized to increase, bundle and scale-up response options (established but incomplete) {5.4.4, 5.4.6}. To progress, more evidence-based health case studies are recommended as extensive data collection of multisector-relevant metrics across the nexus are not yet regularly undertaken (well established) {5.4.5}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.4.

Climate change threatens all nexus elements, but response options are available that facilitate rapid transitions in energy use and production, land, marine and freshwater ecosystem management, urban areas, industrial activities and society for enabling climate change mitigation, adaptation and sustainable development (well established) {5.5.3}. All the selected climate change response options deliver climate change adaptation, mitigation or both, and 4 out of 15 response options contribute positively to all other nexus elements. The remaining options have a range of both positive and negative effects, but most contribute positively to multiple elements (well established) {5.5.5}. Some response options enable the successful implementation of others, highlighting that bundling and sequencing of response options can help deliver transformative change (well established) {5.5.6}. For example, the reduced pressure on land arising from shifting to sustainable health diets in overconsuming regions and/or through sustainable intensification can enable more sustainable forms of farming such as agroecology, which in turn can free up land for other land-based climate change response options, such as forest-based climate solutions, wetland restoration and conservation, and land-based renewable energy, such as solar photovoltaics {5.5.6}. All climate change response options contribute to delivery of the Paris Agreement, support the delivery of at least two Sustainable Development Goals with some contributing to eight, and all contribute to achieving between 2 and 14 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets (well established) {5.5.6}. Participative, rights-based governance, innovations, partnerships and commitments to support global equity and climate justice in line with existing global agreements enhance success. Effective governance for climate action – integrating rights, ethics, values and world views – necessitates synchronized collaboration of state and non-state actors to address climate change through a mix of economic, financial, legal and regulatory instruments (well established) {5.5.4}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.5.

Response options are widely available and feasible across biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. They have strong synergistic effects across nexus elements and can help transform the relationships between people and nature to secure a sustainable and just future for all (established but incomplete) {5.6.7}. The response options can be clustered into ten general categories of actions (conserve and halt conversion of remaining intact ecosystems, restore natural and semi-natural ecosystems, manage ecosystem functions in human-dominated ecosystems, consume sustainably, reduce pollution and waste, integrate planning and governance, manage risk, ensure rights and equity, align financing, and an “others” category) that involve similar actors across nexus elements. Eleven response options were independently identified by two or more subchapters; these shared response options are viable options for actors in multiple elements that often act on their own (established but incomplete) {5.6.3}. Response options interact with each other, either by facilitating impacts through synergies or impeding effectiveness through trade-offs. Coordinating implementation by bundling or sequencing response options can manage these trade-offs, but most response options are currently designed and implemented in isolation (well established) {5.6.4}. Broader enabling conditions strongly influence the likelihood of nexus-wide benefits. Policy design and implementation are the most common enablers for the response options assessed, while financial and material resources are the most common barriers {5.6.5}. Some attributes of response options (e.g., equity) are associated with greater benefits across nexus elements, while other attributes (e.g., feasibility, economic costs) do not appear to be related to nexus-wide impacts {5.6.5.2}. Response options are also well aligned with three global policy frameworks, individually supporting 2 to 10 Sustainable Development Goals, 1 to 16 targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and either or both the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for climate change mitigation and adaptation. Response options can therefore serve as mechanisms to integrate efforts across these global policy objectives (established but incomplete) {5.6.7}. Further information is provided in subchapter 5.6.""" ;
    ipbes:hasDoi "10.5281/zenodo.13850309" ;
    dcterms:identifier "05" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Options for delivering sustainable approaches" ;
